window.MM.biochem=[
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following is TRUE when comparing DNA synthesis to RNA synthesis?","a":"Both use ribonucleotides as substrate","b":"DNA synthesis is usually semiconservative while RNA synthesis is not.","c":"DNA polymerases initiate synthesis without a primer while RNA polymerases require primers","d":"RNA synthesis occurs only during S phase of cell cycle","ans":"B","e":"DNA replication is semiconservative, each daughter helix keeping one parental strand; RNA synthesis copies only selected genes and does not conserve strands. Meselson and Stahl proved this using density-gradient centrifugation of nitrogen-labelled DNA.<br><br>Take Notes: DNA replication = semiconservative | RNA synthesis is selective, not conservative | Meselson-Stahl proved this"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which enzyme synthesizes the RNA primer required for DNA replication?","a":"DNA ligase","b":"DNA primase","c":"DNA helicase","d":"Topoisomerase","ans":"B","e":"DNA primase synthesizes the short RNA primer that provides the free 3-OH group DNA polymerase needs to begin synthesis. DNA polymerase cannot start synthesis de novo, so without primase replication cannot begin.<br><br>Take Notes: Primase makes the RNA primer | Provides 3-OH for DNA pol | DNA pol cannot start de novo"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which DNA polymerase (in prokaryotes) is primarily responsible for leading-strand synthesis?","a":"DNA pol I","b":"DNA pol II","c":"DNA pol III","d":"DNA pol IV","ans":"C","e":"DNA polymerase III is the main replicative enzyme in prokaryotes, carrying out rapid, processive leading-strand synthesis. DNA polymerase I later removes its RNA primers and fills the gaps.<br><br>Take Notes: Pol III = main prokaryotic replicase | High processivity | Pol I removes primers"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which enzyme relieves superhelical tension ahead of the replication fork?","a":"Helicase","b":"Primase","c":"Topoisomerase","d":"Ligase","ans":"C","e":"Topoisomerase relieves the superhelical tension generated ahead of the fork by cutting and resealing DNA strands. Fluoroquinolones kill bacteria by targeting this enzyme (DNA gyrase), causing lethal DNA breaks.<br><br>Take Notes: Topoisomerase relieves supercoiling | Cuts and reseals DNA | Target of quinolones (gyrase)"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which enzyme joins Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand?","a":"DNA primase","b":"DNA ligase","c":"DNA polymerase III","d":"Exonuclease","ans":"B","e":"DNA ligase seals the nicks between Okazaki fragments by forming phosphodiester bonds on the lagging strand. It uses ATP (eukaryotes) or NAD (bacteria) to power bond formation, and defects impair lagging-strand joining.<br><br>Take Notes: Ligase joins Okazaki fragments | Forms phosphodiester bonds | Uses ATP or NAD"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"During transcription, which enzyme synthesizes RNA from a DNA template?","a":"DNA polymerase I","b":"RNA polymerase","c":"Ribosome","d":"Reverse transcriptase","ans":"B","e":"RNA polymerase transcribes RNA from a DNA template and, unlike DNA polymerase, needs no primer to start. This lack of a primer requirement is a key exam distinction from DNA polymerases.<br><br>Take Notes: RNA polymerase transcribes DNA to RNA | No primer required | Lacks strong proofreading"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following is a fundamental difference between DNA polymerases and RNA polymerases?","a":"RNA polymerases require a primer; DNA polymerases do not","b":"DNA polymerases have 5'→3' exonuclease activity universally; RNA polymerases do not","c":"DNA polymerases proofread using 3'→5' exonuclease activity (some), RNA polymerases generally lack strong proofreading","d":"DNA polymerases synthesize single-stranded products; RNA polymerases synthesize double-stranded products","ans":"C","e":"DNA polymerases proofread using 3-to-5 exonuclease activity, while RNA polymerases generally lack strong proofreading. This is why RNA transcripts carry more errors than replicated DNA, though transcription errors are not inherited.<br><br>Take Notes: DNA pol proofreads (3-to-5 exonuclease) | RNA pol does not | Explains higher RNA error rate"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which repair pathway directly reverses specific types of DNA damage without removing bases?","a":"Base excision repair","b":"Nucleotide excision repair","c":"Mismatch repair","d":"Direct reversal (e.g., photoreactivation by photolyase)","ans":"D","e":"Direct reversal repairs damage without excising bases, as photolyase splits UV-induced pyrimidine dimers using light energy. Placental mammals lack photolyase, so humans rely on nucleotide excision repair for the same lesions.<br><br>Take Notes: Direct reversal = no base removal | Photolyase splits dimers | Absent in placental mammals"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Thymine dimers are primarily caused by which environmental agent?","a":"Ionizing radiation (X-rays)","b":"Ultraviolet (UV) light","c":"Alkylating chemicals","d":"Reactive oxygen species","ans":"B","e":"Thymine dimers form when ultraviolet light covalently links adjacent pyrimidines, distorting the helix. These bulky dimers are removed by nucleotide excision repair, which is defective in xeroderma pigmentosum.<br><br>Take Notes: UV light causes thymine dimers | Covalent pyrimidine linkage | Repaired by NER in humans"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which pathway is chiefly responsible for removing bulky helix-distorting lesions such as thymine dimers in humans?","a":"Mismatch repair","b":"Nucleotide excision repair (NER)","c":"Base excision repair (BER)","d":"Homologous recombination","ans":"B","e":"Nucleotide excision repair removes bulky, helix-distorting lesions such as thymine dimers by excising a short oligonucleotide. Its failure underlies xeroderma pigmentosum, with extreme UV sensitivity and early skin cancers.<br><br>Take Notes: NER removes bulky helix-distorting lesions | Excises an oligonucleotide patch | Defective in xeroderma pigmentosum"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which protein complex in eukaryotes helps to open DNA around lesions during NER?","a":"MutSα","b":"TFIIH (containing XPB/XPD helicases)","c":"DNA glycosylase","d":"DNA pol δ","ans":"B","e":"TFIIH, carrying the XPB and XPD helicases, unwinds DNA around the lesion during nucleotide excision repair. Because TFIIH also functions in transcription, its defects can affect both repair and gene expression.<br><br>Take Notes: TFIIH opens DNA in NER | Contains XPB and XPD helicases | Also functions in transcription"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Cytosine deamination converts cytosine into which base, causing a U:G mismatch?","a":"Thymine","b":"Adenine","c":"Uracil","d":"Guanine","ans":"C","e":"Spontaneous deamination of cytosine yields uracil, creating a U:G mismatch that must be repaired. If left unrepaired, this uracil pairs with adenine and fixes a C-to-T transition after replication.<br><br>Take Notes: Cytosine deaminates to uracil | Forms U:G mismatch | Removed by uracil-DNA glycosylase"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which enzyme removes uracil from DNA created by cytosine deamination?","a":"Uracil-DNA glycosylase (UNG)","b":"DNA ligase","c":"Endonuclease V","d":"Photolyase","ans":"A","e":"Uracil-DNA glycosylase recognizes and removes uracil generated by cytosine deamination, initiating base excision repair. The resulting abasic site is then processed by AP endonuclease to complete base excision repair.<br><br>Take Notes: Uracil-DNA glycosylase removes uracil | Starts base excision repair | Creates an AP site"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which repair pathway usually fixes U:G mismatches resulting from cytosine deamination?","a":"Nucleotide excision repair","b":"Base excision repair","c":"Mismatch repair","d":"Direct reversal","ans":"B","e":"Base excision repair corrects small non-distorting lesions such as the U:G mismatch left by cytosine deamination. It handles small chemical lesions, unlike nucleotide excision repair which removes bulky distortions.<br><br>Take Notes: BER fixes U:G mismatches | Glycosylase then AP endonuclease | Small single-base lesions"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"A mismatched base (e.g., G paired with T) after replication is most commonly corrected by which system?","a":"Nucleotide excision repair","b":"Base excision repair","c":"Mismatch repair (MMR)","d":"Homologous recombination","ans":"C","e":"Mismatch repair corrects base-base mismatches like G:T that escape proofreading after replication. Its failure causes Lynch syndrome, marked by microsatellite instability and early colorectal cancer.<br><br>Take Notes: Mismatch repair fixes post-replication mismatches | Corrects proofreading escapes | Defective in Lynch syndrome"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"In bacteria, which proteins are central to mismatch recognition and initiation of MMR?","a":"MutS and MutL (and MutH)","b":"XPA and XPC","c":"DNA glycosylases","d":"BRCA1 and BRCA2","ans":"A","e":"In bacteria, MutS recognizes the mismatch, MutL couples the steps, and MutH nicks the unmethylated new strand. Strand discrimination relies on GATC methylation, so the newer unmethylated strand is corrected.<br><br>Take Notes: MutS recognizes mismatch | MutL couples, MutH nicks | Strand discrimination by methylation"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Lynch syndrome (HNPCC) is primarily due to germline defects in genes involved in which pathway?","a":"Nucleotide excision repair","b":"Base excision repair","c":"Non-homologous end joining","d":"Mismatch repair","ans":"D","e":"Lynch syndrome (HNPCC) arises from germline defects in mismatch repair genes, causing microsatellite instability. Screen colorectal tumours for microsatellite instability to detect this inherited mismatch-repair defect.<br><br>Take Notes: Lynch syndrome = mismatch repair defect | Microsatellite instability | Early colorectal cancer"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following genes is commonly mutated in Lynch syndrome?","a":"XPA","b":"MLH1","c":"OGG1","d":"POLG","ans":"B","e":"MLH1 is a core mismatch repair gene commonly mutated in Lynch syndrome. In sporadic tumours MLH1 is instead silenced by promoter hypermethylation rather than germline mutation.<br><br>Take Notes: MLH1 = Lynch syndrome gene | Mismatch repair | Also silenced by promoter methylation in sporadic cancers"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Xeroderma pigmentosum (XP) patients are deficient in which DNA repair pathway?","a":"Base excision repair","b":"Nucleotide excision repair","c":"Mismatch repair","d":"Direct reversal","ans":"B","e":"Xeroderma pigmentosum results from defective nucleotide excision repair, leaving UV lesions unrepaired. Complete sun avoidance is essential, since these patients develop skin cancers in childhood.<br><br>Take Notes: Xeroderma pigmentosum = defective NER | UV lesions accumulate | Extreme skin cancer risk"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"A patient with XP has extreme sensitivity to sunlight due to inability to repair what lesion?","a":"Single-strand breaks","b":"Thymine dimers and other UV-induced photoproducts","c":"Oxidized guanine (8-oxoG)","d":"Abasic sites","ans":"B","e":"XP patients cannot repair thymine dimers and other UV photoproducts, giving extreme photosensitivity. This explains their thousandfold increased risk of UV-induced skin malignancies.<br><br>Take Notes: XP cannot repair thymine dimers | UV photoproducts persist | Sun protection is essential"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following is a translesion synthesis polymerase that can bypass thymine dimers?","a":"Pol δ","b":"Pol η (eta)","c":"Pol I","d":"Pol III","ans":"B","e":"DNA polymerase eta is a translesion synthesis polymerase that accurately bypasses thymine dimers. Its loss produces the XP-variant phenotype despite intact nucleotide excision repair genes.<br><br>Take Notes: Pol eta bypasses thymine dimers | Translesion synthesis | Its loss causes XP variant"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Failure of which enzyme is specifically linked to XP variant (XP-V) phenotype?","a":"XPA protein","b":"DNA pol η (eta)","c":"XPC protein","d":"MSH2 protein","ans":"B","e":"Loss of DNA polymerase eta causes the XP variant (XP-V) phenotype, as dimers can no longer be bypassed accurately. This distinguishes XP variant from classic XP, where the NER machinery itself is defective.<br><br>Take Notes: Pol eta loss = XP variant | Error-prone dimer bypass | NER genes are intact in XP-V"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which strand discrimination signal is used by the mismatch repair system in E. coli to distinguish the newly synthesized strand?","a":"Presence of RNA primers only","b":"Methylation status of adenines in GATC sequences (hemimethylation)","c":"Presence of single-strand nicks only in leading strand","d":"3'→5' exonuclease activity","ans":"B","e":"E. coli mismatch repair identifies the new strand by transient hemimethylation of adenines in GATC sequences. After repair the new strand becomes methylated, resetting the system for the next replication cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Strand discrimination by GATC methylation | New strand is unmethylated | Directs repair to the error"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which enzyme carries out nick translation to remove RNA primers and replace them with DNA in E. coli?","a":"DNA polymerase I","b":"DNA polymerase III","c":"DNA ligase","d":"DNA gyrase","ans":"A","e":"DNA polymerase I performs nick translation in E. coli, removing RNA primers and replacing them with DNA. This nick-translation activity also fills the gaps left after primer removal.<br><br>Take Notes: Pol I removes RNA primers | Nick translation | 5-to-3 exonuclease activity"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which human DNA polymerase is primarily responsible for bulk DNA synthesis on both leading and lagging strands?","a":"DNA pol α","b":"DNA pol β","c":"DNA pol δ and DNA pol ε (delta and epsilon)","d":"DNA pol γ","ans":"C","e":"DNA polymerases delta and epsilon carry out the bulk of human genomic synthesis on the lagging and leading strands. Polymerase alpha only initiates synthesis, handing off to delta and epsilon for bulk elongation.<br><br>Take Notes: Pol delta and epsilon = bulk human synthesis | Lagging and leading strands | Pol alpha only primes"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Base excision repair (BER) is initiated by which of the following enzyme types?","a":"DNA ligases","b":"Nucleases that make dual incisions flanking lesions","c":"DNA glycosylases that remove damaged bases","d":"Helicases that unwind DNA","ans":"C","e":"Base excision repair is initiated by DNA glycosylases that recognize and excise a single damaged base. The glycosylase leaves an abasic site that AP endonuclease then cleaves.<br><br>Take Notes: BER begins with a glycosylase | Removes the damaged base | Creates an AP site"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which lesion is the classic substrate for base excision repair?","a":"Thymine dimer","b":"Uracil in DNA (from cytosine deamination)","c":"Double-strand break","d":"Interstrand crosslink","ans":"B","e":"Uracil arising from cytosine deamination is the classic substrate for base excision repair. Uracil-DNA glycosylase removes it, preventing fixation of a C-to-T mutation.<br><br>Take Notes: Uracil in DNA = classic BER substrate | From cytosine deamination | Uracil-DNA glycosylase acts"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Mismatch repair deficiency leads to which hallmark molecular feature in tumors?","a":"Microsatellite instability (MSI)","b":"Chromothripsis","c":"Large copy-number neutral LOH exclusively","d":"CpG island hypermethylation only","ans":"A","e":"Mismatch repair deficiency produces microsatellite instability, a hallmark of Lynch-associated and some sporadic tumors. Detecting microsatellite instability is therefore used to screen for Lynch syndrome.<br><br>Take Notes: MMR deficiency = microsatellite instability | Repeat-length changes | Screen colorectal tumors"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following best describes the mutagenic consequence of cytosine deamination if not repaired before replication?","a":"G:C → A:T transition (after replication)","b":"G:C → C:G transversion","c":"No change - cytosine deamination is silent","d":"Indels at microsatellite sequences","ans":"A","e":"Unrepaired cytosine deamination to uracil pairs with adenine at replication, causing a G:C to A:T transition. This is one of the most common spontaneous point mutations in the genome.<br><br>Take Notes: Cytosine deamination = G:C to A:T transition | Uracil pairs with adenine | Common mutation type"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which enzyme has 3'→5' exonuclease activity for proofreading?","a":"RNA polymerase II","b":"DNA polymerase III (and many high-fidelity DNA polymerases)","c":"DNA ligase I","d":"Topoisomerase II","ans":"B","e":"DNA polymerase III and other high-fidelity polymerases have 3-to-5 exonuclease activity for proofreading. This proofreading is the second fidelity layer after correct base selection.<br><br>Take Notes: Proofreading = 3-to-5 exonuclease | Removes mispaired bases | Raises replication fidelity"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following statements about transcription-coupled NER (TC-NER) is CORRECT?","a":"TC-NER repairs lesions only in non-transcribed DNA strands","b":"TC-NER preferentially repairs lesions in actively transcribed genes and is initiated when RNA polymerase II stalls at damage","c":"TC-NER does not involve CSA and CSB proteins in humans","d":"TC-NER is identical in mechanism to base excision repair","ans":"B","e":"Transcription-coupled NER preferentially repairs active genes and is triggered when RNA polymerase II stalls at damage. This coupling ensures actively transcribed genes are repaired preferentially, and its failure causes Cockayne syndrome.<br><br>Take Notes: TC-NER repairs transcribed strands | Triggered by stalled RNA pol II | Faster than global NER"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following proteins is part of eukaryotic global genomic NER lesion recognition?","a":"MSH2","b":"XPC (with RAD23B)","c":"UNG","d":"DNA pol β","ans":"B","e":"XPC with RAD23B recognizes helix-distorting lesions in global genomic NER. This global sensing step is what is lost in the XPC complementation group.<br><br>Take Notes: XPC-RAD23B = global genomic NER sensor | Detects helix distortion | Initiates repair"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which disease is characterized by defects in transcription-coupled NER components (CSA/CSB) and presents with neurodevelopmental defects and photosensitivity but not the high skin cancer risk of XP?","a":"Cockayne syndrome","b":"Bloom syndrome","c":"Ataxia telangiectasia","d":"Werner syndrome","ans":"A","e":"Cockayne syndrome results from defective transcription-coupled NER (CSA/CSB), causing neurodevelopmental defects and photosensitivity without the high cancer risk of XP. Unlike xeroderma pigmentosum, Cockayne patients do not have a markedly raised skin cancer risk.<br><br>Take Notes: Cockayne = TC-NER defect (CSA/CSB) | Photosensitivity, neurodegeneration | No major skin cancer excess"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"A single-strand break in DNA that leaves an abasic (AP) site is most likely repaired by which pathway?","a":"Base excision repair (BER)","b":"Nucleotide excision repair (NER)","c":"Mismatch repair (MMR)","d":"Homologous recombination (HR)","ans":"A","e":"An abasic (AP) site left by base loss is repaired by base excision repair, which fills and seals the gap. Base excision repair fills the single-base gap using polymerase and ligase.<br><br>Take Notes: AP site = base excision repair | AP endonuclease, polymerase, ligase | Single-base restoration"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Watson-Crick base pairing fidelity is most directly monitored and corrected during replication by which activity?","a":"5'→3' exonuclease activity","b":"3'→5' exonuclease (proofreading) activity of DNA polymerases","c":"DNA ligase sealing activity","d":"RNase H removing RNA primers","ans":"B","e":"Watson-Crick fidelity during replication is most directly corrected by the 3-to-5 exonuclease proofreading of DNA polymerases. Loss of this exonuclease sharply raises the mutation rate, as seen in proofreading-deficient cancers.<br><br>Take Notes: Proofreading corrects mispairs | 3-to-5 exonuclease | Second line after base selection"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which type of mutation is most commonly produced by UV-induced thymine dimers if not repaired and bypassed incorrectly?","a":"Frameshift mutation","b":"C→T transition at dipyrimidine sites","c":"Large deletions only","d":"A→T transversion exclusively","ans":"B","e":"Unrepaired or misbypassed thymine dimers most commonly cause C-to-T transitions at dipyrimidine sites. This UV signature mutation is a hallmark of sun-exposed skin cancers.<br><br>Take Notes: UV signature = C-to-T at dipyrimidines | From thymine dimers | Skin cancer mutation hotspot"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"During nucleotide excision repair, dual incisions flanking the lesion are made by which nucleases in humans?","a":"XPF-ERCC1 and XPG","b":"MutH and MutL","c":"FEN1 and DNA2","d":"Endonuclease V and glycosylase","ans":"A","e":"During NER, XPF-ERCC1 makes the 5-incision and XPG the 3-incision, excising the damaged oligonucleotide. These dual incisions release the damaged patch, which polymerase and ligase then replace.<br><br>Take Notes: XPF-ERCC1 and XPG = NER nucleases | Dual incision flanks lesion | Releases damaged patch"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which repair mechanism is primarily used to fix double-strand breaks using an undamaged sister chromatid as template?","a":"Non-homologous end joining (NHEJ)","b":"Homologous recombination (HR)","c":"Base excision repair","d":"Nucleotide excision repair","ans":"B","e":"Homologous recombination repairs double-strand breaks accurately using the sister chromatid as a template. Because it is template-based, it is error-free and confined to S and G2 phases.<br><br>Take Notes: HR repairs double-strand breaks | Uses sister chromatid template | Error-free, active in S/G2"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"A germline MLH1 mutation would most likely predispose to which of the following?","a":"Xeroderma pigmentosum","b":"Lynch syndrome (HNPCC) - early-onset colorectal cancer","c":"Fanconi anemia","d":"Ataxia-telangiectasia","ans":"B","e":"A germline MLH1 mutation predisposes to Lynch syndrome with early-onset colorectal cancer. Endometrial cancer is the second most common Lynch-associated tumour to remember.<br><br>Take Notes: MLH1 mutation = Lynch syndrome | Early colorectal cancer | Mismatch repair defect"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following proteins recognizes 8-oxoguanine (an oxidized guanine) and initiates repair?","a":"OGG1 glycosylase","b":"XPA protein","c":"DNA pol γ","d":"BRCA2","ans":"A","e":"OGG1 glycosylase recognizes 8-oxoguanine, an oxidized guanine, and initiates its base excision repair. It prevents G-to-T transversions by removing this common oxidative lesion.<br><br>Take Notes: OGG1 removes 8-oxoguanine | Oxidative damage | Prevents G-to-T transversions"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which DNA repair pathway is most often responsible for removing small, non-helix-distorting base lesions (e.g., oxidized/alkylated bases)?","a":"Nucleotide excision repair","b":"Base excision repair","c":"Mismatch repair","d":"Direct reversal only","ans":"B","e":"Base excision repair removes small, non-helix-distorting base lesions such as oxidized or alkylated bases. It is distinct from nucleotide excision repair, which handles bulky helix-distorting damage.<br><br>Take Notes: BER removes small base lesions | Oxidized and alkylated bases | Distinct from bulky-lesion NER"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes the rate-limiting step of eukaryotic DNA replication initiation at origins?","a":"DNA helicase only","b":"Origin recognition complex (ORC) recruiting licensing factors and CDC6/Cdt1 leading to MCM helicase loading","c":"DNA pol δ","d":"RNase H","ans":"B","e":"Eukaryotic replication initiation is licensed by the origin recognition complex recruiting CDC6 and Cdt1 to load MCM helicase. This licensing restricts replication to once per cell cycle, preventing re-replication.<br><br>Take Notes: ORC licenses origins | Loads MCM helicase via CDC6/Cdt1 | Ensures once-per-cycle replication"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which event directly allows DNA ligase to seal the nick after Okazaki fragment processing?","a":"Filling of the gap by DNA polymerase and removal of RNA primer leaving a 3'-OH and 5'-phosphate termini","b":"Methylation of the new strand","c":"Ubiquitination of PCNA","d":"Deamination of cytosines","ans":"A","e":"Removing the RNA primer and filling the gap leaves a 3-OH and 5-phosphate, allowing DNA ligase to seal the nick. Ligase then seals the final nick to complete the continuous strand.<br><br>Take Notes: Ligation needs 3-OH and 5-phosphate | After primer removal and gap filling | Ligase seals the nick"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following describes microhomology-mediated end joining (MMEJ)?","a":"Error-free DSB repair using sister chromatid","b":"Error-prone repair that uses short homologous sequences and often causes deletions","c":"A pathway identical to classical NHEJ","d":"A sub-pathway of BER","ans":"B","e":"Microhomology-mediated end joining is an error-prone repair using short homologies and often causing deletions. Because it uses microhomology, it frequently produces small deletions at the junction.<br><br>Take Notes: MMEJ = error-prone end joining | Uses microhomology | Causes deletions"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"The MutS homologs in humans (MSH2/MSH6) primarily recognize which type of mismatch?","a":"Large insertions only","b":"Single-base mismatches and small insertion-deletion loops","c":"Thymine dimers","d":"Oxidized bases exclusively","ans":"B","e":"Human MSH2/MSH6 (MutS-alpha) recognizes single-base mismatches and small insertion-deletion loops. Its failure causes Lynch syndrome through defective post-replication mismatch correction.<br><br>Take Notes: MSH2/MSH6 = MutS-alpha | Detects mismatches and small loops | Mismatch repair initiation"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"In eukaryotes, which DNA polymerase is associated with initiating DNA synthesis by synthesizing the RNA-DNA primer?","a":"DNA pol α (alpha) complexed with primase","b":"DNA pol β","c":"DNA pol ε","d":"DNA pol γ","ans":"A","e":"In eukaryotes, DNA polymerase alpha complexed with primase initiates synthesis by making the RNA-DNA primer. Its RNA-DNA primer is later extended by the high-fidelity delta and epsilon polymerases.<br><br>Take Notes: Pol alpha-primase initiates synthesis | Makes RNA-DNA primer | Handed off to pol delta/epsilon"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which molecular test is commonly used to detect microsatellite instability (MSI) in tumors?","a":"Southern blot only","b":"PCR-based amplification of microsatellite loci and comparison to normal tissue","c":"Sanger sequencing of telomerase gene only","d":"Western blot for MSH2 protein only","ans":"B","e":"Microsatellite instability is detected by PCR amplification of microsatellite loci compared with normal tissue. A shift in microsatellite length compared with normal tissue confirms mismatch-repair loss.<br><br>Take Notes: MSI detected by PCR of microsatellites | Compare tumor and normal | Screens for MMR defect"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following statements about DNA replication fidelity is CORRECT?","a":"Fidelity depends only on base-pairing rules and not on polymerase functions.","b":"Three layers of fidelity - nucleotide selectivity, proofreading (3'→5' exonuclease), and post-replicative mismatch repair - contribute to overall accuracy.","c":"Eukaryotic replication lacks proofreading entirely.","d":"Post-replicative mismatch repair increases error rates.","ans":"B","e":"Replication fidelity relies on three layers: nucleotide selectivity, proofreading, and post-replicative mismatch repair. Together these three layers reduce the error rate to roughly one in a billion bases.<br><br>Take Notes: Fidelity = selection, proofreading, mismatch repair | Three layers combine | Very low error rate"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which protein complex loads sliding clamp (PCNA) onto DNA in eukaryotic replication?","a":"RFC (replication factor C) clamp loader","b":"ORC complex","c":"DNA ligase IV complex","d":"RPA complex","ans":"A","e":"Replication factor C (RFC) is the clamp loader that loads the PCNA sliding clamp onto DNA in eukaryotes. The loaded PCNA clamp then tethers polymerase to DNA for processive synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: RFC loads PCNA clamp | Clamp loader | Boosts polymerase processivity"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following is NOT a common consequence of defective NER?","a":"Photosensitivity","b":"Increased skin cancer risk (e.g., XP)","c":"Immunodeficiency due to V(D)J recombination defects","d":"Neurological degeneration in some patients","ans":"C","e":"Defective NER causes photosensitivity and cancer, but not the V(D)J immunodeficiency caused by end-joining defects. So NER defects give skin cancer, while end-joining defects give immunodeficiency, a common exam contrast.<br><br>Take Notes: NER defect does not impair V(D)J | Immunodeficiency comes from NHEJ defects | NER = UV lesion repair"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which type of DNA damage is most directly caused by spontaneous hydrolytic deamination?","a":"Formation of cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers","b":"Cytosine → uracil and 5-methylcytosine → thymine conversions","c":"8-oxoG formation exclusively","d":"Interstrand crosslinks","ans":"B","e":"Spontaneous hydrolytic deamination converts cytosine to uracil and 5-methylcytosine to thymine. The 5-methylcytosine-to-thymine change explains why CpG sites are mutation hotspots.<br><br>Take Notes: Deamination: cytosine to uracil | 5-methylcytosine to thymine | Source of CpG mutations"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following best describes the initial step of base excision repair?","a":"Excision of an oligonucleotide ~30 nt by XPF/XPG","b":"Recognition and removal of altered base by DNA glycosylase creating an AP site","c":"Recruitment of MutS to the mismatch","d":"Photoreactivation by photolyase","ans":"B","e":"Base excision repair begins when a DNA glycosylase recognizes and removes the altered base, creating an AP site. AP endonuclease then nicks this site to continue base excision repair.<br><br>Take Notes: BER first step = glycosylase removes base | Forms AP site | Then AP endonuclease acts"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which laboratory finding would strongly suggest defective mismatch repair in a colorectal tumor?","a":"Low tumor mutational burden","b":"Microsatellite stability (MSS)","c":"Microsatellite instability-high (MSI-H) and loss of MSH2/MSH6 protein expression by IHC","d":"Absence of C>T transitions at CpG sites only","ans":"C","e":"MSI-high status with loss of MSH2/MSH6 on immunohistochemistry strongly indicates defective mismatch repair. Immunohistochemistry showing which protein is absent guides confirmatory germline testing.<br><br>Take Notes: MSI-high plus MMR protein loss = MMR deficiency | IHC identifies the missing protein | Suggests Lynch syndrome"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following enzymes is directly responsible for removing UV-induced cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers in bacteria via light-dependent repair?","a":"Photolyase (DNA photolyase)","b":"XPA","c":"DNA glycosylase","d":"DNA pol I","ans":"A","e":"Photolyase directly removes UV-induced cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers in bacteria using light-dependent repair. Placental mammals lack this enzyme, relying instead on nucleotide excision repair.<br><br>Take Notes: Photolyase splits pyrimidine dimers | Light-dependent | Absent in placental mammals"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following is TRUE about translesion synthesis (TLS) polymerases?","a":"They are high-fidelity and proofread frequently.","b":"They allow DNA replication to continue past lesions but are often error-prone.","c":"They are the main polymerases used during normal replication of undamaged DNA.","d":"TLS polymerases cannot incorporate nucleotides opposite abasic sites.","ans":"B","e":"Translesion synthesis polymerases let replication continue past lesions but are often error-prone. They allow replication to bypass lesions but at the cost of introducing mutations.<br><br>Take Notes: TLS polymerases bypass lesions | Often error-prone | Allow replication to proceed"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following proteins is frequently mutated in mismatch-repair-deficient colorectal cancers and causes the inherited Lynch phenotype when mutated germline?","a":"BRCA1","b":"MSH2","c":"XPC","d":"POLB","ans":"B","e":"MSH2 is frequently mutated in mismatch-repair-deficient colorectal cancers and causes Lynch syndrome when germline. A germline MSH2 mutation causes Lynch syndrome, like MLH1.<br><br>Take Notes: MSH2 = Lynch gene | Mismatch repair | Germline mutation predisposes to cancer"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which human disease is characterized by photosensitivity, pigmentation abnormalities, and a markedly increased risk of skin cancers due to defective NER genes (XPA-XPG)?","a":"Xeroderma pigmentosum","b":"Lynch syndrome","c":"Fanconi anemia","d":"Bloom syndrome","ans":"A","e":"Xeroderma pigmentosum presents with photosensitivity, pigmentation changes, and high skin cancer risk from defective NER genes XPA to XPG. Strict UV protection and skin surveillance are the cornerstones of management.<br><br>Take Notes: XP = defective NER (XPA-XPG) | Photosensitivity and skin cancer | Sun avoidance essential"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which DNA repair pathway is most active during the S phase and helps to correct replication-associated lesions?","a":"Homologous recombination (HR) and Mismatch repair (MMR) play key roles during/after replication","b":"Nucleotide excision repair only","c":"Non-homologous end joining only","d":"Photoreactivation only","ans":"A","e":"Homologous recombination and mismatch repair are most active around S phase, correcting replication-associated lesions. Both use the newly replicated strand or sister chromatid to correct errors accurately.<br><br>Take Notes: HR and MMR act during/after replication | S-phase repair | Use the template and correct errors"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which clinical test can be used to identify XP patients by detecting defective NER in cultured fibroblasts?","a":"Measurement of 8-oxoG by mass spectrometry only","b":"Unscheduled DNA synthesis (UDS) assay after UV irradiation","c":"Western blot for MLH1 only","d":"Karyotype analysis only","ans":"B","e":"An unscheduled DNA synthesis assay after UV irradiation identifies XP by detecting defective NER in fibroblasts. Reduced repair synthesis after UV in cultured fibroblasts confirms the diagnosis.<br><br>Take Notes: UDS assay diagnoses XP | Measures NER after UV | Reduced synthesis confirms defect"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"In prokaryotic replication, which enzyme unwinds the double helix at replication origin?","a":"DNA primase","b":"DNA helicase (DnaB in E. coli)","c":"DNA ligase","d":"DNA topoisomerase IV","ans":"B","e":"DNA helicase (DnaB in E. coli) unwinds the double helix at the replication origin. It creates the single-stranded templates that primase and polymerase then use.<br><br>Take Notes: DnaB helicase unwinds DNA | At the origin | Creates single-stranded templates"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of these is a direct consequence of a G→A transition at a codon that converts an amino-acid codon into a stop codon?","a":"Silent mutation","b":"Nonsense mutation leading to truncated protein","c":"Frameshift mutation","d":"Balanced translocation","ans":"B","e":"A G-to-A transition creating a premature stop codon is a nonsense mutation, producing a truncated protein. The premature stop codon yields a shortened, usually nonfunctional protein.<br><br>Take Notes: Nonsense mutation = premature stop | Truncated protein | Often nonfunctional"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following mechanisms contributes to the prevention of incorporation of ribonucleotides into DNA?","a":"Steric exclusion by DNA polymerases' active site","b":"RNase H1 proofreads nascent DNA for ribonucleotides","c":"Topoisomerase directly removes ribonucleotides","d":"NER eliminates ribonucleotides incorporated in DNA","ans":"A","e":"DNA polymerases exclude ribonucleotides mainly by steric exclusion at the active site, discriminating the 2-OH group. This sensing of the extra 2-OH group keeps ribonucleotides out of DNA.<br><br>Take Notes: Steric exclusion blocks ribonucleotides | Active site senses 2-OH | Maintains DNA composition"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which enzyme removes RNA primers in eukaryotes?","a":"DNA polymerase δ alone removes primers without help","b":"RNase H and FEN1 cooperate to remove RNA primers and flap structures","c":"DNA ligase directly removes primers","d":"Photolyase removes primers","ans":"B","e":"In eukaryotes, RNase H and FEN1 cooperate to remove RNA primers and displaced flap structures. This processing prepares the lagging strand for ligation into a continuous strand.<br><br>Take Notes: RNase H and FEN1 remove primers | Process lagging-strand flaps | Prepare for ligation"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which hallmark clinical feature is common in individuals with Lynch syndrome besides colorectal cancer?","a":"Early-onset endometrial cancer","b":"Severe photosensitivity only","c":"Immunodeficiency with recurrent infections","d":"Short stature and microcephaly","ans":"A","e":"Besides colorectal cancer, Lynch syndrome commonly features early-onset endometrial cancer. Endometrial cancer is the second most common tumour in this mismatch-repair syndrome.<br><br>Take Notes: Lynch syndrome = endometrial cancer too | Second most common tumor | Mismatch repair defect"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following enzymes is central to base excision repair gap-filling synthesis in eukaryotes?","a":"DNA pol β (in short-patch BER) and DNA pol δ/ε (in long-patch BER)","b":"DNA pol α only","c":"DNA pol γ only","d":"Reverse transcriptase only","ans":"A","e":"Base excision repair gap-filling uses DNA polymerase beta in short-patch and pol delta/epsilon in long-patch repair. Short-patch handles single nucleotides; long-patch replaces several, using the sliding clamp.<br><br>Take Notes: Pol beta = short-patch BER | Pol delta/epsilon = long-patch | Fills the excised gap"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following best explains how 5-methylcytosine deamination leads to mutation hotspots?","a":"5-methylcytosine deaminates to thymine, producing T:G mismatches that are less efficiently recognized and repaired, leading to C→T transitions at CpG sites.","b":"5-methylcytosine deaminates to uracil and is efficiently repaired with no mutations.","c":"5-methylcytosine is resistant to deamination.","d":"Deamination of 5-methylcytosine always causes large deletions.","ans":"A","e":"5-methylcytosine deaminates to thymine, producing T:G mismatches that are repaired poorly, causing C-to-T transitions at CpG sites. These T:G mismatches often escape repair, making CpG sites frequent mutation hotspots.<br><br>Take Notes: 5-methylcytosine to thymine | T:G mismatch escapes repair | CpG mutation hotspots"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following choices correctly pairs a repair disease with its primary defective pathway?","a":"Xeroderma pigmentosum - mismatch repair","b":"Lynch syndrome - nucleotide excision repair","c":"Ataxia-telangiectasia - double-strand break signaling/repair (ATM)","d":"Cockayne syndrome - mismatch repair","ans":"C","e":"Ataxia-telangiectasia is caused by defective ATM, impairing double-strand break signaling and repair. Affected children show cerebellar ataxia, telangiectasias, and marked radiosensitivity.<br><br>Take Notes: Ataxia-telangiectasia = ATM defect | Double-strand break signalling | Radiosensitivity and cancer"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which factor recruits nucleotide excision repair factors to global genomic lesions in humans?","a":"XPC complex recognizes helix-distorting lesions globally.","b":"MutH recognizes hemimethylated DNA.","c":"Photolyase recruits XPA.","d":"OGG1 recruits XPF-ERCC1.","ans":"A","e":"The XPC complex recognizes helix-distorting lesions globally, recruiting NER factors in global genomic repair. This global sensing step is the function lost in the XPC group of xeroderma pigmentosum.<br><br>Take Notes: XPC recognizes global genomic lesions | Detects helix distortion | Initiates global NER"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"A patient's tumor shows high MSI and loss of MLH1 by IHC. Which secondary mechanism commonly explains MLH1 loss in sporadic colorectal cancers?","a":"MLH1 promoter hypermethylation (epigenetic silencing) often associated with BRAF mutations","b":"Germline MLH1 deletion only","c":"XPA gene mutation","d":"Viral insertion into MLH1 coding sequence exclusively","ans":"A","e":"In sporadic colorectal cancer, MLH1 promoter hypermethylation, often with BRAF mutation, silences MLH1 and causes MSI. This epigenetic silencing, unlike Lynch syndrome, is somatic rather than germline.<br><br>Take Notes: Sporadic MSI = MLH1 promoter methylation | Often BRAF mutation | Epigenetic silencing, not germline"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which end-processing enzyme is critical for canonical non-homologous end joining (NHEJ)?","a":"DNA ligase IV (with XRCC4)","b":"DNA pol β only","c":"XPA","d":"MSH6","ans":"A","e":"DNA ligase IV, with XRCC4, is critical for sealing ends in canonical non-homologous end joining. Its role in joining broken ends also underlies V(D)J recombination in immune cells.<br><br>Take Notes: Ligase IV-XRCC4 = NHEJ ligation | Joins double-strand break ends | Error-prone but fast"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following is MOST directly responsible for recognizing base misincorporation during replication in eukaryotes?","a":"PCNA only","b":"MutSα (MSH2-MSH6 complex)","c":"XPG nuclease","d":"DNA glycosylase UNG only","ans":"B","e":"MutS-alpha (MSH2-MSH6) is most directly responsible for recognizing base misincorporation during eukaryotic replication. Its defect causes Lynch syndrome through failed post-replication mismatch correction.<br><br>Take Notes: MutS-alpha recognizes mismatches | MSH2-MSH6 complex | Initiates mismatch repair"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which DNA repair pathway is typically defective in patients with high sensitivity to interstrand crosslinking agents (e.g., cisplatin) and congenital bone marrow failure?","a":"Fanconi anemia pathway (interstrand crosslink repair)","b":"Nucleotide excision repair only","c":"Mismatch repair only","d":"Transcription-coupled NER only","ans":"A","e":"Fanconi anemia pathway defects cause hypersensitivity to interstrand crosslinking agents and congenital bone marrow failure. These patients also show short stature, marrow failure, and increased leukaemia risk.<br><br>Take Notes: Fanconi anemia = interstrand crosslink repair defect | Crosslinker sensitivity | Bone marrow failure"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which DNA glycosylase family member is specialized for removing oxidatively damaged bases such as 8-oxoG from DNA?","a":"UNG only","b":"OGG1 (8-oxoguanine glycosylase)","c":"XPG only","d":"POLB only","ans":"B","e":"OGG1 is the glycosylase specialized for removing oxidatively damaged bases such as 8-oxoguanine. Removing 8-oxoguanine prevents the G-to-T transversions it would otherwise cause.<br><br>Take Notes: OGG1 removes 8-oxoguanine | Oxidative base damage | Prevents G-to-T transversions"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following repair processes is most error-prone and therefore can increase mutagenesis when used?","a":"Homologous recombination","b":"Translesion synthesis (TLS) polymerases","c":"Mismatch repair","d":"Base excision repair","ans":"B","e":"Translesion synthesis is the most error-prone repair process and can increase mutagenesis when used. It is used only when accurate repair fails, trading survival for higher mutation risk.<br><br>Take Notes: Translesion synthesis = most error-prone | Bypasses lesions | Raises mutation rate"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which protein is an essential sensor kinase for the DNA double-strand break response in humans?","a":"ATM (ataxia telangiectasia mutated)","b":"XPA","c":"PCNA","d":"RNase H2","ans":"A","e":"ATM is the essential sensor kinase for the double-strand break response in humans. Its loss causes ataxia-telangiectasia, with radiosensitivity and cancer predisposition.<br><br>Take Notes: ATM = double-strand break sensor kinase | Activates checkpoint | Defective in ataxia-telangiectasia"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following best explains why CpG dinucleotides are mutation hotspots?","a":"Cytosines in CpG are often methylated to 5-methylcytosine which deaminates to thymine, creating T:G mismatches that can escape repair.","b":"CpG dinucleotides are protected and never mutate.","c":"CpG dinucleotides always form thymine dimers.","d":"CpG dinucleotides are targets for photolyase.","ans":"A","e":"CpG dinucleotides are mutation hotspots because methylated cytosines deaminate to thymine, creating T:G mismatches that often escape repair. This is why CpG islands accumulate C-to-T changes more than other sequences.<br><br>Take Notes: CpG hotspots from 5-methylcytosine deamination | T:G mismatch escapes repair | Frequent C-to-T changes"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"In the context of replication, what is the primary role of RPA (replication protein A) in eukaryotes?","a":"Cleave damaged bases","b":"Bind single-stranded DNA to protect it and coordinate replication/repair proteins","c":"Seal nicks between Okazaki fragments","d":"Methylate DNA at CpG sites","ans":"B","e":"RPA binds single-stranded DNA to protect it and coordinate replication and repair proteins. By coating exposed single strands, it prevents nucleases and secondary structures from forming.<br><br>Take Notes: RPA coats single-stranded DNA | Protects and organizes | Recruits repair factors"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following best describes how nucleotide excision repair restores DNA after dual incision?","a":"Gap is filled by DNA polymerase and nick sealed by DNA ligase","b":"Damaged base directly reversed by photolyase only","c":"RNA polymerase performs translesion synthesis filling the gap","d":"PCNA excises the lesion and degrades it completely","ans":"A","e":"After dual incision in NER, DNA polymerase fills the gap and DNA ligase seals the nick. This final fill-and-seal step restores an intact, error-free duplex.<br><br>Take Notes: NER gap filled by polymerase | Ligase seals the nick | Restores intact DNA"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following tests is recommended to screen for Lynch syndrome in colorectal tumors?","a":"MSI testing and/or immunohistochemistry (IHC) for MMR proteins","b":"Karyotype alone","c":"Southern blot for telomeric repeats","d":"UDS assay only","ans":"A","e":"Lynch syndrome is screened in colorectal tumors by MSI testing and/or immunohistochemistry for mismatch repair proteins. Loss of the relevant protein on immunohistochemistry helps localise the defective gene.<br><br>Take Notes: Lynch screening = MSI plus IHC | Detect protein loss | Then confirm with germline testing"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following repair pathways is evolutionarily most ancient and widespread (present in bacteria to humans) for removing single-base lesions like uracil?","a":"Nucleotide excision repair","b":"Base excision repair (BER)","c":"Mismatch repair only in eukaryotes","d":"Homologous recombination only in eukaryotes","ans":"B","e":"Base excision repair is the most ancient and widespread pathway, removing single-base lesions like uracil across all life. Its conservation from bacteria to humans reflects how essential single-base repair is.<br><br>Take Notes: BER is evolutionarily ancient | Bacteria to humans | Removes single-base lesions"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which event most directly triggers DNA damage checkpoint activation in eukaryotic cells?","a":"Accumulation of single-stranded DNA and DNA breaks sensed by ATR/ATM leading to Chk1/Chk2 signaling","b":"Presence of RNA primers only","c":"Excess ribonucleotides in DNA only","d":"Methylation of histones exclusively","ans":"A","e":"Single-stranded DNA and breaks sensed by ATR/ATM activate Chk1/Chk2 signalling, triggering the DNA damage checkpoint. This checkpoint halts the cycle to allow repair before damaged DNA is replicated.<br><br>Take Notes: ATR/ATM sense DNA damage | Activate Chk1/Chk2 | Halt the cell cycle"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following proteins is NOT a canonical XP (xeroderma pigmentosum) group protein?","a":"XPA","b":"XPC","c":"MLH1","d":"XPD","ans":"C","e":"MLH1 is a mismatch repair protein, not a canonical xeroderma pigmentosum (XP) group protein. MLH1 belongs to mismatch repair, so it is the odd one out among XP proteins in exams.<br><br>Take Notes: MLH1 = mismatch repair, not XP | XP proteins are XPA-XPG | Different pathway"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following repair defects is most likely to present with early childhood photosensitivity and skin cancer predisposition?","a":"Germline defects in NER genes (XPA-XPG) - Xeroderma pigmentosum","b":"Lynch syndrome only in adults","c":"BRCA1 mutation only leads to photosensitivity","d":"OGG1 mutations cause immediate photosensitivity in childhood","ans":"A","e":"Germline NER defects (XPA-XPG) cause xeroderma pigmentosum with early photosensitivity and skin cancer. These children develop skin cancers early, making sun protection lifelong and essential.<br><br>Take Notes: NER defect = xeroderma pigmentosum | Early photosensitivity | Skin cancer predisposition"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"During lagging-strand replication, what structure must be removed by flap endonuclease 1 (FEN1)?","a":"RNA primer flap generated during displacement synthesis","b":"Double-strand break ends only","c":"Single-strand nicks on leading strand only","d":"Telomeric repeats only","ans":"A","e":"During lagging-strand synthesis, FEN1 removes the RNA primer flap generated by displacement synthesis. FEN1 loss impairs Okazaki fragment maturation, causing replication stress.<br><br>Take Notes: FEN1 removes primer flaps | Lagging-strand processing | Prepares for ligation"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following enzymes plays a central role in mitochondrial DNA replication?","a":"DNA pol γ (gamma)","b":"DNA pol α only","c":"DNA pol δ only","d":"Pol η only","ans":"A","e":"DNA polymerase gamma replicates mitochondrial DNA. Because it is the sole mitochondrial polymerase, its inhibition underlies some nucleoside-analogue toxicities.<br><br>Take Notes: Pol gamma = mitochondrial DNA | Only mitochondrial polymerase | Target of some nucleoside analogues"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following describes an actionable prevention strategy for patients with Xeroderma pigmentosum?","a":"Strict protection from UV exposure and regular dermatologic surveillance for skin cancers","b":"Exposure to sunlight to increase pigmentation only","c":"Use of alkylating agents prophylactically","d":"No special measures required","ans":"A","e":"XP patients need strict UV protection and regular dermatologic surveillance for skin cancers. Skin cancers still arise despite these measures, so surveillance must be lifelong.<br><br>Take Notes: XP management = UV avoidance | Regular skin surveillance | Prevents and detects skin cancers"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which repair protein interaction is MOST important to coordinate MMR excision from the nick to the mismatch in humans?","a":"PCNA interacting with MutLα (MLH1-PMS2) to coordinate excision/repair","b":"XPA interacting with TFIIH only","c":"OGG1 binding to XPF only","d":"Photolyase interacting with RNase H2 only","ans":"A","e":"PCNA interacting with MutL-alpha (MLH1-PMS2) coordinates excision from the nick to the mismatch in human MMR. The strand nick provides directionality, ensuring the new strand is the one corrected.<br><br>Take Notes: PCNA-MutL-alpha coordinates MMR | Directs excision to the mismatch | Uses the strand nick"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"A patient with tumors showing MSI-H responds well to which class of cancer therapy in many studies?","a":"Immune checkpoint inhibitors (e.g., anti-PD-1) due to high neoantigen load in MSI-H tumors","b":"Broad-spectrum antibiotics only","c":"Phototherapy exclusively","d":"Antioxidants only","ans":"A","e":"MSI-high tumors carry a high neoantigen load and often respond well to immune checkpoint inhibitors such as anti-PD-1. This high neoantigen load is why MSI-high tumours respond well to checkpoint inhibitors.<br><br>Take Notes: MSI-high = high neoantigen load | Responds to checkpoint inhibitors | Anti-PD-1 therapy"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following is a direct enzymatic repair of alkylated guanine (O6-methylguanine) by transfer of the alkyl group to a cysteine residue on a protein?","a":"O6-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase (MGMT) - direct repair via suicide enzyme","b":"XPG nuclease action","c":"OGG1 glycosylase only","d":"DNA ligase direct reversal","ans":"A","e":"MGMT directly repairs O6-methylguanine by transferring the alkyl group to its own cysteine, a suicide enzyme. Because it is consumed in the reaction, MGMT is a single-use suicide enzyme.<br><br>Take Notes: MGMT repairs O6-methylguanine | Direct alkyl transfer | Suicide enzyme, consumed once"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following is a common diagnostic feature in XP patients at a molecular level?","a":"Defective NER leading to reduced unscheduled DNA synthesis (UDS) after UV exposure","b":"Excessive mismatch repair activity only","c":"Elevated photolyase activity only","d":"Elevated mitochondrial DNA replication only","ans":"A","e":"A common molecular feature in XP is reduced unscheduled DNA synthesis after UV exposure, reflecting defective NER. This reduced repair synthesis after UV is used diagnostically for xeroderma pigmentosum.<br><br>Take Notes: XP = reduced UDS after UV | Reflects defective NER | Diagnostic marker"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following enzymes synthesizes the bulk of mitochondrial DNA?","a":"DNA pol γ (gamma)","b":"DNA pol δ only","c":"DNA pol ε only","d":"DNA pol β only","ans":"A","e":"DNA polymerase gamma synthesizes the bulk of mitochondrial DNA. Being distinct from nuclear polymerases, it is the target of certain antiviral drug toxicities.<br><br>Take Notes: Pol gamma = mitochondrial DNA synthesis | Sole mitochondrial replicase | Distinct from nuclear polymerases"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following best describes the role of PCNA in DNA metabolism?","a":"Sliding clamp that increases DNA polymerase processivity and coordinates repair factors","b":"Helicase unwinding DNA strands","c":"Nuclease cutting out damaged oligonucleotides","d":"Ligase sealing Okazaki fragments","ans":"A","e":"PCNA is a sliding clamp that increases DNA polymerase processivity and coordinates repair factors. Beyond replication, it also serves as a scaffold coordinating repair proteins.<br><br>Take Notes: PCNA = sliding clamp | Boosts processivity | Also a repair scaffold"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"What is the immediate molecular product after a DNA glycosylase removes a damaged base?","a":"Double-strand break","b":"Abasic (AP) site","c":"Thymine dimer","d":"Unrepaired mismatch only","ans":"B","e":"When a DNA glycosylase removes a damaged base, the immediate product is an abasic (AP) site. AP endonuclease then processes this abasic site to continue base excision repair.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycosylase leaves an AP site | Missing base, intact backbone | AP endonuclease acts next"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which human disease is associated with Bloom syndrome helicase (BLM) mutations and high sister chromatid exchange rates?","a":"Bloom syndrome - linked to defects in RecQ helicase, genomic instability and cancer predisposition","b":"Xeroderma pigmentosum only","c":"Lynch syndrome only","d":"Fanconi anemia only","ans":"A","e":"Bloom syndrome arises from RecQ (BLM) helicase defects, causing genomic instability, high sister chromatid exchange, and cancer risk. The high sister-chromatid exchange rate is a diagnostic cytogenetic feature.<br><br>Take Notes: Bloom syndrome = BLM helicase defect | High sister chromatid exchange | Cancer predisposition"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following events is MOST likely to cause a G:C → A:T transition?","a":"Cytosine deamination to uracil followed by replication (if unrepaired)","b":"Direct reversal of O6-methylguanine only","c":"Double-strand break repair exclusively","d":"Thymine dimer formation only","ans":"A","e":"Cytosine deamination to uracil, followed by unrepaired replication, most commonly causes a G:C to A:T transition. This is among the most common spontaneous transitions occurring in the genome.<br><br>Take Notes: Cytosine deamination = G:C to A:T | Uracil pairs with adenine | Classic transition"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which repair pathway would be primarily responsible for removing bulky DNA adducts formed by chemical carcinogens (e.g., benzo[a]pyrene adducts)?","a":"Nucleotide excision repair (NER)","b":"Base excision repair (BER)","c":"Mismatch repair (MMR)","d":"Photoreactivation only","ans":"A","e":"Nucleotide excision repair removes bulky DNA adducts formed by chemical carcinogens such as benzo[a]pyrene. The same pathway repairs UV dimers, linking chemical and UV carcinogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: NER removes bulky adducts | Chemical carcinogen lesions | Same pathway as UV dimers"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following best explains how mismatch repair reduces mutation rates after replication?","a":"By remethylating the daughter strand","b":"By recognizing and excising mismatches in the newly synthesized strand and resynthesizing the correct sequence","c":"By directly ligating incorrect bases into place","d":"By accelerating replication fork speed only","ans":"B","e":"Mismatch repair reduces mutation rates by excising mismatches in the new strand and resynthesizing the correct sequence. This new-strand correction is what makes mismatch repair reduce replication errors.<br><br>Take Notes: MMR excises new-strand mismatches | Resynthesizes correctly | Post-replication correction"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following is a recognized molecular cause for sporadic microsatellite instability-high colorectal cancers?","a":"Promoter hypermethylation of MLH1 leading to loss of MLH1 expression","b":"Germline MLH1 mutation in all sporadic cases exclusively","c":"Photolyase deficiency only","d":"XPA mutation only","ans":"A","e":"Sporadic MSI-high colorectal cancer commonly results from MLH1 promoter hypermethylation silencing MLH1. Unlike Lynch syndrome, this silencing is epigenetic and somatic rather than inherited.<br><br>Take Notes: Sporadic MSI = MLH1 promoter methylation | Epigenetic silencing | Not a germline mutation"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"In the canonical eukaryotic replication fork, which of the following proteins stabilizes single-stranded DNA and prevents secondary structure formation?","a":"RPA (replication protein A)","b":"PCNA","c":"DNA ligase IV","d":"XPG","ans":"A","e":"RPA stabilizes single-stranded DNA at the replication fork and prevents secondary structure formation. By preventing secondary structures, it keeps the template accessible to the polymerase.<br><br>Take Notes: RPA stabilizes single-stranded DNA | Prevents secondary structures | Coordinates fork proteins"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following statements accurately contrasts DNA replication with transcription?","a":"Both use the same polymerases and proceed at the same fidelity.","b":"Replication copies the entire genome once per cell cycle with high fidelity (including proofreading and MMR), while transcription selectively synthesizes RNA from genes without extensive proofreading and with lower fidelity.","c":"Transcription is semiconservative while replication is not.","d":"Only transcription requires a primer.","ans":"B","e":"Replication copies the whole genome once per cycle with high fidelity, while transcription selectively makes RNA with lower fidelity and no extensive proofreading. So proofreading and high fidelity, seen in replication, are the key contrast with transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: Replication = whole genome, high fidelity | Transcription = selective, lower fidelity | Proofreading distinguishes them"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which tumor suppressor protein is known as the \"guardian of the genome\" due to its role in DNA damage response?","a":"Rb","b":"p53","c":"Cyclin D","d":"CDK4","ans":"B","e":"p53 is the guardian of the genome, arresting the cycle for DNA repair or triggering apoptosis after damage. It is the most commonly mutated gene in human cancers, and its loss removes a key tumour-suppressor checkpoint.<br><br>Take Notes: p53 = guardian of the genome | Arrest, repair, or apoptosis | Most commonly mutated tumor suppressor"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Activation of p53 following DNA damage primarily leads to which immediate cellular outcome?","a":"Entry into S phase","b":"Cell cycle arrest at G1/S checkpoint","c":"Increased ribosomal RNA synthesis","d":"Activation of telomerase","ans":"B","e":"Activated p53 after DNA damage causes cell cycle arrest at the G1/S checkpoint, allowing repair before replication. This pause gives time to fix DNA before replication, preventing mutations from being copied.<br><br>Take Notes: p53 arrests at G1/S | Time for DNA repair | Via p21 induction"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which gene product inhibits cyclin-dependent kinases in a p53-dependent manner?","a":"p21","b":"p16","c":"E2F","d":"MDM2","ans":"A","e":"p21, a p53 target, inhibits cyclin-dependent kinases to enforce cell cycle arrest. By blocking cyclin-CDK complexes, p21 keeps Rb hypophosphorylated and cells in G1.<br><br>Take Notes: p21 inhibits CDKs | p53-dependent | Enforces G1 arrest"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Loss of Rb protein function most directly results in dysregulation of which transcription factor?","a":"p53","b":"NF-κB","c":"E2F","d":"STAT3","ans":"C","e":"Loss of Rb function releases E2F, driving unregulated entry into S phase. This is why HPV E7, which inactivates Rb, drives uncontrolled proliferation.<br><br>Take Notes: Rb loss frees E2F | Drives S-phase entry | Uncontrolled proliferation"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"The hypophosphorylated form of Rb protein primarily functions to:","a":"Promote apoptosis","b":"Inhibit transcription of S-phase genes","c":"Activate DNA polymerase","d":"Degrade cyclins","ans":"B","e":"Hypophosphorylated Rb binds E2F and inhibits transcription of S-phase genes, holding cells in G1. Phosphorylation of Rb by cyclin-CDK releases E2F and permits S-phase entry.<br><br>Take Notes: Active (hypophosphorylated) Rb binds E2F | Blocks S-phase genes | Phosphorylation releases E2F"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which viral oncoprotein binds and inactivates p53?","a":"HPV E6","b":"HPV E7","c":"EBV EBNA","d":"HBx","ans":"A","e":"HPV E6 binds and promotes degradation of p53, removing its tumor-suppressor function. With p53 degraded, damaged cells escape apoptosis, a key step in cervical carcinogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: HPV E6 inactivates p53 | Promotes p53 degradation | Drives cervical carcinogenesis"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which viral protein promotes cell cycle progression by inactivating Rb?","a":"HPV E6","b":"HPV E7","c":"SV40 T antigen","d":"Adenovirus E1A","ans":"B","e":"HPV E7 inactivates Rb, releasing E2F and promoting cell cycle progression. Together with E6 inactivating p53, this explains how high-risk HPV transforms cells.<br><br>Take Notes: HPV E7 inactivates Rb | Releases E2F | Promotes proliferation"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"p53-mediated apoptosis is primarily initiated through activation of which pathway?","a":"Death receptor (extrinsic) pathway","b":"Mitochondrial (intrinsic) pathway","c":"Lysosomal pathway","d":"Autophagy pathway","ans":"B","e":"p53-mediated apoptosis proceeds mainly through the mitochondrial (intrinsic) pathway. Mitochondrial cytochrome c release then activates caspase-9 to execute cell death.<br><br>Take Notes: p53 apoptosis = intrinsic pathway | Mitochondrial cytochrome c release | Activates caspase-9"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which protein negatively regulates p53 under normal cellular conditions?","a":"ATM","b":"ATR","c":"MDM2","d":"p21","ans":"C","e":"MDM2 negatively regulates p53 under normal conditions by targeting it for degradation. DNA damage disrupts this interaction, allowing p53 to accumulate and act.<br><br>Take Notes: MDM2 degrades p53 | Keeps p53 low normally | DNA damage disrupts this"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"DNA damage leads to phosphorylation of p53 by which kinase, preventing its degradation?","a":"CDK2","b":"ATM","c":"GSK3β","d":"MAPK","ans":"B","e":"After DNA damage, ATM phosphorylates p53, blocking MDM2-mediated degradation and stabilizing it. This phosphorylation stabilises p53 so it can drive repair or apoptosis after damage.<br><br>Take Notes: ATM phosphorylates p53 | Prevents MDM2 degradation | Stabilizes and activates p53"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Regulation of gene expression at the transcriptional level in eukaryotes primarily involves:","a":"Ribosomal assembly","b":"Chromatin structure and transcription factors","c":"mRNA splicing only","d":"Protein degradation","ans":"B","e":"Eukaryotic transcriptional regulation depends largely on chromatin structure and transcription factors. This is why chromatin-modifying drugs can reactivate silenced genes in cancer.<br><br>Take Notes: Transcription control = chromatin plus transcription factors | Accessibility matters | Key regulatory level"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Acetylation of histone tails generally results in:","a":"Chromatin condensation","b":"Transcriptional repression","c":"Chromatin relaxation and increased transcription","d":"DNA methylation","ans":"C","e":"Histone acetylation loosens chromatin and increases transcription by neutralizing histone charge. Histone acetyltransferases add these marks; histone deacetylase inhibitors are used therapeutically.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetylation relaxes chromatin | Increases transcription | HATs add, HDACs remove"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"DNA methylation at CpG islands is usually associated with:","a":"Gene activation","b":"Increased transcription factor binding","c":"Gene silencing","d":"Enhanced translation","ans":"C","e":"DNA methylation at CpG islands usually silences genes. Aberrant CpG methylation silences tumour-suppressor genes such as MLH1 in cancer.<br><br>Take Notes: CpG methylation = gene silencing | Epigenetic control | Silences tumor suppressors in cancer"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which RNA polymerase transcribes mRNA in eukaryotic cells?","a":"RNA polymerase I","b":"RNA polymerase II","c":"RNA polymerase III","d":"RNA-dependent RNA polymerase","ans":"B","e":"RNA polymerase II transcribes mRNA in eukaryotic cells. It is selectively inhibited by alpha-amanitin, the toxin of the death cap mushroom.<br><br>Take Notes: RNA pol II = mRNA synthesis | Also some small RNAs | Inhibited by alpha-amanitin"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Enhancers regulate gene expression by:","a":"Binding ribosomes directly","b":"Acting only when adjacent to promoters","c":"Increasing transcription independent of orientation and distance","d":"Degrading repressors","ans":"C","e":"Enhancers increase transcription independent of orientation and distance from the promoter. They can act far upstream or downstream, looping to contact the promoter.<br><br>Take Notes: Enhancers act at a distance | Orientation-independent | Loop to the promoter"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which post-transcriptional modification increases mRNA stability and nuclear export?","a":"Polyadenylation at 3′ end","b":"Removal of introns","c":"RNA editing","d":"Deamination","ans":"A","e":"Polyadenylation at the 3-prime end increases mRNA stability and promotes nuclear export. The poly-A tail also determines how long the transcript survives before degradation.<br><br>Take Notes: Poly-A tail = stability plus export | 3-prime modification | Protects from degradation"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Alternative splicing primarily contributes to:","a":"Increased DNA replication","b":"Increased protein diversity from a single gene","c":"Reduced transcription rates","d":"Ribosomal RNA synthesis","ans":"B","e":"Alternative splicing produces multiple protein isoforms from a single gene, increasing diversity. This lets one gene encode many proteins, greatly expanding proteome diversity.<br><br>Take Notes: Alternative splicing raises protein diversity | One gene, many proteins | Common in humans"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"MicroRNAs (miRNAs) regulate gene expression by:","a":"Enhancing DNA replication","b":"Promoting translation initiation","c":"Binding mRNA and inhibiting translation or inducing degradation","d":"Increasing transcription","ans":"C","e":"MicroRNAs regulate gene expression by binding mRNA and inhibiting translation or inducing its degradation. Their dysregulation is common in cancer, where they can act as oncogenes or suppressors.<br><br>Take Notes: miRNAs bind mRNA | Block translation or degrade it | Post-transcriptional control"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which cellular structure is the site of translation in eukaryotic cells?","a":"Nucleus","b":"Golgi apparatus","c":"Ribosome","d":"Lysosome","ans":"C","e":"The ribosome is the site of translation in eukaryotic cells. Its large and small subunits assemble on the mRNA to begin protein synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Ribosome = site of translation | rRNA and protein | Free or ER-bound"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"The initiation codon for protein synthesis is:","a":"UGA","b":"UAA","c":"AUG","d":"UAG","ans":"C","e":"AUG is the initiation codon for protein synthesis. It sets the reading frame, so any upstream error shifts the entire code.<br><br>Take Notes: AUG = start codon | Codes methionine | Sets the reading frame"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which amino acid is encoded by the start codon in eukaryotes?","a":"Methionine","b":"Valine","c":"Leucine","d":"Alanine","ans":"A","e":"The start codon AUG encodes methionine in eukaryotes. In prokaryotes the initiator is formylmethionine, a useful exam distinction.<br><br>Take Notes: AUG codes methionine | Initiator amino acid | Formylmethionine in prokaryotes"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"The ribosomal site that holds the growing polypeptide chain is the:","a":"A site","b":"P site","c":"E site","d":"T site","ans":"B","e":"The P site holds the growing polypeptide chain during translation. The A site accepts the incoming aminoacyl-tRNA during elongation.<br><br>Take Notes: P site holds the peptidyl-tRNA | Growing chain | A site accepts new tRNA"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Peptide bond formation during translation is catalyzed by:","a":"Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase","b":"Peptidyl transferase (rRNA-based)","c":"Elongation factor EF-Tu","d":"Release factor","ans":"B","e":"Peptide bond formation is catalyzed by peptidyl transferase, an rRNA-based ribozyme. Being an rRNA ribozyme, it shows that RNA, not protein, catalyses this key step.<br><br>Take Notes: Peptidyl transferase = rRNA ribozyme | Forms peptide bonds | Catalytic RNA"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which factor is responsible for translocation of ribosome along mRNA in eukaryotes?","a":"eIF2","b":"eEF1","c":"eEF2","d":"eRF1","ans":"C","e":"eEF2 mediates translocation of the ribosome along mRNA in eukaryotes. Diphtheria toxin inactivates eEF2 by ADP-ribosylation, halting human protein synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: eEF2 = translocation | Moves ribosome along mRNA | Inactivated by diphtheria toxin"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Termination of translation occurs when:","a":"Ribosome reaches 5′ cap","b":"Start codon is repeated","c":"Stop codon enters A site","d":"Poly-A tail is removed","ans":"C","e":"Translation terminates when a stop codon enters the A site, recruiting release factors. Release factors then trigger hydrolysis and release of the completed polypeptide.<br><br>Take Notes: Termination = stop codon in A site | Release factors bind | Polypeptide released"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which of the following is a stop codon?","a":"AUG","b":"UAC","c":"UGG","d":"UGA","ans":"D","e":"UGA is a stop codon that signals termination of translation. The three stop codons are UGA, UAA, and UAG, and none has a matching tRNA.<br><br>Take Notes: Stop codons = UGA, UAA, UAG | No corresponding tRNA | Release factors act"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which antibiotic inhibits protein synthesis by binding to the 30S ribosomal subunit?","a":"Chloramphenicol","b":"Tetracycline","c":"Erythromycin","d":"Linezolid","ans":"B","e":"Tetracycline inhibits protein synthesis by binding the 30S ribosomal subunit, blocking aminoacyl-tRNA entry. Its binding to the 30S subunit makes it bacteriostatic and broad-spectrum.<br><br>Take Notes: Tetracycline binds 30S | Blocks aminoacyl-tRNA at A site | Bacteriostatic"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which toxin inhibits elongation factor-2 and blocks protein synthesis in humans?","a":"Botulinum toxin","b":"Diphtheria toxin","c":"Cholera toxin","d":"Tetanus toxin","ans":"B","e":"Diphtheria toxin inhibits elongation factor-2 by ADP-ribosylation, blocking human protein synthesis. This same mechanism explains why diphtheria produces its characteristic tissue damage.<br><br>Take Notes: Diphtheria toxin ADP-ribosylates eEF2 | Blocks translocation | Halts protein synthesis"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Recombinant DNA technology primarily involves:","a":"Protein sequencing","b":"DNA hybridization only","c":"Insertion of foreign DNA into a host genome","d":"RNA splicing","ans":"C","e":"Recombinant DNA technology involves inserting foreign DNA into a host genome for expression. Restriction enzymes and ligase are the essential tools that make this possible.<br><br>Take Notes: Recombinant DNA = foreign DNA in host | Enables protein production | Basis of biotech"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which enzyme is essential for cutting DNA at specific sequences in biotechnology?","a":"DNA ligase","b":"Reverse transcriptase","c":"Restriction endonuclease","d":"RNA polymerase","ans":"C","e":"Restriction endonucleases cut DNA at specific recognition sequences, essential for cloning. Their sequence-specific cutting generates the defined fragments used in cloning.<br><br>Take Notes: Restriction enzymes cut at specific sites | Produce defined fragments | Often leave sticky ends"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which enzyme is used to join DNA fragments in recombinant DNA technology?","a":"DNA polymerase","b":"RNA ligase","c":"DNA ligase","d":"Topoisomerase","ans":"C","e":"DNA ligase joins DNA fragments in recombinant DNA technology by sealing phosphodiester bonds. It forms the phosphodiester bonds that covalently insert DNA into a vector.<br><br>Take Notes: DNA ligase joins fragments | Seals phosphodiester bonds | Inserts DNA into vectors"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) requires all EXCEPT:","a":"Template DNA","b":"DNA polymerase","c":"RNA primers","d":"dNTPs","ans":"C","e":"PCR requires primers, template, dNTPs, and polymerase, but not RNA primers, which are a replication feature. RNA primers belong to replication, so their inclusion is a classic PCR distractor.<br><br>Take Notes: PCR uses DNA primers, not RNA | Needs template, dNTPs, Taq | RNA primers are for replication"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"The thermostable DNA polymerase commonly used in PCR is derived from:","a":"E. coli","b":"Bacillus subtilis","c":"Thermus aquaticus","d":"Streptococcus","ans":"C","e":"The thermostable Taq polymerase used in PCR comes from Thermus aquaticus. Its heat stability lets it survive the repeated denaturation cycles of PCR.<br><br>Take Notes: Taq polymerase from Thermus aquaticus | Heat-stable | Survives denaturation step"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which step of PCR involves separation of DNA strands?","a":"Annealing","b":"Extension","c":"Denaturation","d":"Ligation","ans":"C","e":"The denaturation step of PCR separates the DNA strands by heat. Heating breaks the hydrogen bonds so primers can anneal in the next step.<br><br>Take Notes: Denaturation separates strands | High temperature | First PCR step per cycle"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which biotechnology technique is used to detect specific DNA sequences?","a":"Western blotting","b":"Southern blotting","c":"Northern blotting","d":"ELISA","ans":"B","e":"Southern blotting detects specific DNA sequences using a labeled probe. Northern blotting detects RNA and Western blotting detects protein, a common exam triad.<br><br>Take Notes: Southern blot = DNA detection | Labeled probe hybridization | Northern for RNA, Western for protein"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which blotting technique detects RNA?","a":"Southern blot","b":"Western blot","c":"Northern blot","d":"Eastern blot","ans":"C","e":"Northern blotting detects RNA. Southern blot detects DNA and Western blot detects protein, completing the triad.<br><br>Take Notes: Northern blot = RNA detection | Gel plus probe | Assesses gene expression"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which technique is commonly used for prenatal diagnosis of genetic diseases?","a":"Karyotyping only","b":"PCR-based assays","c":"Blood culture","d":"Enzyme assays only","ans":"B","e":"PCR-based assays are commonly used for prenatal diagnosis of genetic diseases. They can detect mutations or repeat expansions directly from fetal DNA.<br><br>Take Notes: PCR for prenatal genetic diagnosis | Detects mutations and expansions | Rapid and sensitive"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which disease is classically diagnosed using PCR for trinucleotide repeat expansion?","a":"Cystic fibrosis","b":"Huntington disease","c":"Sickle cell anemia","d":"Thalassemia","ans":"B","e":"Huntington disease is classically diagnosed by PCR for CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion. The number of CAG repeats correlates inversely with the age of onset.<br><br>Take Notes: Huntington = CAG repeat expansion | PCR-based diagnosis | Autosomal dominant"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"CRISPR-Cas9 technology allows:","a":"Random mutagenesis only","b":"Targeted genome editing","c":"Protein sequencing","d":"RNA interference","ans":"B","e":"CRISPR-Cas9 enables targeted genome editing at specific sequences. A short guide RNA directs Cas9 to cut precisely at the matching sequence.<br><br>Take Notes: CRISPR-Cas9 = targeted genome editing | Guide RNA directs Cas9 | Precise cuts"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which component of CRISPR determines DNA sequence specificity?","a":"Cas9 protein","b":"PAM sequence only","c":"Guide RNA","d":"DNA ligase","ans":"C","e":"The guide RNA determines DNA sequence specificity in CRISPR. By base-pairing with the target, it makes CRISPR highly programmable.<br><br>Take Notes: Guide RNA sets CRISPR specificity | Base-pairs with target | Directs Cas9 cleavage"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Gene therapy primarily aims to:","a":"Remove all mutant genes","b":"Replace or supplement defective genes","c":"Increase transcription globally","d":"Silence all oncogenes","ans":"B","e":"Gene therapy aims to replace or supplement defective genes. Viral vectors are the most common delivery system to achieve this in patients.<br><br>Take Notes: Gene therapy replaces defective genes | Corrects genetic disease | Uses vectors for delivery"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which vector is most commonly used for gene delivery in humans?","a":"Plasmids only","b":"Viral vectors","c":"Ribosomes","d":"Lipoproteins","ans":"B","e":"Viral vectors are the most commonly used delivery system for gene therapy in humans. Adenovirus, adeno-associated virus, and lentivirus are the usual vector choices.<br><br>Take Notes: Viral vectors = common gene delivery | Efficient transduction | Adenovirus, AAV, lentivirus"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which disease was among the first successfully treated using gene therapy approaches?","a":"Cystic fibrosis","b":"SCID (ADA deficiency)","c":"Duchenne muscular dystrophy","d":"Hemophilia A","ans":"B","e":"SCID due to ADA deficiency was among the first diseases successfully treated by gene therapy. Correcting this immune defect was an early proof of concept for gene therapy.<br><br>Take Notes: ADA-SCID = early gene therapy success | Corrected immune defect | Proof of concept"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which biotechnology product is produced using recombinant DNA technology?","a":"Insulin","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Cortisol","d":"Thyroxine","ans":"A","e":"Insulin is a biotechnology product made using recombinant DNA technology. It was the first recombinant drug, produced in bacteria or yeast.<br><br>Take Notes: Recombinant insulin | First recombinant drug | Produced in bacteria or yeast"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Monoclonal antibodies are produced using:","a":"PCR","b":"Hybridoma technology","c":"Southern blotting","d":"CRISPR","ans":"B","e":"Monoclonal antibodies are produced using hybridoma technology. Fusing a B cell with a myeloma cell yields a single-specificity antibody line.<br><br>Take Notes: Monoclonal antibodies = hybridoma technology | Fuse B cell and myeloma | Single specificity"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which human disease is diagnosed using ELISA-based detection of antibodies?","a":"Tuberculosis","b":"HIV infection","c":"Sickle cell anemia","d":"Phenylketonuria","ans":"B","e":"HIV infection is diagnosed using ELISA-based detection of antibodies. A reactive ELISA is confirmed by Western blot or nucleic acid testing.<br><br>Take Notes: ELISA detects HIV antibodies | Screening test | Confirm with Western blot or PCR"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which protein is defective in retinoblastoma due to RB gene mutation?","a":"p53","b":"Cyclin E","c":"Retinoblastoma protein","d":"ATM","ans":"C","e":"The retinoblastoma protein is defective in retinoblastoma due to RB gene mutation. Its loss follows Knudson's two-hit model, requiring both alleles to be inactivated.<br><br>Take Notes: RB mutation = defective Rb protein | Loss of cell cycle control | Retinoblastoma"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Loss of heterozygosity is a key feature of which tumor suppressor gene disorder?","a":"p53 mutation","b":"RB gene mutation","c":"KRAS mutation","d":"MYC amplification","ans":"B","e":"Loss of heterozygosity is a key feature of RB gene disorder, unmasking the second mutation. This unmasks the second, already-mutated allele in hereditary retinoblastoma.<br><br>Take Notes: Loss of heterozygosity in RB | Second-hit inactivation | Tumor suppressor loss"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which mechanism explains how one functional RB allele is lost in somatic cells?","a":"Dominant negative effect","b":"Two-hit hypothesis","c":"Gain-of-function mutation","d":"Epigenetic silencing only","ans":"B","e":"The two-hit hypothesis explains how the remaining functional RB allele is lost in somatic cells. This two-hit model explains why hereditary cases present earlier and bilaterally.<br><br>Take Notes: Two-hit hypothesis (Knudson) | Both RB alleles must be lost | Explains hereditary cancer"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"p53 mutations are most commonly associated with:","a":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome","b":"Lynch syndrome","c":"Fanconi anemia","d":"Bloom syndrome","ans":"A","e":"p53 germline mutations are most commonly associated with Li-Fraumeni syndrome. These patients develop multiple early cancers, including sarcomas and breast cancer.<br><br>Take Notes: Li-Fraumeni = germline p53 mutation | Multiple early cancers | Autosomal dominant"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which cellular process is directly halted by p53 to allow DNA repair?","a":"Translation","b":"Transcription","c":"Cell cycle progression","d":"Apoptosis","ans":"C","e":"p53 halts cell cycle progression to allow time for DNA repair. By pausing the cycle, p53 lets repair finish before replication proceeds.<br><br>Take Notes: p53 halts the cell cycle | Allows DNA repair | Or triggers apoptosis"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Regulation of gene expression at the translational level can occur via:","a":"Histone modification","b":"mRNA stability and initiation factors","c":"DNA methylation","d":"Chromatin remodeling","ans":"B","e":"Translational regulation can occur via mRNA stability and initiation factors. Because it acts on existing mRNA, it is the fastest way to adjust protein output.<br><br>Take Notes: Translational control = mRNA stability plus initiation factors | Fast response | Adjusts protein output"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which structure recognizes the 5′ cap of mRNA during translation initiation?","a":"Ribosomal P site","b":"eIF4E","c":"eEF2","d":"Release factor","ans":"B","e":"eIF4E recognizes the 5-prime cap of mRNA during translation initiation. Its availability is a key rate-limiting factor for starting translation.<br><br>Take Notes: eIF4E binds the 5-prime cap | Initiation factor | Recruits the ribosome"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which laboratory technique amplifies RNA by converting it to DNA first?","a":"PCR","b":"RT-PCR","c":"Southern blot","d":"Western blot","ans":"B","e":"RT-PCR amplifies RNA by first converting it to complementary DNA. This lets it detect RNA viruses and quantify gene expression.<br><br>Take Notes: RT-PCR = reverse transcription then PCR | Amplifies RNA | Detects expression and RNA viruses"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which disease is diagnosed by detecting BCR-ABL fusion gene using PCR?","a":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","b":"Chronic myeloid leukemia","c":"Hodgkin lymphoma","d":"Burkitt lymphoma","ans":"B","e":"Chronic myeloid leukemia is diagnosed by detecting the BCR-ABL fusion gene using PCR. The Philadelphia chromosome creates this fusion, the target of imatinib.<br><br>Take Notes: CML = BCR-ABL fusion | Philadelphia chromosome | PCR detects the transcript"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which biotechnology application involves mass production of vaccines?","a":"Hybridoma technology","b":"Recombinant protein expression","c":"DNA fingerprinting","d":"Southern blotting","ans":"B","e":"Recombinant protein expression enables mass production of vaccines. The hepatitis B vaccine is a classic recombinant protein product.<br><br>Take Notes: Recombinant expression makes vaccines | Mass antigen production | Hepatitis B vaccine example"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"DNA fingerprinting is based on polymorphisms in:","a":"Coding regions only","b":"Introns only","c":"Variable number tandem repeats","d":"Mitochondrial DNA only","ans":"C","e":"DNA fingerprinting is based on polymorphisms in variable number tandem repeats. These highly variable repeats give each individual a unique DNA profile.<br><br>Take Notes: DNA fingerprinting uses VNTRs | Individual-specific patterns | Forensic and paternity use"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which human disease diagnosis relies on detection of point mutations via allele-specific PCR?","a":"Sickle cell anemia","b":"Hemophilia A","c":"Duchenne muscular dystrophy","d":"Tay-Sachs disease","ans":"A","e":"Sickle cell anemia diagnosis can rely on detecting the point mutation via allele-specific PCR. The single base change in beta-globin can be detected by allele-specific PCR.<br><br>Take Notes: Sickle cell = point mutation | Allele-specific PCR detects it | Single base change in beta-globin"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which translational control mechanism prevents protein synthesis under cellular stress?","a":"Increased histone acetylation","b":"Phosphorylation of eIF2α","c":"DNA methylation","d":"Activation of enhancers","ans":"B","e":"Phosphorylation of eIF2-alpha under stress prevents protein synthesis by blocking translation initiation. This stress response conserves resources by globally halting protein synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: eIF2-alpha phosphorylation halts translation | Cellular stress response | Conserves resources"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which feature distinguishes prokaryotic from eukaryotic translation?","a":"Use of AUG start codon","b":"Presence of Shine-Dalgarno sequence","c":"Requirement of ribosomes","d":"Peptide bond formation","ans":"B","e":"The Shine-Dalgarno sequence distinguishes prokaryotic from eukaryotic translation by positioning the ribosome. Eukaryotes instead use the 5-prime cap, making this a key prokaryotic distinction.<br><br>Take Notes: Shine-Dalgarno = prokaryotic feature | Aligns ribosome on mRNA | Eukaryotes use the 5-prime cap"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which biotechnology technique separates DNA fragments based on size?","a":"Chromatography","b":"Gel electrophoresis","c":"Centrifugation","d":"ELISA","ans":"B","e":"Gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments by size. Smaller fragments migrate faster, allowing size-based separation of DNA.<br><br>Take Notes: Gel electrophoresis separates by size | Smaller fragments migrate faster | Charge-based movement"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which enzyme synthesizes complementary DNA (cDNA) from RNA template?","a":"DNA polymerase I","b":"RNA polymerase","c":"Reverse transcriptase","d":"DNA ligase","ans":"C","e":"Reverse transcriptase synthesizes complementary DNA (cDNA) from an RNA template. This RNA-to-DNA step is also how retroviruses replicate their genomes.<br><br>Take Notes: Reverse transcriptase makes cDNA | RNA to DNA | Used in RT-PCR and retroviruses"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which disease is commonly screened using newborn biochemical and molecular testing?","a":"Phenylketonuria","b":"Alzheimer disease","c":"Parkinson disease","d":"Multiple sclerosis","ans":"A","e":"Phenylketonuria is commonly screened using newborn biochemical and molecular testing. Early detection prevents the intellectual disability of untreated PKU.<br><br>Take Notes: PKU = newborn screening | Biochemical and molecular tests | Prevents intellectual disability"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which mutation type most commonly disrupts protein synthesis by altering reading frame?","a":"Missense mutation","b":"Silent mutation","c":"Frameshift mutation","d":"Nonsense mutation","ans":"C","e":"A frameshift mutation most commonly disrupts protein synthesis by altering the reading frame. Because it is not a multiple of three, it shifts everything downstream, often to a premature stop.<br><br>Take Notes: Frameshift alters reading frame | Insertion or deletion not a multiple of three | Often premature stop"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which gene regulatory element binds repressors to inhibit transcription?","a":"Enhancer","b":"Promoter","c":"Operator","d":"Poly-A tail","ans":"C","e":"The operator binds repressors to inhibit transcription in gene regulation. Binding of the repressor here is central to control of the lac operon.<br><br>Take Notes: Operator binds repressor | Blocks transcription | Key in the lac operon"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which molecular biology technique allows simultaneous analysis of thousands of genes?","a":"Southern blot","b":"Northern blot","c":"DNA microarray","d":"PCR","ans":"C","e":"DNA microarrays allow simultaneous analysis of thousands of genes. This allows genome-wide expression profiling in a single experiment.<br><br>Take Notes: Microarray = many genes at once | Expression profiling | Hybridization-based"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which disease involves trinucleotide repeat expansion detected by molecular methods?","a":"Fragile X syndrome","b":"Cystic fibrosis","c":"β-thalassemia","d":"G6PD deficiency","ans":"A","e":"Fragile X syndrome involves CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion detected by molecular methods. The expanded CGG repeat silences FMR1, causing intellectual disability, mainly in males.<br><br>Take Notes: Fragile X = CGG repeat expansion | X-linked | Molecular diagnosis"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which component ensures accuracy of amino acid attachment to tRNA?","a":"Ribosome","b":"Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase","c":"Peptidyl transferase","d":"eEF2","ans":"B","e":"Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase ensures accurate attachment of the correct amino acid to its tRNA. Its proofreading of amino acid selection is a second layer ensuring translational fidelity.<br><br>Take Notes: Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase charges tRNA | Ensures fidelity | Proofreads amino acid selection"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which biotechnology product is used for treatment of hemophilia A?","a":"Recombinant insulin","b":"Recombinant factor VIII","c":"Recombinant erythropoietin","d":"Recombinant interferon","ans":"B","e":"Recombinant factor VIII is used for treatment of hemophilia A. It replaces the deficient factor and avoids the infection risk of pooled plasma.<br><br>Take Notes: Recombinant factor VIII = hemophilia A | Replaces deficient factor | Avoids blood-borne infection"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which mutation in p53 is most commonly observed in human cancers?","a":"Silent mutation","b":"Missense mutation in DNA-binding domain","c":"Frameshift mutation","d":"Deletion of entire gene","ans":"B","e":"The most common p53 mutation in cancers is a missense mutation in the DNA-binding domain. This missense change in the DNA-binding domain abolishes p53 target-gene activation.<br><br>Take Notes: p53 missense in DNA-binding domain | Most common cancer mutation | Abolishes target gene activation"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which gene expression regulatory mechanism acts fastest?","a":"Transcriptional regulation","b":"Post-transcriptional regulation","c":"Translational regulation","d":"Epigenetic modification","ans":"C","e":"Translational regulation acts fastest among gene expression control mechanisms. Because it uses existing mRNA, no new transcription is needed for a rapid response.<br><br>Take Notes: Translational control = fastest response | Adjusts existing mRNA use | No new transcription needed"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which human disease is associated with abnormal regulation of apoptosis due to p53 loss?","a":"Retinoblastoma","b":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome","c":"SCID","d":"Xeroderma pigmentosum","ans":"B","e":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome features abnormal apoptosis regulation due to p53 loss. Impaired apoptosis from p53 loss underlies the multiple cancers of this syndrome.<br><br>Take Notes: Li-Fraumeni = p53 loss | Impaired apoptosis | Multiple cancers"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which translational inhibitor binds 50S ribosomal subunit?","a":"Tetracycline","b":"Streptomycin","c":"Chloramphenicol","d":"Gentamicin","ans":"C","e":"Chloramphenicol inhibits translation by binding the 50S ribosomal subunit and blocking peptidyl transferase. By blocking peptidyl transferase on the 50S subunit, it is bacteriostatic.<br><br>Take Notes: Chloramphenicol binds 50S | Blocks peptidyl transferase | Bacteriostatic"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which biotechnology method detects protein expression levels?","a":"Southern blot","b":"Northern blot","c":"Western blot","d":"PCR","ans":"C","e":"Western blot detects protein expression levels. It confirms HIV after a reactive ELISA by detecting specific viral proteins.<br><br>Take Notes: Western blot = protein detection | Antibody-based | Confirms HIV and protein presence"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which cellular event is directly controlled by Rb phosphorylation status?","a":"DNA repair enzyme activation","b":"Entry into S phase","c":"Apoptosis induction","d":"Translation initiation","ans":"B","e":"Entry into S phase is directly controlled by Rb phosphorylation status. Phosphorylated Rb releases E2F, so Rb phosphorylation status gates S-phase entry.<br><br>Take Notes: Rb phosphorylation controls S-phase entry | Phosphorylated Rb releases E2F | Cell cycle checkpoint"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which genetic technique is used for paternity testing?","a":"PCR only","b":"DNA fingerprinting","c":"ELISA","d":"Western blot","ans":"B","e":"DNA fingerprinting is used for paternity testing based on individual repeat patterns. Matching STR or VNTR profiles establishes or excludes biological relationships.<br><br>Take Notes: DNA fingerprinting = paternity testing | VNTR/STR patterns | Individual-specific"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which disease is associated with defective DNA damage checkpoints due to p53 mutation?","a":"Bloom syndrome","b":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome","c":"Lynch syndrome","d":"Cockayne syndrome","ans":"B","e":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome features defective DNA damage checkpoints due to p53 mutation. Loss of these checkpoints causes the genomic instability driving its cancers.<br><br>Take Notes: Li-Fraumeni = defective checkpoints | Germline p53 mutation | Genomic instability"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which molecular change most commonly activates oncogenes?","a":"Loss-of-function mutation","b":"Gain-of-function mutation","c":"Frameshift deletion","d":"Promoter methylation","ans":"B","e":"Oncogenes are most commonly activated by gain-of-function mutations. A single activating mutation suffices, unlike tumour suppressors needing two hits.<br><br>Take Notes: Oncogene activation = gain-of-function | One allele suffices | Contrast tumor suppressor loss"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which gene regulatory protein binds promoter proximal elements?","a":"RNA polymerase","b":"General transcription factors","c":"Ribosomes","d":"DNA ligase","ans":"B","e":"General transcription factors bind promoter-proximal elements to assemble the transcription machinery. They position RNA polymerase II at the promoter to begin transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: General transcription factors bind promoter | Assemble RNA pol II complex | TFIID, TFIIH and others"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which biotechnology-based therapy uses patient's own modified T cells?","a":"Monoclonal antibody therapy","b":"CAR-T cell therapy","c":"Gene silencing therapy","d":"Enzyme replacement therapy","ans":"B","e":"CAR-T cell therapy uses a patient's own genetically modified T cells to target cancer. These engineered T cells recognise tumour antigens, as used in some leukaemias.<br><br>Take Notes: CAR-T = modified patient T cells | Target tumor antigens | Used in some leukemias and lymphomas"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which step of translation requires GTP hydrolysis?","a":"Amino acid charging","b":"Initiation and elongation","c":"Peptide bond formation","d":"Termination only","ans":"B","e":"Both initiation and elongation of translation require GTP hydrolysis. This GTP requirement powers the cycling of translation factors at each step.<br><br>Take Notes: Translation uses GTP | Initiation and elongation steps | Powers factor cycling"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which disease is associated with loss of cell cycle control due to RB gene mutation?","a":"Neuroblastoma","b":"Retinoblastoma","c":"Medulloblastoma","d":"Glioblastoma","ans":"B","e":"Retinoblastoma results from loss of cell cycle control due to RB gene mutation. It follows Knudson's two-hit model, with hereditary cases appearing early and bilaterally.<br><br>Take Notes: Retinoblastoma = RB gene mutation | Loss of cell cycle control | Two-hit mechanism"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which technique allows detection of single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs)?","a":"PCR followed by sequencing","b":"Western blot","c":"ELISA","d":"Karyotyping","ans":"A","e":"PCR followed by sequencing allows detection of single nucleotide polymorphisms. This directly reads the base change, confirming a suspected point mutation.<br><br>Take Notes: PCR plus sequencing detects SNPs | Direct base readout | Confirms point mutations"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which process links biotechnology directly to personalized medicine?","a":"Gel electrophoresis","b":"Genomic sequencing","c":"Southern blotting","d":"Protein purification","ans":"B","e":"Genomic sequencing links biotechnology directly to personalized medicine. Identifying actionable mutations lets therapy be tailored to the individual tumour.<br><br>Take Notes: Genomic sequencing enables personalized medicine | Tailors therapy | Identifies actionable mutations"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which cellular stress most strongly activates p53?","a":"Hypoxia only","b":"DNA double-strand breaks","c":"Nutrient excess","d":"Increased translation","ans":"B","e":"DNA double-strand breaks most strongly activate p53 through ATM signalling. ATM then phosphorylates and stabilises p53 to trigger the damage response.<br><br>Take Notes: Double-strand breaks activate p53 | Via ATM | Strongest p53 trigger"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which gene expression mechanism involves RNA-binding proteins?","a":"Transcriptional regulation","b":"Post-transcriptional regulation","c":"DNA methylation","d":"Chromatin remodeling","ans":"B","e":"Post-transcriptional regulation involves RNA-binding proteins controlling mRNA fate. By binding transcripts, these proteins control mRNA stability and translation.<br><br>Take Notes: Post-transcriptional control = RNA-binding proteins | Affect stability and translation | After transcription"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which biotechnology application is used in forensic science?","a":"Western blot","b":"DNA fingerprinting","c":"ELISA","d":"Northern blot","ans":"B","e":"DNA fingerprinting is a biotechnology application used in forensic science. STR profiling is the standard method for identifying individuals in casework.<br><br>Take Notes: DNA fingerprinting = forensics | Identifies individuals | STR profiling"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which translational inhibitor causes premature chain termination?","a":"Puromycin","b":"Tetracycline","c":"Chloramphenicol","d":"Erythromycin","ans":"A","e":"Puromycin causes premature chain termination by mimicking aminoacyl-tRNA. By mimicking aminoacyl-tRNA, it is incorporated and releases an incomplete peptide.<br><br>Take Notes: Puromycin = premature chain termination | Mimics aminoacyl-tRNA | Releases incomplete peptide"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which molecular event is prevented by functional Rb protein?","a":"Cyclin synthesis","b":"Uncontrolled S-phase entry","c":"DNA repair","d":"Apoptosis","ans":"B","e":"Functional Rb protein prevents uncontrolled S-phase entry. Its loss frees E2F and drives the uncontrolled proliferation of retinoblastoma.<br><br>Take Notes: Rb prevents S-phase entry | Binds E2F | Loss allows unchecked proliferation"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which technique is used to quantify gene expression levels?","a":"Southern blot","b":"qRT-PCR","c":"Western blot","d":"ELISA","ans":"B","e":"qRT-PCR is used to quantify gene expression levels. Real-time amplification allows precise measurement of transcript levels.<br><br>Take Notes: qRT-PCR quantifies expression | Real-time amplification | Measures mRNA levels"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which type of mutation in p53 abolishes DNA-binding ability?","a":"Silent mutation","b":"Missense mutation","c":"Synonymous mutation","d":"Intron mutation","ans":"B","e":"A missense mutation in p53 abolishes its DNA-binding ability. Losing DNA binding abolishes p53's ability to activate its target genes.<br><br>Take Notes: p53 missense mutation | Abolishes DNA binding | Loses transcriptional function"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which component is essential for initiation of eukaryotic translation?","a":"Shine-Dalgarno sequence","b":"5′ cap structure","c":"Operator region","d":"Poly-T tail","ans":"B","e":"The 5-prime cap structure is essential for initiation of eukaryotic translation. Recognised by eIF4E, the cap is required to recruit the ribosome and start translation.<br><br>Take Notes: 5-prime cap needed for translation initiation | Recognized by eIF4E | Also aids stability"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which biotechnology product treats chronic kidney disease-related anemia?","a":"Recombinant insulin","b":"Recombinant erythropoietin","c":"Recombinant interferon","d":"Recombinant growth hormone","ans":"B","e":"Recombinant erythropoietin treats chronic kidney disease-related anemia. By stimulating red cell production, it corrects the anaemia of chronic kidney disease.<br><br>Take Notes: Recombinant erythropoietin = renal anemia | Stimulates red cell production | Biotechnology product"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which disease involves defective mismatch repair detected using molecular testing?","a":"Xeroderma pigmentosum","b":"Lynch syndrome","c":"Bloom syndrome","d":"Fanconi anemia","ans":"B","e":"Lynch syndrome involves defective mismatch repair detected using molecular testing. Microsatellite instability and immunohistochemistry are used to detect the defect.<br><br>Take Notes: Lynch syndrome = mismatch repair defect | MSI and IHC testing | Hereditary colorectal cancer"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which regulatory protein complex promotes progression from G1 to S phase?","a":"Cyclin D-CDK4/6","b":"p53-p21","c":"Rb-E2F","d":"ATM-ATR","ans":"A","e":"The cyclin D-CDK4/6 complex promotes progression from G1 to S phase. By phosphorylating Rb, it releases E2F to drive the G1-to-S transition.<br><br>Take Notes: Cyclin D-CDK4/6 drives G1 to S | Phosphorylates Rb | Releases E2F"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which molecular tool allows targeted gene silencing at mRNA level?","a":"CRISPR-Cas9","b":"RNA interference (siRNA)","c":"Restriction enzymes","d":"DNA ligase","ans":"B","e":"RNA interference using siRNA allows targeted gene silencing at the mRNA level. This targeted degradation of specific mRNA underlies RNA interference therapeutics.<br><br>Take Notes: siRNA = targeted gene silencing | Degrades specific mRNA | RNA interference pathway"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which translational site releases deacylated tRNA?","a":"A site","b":"P site","c":"E site","d":"T site","ans":"C","e":"The E site releases the deacylated tRNA during translation. This exit step frees the tRNA after the P site, completing the elongation cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: E site releases deacylated tRNA | Exit site | After the P site"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which mutation type most often leads to truncated protein?","a":"Missense","b":"Nonsense","c":"Silent","d":"In-frame insertion","ans":"B","e":"A nonsense mutation most often leads to a truncated protein by creating a premature stop codon. The premature stop yields a shortened, usually nonfunctional protein.<br><br>Take Notes: Nonsense mutation = premature stop | Truncated protein | Often nonfunctional"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Which biotechnology technique is used to study protein-protein interactions?","a":"Yeast two-hybrid system","b":"PCR","c":"Southern blot","d":"Northern blot","ans":"A","e":"The yeast two-hybrid system studies protein-protein interactions. Reconstituting a split transcription factor reports when two proteins interact.<br><br>Take Notes: Yeast two-hybrid = protein interaction study | Reconstitutes a transcription factor | Detects binding partners"},
{"t":"Genetics","q":"Loss of p53 function most directly contributes to cancer development by:","a":"Enhancing DNA repair","b":"Preventing apoptosis and allowing accumulation of mutations","c":"Increasing translational fidelity","d":"Suppressing oncogene expression","ans":"B","e":"Loss of p53 contributes to cancer by preventing apoptosis and allowing accumulation of mutations. With apoptosis blocked, mutations accumulate and drive tumour progression.<br><br>Take Notes: p53 loss prevents apoptosis | Mutations accumulate | Drives carcinogenesis"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which component is common to the general structure of all α-amino acids except proline?","a":"Secondary amine group","b":"α-carbon bonded to four different groups","c":"Aromatic side chain","d":"Sulfur-containing side chain","ans":"B","e":"All alpha-amino acids except proline have an alpha-carbon bonded to four different groups, making it chiral. This chirality gives L- and D-forms, though only L-amino acids are used in human proteins.<br><br>Take Notes: Alpha-carbon bonded to four groups | Chiral in all but glycine | Proline is an imino acid"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"The four substituents attached to the α-carbon of an amino acid are:","a":"NH₂, COOH, H, R group","b":"NH₃⁺, COO⁻, phosphate, R group","c":"NH₂, aldehyde, H, R group","d":"NH₂, COOH, OH, R group","ans":"A","e":"The four substituents on the alpha-carbon are the amino group, carboxyl group, hydrogen, and R side chain. The R group alone differs between amino acids and determines each one's properties.<br><br>Take Notes: Alpha-carbon has NH2, COOH, H, and R | R group defines identity | Basis of amino acid structure"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid lacks an asymmetric (chiral) carbon?","a":"Alanine","b":"Valine","c":"Glycine","d":"Leucine","ans":"C","e":"Glycine lacks an asymmetric carbon because its R group is a hydrogen, making it the only achiral amino acid. Its small size lets glycine fit tight turns, which is why it recurs in collagen.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycine is achiral | R group is hydrogen | No stereoisomers"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is classified as an imino acid?","a":"Proline","b":"Histidine","c":"Arginine","d":"Lysine","ans":"A","e":"Proline is classified as an imino acid because its side chain forms a ring with the alpha-amino nitrogen. Its rigid ring introduces kinks and disrupts alpha-helices and beta-sheets.<br><br>Take Notes: Proline = imino acid | Ring includes the amino nitrogen | Creates kinks in proteins"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"At physiological pH (7.4), amino acids predominantly exist as:","a":"Neutral molecules","b":"Anions","c":"Cations","d":"Zwitterions","ans":"D","e":"At physiological pH, amino acids exist predominantly as zwitterions, with both charged amino and carboxyl groups. This dual charge gives them their characteristic buffering behaviour near neutral pH.<br><br>Take Notes: Amino acids = zwitterions at pH 7.4 | Positive amino, negative carboxyl | Net charge near zero"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid has a side chain containing a sulfhydryl group?","a":"Methionine","b":"Cysteine","c":"Serine","d":"Threonine","ans":"B","e":"Cysteine has a side chain containing a sulfhydryl group, allowing disulfide bond formation. Its disulfide bonds stabilise tertiary structure, as in insulin and antibodies.<br><br>Take Notes: Cysteine has a sulfhydryl group | Forms disulfide bonds | Stabilizes protein structure"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which of the following is an essential amino acid?","a":"Alanine","b":"Glycine","c":"Lysine","d":"Glutamate","ans":"C","e":"Lysine is an essential amino acid that must be obtained from the diet. The nine essential amino acids must all come from the diet, a common exam list.<br><br>Take Notes: Lysine = essential amino acid | Not synthesized in the body | Dietary requirement"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"All of the following are essential amino acids EXCEPT:","a":"Leucine","b":"Isoleucine","c":"Valine","d":"Tyrosine","ans":"D","e":"Tyrosine is not essential because it can be synthesized from phenylalanine. In PKU, blocked phenylalanine conversion makes tyrosine conditionally essential.<br><br>Take Notes: Tyrosine is non-essential | Made from phenylalanine | Conditionally essential in PKU"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Tyrosine becomes a conditionally essential amino acid in deficiency of:","a":"Tryptophan","b":"Phenylalanine","c":"Methionine","d":"Histidine","ans":"B","e":"Tyrosine becomes conditionally essential when phenylalanine is deficient or cannot be converted, as in PKU. This is why tyrosine becomes essential when phenylalanine hydroxylase is deficient.<br><br>Take Notes: Tyrosine conditionally essential | Depends on phenylalanine | Essential in PKU"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is essential during periods of rapid growth in children?","a":"Alanine","b":"Histidine","c":"Aspartate","d":"Glutamate","ans":"B","e":"Histidine is essential during periods of rapid growth in children. In adults it is not strictly essential, an important qualification to remember.<br><br>Take Notes: Histidine essential in children | Rapid growth demand | Conditionally essential in adults"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid serves as a precursor for serotonin?","a":"Tyrosine","b":"Tryptophan","c":"Phenylalanine","d":"Histidine","ans":"B","e":"Tryptophan is the precursor for serotonin synthesis. It also gives rise to niacin and melatonin, key tryptophan-derived products.<br><br>Take Notes: Tryptophan to serotonin | Also niacin and melatonin | Precursor amino acid"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is a precursor of nitric oxide (NO)?","a":"Arginine","b":"Citrulline","c":"Ornithine","d":"Lysine","ans":"A","e":"Arginine is the precursor of nitric oxide, produced by nitric oxide synthase. This nitric oxide pathway underlies vasodilation and the action of nitrates.<br><br>Take Notes: Arginine to nitric oxide | Via nitric oxide synthase | Vasodilator signalling"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which of the following amino acids is both glucogenic and ketogenic?","a":"Leucine","b":"Lysine","c":"Isoleucine","d":"Alanine","ans":"C","e":"Isoleucine is both glucogenic and ketogenic, yielding both glucose and ketone precursors. As a branched-chain amino acid, it is metabolised mainly in muscle.<br><br>Take Notes: Isoleucine = glucogenic and ketogenic | Dual fate | Branched-chain amino acid"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acids are purely ketogenic?","a":"Leucine and Lysine","b":"Valine and Leucine","c":"Isoleucine and Threonine","d":"Phenylalanine and Tyrosine","ans":"A","e":"Leucine and lysine are the only purely ketogenic amino acids. Being purely ketogenic, they cannot contribute to gluconeogenesis, a frequent exam point.<br><br>Take Notes: Leucine and lysine = purely ketogenic | Yield only ketone precursors | Cannot form glucose"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Positive nitrogen balance is most commonly seen in:","a":"Starvation","b":"Severe infection","c":"Growth and pregnancy","d":"Trauma","ans":"C","e":"Positive nitrogen balance, where intake exceeds excretion, is seen in growth and pregnancy. This anabolic state reflects net protein synthesis exceeding breakdown.<br><br>Take Notes: Positive nitrogen balance = growth, pregnancy | Intake exceeds loss | Net protein synthesis"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Negative nitrogen balance is characteristically seen in:","a":"Childhood growth","b":"Recovery from illness","c":"Severe burns and trauma","d":"Pregnancy","ans":"C","e":"Negative nitrogen balance, where excretion exceeds intake, is seen in severe burns and trauma. This catabolic state reflects net protein breakdown, as in critical illness.<br><br>Take Notes: Negative nitrogen balance = burns, trauma | Excretion exceeds intake | Net protein breakdown"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Nitrogen balance is defined as the difference between:","a":"Nitrogen absorbed and nitrogen stored","b":"Nitrogen intake and nitrogen excretion","c":"Protein intake and fat intake","d":"Urea synthesis and ammonia production","ans":"B","e":"Nitrogen balance is the difference between nitrogen intake and nitrogen excretion. A positive balance signals growth, a negative one signals net protein loss.<br><br>Take Notes: Nitrogen balance = intake minus excretion | Reflects protein turnover | Positive, negative, or zero"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which condition is associated with prolonged negative nitrogen balance?","a":"Anabolic state","b":"Acromegaly","c":"Cachexia","d":"Pregnancy","ans":"C","e":"Cachexia is associated with prolonged negative nitrogen balance from chronic protein catabolism. This chronic protein wasting is common in cancer and advanced organ failure.<br><br>Take Notes: Cachexia = negative nitrogen balance | Chronic wasting | Muscle protein loss"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Transamination reactions require which coenzyme?","a":"FAD","b":"NAD⁺","c":"Pyridoxal phosphate","d":"Thiamine pyrophosphate","ans":"C","e":"Transamination reactions require pyridoxal phosphate, the active form of vitamin B6. A deficiency of vitamin B6 therefore impairs transamination and amino acid metabolism.<br><br>Take Notes: Transamination needs pyridoxal phosphate | Vitamin B6 derivative | Transfers amino groups"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes transamination reactions?","a":"Dehydrogenase","b":"Transaminase (aminotransferase)","c":"Decarboxylase","d":"Synthetase","ans":"B","e":"Transaminases (aminotransferases) catalyze transamination reactions, transferring amino groups. Serum ALT and AST are transaminases used clinically to assess liver injury.<br><br>Take Notes: Transaminases catalyze transamination | ALT and AST examples | Use pyridoxal phosphate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"The amino group donor in most transamination reactions is:","a":"Alanine","b":"Glutamate","c":"Aspartate","d":"Glutamine","ans":"B","e":"Glutamate is the amino group donor in most transamination reactions. It links to alpha-ketoglutarate, connecting amino acid metabolism to the TCA cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Glutamate = common amino donor | Central to nitrogen metabolism | Links to alpha-ketoglutarate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Oxidative deamination of glutamate occurs primarily in the:","a":"Cytosol","b":"Nucleus","c":"Mitochondria","d":"Lysosome","ans":"C","e":"Oxidative deamination of glutamate occurs primarily in the mitochondria. This releases free ammonia, which the urea cycle then detoxifies.<br><br>Take Notes: Oxidative deamination in mitochondria | Glutamate releases ammonia | Feeds the urea cycle"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes oxidative deamination of glutamate?","a":"Glutamate synthase","b":"Glutamate dehydrogenase","c":"Alanine aminotransferase","d":"Aspartate aminotransferase","ans":"B","e":"Glutamate dehydrogenase catalyzes oxidative deamination of glutamate, releasing ammonia. It is the main enzyme funnelling amino nitrogen into the urea cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Glutamate dehydrogenase deaminates glutamate | Releases free ammonia | Mitochondrial enzyme"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid acts as a major nitrogen carrier in blood?","a":"Alanine","b":"Glutamine","c":"Aspartate","d":"Valine","ans":"B","e":"Glutamine acts as a major nitrogen carrier in blood, transporting ammonia safely. This nontoxic transport form delivers ammonia safely to liver and kidney.<br><br>Take Notes: Glutamine = major blood nitrogen carrier | Transports ammonia | Nontoxic form"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"The glucose-alanine cycle primarily functions to:","a":"Transport ammonia to kidneys","b":"Transport nitrogen from muscle to liver","c":"Synthesize essential amino acids","d":"Remove CO₂ from muscle","ans":"B","e":"The glucose-alanine cycle transports nitrogen from muscle to liver while returning glucose. Alanine carries the amino group to the liver while glucose returns to muscle.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucose-alanine cycle moves nitrogen | Muscle to liver | Alanine carries the amino group"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which reaction removes the carboxyl group from amino acids?","a":"Transamination","b":"Deamination","c":"Decarboxylation","d":"Racemization","ans":"C","e":"Decarboxylation removes the carboxyl group from amino acids, releasing carbon dioxide. This forms biogenic amines such as histamine, serotonin, and GABA.<br><br>Take Notes: Decarboxylation removes the carboxyl group | Releases carbon dioxide | Forms biogenic amines"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Biogenic amines such as histamine are formed by:","a":"Oxidation","b":"Reduction","c":"Decarboxylation of amino acids","d":"Transamination","ans":"C","e":"Biogenic amines such as histamine are formed by decarboxylation of amino acids. These reactions require pyridoxal phosphate, the active form of vitamin B6.<br><br>Take Notes: Biogenic amines from decarboxylation | Histamine, serotonin, GABA | Pyridoxal phosphate dependent"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency impairs amino acid metabolism due to defective transamination?","a":"Vitamin B1","b":"Vitamin B6","c":"Vitamin B12","d":"Vitamin C","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin B6 deficiency impairs amino acid metabolism by disrupting transamination. This is why isoniazid, which depletes B6, can cause neuropathy and seizures.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin B6 deficiency impairs transamination | Pyridoxal phosphate needed | Affects amino acid metabolism"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Enzyme kinetics primarily studies the relationship between:","a":"Enzyme structure and gene expression","b":"Enzyme concentration and pH","c":"Reaction velocity and substrate concentration","d":"Temperature and reaction time","ans":"C","e":"Enzyme kinetics studies the relationship between reaction velocity and substrate concentration. The hyperbolic velocity curve it produces defines Km and Vmax.<br><br>Take Notes: Enzyme kinetics = velocity versus substrate | Michaelis-Menten model | Defines Km and Vmax"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"The Michaelis-Menten equation describes:","a":"All enzyme-catalyzed reactions","b":"Enzymes showing cooperativity","c":"Simple enzyme-substrate kinetics","d":"Inhibition only","ans":"C","e":"The Michaelis-Menten equation describes simple enzyme-substrate kinetics of a single-substrate reaction. It assumes a single substrate and a steady-state enzyme-substrate complex.<br><br>Take Notes: Michaelis-Menten = simple enzyme kinetics | Hyperbolic curve | Yields Km and Vmax"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Vmax of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction is defined as:","a":"Maximum substrate concentration","b":"Maximum velocity at low substrate concentration","c":"Maximum velocity when enzyme is saturated","d":"Velocity at Km","ans":"C","e":"Vmax is the maximum velocity reached when the enzyme is fully saturated with substrate. At saturation, adding more substrate no longer increases the reaction rate.<br><br>Take Notes: Vmax = maximum velocity at saturation | All active sites occupied | Proportional to enzyme amount"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Km of an enzyme is defined as the substrate concentration at which:","a":"Reaction velocity is zero","b":"Reaction velocity is maximum","c":"Reaction velocity is half of Vmax","d":"Enzyme is completely inhibited","ans":"C","e":"Km is the substrate concentration at which the reaction velocity is half of Vmax. A low Km means high affinity, an inverse relationship worth memorising.<br><br>Take Notes: Km = substrate at half-Vmax | Reflects affinity | Low Km means high affinity"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"A low Km value indicates:","a":"Low affinity of enzyme for substrate","b":"High affinity of enzyme for substrate","c":"Low Vmax","d":"Competitive inhibition","ans":"B","e":"A low Km indicates high affinity of the enzyme for its substrate. So a lower Km reflects tighter substrate binding by the enzyme.<br><br>Take Notes: Low Km = high affinity | Reaches half-Vmax at low substrate | Inverse relationship"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which parameter reflects enzyme affinity for substrate?","a":"Vmax","b":"Turnover number","c":"Km","d":"Enzyme concentration","ans":"C","e":"Km reflects the affinity of an enzyme for its substrate. Km is intrinsic to the enzyme-substrate pair and independent of enzyme amount.<br><br>Take Notes: Km reflects substrate affinity | Independent of enzyme concentration | Low Km, high affinity"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Increasing enzyme concentration affects which kinetic parameter?","a":"Decreases Km only","b":"Increases Km only","c":"Increases Vmax only","d":"Decreases Vmax only","ans":"C","e":"Increasing enzyme concentration increases Vmax only, without changing Km. Only Vmax scales with enzyme concentration, since affinity is intrinsic.<br><br>Take Notes: More enzyme raises Vmax | Km unchanged | Affinity is intrinsic"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Competitive inhibition is characterized by:","a":"Decreased Vmax and increased Km","b":"Increased Vmax and decreased Km","c":"Unchanged Vmax and increased Km","d":"Decreased Vmax and unchanged Km","ans":"C","e":"Competitive inhibition shows unchanged Vmax and increased Km, as more substrate overcomes it. More substrate overcomes it, so it is surmountable, unlike non-competitive inhibition.<br><br>Take Notes: Competitive inhibition = higher Km, same Vmax | Surmountable by substrate | Binds the active site"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"A competitive inhibitor binds at the:","a":"Allosteric site","b":"Active site","c":"Enzyme-substrate complex only","d":"Cofactor binding site","ans":"B","e":"A competitive inhibitor binds at the active site, competing with the substrate. Because it binds the active site, adding substrate outcompetes it.<br><br>Take Notes: Competitive inhibitor binds active site | Competes with substrate | Overcome by more substrate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Non-competitive inhibition typically results in:","a":"Increased Vmax","b":"Decreased Vmax with unchanged Km","c":"Increased Km only","d":"Increased Vmax and Km","ans":"B","e":"Non-competitive inhibition typically results in decreased Vmax with unchanged Km. It binds a separate site, so extra substrate cannot displace it.<br><br>Take Notes: Non-competitive = lower Vmax, same Km | Binds elsewhere | Not overcome by substrate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which inhibition cannot be overcome by increasing substrate concentration?","a":"Competitive inhibition","b":"Reversible inhibition","c":"Non-competitive inhibition","d":"Substrate inhibition","ans":"C","e":"Non-competitive inhibition cannot be overcome by increasing substrate concentration. It effectively removes active enzyme rather than competing for substrate.<br><br>Take Notes: Non-competitive inhibition not surmountable | Binds a separate site | Lowers Vmax"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which plot is used to determine Km and Vmax graphically?","a":"Michaelis plot","b":"Lineweaver-Burk plot","c":"Dixon plot","d":"Hill plot","ans":"B","e":"The Lineweaver-Burk plot is used to determine Km and Vmax graphically as a double reciprocal. Its double-reciprocal lines make Km and Vmax easy to read graphically.<br><br>Take Notes: Lineweaver-Burk = double reciprocal plot | Linearizes kinetics | Gives Km and Vmax"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"In a Lineweaver-Burk plot, Km is represented by:","a":"Y-intercept","b":"Slope","c":"X-intercept","d":"Area under curve","ans":"C","e":"In a Lineweaver-Burk plot, Km is represented by the x-intercept (as minus 1 over Km). The y-intercept simultaneously gives 1 over Vmax on the same plot.<br><br>Take Notes: Km from Lineweaver-Burk x-intercept | Equals minus 1 over Km | Vmax from y-intercept"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"In a Lineweaver-Burk plot, Vmax is represented by:","a":"X-intercept","b":"Slope","c":"Y-intercept","d":"Curvature","ans":"C","e":"In a Lineweaver-Burk plot, Vmax is represented by the y-intercept (as 1 over Vmax). The x-intercept gives minus 1 over Km, completing the graphical readout.<br><br>Take Notes: Vmax from Lineweaver-Burk y-intercept | Equals 1 over Vmax | Km from x-intercept"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme shows cooperative kinetics rather than Michaelis-Menten kinetics?","a":"Hexokinase","b":"Glucokinase","c":"Lactate dehydrogenase","d":"Alanine aminotransferase","ans":"B","e":"Glucokinase shows cooperative sigmoidal kinetics rather than classic Michaelis-Menten kinetics. Its high Km lets it act as a glucose sensor in liver and beta-cells.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucokinase = cooperative kinetics | Sigmoidal curve | High Km, glucose sensor"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Cooperative enzymes typically show which type of curve?","a":"Hyperbolic","b":"Linear","c":"Sigmoidal","d":"Exponential","ans":"C","e":"Cooperative enzymes typically show a sigmoidal velocity curve. This cooperativity mirrors haemoglobin's oxygen-binding behaviour.<br><br>Take Notes: Cooperative enzymes = sigmoidal curve | Subunit interaction | Hemoglobin-like behaviour"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Allosteric enzymes are usually regulated by:","a":"Competitive inhibitors only","b":"Covalent modification only","c":"Effectors binding at regulatory sites","d":"Substrate concentration alone","ans":"C","e":"Allosteric enzymes are usually regulated by effectors binding at regulatory sites distinct from the active site. AMP and ATP act at these sites to switch pathways like glycolysis on or off.<br><br>Take Notes: Allosteric enzymes bind effectors at regulatory sites | Away from active site | Positive or negative regulation"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is the precursor of catecholamines?","a":"Tryptophan","b":"Tyrosine","c":"Histidine","d":"Glutamate","ans":"B","e":"Tyrosine is the precursor of the catecholamines dopamine, norepinephrine, and epinephrine. It also gives rise to melanin, thyroid hormone, and dopamine.<br><br>Take Notes: Tyrosine to catecholamines | Dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine | Also melanin and thyroid hormone"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid contributes nitrogen for purine synthesis?","a":"Alanine","b":"Glutamine","c":"Valine","d":"Serine","ans":"B","e":"Glutamine contributes nitrogen atoms for purine synthesis. Its amide nitrogen is also used in pyrimidine synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Glutamine donates nitrogen for purines | Amide nitrogen | Also pyrimidine synthesis"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"The turnover number (kcat) of an enzyme represents:","a":"Substrate affinity","b":"Number of substrate molecules converted per enzyme per second","c":"Maximum velocity","d":"Binding constant","ans":"B","e":"The turnover number (kcat) is the number of substrate molecules converted per enzyme per second. A high kcat over Km defines a catalytically efficient enzyme.<br><br>Take Notes: kcat = substrate converted per enzyme per second | Catalytic rate | Maximal turnover"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which condition increases Km without affecting Vmax?","a":"Increased enzyme concentration","b":"Competitive inhibition","c":"Non-competitive inhibition","d":"Irreversible inhibition","ans":"B","e":"Competitive inhibition increases Km without affecting Vmax. More substrate overcomes it, distinguishing it from non-competitive inhibition.<br><br>Take Notes: Competitive inhibition raises Km | Vmax unchanged | Surmountable by substrate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Essential amino acids must be supplied in diet because:","a":"They are toxic if synthesized","b":"Human body lacks pathways to synthesize them","c":"They are rapidly degraded","d":"They cannot be absorbed","ans":"B","e":"Essential amino acids must be supplied in the diet because the body lacks pathways to synthesize them. The nine essential amino acids must therefore be supplied by diet.<br><br>Take Notes: Essential amino acids not synthesized | Must come from diet | Nine in humans"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is most important for one-carbon metabolism?","a":"Glycine","b":"Alanine","c":"Valine","d":"Leucine","ans":"A","e":"Glycine is central to one-carbon metabolism, donating and accepting single-carbon units. Its links to folate make it central to nucleotide and methyl-group metabolism.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycine = one-carbon metabolism | Links to folate | Smallest amino acid"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid links amino acid metabolism with TCA cycle via α-ketoglutarate?","a":"Alanine","b":"Aspartate","c":"Glutamate","d":"Serine","ans":"C","e":"Glutamate links amino acid metabolism to the TCA cycle via its keto acid alpha-ketoglutarate. This transamination product feeds carbon skeletons into energy metabolism.<br><br>Take Notes: Glutamate connects to TCA via alpha-ketoglutarate | Central nitrogen hub | Transamination product"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme kinetic parameter is independent of enzyme concentration?","a":"Vmax","b":"Reaction velocity","c":"Km","d":"Turnover number","ans":"C","e":"Km is independent of enzyme concentration, being an intrinsic property of the enzyme-substrate pair. Only Vmax changes with enzyme amount, since Km is intrinsic.<br><br>Take Notes: Km independent of enzyme amount | Intrinsic constant | Vmax scales with enzyme"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is precursor of heme synthesis?","a":"Glycine","b":"Valine","c":"Methionine","d":"Threonine","ans":"A","e":"Glycine is a precursor of heme synthesis, combining with succinyl-CoA. With succinyl-CoA it starts porphyrin and heme synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycine + succinyl-CoA start heme | ALA synthesis | Also purine and porphyrin precursor"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is primarily ketogenic and raises ketone body formation?","a":"Valine","b":"Alanine","c":"Leucine","d":"Aspartate","ans":"C","e":"Leucine is primarily ketogenic and increases ketone body formation. Being purely ketogenic, it cannot form glucose, a common exam trap.<br><br>Take Notes: Leucine = purely ketogenic | Raises ketone formation | Cannot form glucose"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Enzyme saturation occurs when:","a":"All substrate is consumed","b":"All enzyme active sites are occupied","c":"Km equals zero","d":"Product concentration is high","ans":"B","e":"Enzyme saturation occurs when all enzyme active sites are occupied by substrate. Beyond this point, velocity plateaus at Vmax regardless of added substrate.<br><br>Take Notes: Saturation = all active sites occupied | Velocity reaches Vmax | Adding substrate no longer helps"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is the main precursor for GABA?","a":"Glutamate","b":"Glycine","c":"Alanine","d":"Serine","ans":"A","e":"Glutamate is the main precursor for GABA via decarboxylation. This reaction requires pyridoxal phosphate, linking GABA synthesis to vitamin B6.<br><br>Take Notes: Glutamate to GABA | Glutamate decarboxylase | Needs pyridoxal phosphate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which condition is associated with increased Km due to structural enzyme mutation?","a":"Increased substrate affinity","b":"Decreased substrate affinity","c":"Increased Vmax","d":"Increased enzyme concentration","ans":"B","e":"A structural enzyme mutation that lowers substrate affinity increases Km. Weaker binding means more substrate is needed to reach half-Vmax.<br><br>Take Notes: Decreased affinity raises Km | Structural change | Weaker substrate binding"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is involved in detoxification reactions via conjugation?","a":"Glycine","b":"Valine","c":"Leucine","d":"Phenylalanine","ans":"A","e":"Glycine participates in detoxification reactions via conjugation, as in bile acid and benzoate conjugation. These conjugation reactions enhance excretion of bile acids and drugs.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycine conjugates in detoxification | Bile acids and benzoate | Enhances excretion"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which statement about Vmax is CORRECT?","a":"It decreases with competitive inhibition","b":"It depends on substrate concentration","c":"It reflects catalytic capacity of enzyme","d":"It is independent of enzyme concentration","ans":"C","e":"Vmax reflects the catalytic capacity of an enzyme at saturating substrate. It is proportional to enzyme amount, unlike the intrinsic constant Km.<br><br>Take Notes: Vmax reflects catalytic capacity | At saturation | Proportional to enzyme amount"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid acts as a buffer due to its imidazole group?","a":"Histidine","b":"Lysine","c":"Arginine","d":"Aspartate","ans":"A","e":"Histidine acts as a buffer near physiological pH due to its imidazole side chain. Its pKa near 6 makes histidine a key buffer, including in haemoglobin.<br><br>Take Notes: Histidine buffers via imidazole | pKa near 6 | Important in hemoglobin buffering"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme shows induced-fit model of catalysis?","a":"All enzymes without exception","b":"Only allosteric enzymes","c":"Many enzymes including hexokinase","d":"Only digestive enzymes","ans":"C","e":"Many enzymes including hexokinase show the induced-fit model, molding around the substrate. The enzyme moulds around the substrate, improving catalytic alignment.<br><br>Take Notes: Hexokinase shows induced fit | Enzyme changes shape on binding | Improves catalysis"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid contributes to creatine synthesis?","a":"Glycine","b":"Arginine","c":"Methionine","d":"All of the above","ans":"D","e":"Creatine synthesis involves glycine, arginine, and methionine (as S-adenosylmethionine). Because several amino acids contribute, all-of-the-above is the correct choice.<br><br>Take Notes: Creatine from glycine, arginine, methionine | Multiple contributors | Energy storage in muscle"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"A Lineweaver-Burk plot is also known as:","a":"Saturation curve","b":"Double reciprocal plot","c":"Dixon plot","d":"Hill plot","ans":"B","e":"The Lineweaver-Burk plot is also known as the double reciprocal plot. Plotting 1 over velocity against 1 over substrate linearises the kinetics.<br><br>Take Notes: Lineweaver-Burk = double reciprocal plot | Plots 1/V against 1/S | Linearizes kinetics"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is most abundant in collagen?","a":"Glycine","b":"Proline","c":"Hydroxyproline","d":"Alanine","ans":"A","e":"Glycine is the most abundant amino acid in collagen, fitting its triple helix. Its abundance and small size allow the tight packing of the collagen helix.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycine = most abundant in collagen | Every third residue | Allows tight helix packing"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which type of inhibition decreases both Vmax and Km?","a":"Competitive","b":"Non-competitive","c":"Uncompetitive","d":"Irreversible","ans":"C","e":"Uncompetitive inhibition decreases both Vmax and Km. Binding the enzyme-substrate complex gives parallel Lineweaver-Burk lines.<br><br>Take Notes: Uncompetitive inhibition lowers Vmax and Km | Binds the enzyme-substrate complex | Parallel Lineweaver-Burk lines"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is a precursor of melanin?","a":"Tryptophan","b":"Phenylalanine","c":"Tyrosine","d":"Histidine","ans":"C","e":"Tyrosine is the precursor of melanin via the tyrosinase pathway. Loss of tyrosinase in this pathway causes albinism.<br><br>Take Notes: Tyrosine to melanin | Via tyrosinase | Also catecholamines and thyroid hormone"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"The optimal pH for most human enzymes is close to:","a":"2","b":"5","c":"7","d":"9","ans":"C","e":"The optimal pH for most human enzymes is close to 7. Pepsin and lysosomal enzymes are notable acidic-pH exceptions.<br><br>Take Notes: Optimal pH near 7 for most enzymes | Exceptions like pepsin and lysosomal enzymes | Extremes denature enzymes"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is involved in detoxification of ammonia in brain?","a":"Alanine","b":"Glutamine","c":"Aspartate","d":"Valine","ans":"B","e":"Glutamine detoxifies ammonia in the brain by combining with glutamate. This glutamine synthesis protects the brain from ammonia toxicity.<br><br>Take Notes: Glutamine detoxifies brain ammonia | Glutamine synthetase | Protects the CNS"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which factor does NOT affect enzyme activity?","a":"Temperature","b":"pH","c":"Substrate concentration","d":"DNA sequence","ans":"D","e":"Enzyme activity is affected by pH, temperature, and substrate, but not directly by DNA sequence. DNA sequence sets structure earlier but does not directly change activity, a subtle distractor.<br><br>Take Notes: DNA sequence does not directly alter enzyme activity | pH, temperature, substrate do | Sequence sets structure earlier"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is classified as branched-chain amino acid?","a":"Alanine","b":"Leucine","c":"Glycine","d":"Tyrosine","ans":"B","e":"Leucine is a branched-chain amino acid, along with isoleucine and valine. Branched-chain amino acids are degraded in muscle and impaired in maple syrup urine disease.<br><br>Take Notes: Leucine = branched-chain amino acid | With isoleucine and valine | Metabolized in muscle"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which condition results in zero nitrogen balance?","a":"Growth","b":"Starvation","c":"Adult maintenance state","d":"Pregnancy","ans":"C","e":"Adult maintenance state results in zero nitrogen balance, with intake matching excretion. Intake matching excretion defines the stable protein mass of healthy adults.<br><br>Take Notes: Zero nitrogen balance = adult maintenance | Intake equals excretion | Stable protein mass"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is precursor of porphyrins?","a":"Glycine","b":"Serine","c":"Methionine","d":"Valine","ans":"A","e":"Glycine is a precursor of porphyrins, combining with succinyl-CoA to form ALA. This delta-aminolevulinic acid step begins heme synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycine starts porphyrin synthesis | With succinyl-CoA | Forms delta-aminolevulinic acid"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which kinetic parameter is affected in irreversible inhibition?","a":"Km only","b":"Vmax only","c":"Both Km and Vmax","d":"Neither Km nor Vmax","ans":"B","e":"Irreversible inhibition affects Vmax only, permanently reducing active enzyme. Permanent inactivation lowers Vmax, mimicking non-competitive inhibition.<br><br>Take Notes: Irreversible inhibition lowers Vmax | Permanent enzyme inactivation | Behaves like non-competitive"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid can be synthesized from pyruvate?","a":"Alanine","b":"Valine","c":"Leucine","d":"Isoleucine","ans":"A","e":"Alanine can be synthesized from pyruvate by transamination. Being glucogenic, alanine is a key gluconeogenic substrate during fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Alanine from pyruvate | Transamination | Glucogenic and non-essential"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid serves as a precursor for glutathione?","a":"Glycine","b":"Cysteine","c":"Glutamate","d":"All of the above","ans":"D","e":"Glutathione is synthesized from glutamate, cysteine, and glycine. Because several amino acids contribute, all-of-the-above is correct here too.<br><br>Take Notes: Glutathione from glutamate, cysteine, glycine | Tripeptide antioxidant | Cysteine is rate-limiting"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme kinetic condition reflects enzyme efficiency?","a":"Km alone","b":"Vmax alone","c":"kcat/Km","d":"Substrate concentration","ans":"C","e":"The catalytic efficiency of an enzyme is reflected by the kcat/Km ratio. A higher ratio marks a more catalytically efficient enzyme.<br><br>Take Notes: Enzyme efficiency = kcat/Km | Specificity constant | Higher means more efficient"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is precursor of niacin?","a":"Tryptophan","b":"Tyrosine","c":"Histidine","d":"Alanine","ans":"A","e":"Tryptophan is a precursor of niacin (vitamin B3). Deficiency of both tryptophan and niacin contributes to pellagra.<br><br>Take Notes: Tryptophan to niacin | Also serotonin and melatonin | Deficiency contributes to pellagra"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is most important for nitrogen transport from muscle?","a":"Glutamine","b":"Alanine","c":"Aspartate","d":"Glycine","ans":"B","e":"Alanine is the most important amino acid for nitrogen transport from muscle to liver. The glucose-alanine cycle delivers this nitrogen to the liver.<br><br>Take Notes: Alanine carries nitrogen from muscle | Glucose-alanine cycle | Delivers amino groups to liver"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which statement about Km is TRUE?","a":"It increases with enzyme concentration","b":"It is constant for a given enzyme-substrate pair","c":"It represents maximum velocity","d":"It depends on Vmax","ans":"B","e":"Km is constant for a given enzyme-substrate pair under defined conditions. It is intrinsic and independent of enzyme concentration.<br><br>Take Notes: Km is constant for an enzyme-substrate pair | Intrinsic property | Independent of enzyme amount"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is precursor of serotonin and melatonin?","a":"Tyrosine","b":"Histidine","c":"Tryptophan","d":"Phenylalanine","ans":"C","e":"Tryptophan is the precursor of both serotonin and melatonin. It also gives rise to niacin, another key tryptophan product.<br><br>Take Notes: Tryptophan to serotonin and melatonin | Also niacin | Precursor amino acid"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which inhibition shows parallel lines in Lineweaver-Burk plot?","a":"Competitive","b":"Non-competitive","c":"Uncompetitive","d":"Irreversible","ans":"C","e":"Uncompetitive inhibition shows parallel lines in a Lineweaver-Burk plot. Parallel lines are the graphical signature of uncompetitive inhibition.<br><br>Take Notes: Uncompetitive inhibition = parallel Lineweaver-Burk lines | Lowers Vmax and Km | Binds enzyme-substrate complex"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid participates in urea cycle as a carrier of nitrogen?","a":"Alanine","b":"Aspartate","c":"Glycine","d":"Valine","ans":"B","e":"Aspartate participates in the urea cycle as a carrier of nitrogen, forming argininosuccinate. It supplies the second urea nitrogen atom via argininosuccinate.<br><br>Take Notes: Aspartate donates nitrogen in urea cycle | Forms argininosuccinate | Second urea nitrogen source"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is aromatic?","a":"Alanine","b":"Valine","c":"Phenylalanine","d":"Proline","ans":"C","e":"Phenylalanine is an aromatic amino acid, along with tyrosine and tryptophan. These aromatic amino acids accumulate in disorders like PKU.<br><br>Take Notes: Phenylalanine = aromatic amino acid | With tyrosine and tryptophan | Ring side chain"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme parameter decreases in presence of non-competitive inhibitor?","a":"Km","b":"Substrate concentration","c":"Vmax","d":"Turnover number only","ans":"C","e":"Vmax decreases in the presence of a non-competitive inhibitor. It binds a separate site, so substrate cannot restore Vmax.<br><br>Take Notes: Non-competitive inhibitor lowers Vmax | Km unchanged | Binds a separate site"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is precursor of epinephrine and norepinephrine?","a":"Tryptophan","b":"Tyrosine","c":"Histidine","d":"Glycine","ans":"B","e":"Tyrosine is the precursor of epinephrine and norepinephrine. This catecholamine pathway proceeds via dopa and dopamine.<br><br>Take Notes: Tyrosine to epinephrine and norepinephrine | Catecholamine pathway | Via dopa and dopamine"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which reaction incorporates ammonia into organic molecules?","a":"Transamination","b":"Deamination","c":"Amination","d":"Decarboxylation","ans":"C","e":"Amination incorporates ammonia into organic molecules, as in glutamate formation. Reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate forms glutamate.<br><br>Take Notes: Amination adds ammonia to molecules | Forms glutamate | Reductive amination"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid contributes sulfur to the body?","a":"Serine","b":"Methionine","c":"Valine","d":"Glycine","ans":"B","e":"Methionine contributes sulfur to the body and is a methyl group donor. As S-adenosylmethionine, it is the body's main methyl-group donor.<br><br>Take Notes: Methionine supplies sulfur | Methyl donor as SAM | Essential amino acid"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme kinetics assumption applies to Michaelis-Menten model?","a":"Steady-state assumption","b":"Cooperative binding","c":"Allosteric regulation","d":"Irreversible reactions only","ans":"A","e":"The Michaelis-Menten model relies on the steady-state assumption for the enzyme-substrate complex. This assumption keeps the enzyme-substrate complex concentration constant.<br><br>Take Notes: Michaelis-Menten uses steady-state assumption | Complex concentration constant | Basis of the equation"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is precursor of histamine?","a":"Histidine","b":"Tryptophan","c":"Tyrosine","d":"Phenylalanine","ans":"A","e":"Histidine is the precursor of histamine via decarboxylation. Histidine decarboxylase produces histamine, a key inflammatory mediator.<br><br>Take Notes: Histidine to histamine | Histidine decarboxylase | Inflammatory mediator"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which kinetic parameter increases when enzyme affinity decreases?","a":"Vmax","b":"Km","c":"kcat","d":"Enzyme concentration","ans":"B","e":"Km increases when enzyme affinity for substrate decreases. Weaker binding raises Km, needing more substrate for half-maximal velocity.<br><br>Take Notes: Lower affinity raises Km | Weaker binding | Needs more substrate for half-Vmax"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is most abundant in plasma?","a":"Alanine","b":"Glutamine","c":"Glycine","d":"Valine","ans":"B","e":"Glutamine is the most abundant amino acid in plasma. It also serves as a major nitrogen carrier and fuel for enterocytes.<br><br>Take Notes: Glutamine = most abundant plasma amino acid | Nitrogen carrier | Fuel for enterocytes"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is glucogenic only?","a":"Leucine","b":"Lysine","c":"Alanine","d":"Isoleucine","ans":"C","e":"Alanine is glucogenic only, converted to pyruvate and then glucose. As a purely glucogenic amino acid, it is central to gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Alanine = glucogenic only | Forms pyruvate | Key in gluconeogenesis"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which factor increases reaction velocity without changing Km?","a":"Competitive inhibitor","b":"Non-competitive inhibitor","c":"Increased enzyme concentration","d":"Decreased substrate concentration","ans":"C","e":"Increasing enzyme concentration raises reaction velocity without changing Km. Affinity is intrinsic, so Km stays unchanged as velocity rises.<br><br>Take Notes: More enzyme raises velocity | Km unchanged | Affinity is intrinsic"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is synthesized from oxaloacetate?","a":"Alanine","b":"Aspartate","c":"Glutamate","d":"Glycine","ans":"B","e":"Aspartate is synthesized from oxaloacetate by transamination. This transamination makes aspartate, a glucogenic non-essential amino acid.<br><br>Take Notes: Aspartate from oxaloacetate | Transamination | Non-essential, glucogenic"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which kinetic plot is used to identify type of inhibition?","a":"Hill plot","b":"Michaelis plot","c":"Lineweaver-Burk plot","d":"Arrhenius plot","ans":"C","e":"The Lineweaver-Burk plot is used to identify the type of inhibition from line patterns. Changes in intercepts reveal competitive, non-competitive, or uncompetitive patterns.<br><br>Take Notes: Lineweaver-Burk identifies inhibition type | Competitive, non-competitive, uncompetitive | From intercept changes"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is precursor of creatinine (indirectly)?","a":"Glycine","b":"Arginine","c":"Methionine","d":"All of the above","ans":"D","e":"Creatinine is derived indirectly from glycine, arginine, and methionine via creatine. Because creatine is made from all three, all-of-the-above is correct.<br><br>Take Notes: Creatinine from creatine breakdown | Creatine from glycine, arginine, methionine | Muscle marker"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which amino acid is essential and branched-chain?","a":"Valine","b":"Alanine","c":"Glycine","d":"Aspartate","ans":"A","e":"Valine is an essential branched-chain amino acid. With leucine and isoleucine, it is a branched-chain amino acid metabolised in muscle.<br><br>Take Notes: Valine = essential branched-chain amino acid | With leucine and isoleucine | Dietary requirement"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme kinetic parameter reflects maximum catalytic activity?","a":"Km","b":"Vmax","c":"kcat/Km","d":"Substrate affinity","ans":"B","e":"Vmax reflects the maximum catalytic activity of an enzyme at substrate saturation. It is proportional to enzyme amount, unlike the intrinsic constant Km.<br><br>Take Notes: Vmax = maximum catalytic activity | At saturation | Proportional to enzyme amount"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which condition results in increased Km with unchanged Vmax?","a":"Non-competitive inhibition","b":"Uncompetitive inhibition","c":"Competitive inhibition","d":"Irreversible inhibition","ans":"C","e":"Competitive inhibition results in increased Km with unchanged Vmax. Because it binds the active site, more substrate overcomes it.<br><br>Take Notes: Competitive inhibition = higher Km, same Vmax | Active-site competition | Surmountable by substrate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Enzymes increase the rate of biochemical reactions primarily by:","a":"Increasing free energy change","b":"Lowering activation energy","c":"Shifting equilibrium to products","d":"Increasing substrate concentration","ans":"B","e":"Enzymes accelerate reactions by lowering the activation energy, stabilizing the transition state. They speed the rate without altering the reaction's equilibrium or free-energy change.<br><br>Take Notes: Enzymes lower activation energy | Stabilize transition state | Do not change equilibrium"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which component of an enzyme directly participates in catalysis?","a":"Apoenzyme","b":"Cofactor","c":"Active site","d":"Allosteric site","ans":"C","e":"The active site directly participates in catalysis, binding substrate and carrying out the reaction. Its specific shape and chemistry give the enzyme its substrate specificity.<br><br>Take Notes: Active site does the catalysis | Binds substrate | Specific shape and chemistry"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"An apoenzyme combined with its cofactor forms a:","a":"Zymogen","b":"Isoenzyme","c":"Holoenzyme","d":"Metalloenzyme","ans":"C","e":"An apoenzyme combined with its cofactor forms a catalytically active holoenzyme. Without the cofactor, the inactive apoenzyme cannot catalyse the reaction.<br><br>Take Notes: Apoenzyme plus cofactor = holoenzyme | Active complete enzyme | Cofactor is essential"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which of the following is an organic cofactor?","a":"Zn²⁺","b":"Mg²⁺","c":"NAD⁺","d":"Fe²⁺","ans":"C","e":"NAD+ is an organic cofactor (coenzyme) derived from niacin. As an electron carrier, it cycles between NAD+ and NADH in redox reactions.<br><br>Take Notes: NAD+ = organic cofactor | Derived from niacin | Electron carrier in redox reactions"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Isoenzymes are best defined as enzymes that:","a":"Catalyze different reactions","b":"Have different structures but same function","c":"Have same structure but different functions","d":"Are inactive forms of enzymes","ans":"B","e":"Isoenzymes are enzymes with different structures but the same catalytic function. Their tissue-specific patterns make isoenzymes useful diagnostic markers.<br><br>Take Notes: Isoenzymes = different structure, same function | Tissue-specific forms | Useful diagnostic markers"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which isoenzyme is predominantly increased in myocardial infarction?","a":"LDH-1","b":"LDH-5","c":"CK-MM","d":"AST","ans":"A","e":"LDH-1 is the isoenzyme predominantly increased in myocardial infarction. Troponin has now largely replaced LDH isoenzymes for diagnosing infarction.<br><br>Take Notes: LDH-1 rises in myocardial infarction | Cardiac isoenzyme | Now largely replaced by troponin"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Competitive inhibitors typically resemble the:","a":"Product","b":"Enzyme","c":"Substrate","d":"Cofactor","ans":"C","e":"Competitive inhibitors typically resemble the substrate, competing for the active site. Because they resemble substrate, more substrate can outcompete them.<br><br>Take Notes: Competitive inhibitors resemble substrate | Compete at active site | Overcome by more substrate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Competitive inhibition can be overcome by:","a":"Decreasing substrate concentration","b":"Increasing substrate concentration","c":"Increasing inhibitor concentration","d":"Decreasing enzyme concentration","ans":"B","e":"Competitive inhibition can be overcome by increasing substrate concentration. This surmountability distinguishes it from non-competitive inhibition.<br><br>Take Notes: Competitive inhibition surmountable | More substrate outcompetes inhibitor | Km rises, Vmax unchanged"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which parameter is increased in competitive inhibition?","a":"Vmax","b":"Km","c":"Turnover number","d":"Enzyme concentration","ans":"B","e":"Km is increased in competitive inhibition, while Vmax is unchanged. Vmax stays unchanged, since the inhibitor only competes for the active site.<br><br>Take Notes: Competitive inhibition raises Km | Vmax unchanged | Reflects apparent lower affinity"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Non-competitive inhibitors bind to:","a":"Active site only","b":"Substrate only","c":"Enzyme-substrate complex only","d":"Allosteric site","ans":"D","e":"Non-competitive inhibitors bind to an allosteric site distinct from the active site. Because it binds elsewhere, extra substrate cannot displace it.<br><br>Take Notes: Non-competitive inhibitor binds allosteric site | Not the active site | Not overcome by substrate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which parameter is decreased in non-competitive inhibition?","a":"Km","b":"Substrate affinity","c":"Vmax","d":"Km and substrate affinity","ans":"C","e":"Vmax is decreased in non-competitive inhibition, while Km stays the same. It effectively removes active enzyme, lowering Vmax without changing Km.<br><br>Take Notes: Non-competitive inhibition lowers Vmax | Km unchanged | Effectively removes active enzyme"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Non-competitive inhibition cannot be reversed by:","a":"Removing inhibitor","b":"Increasing enzyme concentration","c":"Increasing substrate concentration","d":"Decreasing temperature","ans":"C","e":"Non-competitive inhibition cannot be reversed by increasing substrate concentration. Binding a separate site means substrate cannot restore activity.<br><br>Take Notes: Non-competitive inhibition not surmountable | Binds separate site | Substrate does not displace it"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Allosteric enzymes typically show which kinetic behavior?","a":"Linear","b":"Hyperbolic","c":"Sigmoidal","d":"Exponential","ans":"C","e":"Allosteric enzymes typically show sigmoidal kinetic behaviour due to cooperativity. Phosphofructokinase-1 is the classic sigmoidal allosteric enzyme.<br><br>Take Notes: Allosteric enzymes = sigmoidal curve | Cooperative subunits | Phosphofructokinase-1 example"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Allosteric regulation occurs due to binding of effectors at:","a":"Active site","b":"Catalytic site","c":"Regulatory site","d":"Cofactor site","ans":"C","e":"Allosteric regulation occurs when effectors bind at a regulatory site separate from the active site. These effectors act as activators or inhibitors without competing for substrate.<br><br>Take Notes: Allosteric regulation at a regulatory site | Away from active site | Activators or inhibitors"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which molecule is a classic allosteric activator of phosphofructokinase-1?","a":"ATP","b":"Citrate","c":"AMP","d":"NADH","ans":"C","e":"AMP is a classic allosteric activator of phosphofructokinase-1, signalling low energy. Rising AMP signals low energy and stimulates glycolysis at this step.<br><br>Take Notes: AMP activates phosphofructokinase-1 | Signals low energy | Stimulates glycolysis"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which molecule is an allosteric inhibitor of phosphofructokinase-1?","a":"AMP","b":"ADP","c":"Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate","d":"ATP","ans":"D","e":"ATP is an allosteric inhibitor of phosphofructokinase-1, signalling high energy. High ATP signals energy sufficiency and slows glycolysis here.<br><br>Take Notes: ATP inhibits phosphofructokinase-1 | Signals high energy | Slows glycolysis"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Feedback inhibition is best described as:","a":"Substrate inhibiting first enzyme","b":"Product inhibiting last enzyme","c":"End product inhibiting first committed step","d":"Cofactor inhibiting enzyme","ans":"C","e":"Feedback inhibition is when the end product inhibits the first committed step of a pathway. This prevents wasteful overproduction of the pathway's end product.<br><br>Take Notes: Feedback inhibition = end product blocks first step | Prevents overproduction | Common regulatory motif"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme inhibition decreases both Km and Vmax?","a":"Competitive","b":"Non-competitive","c":"Uncompetitive","d":"Irreversible","ans":"C","e":"Uncompetitive inhibition decreases both Km and Vmax by binding the enzyme-substrate complex. Binding the enzyme-substrate complex gives its characteristic parallel plot lines.<br><br>Take Notes: Uncompetitive inhibition lowers Km and Vmax | Binds enzyme-substrate complex | Parallel Lineweaver-Burk lines"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Transport kinetics most closely resemble which enzyme kinetic model?","a":"Hill equation","b":"Michaelis-Menten equation","c":"Lineweaver-Burk equation","d":"Arrhenius equation","ans":"B","e":"Transport kinetics closely resemble the Michaelis-Menten model, showing saturable carrier behaviour. Carriers show a transport Km and Tmax, mirroring enzyme kinetics.<br><br>Take Notes: Carrier transport = Michaelis-Menten-like | Saturable | Has Tmax and transport Km"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"The maximum rate of transport across a membrane is called:","a":"Km","b":"Tmax","c":"Vmax","d":"Flux constant","ans":"B","e":"The maximum rate of transport across a membrane is called Tmax. It is the transport analogue of Vmax, reached when carriers are saturated.<br><br>Take Notes: Tmax = maximum transport rate | Analogous to Vmax | Seen when carriers are saturated"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Transport Km represents:","a":"Maximum transport rate","b":"Transporter concentration","c":"Affinity of transporter for substrate","d":"Energy requirement","ans":"C","e":"Transport Km represents the affinity of the transporter for its substrate. A low transport Km means high transporter affinity for the substrate.<br><br>Take Notes: Transport Km = transporter affinity | Substrate at half-Tmax | Lower means higher affinity"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Glucose transport in RBCs occurs via:","a":"Active transport","b":"Facilitated diffusion","c":"Secondary active transport","d":"Simple diffusion","ans":"B","e":"Glucose transport in red blood cells occurs via facilitated diffusion through GLUT1. Being carrier-mediated and ATP-independent, it moves glucose down its gradient.<br><br>Take Notes: RBC glucose uptake = facilitated diffusion | GLUT1 transporter | No ATP required"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which GLUT transporter is insulin-dependent?","a":"GLUT1","b":"GLUT2","c":"GLUT3","d":"GLUT4","ans":"D","e":"GLUT4 is the insulin-dependent glucose transporter in muscle and adipose tissue. Insulin triggers GLUT4 to move to the membrane, increasing glucose uptake.<br><br>Take Notes: GLUT4 = insulin-dependent | Muscle and adipose | Translocates to membrane with insulin"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Active transport is characterized by:","a":"No energy requirement","b":"Movement down concentration gradient","c":"Saturability and ATP dependence","d":"Non-specificity","ans":"C","e":"Active transport is characterized by saturability and direct ATP dependence, moving against gradients. Moving solutes against their gradient is what requires the direct ATP input.<br><br>Take Notes: Active transport = saturable and ATP-dependent | Moves against gradient | Primary uses ATP directly"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin acts as a coenzyme for transamination reactions?","a":"Vitamin B1","b":"Vitamin B2","c":"Vitamin B6","d":"Vitamin B12","ans":"C","e":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxal phosphate) acts as a coenzyme for transamination reactions. Its deficiency, as in isoniazid therapy, impairs transamination.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin B6 = transamination coenzyme | Pyridoxal phosphate | Also decarboxylation"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Thiamine (Vitamin B1) deficiency causes:","a":"Pellagra","b":"Beriberi","c":"Scurvy","d":"Rickets","ans":"B","e":"Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency causes beriberi. Wet beriberi affects the heart; dry beriberi affects nerves.<br><br>Take Notes: Thiamine deficiency = beriberi | Wet and dry forms | Also Wernicke-Korsakoff"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency leads to night blindness?","a":"Vitamin D","b":"Vitamin A","c":"Vitamin E","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin A deficiency leads to night blindness by impairing rhodopsin regeneration. Deficiency also causes xerophthalmia and impaired immunity.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin A deficiency = night blindness | Impaired rhodopsin | Retinal is the active form"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Vitamin D deficiency in children causes:","a":"Osteomalacia","b":"Scurvy","c":"Rickets","d":"Beriberi","ans":"C","e":"Vitamin D deficiency in children causes rickets, with defective bone mineralization. The adult equivalent is osteomalacia, with defective bone mineralisation.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin D deficiency in children = rickets | Defective mineralization | Osteomalacia in adults"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin is essential for γ-carboxylation of clotting factors?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin D","c":"Vitamin E","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"D","e":"Vitamin K is essential for gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X. Warfarin blocks this carboxylation, which is why it is an anticoagulant.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin K = gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors | Factors II, VII, IX, X | Warfarin blocks it"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Deficiency of Vitamin K results in:","a":"Hemolytic anemia","b":"Bleeding tendency","c":"Night blindness","d":"Pellagra","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin K deficiency results in a bleeding tendency from impaired clotting factor activation. This prolongs the prothrombin time, reflecting the extrinsic pathway.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin K deficiency = bleeding tendency | Impaired clotting factors | Prolonged PT"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin acts as an antioxidant protecting cell membranes?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin E","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"C","e":"Vitamin E acts as an antioxidant, protecting cell membranes from lipid peroxidation. It is especially protective of red cell membranes against oxidative damage.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin E = membrane antioxidant | Prevents lipid peroxidation | Protects red cells"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Vitamin C deficiency leads to:","a":"Rickets","b":"Pellagra","c":"Scurvy","d":"Beriberi","ans":"C","e":"Vitamin C deficiency leads to scurvy from impaired collagen synthesis. Failure to hydroxylate collagen causes bleeding gums and poor wound healing.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin C deficiency = scurvy | Impaired collagen | Bleeding gums and poor healing"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin is required for hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen synthesis?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin D","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin C is required for hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen synthesis. Without this hydroxylation, collagen is unstable, producing scurvy.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin C for proline and lysine hydroxylation | Collagen synthesis | Deficiency causes scurvy"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Pellagra is caused by deficiency of:","a":"Vitamin B1","b":"Vitamin B2","c":"Vitamin B3","d":"Vitamin B6","ans":"C","e":"Pellagra is caused by deficiency of vitamin B3 (niacin). It can also arise from tryptophan deficiency, since niacin is made from tryptophan.<br><br>Take Notes: Pellagra = niacin deficiency | Dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia | Also tryptophan deficiency"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"The triad of pellagra includes all EXCEPT:","a":"Dermatitis","b":"Diarrhea","c":"Dementia","d":"Dysphagia","ans":"D","e":"The triad of pellagra is dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia; dysphagia is not part of it. The three Ds, dermatitis, diarrhoea, and dementia, are the exam mnemonic.<br><br>Take Notes: Pellagra triad = dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia | Three Ds | Niacin deficiency"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin is both water-soluble and involved in redox reactions as FAD/FMN?","a":"Vitamin B1","b":"Vitamin B2","c":"Vitamin B6","d":"Vitamin B12","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin B2 (riboflavin) is water-soluble and functions in redox reactions as FAD and FMN. As FAD and FMN, it is essential for oxidation-reduction reactions.<br><br>Take Notes: Riboflavin = FAD and FMN | Redox coenzymes | Water-soluble"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Riboflavin deficiency causes:","a":"Glossitis and cheilosis","b":"Night blindness","c":"Scurvy","d":"Hemorrhage","ans":"A","e":"Riboflavin deficiency causes glossitis and cheilosis. Angular stomatitis and corneal vascularisation are also features.<br><br>Take Notes: Riboflavin deficiency = glossitis and cheilosis | Angular stomatitis | Also corneal vascularization"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia?","a":"Vitamin B6","b":"Vitamin B12","c":"Vitamin C","d":"Vitamin D","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia. Neurological signs distinguish it from folate deficiency, which lacks them.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin B12 deficiency = megaloblastic anemia | Also neurological signs | Needs intrinsic factor"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Vitamin B12 absorption requires:","a":"Bile salts","b":"Intrinsic factor","c":"Pancreatic enzymes","d":"Albumin","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin B12 absorption requires intrinsic factor secreted by gastric parietal cells. Absorbed in the terminal ileum, its uptake fails after ileal resection.<br><br>Take Notes: B12 absorption needs intrinsic factor | Parietal cell product | Absorbed in terminal ileum"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Deficiency of Vitamin B12 leads to:","a":"Peripheral neuropathy","b":"Microcytic anemia","c":"Hemolysis only","d":"Night blindness","ans":"A","e":"Vitamin B12 deficiency leads to peripheral neuropathy and subacute combined degeneration. Subacute combined degeneration affects the dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts.<br><br>Take Notes: B12 deficiency = peripheral neuropathy | Subacute combined degeneration | Neurological plus anemia"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency results in neural tube defects?","a":"Vitamin B12","b":"Folate","c":"Vitamin B6","d":"Vitamin A","ans":"B","e":"Folate deficiency results in neural tube defects during fetal development. Supplementation before conception is key to preventing these defects.<br><br>Take Notes: Folate deficiency = neural tube defects | Supplement before conception | Also megaloblastic anemia"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Folate deficiency causes:","a":"Iron deficiency anemia","b":"Megaloblastic anemia","c":"Hemolytic anemia","d":"Aplastic anemia","ans":"B","e":"Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia. Unlike B12 deficiency, it causes no neurological signs, a crucial distinction.<br><br>Take Notes: Folate deficiency = megaloblastic anemia | No neurological signs unlike B12 | Impaired DNA synthesis"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin is lipid-soluble?","a":"Vitamin B1","b":"Vitamin B6","c":"Vitamin C","d":"Vitamin D","ans":"D","e":"Vitamin D is a lipid-soluble vitamin. Being fat-soluble, it is stored in the body and can accumulate to toxicity.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin D = fat-soluble | With A, E, K | Stored in the body"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Water-soluble vitamins include all EXCEPT:","a":"Vitamin B12","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin A","d":"Vitamin B6","ans":"C","e":"Vitamin A is fat-soluble, so it is the exception among water-soluble vitamins listed. Being fat-soluble, vitamin A is the odd one out among water-soluble vitamins.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin A is fat-soluble | Not water-soluble | B and C vitamins are water-soluble"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Hypervitaminosis is most commonly associated with:","a":"Vitamin C","b":"Vitamin B12","c":"Vitamin A","d":"Vitamin B1","ans":"C","e":"Hypervitaminosis is most commonly associated with vitamin A, a stored fat-soluble vitamin. Because it is stored, excess vitamin A accumulates and becomes toxic.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin A = common hypervitaminosis | Fat-soluble, accumulates | Toxicity from excess"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Vitamin A toxicity may cause:","a":"Night blindness","b":"Liver damage","c":"Bleeding tendency","d":"Anemia","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin A toxicity may cause liver damage. Toxicity also raises intracranial pressure, causing headache and papilloedema.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin A toxicity = liver damage | Also raised intracranial pressure | Fat-soluble accumulation"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome?","a":"Vitamin B1","b":"Vitamin B6","c":"Vitamin B12","d":"Vitamin C","ans":"A","e":"Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency causes Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. It classically occurs in alcoholics and presents with confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia.<br><br>Take Notes: Thiamine deficiency = Wernicke-Korsakoff | Confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia | Common in alcoholics"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin acts as a coenzyme for carboxylation reactions?","a":"Biotin","b":"Thiamine","c":"Riboflavin","d":"Niacin","ans":"A","e":"Biotin acts as a coenzyme for carboxylation reactions. Its carboxylase reactions include pyruvate carboxylase and acetyl-CoA carboxylase.<br><br>Take Notes: Biotin = carboxylation coenzyme | Carboxylase reactions | Pyruvate carboxylase example"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Biotin deficiency may result from:","a":"Excess citrus intake","b":"Raw egg white consumption","c":"Sunlight deficiency","d":"Alcohol intake","ans":"B","e":"Biotin deficiency may result from excessive raw egg white consumption, which contains avidin. Avidin in raw egg white binds biotin and blocks its absorption.<br><br>Take Notes: Raw egg white causes biotin deficiency | Avidin binds biotin | Blocks absorption"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency presents with dermatitis, alopecia, and enteritis?","a":"Biotin","b":"Niacin","c":"Vitamin A","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"A","e":"Biotin deficiency presents with dermatitis, alopecia, and enteritis. Its carboxylase reactions link biotin to gluconeogenesis and fatty acid synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Biotin deficiency = dermatitis, alopecia, enteritis | From raw egg white or antibiotics | Carboxylation coenzyme"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme inhibitor permanently inactivates enzyme molecules?","a":"Competitive","b":"Non-competitive","c":"Reversible","d":"Irreversible","ans":"D","e":"An irreversible inhibitor permanently inactivates enzyme molecules, often by covalent binding. Organophosphates are a classic example, covalently inactivating acetylcholinesterase.<br><br>Take Notes: Irreversible inhibitor = permanent inactivation | Often covalent | Reduces active enzyme"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Organophosphates inhibit enzymes by:","a":"Competitive inhibition","b":"Non-competitive inhibition","c":"Irreversible inhibition","d":"Feedback inhibition","ans":"C","e":"Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase by irreversible covalent inhibition. This is the mechanism of nerve agents and many insecticides.<br><br>Take Notes: Organophosphates = irreversible inhibition | Covalent binding | Inhibit acetylcholinesterase"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency leads to angular stomatitis?","a":"Vitamin B2","b":"Vitamin B12","c":"Vitamin D","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"A","e":"Vitamin B2 (riboflavin) deficiency leads to angular stomatitis. Cheilosis and glossitis are the other classic riboflavin deficiency signs.<br><br>Take Notes: Riboflavin deficiency = angular stomatitis | Also cheilosis and glossitis | FAD and FMN coenzyme"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin acts as a reducing agent in hydroxylation reactions?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin D","d":"Vitamin E","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin C acts as a reducing agent in hydroxylation reactions, such as collagen synthesis. Without it, proline and lysine hydroxylation fails, producing scurvy.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin C = reducing agent | Hydroxylation of proline and lysine | Collagen synthesis"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes impaired dark adaptation?","a":"Vitamin D","b":"Vitamin K","c":"Vitamin A","d":"Vitamin E","ans":"C","e":"Vitamin A deficiency causes impaired dark adaptation due to reduced rhodopsin. Reduced rhodopsin regeneration causes the characteristic night blindness.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin A deficiency impairs dark adaptation | Reduced rhodopsin | Night blindness"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme activator increases reaction rate without being consumed?","a":"Inhibitor","b":"Substrate","c":"Cofactor","d":"Product","ans":"C","e":"A cofactor is an enzyme activator that increases the reaction rate without being consumed. It is not consumed in the reaction, distinguishing it from a substrate.<br><br>Take Notes: Cofactor increases rate | Not consumed | Metal ions or coenzymes"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes bleeding gums?","a":"Vitamin K","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin A","d":"Vitamin D","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin C deficiency causes bleeding gums, a feature of scurvy. Bleeding gums reflect defective collagen in vessel walls.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin C deficiency = bleeding gums | Scurvy | Impaired collagen in vessels"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Fat-soluble vitamins include:","a":"B and C","b":"A, D, E, K","c":"B1, B2, B6","d":"C and K","ans":"B","e":"The fat-soluble vitamins are A, D, E, and K. They are absorbed with dietary fat and stored, unlike water-soluble vitamins.<br><br>Take Notes: Fat-soluble vitamins = A, D, E, K | Stored in the body | Absorbed with dietary fat"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency leads to hemolytic anemia in premature infants?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin D","d":"Vitamin E","ans":"D","e":"Vitamin E deficiency leads to hemolytic anemia in premature infants from oxidative red cell damage. Premature infants are especially vulnerable to this oxidative haemolysis.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin E deficiency = hemolytic anemia in preemies | Oxidative membrane damage | Antioxidant vitamin"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Transport maximum (Tmax) is characteristic of:","a":"Simple diffusion","b":"Facilitated diffusion","c":"Saturable transport systems","d":"Passive diffusion only","ans":"C","e":"A transport maximum (Tmax) is characteristic of saturable transport systems. Like enzymes, saturable carriers show a maximum rate and a transport Km.<br><br>Take Notes: Tmax = saturable transport | Carrier-mediated | Analogous to Vmax"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin is involved in visual cycle as retinal?","a":"Vitamin D","b":"Vitamin A","c":"Vitamin E","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin A is involved in the visual cycle as retinal, the light-absorbing chromophore. Retinal forms rhodopsin, and its deficiency causes night blindness.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin A as retinal in vision | Forms rhodopsin | Deficiency causes night blindness"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme parameter decreases in irreversible inhibition?","a":"Km","b":"Substrate affinity","c":"Vmax","d":"Km increases only","ans":"C","e":"Vmax decreases in irreversible inhibition as active enzyme is permanently lost. Permanent loss of active enzyme lowers Vmax, mimicking non-competitive inhibition.<br><br>Take Notes: Irreversible inhibition lowers Vmax | Permanent enzyme loss | Km may appear unchanged"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes cheilosis and corneal vascularization?","a":"Vitamin B2","b":"Vitamin A","c":"Vitamin C","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"A","e":"Vitamin B2 (riboflavin) deficiency causes cheilosis and corneal vascularization. Angular stomatitis is another hallmark of riboflavin deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: Riboflavin deficiency = cheilosis and corneal vascularization | Also glossitis | FAD and FMN coenzyme"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin acts as coenzyme in oxidative decarboxylation?","a":"Thiamine","b":"Riboflavin","c":"Niacin","d":"Biotin","ans":"A","e":"Thiamine acts as a coenzyme in oxidative decarboxylation reactions. As thiamine pyrophosphate, it serves pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.<br><br>Take Notes: Thiamine = oxidative decarboxylation coenzyme | Pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase | As TPP"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which inhibitor shows same Km but lower Vmax?","a":"Competitive","b":"Non-competitive","c":"Uncompetitive","d":"Reversible","ans":"B","e":"A non-competitive inhibitor shows the same Km but a lower Vmax. Binding a separate site, it cannot be overcome by more substrate.<br><br>Take Notes: Non-competitive = same Km, lower Vmax | Binds separate site | Not overcome by substrate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency leads to glossitis and anemia?","a":"Vitamin B6","b":"Vitamin B12","c":"Vitamin C","d":"Vitamin D","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin B12 deficiency leads to glossitis and anemia. Neurological signs distinguish B12 deficiency from folate deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: B12 deficiency = glossitis and anemia | Megaloblastic | Also neurological signs"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which transport mechanism requires ATP directly?","a":"Facilitated diffusion","b":"Secondary active transport","c":"Primary active transport","d":"Passive diffusion","ans":"C","e":"Primary active transport requires ATP directly to move solutes against a gradient. The sodium-potassium pump is the classic example using ATP directly.<br><br>Take Notes: Primary active transport uses ATP directly | Against gradient | Sodium-potassium pump"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Sodium-glucose cotransporter is an example of:","a":"Passive diffusion","b":"Facilitated diffusion","c":"Secondary active transport","d":"Simple diffusion","ans":"C","e":"The sodium-glucose cotransporter is an example of secondary active transport using the sodium gradient. It harnesses the sodium gradient set up by the sodium-potassium pump.<br><br>Take Notes: SGLT = secondary active transport | Uses sodium gradient | Indirect ATP use"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes pellagra-like symptoms in isoniazid therapy?","a":"Vitamin B1","b":"Vitamin B6","c":"Vitamin B12","d":"Vitamin B2","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin B6 deficiency causes pellagra-like symptoms during isoniazid therapy. Giving pyridoxine prevents this isoniazid-induced neuropathy.<br><br>Take Notes: Isoniazid depletes vitamin B6 | Pellagra-like symptoms and neuropathy | Give pyridoxine"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme is activated by phosphorylation?","a":"Hexokinase","b":"Glycogen phosphorylase","c":"Glucokinase","d":"Pyruvate kinase","ans":"B","e":"Glycogen phosphorylase is activated by phosphorylation. Glycogen synthase is instead inactivated by phosphorylation, a reciprocal control.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycogen phosphorylase activated by phosphorylation | Breaks down glycogen | Opposite for glycogen synthase"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency leads to osteoporosis?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin D","c":"Vitamin E","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin D deficiency contributes to osteoporosis through impaired calcium absorption. Impaired calcium absorption drives the bone loss of vitamin D deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin D deficiency = osteoporosis | Impaired calcium absorption | Also rickets and osteomalacia"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin is synthesized in skin under sunlight?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin D","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"C","e":"Vitamin D is synthesized in the skin under sunlight from 7-dehydrocholesterol. It is then activated by hydroxylation in the liver and kidney.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin D made in skin with sunlight | From 7-dehydrocholesterol | Activated in liver and kidney"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme is inhibited by cyanide?","a":"Cytochrome oxidase","b":"Hexokinase","c":"Catalase","d":"ATP synthase","ans":"A","e":"Cytochrome oxidase (complex IV) is inhibited by cyanide, blocking the electron transport chain. This is the target of cyanide, which halts oxidative phosphorylation.<br><br>Take Notes: Cyanide inhibits cytochrome oxidase | Blocks complex IV | Halts oxidative phosphorylation"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes peripheral neuropathy without anemia?","a":"Vitamin B1","b":"Vitamin B6","c":"Vitamin B12","d":"Folate","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin B6 deficiency causes peripheral neuropathy without anemia. Unlike B12 deficiency, it produces neuropathy without anaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: B6 deficiency = peripheral neuropathy | Without anemia | Pyridoxal phosphate coenzyme"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin is required for methylation reactions?","a":"Vitamin B12","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin D","d":"Vitamin E","ans":"A","e":"Vitamin B12 is required for methylation reactions, as a cofactor for methionine synthase. It also serves methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, linking B12 to odd-chain fatty acids.<br><br>Take Notes: B12 for methylation | Methionine synthase cofactor | Also methylmalonyl-CoA mutase"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which inhibitor reduces enzyme activity by binding covalently?","a":"Competitive","b":"Reversible","c":"Irreversible","d":"Allosteric","ans":"C","e":"An irreversible inhibitor reduces enzyme activity by binding covalently and permanently. Aspirin and organophosphates are classic covalent, irreversible inhibitors.<br><br>Take Notes: Irreversible inhibitor binds covalently | Permanent inactivation | Aspirin, organophosphates"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency leads to impaired wound healing?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin D","d":"Vitamin E","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin C deficiency leads to impaired wound healing from defective collagen. Defective collagen from scurvy underlies the impaired healing.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin C deficiency impairs wound healing | Defective collagen | Feature of scurvy"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin acts as coenzyme for transketolase?","a":"Riboflavin","b":"Niacin","c":"Thiamine","d":"Biotin","ans":"C","e":"Thiamine acts as a coenzyme for transketolase in the pentose phosphate pathway. As thiamine pyrophosphate, it also serves the dehydrogenase complexes.<br><br>Take Notes: Thiamine = transketolase coenzyme | Pentose phosphate pathway | As thiamine pyrophosphate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme is an example of allosteric enzyme?","a":"Hexokinase","b":"Glucokinase","c":"Phosphofructokinase-1","d":"Lactate dehydrogenase","ans":"C","e":"Phosphofructokinase-1 is a classic example of an allosteric enzyme. AMP activates and ATP inhibits it, matching cellular energy status.<br><br>Take Notes: Phosphofructokinase-1 = allosteric enzyme | Activated by AMP, inhibited by ATP | Rate-limiting in glycolysis"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes scaly dermatitis and alopecia?","a":"Biotin","b":"Vitamin A","c":"Vitamin C","d":"Vitamin D","ans":"A","e":"Biotin deficiency causes scaly dermatitis and alopecia. Raw egg white avidin is the classic cause of this deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: Biotin deficiency = dermatitis and alopecia | From raw egg white | Carboxylation coenzyme"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which transport process is non-saturable?","a":"Facilitated diffusion","b":"Active transport","c":"Simple diffusion","d":"Carrier-mediated transport","ans":"C","e":"Simple diffusion is a non-saturable transport process, driven only by the concentration gradient. Having no carrier, it shows neither saturation nor a transport maximum.<br><br>Take Notes: Simple diffusion = non-saturable | Gradient-driven | No carrier or Tmax"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency leads to hypersegmented neutrophils?","a":"Vitamin B12","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin A","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"A","e":"Vitamin B12 deficiency leads to hypersegmented neutrophils on the blood film. Hypersegmented neutrophils also appear in folate deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: B12 deficiency = hypersegmented neutrophils | Megaloblastic change | Also seen in folate deficiency"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin is essential for calcium absorption?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin D","c":"Vitamin E","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin D is essential for calcium absorption from the intestine. Its deficiency causes rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin D promotes calcium absorption | Intestinal uptake | Deficiency causes rickets and osteomalacia"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme activator binds reversibly to enzyme?","a":"Irreversible activator","b":"Competitive inhibitor","c":"Cofactor","d":"Toxin","ans":"C","e":"A cofactor is an enzyme activator that binds reversibly to the enzyme. It binds reversibly and enhances activity without being consumed.<br><br>Take Notes: Cofactor binds reversibly | Enhances activity | Metal ion or coenzyme"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes photophobia and corneal dryness?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin D","d":"Vitamin E","ans":"A","e":"Vitamin A deficiency causes photophobia and corneal dryness (xerophthalmia). Severe deficiency can progress to corneal ulceration and blindness.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin A deficiency = xerophthalmia | Corneal dryness and photophobia | Can progress to blindness"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which inhibitor increases apparent Km but does not change Vmax?","a":"Non-competitive","b":"Uncompetitive","c":"Competitive","d":"Irreversible","ans":"C","e":"A competitive inhibitor increases apparent Km but does not change Vmax. Vmax is unchanged, since the inhibitor only competes for the active site.<br><br>Take Notes: Competitive inhibitor raises apparent Km | Vmax unchanged | Overcome by substrate"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin is required for synthesis of NAD⁺/NADP⁺?","a":"Riboflavin","b":"Niacin","c":"Thiamine","d":"Biotin","ans":"B","e":"Niacin is required for synthesis of NAD+ and NADP+. Deficiency of niacin, needed for these coenzymes, causes pellagra.<br><br>Take Notes: Niacin makes NAD+ and NADP+ | Redox coenzymes | Deficiency causes pellagra"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes cheilosis and glossitis?","a":"Vitamin B2","b":"Vitamin B6","c":"Vitamin B12","d":"Vitamin C","ans":"A","e":"Vitamin B2 (riboflavin) deficiency causes cheilosis and glossitis. Angular stomatitis completes the classic riboflavin deficiency picture.<br><br>Take Notes: Riboflavin deficiency = cheilosis and glossitis | Also angular stomatitis | FAD and FMN coenzyme"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which transport parameter is analogous to Vmax in enzyme kinetics?","a":"Km","b":"Tmax","c":"kcat","d":"Flux constant","ans":"B","e":"Tmax is the transport parameter analogous to Vmax in enzyme kinetics. It is reached when all carriers are saturated, like Vmax at saturation.<br><br>Take Notes: Tmax analogous to Vmax | Maximum transport rate | At carrier saturation"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency is associated with convulsions in infants?","a":"Vitamin B1","b":"Vitamin B6","c":"Vitamin B12","d":"Vitamin D","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin B6 deficiency is associated with convulsions in infants. These pyridoxine-responsive seizures reflect impaired GABA synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: B6 deficiency = infant convulsions | Impaired GABA synthesis | Pyridoxine-responsive seizures"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme is inhibited by heavy metals like lead?","a":"ALA dehydratase","b":"Hexokinase","c":"Pyruvate kinase","d":"LDH","ans":"A","e":"ALA dehydratase is inhibited by heavy metals like lead, impairing heme synthesis. This is why lead poisoning causes a microcytic anaemia with basophilic stippling.<br><br>Take Notes: Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase | Impairs heme synthesis | Causes anemia"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency leads to sideroblastic anemia?","a":"Vitamin B6","b":"Vitamin B12","c":"Folate","d":"Vitamin C","ans":"A","e":"Vitamin B6 deficiency leads to sideroblastic anemia from impaired heme synthesis. Pyridoxal phosphate is needed by ALA synthase, the first heme-synthesis enzyme.<br><br>Take Notes: B6 deficiency = sideroblastic anemia | Impaired heme synthesis | Pyridoxal phosphate needed"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin acts as coenzyme for acetylation reactions?","a":"Pantothenic acid","b":"Riboflavin","c":"Thiamine","d":"Niacin","ans":"A","e":"Pantothenic acid acts as a coenzyme for acetylation reactions as part of coenzyme A. As part of coenzyme A, it carries acyl groups in metabolism.<br><br>Take Notes: Pantothenic acid = part of coenzyme A | Acetylation reactions | Acetyl-CoA carrier"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which inhibitor binds only to enzyme-substrate complex?","a":"Competitive","b":"Non-competitive","c":"Uncompetitive","d":"Irreversible","ans":"C","e":"An uncompetitive inhibitor binds only to the enzyme-substrate complex. Binding the enzyme-substrate complex gives parallel Lineweaver-Burk lines.<br><br>Take Notes: Uncompetitive inhibitor binds enzyme-substrate complex | Lowers Km and Vmax | Parallel Lineweaver-Burk lines"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes paresthesia and burning feet syndrome?","a":"Vitamin B1","b":"Vitamin B2","c":"Vitamin B3","d":"Vitamin B5","ans":"A","e":"Vitamin B1 (thiamine) deficiency causes paresthesia and burning feet syndrome. Dry beriberi is the peripheral neuropathy form of thiamine deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: Thiamine deficiency = burning feet and paresthesia | Dry beriberi | Peripheral neuropathy"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin is required for synthesis of coagulation factors II, VII, IX, X?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin D","c":"Vitamin E","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"D","e":"Vitamin K is required for synthesis of coagulation factors II, VII, IX, and X. Warfarin blocks their gamma-carboxylation, producing anticoagulation.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin K for factors II, VII, IX, X | Gamma-carboxylation | Warfarin antagonizes it"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which transport system exhibits competitive inhibition?","a":"Simple diffusion","b":"Carrier-mediated transport","c":"Osmosis","d":"Filtration","ans":"B","e":"Carrier-mediated transport exhibits competitive inhibition when similar substrates compete. Similar substrates compete for the same carrier, mimicking enzyme inhibition.<br><br>Take Notes: Carrier transport shows competitive inhibition | Similar substrates compete | Saturable system"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes impaired taste sensation?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin B12","c":"Vitamin C","d":"Zinc deficiency","ans":"D","e":"Zinc deficiency causes impaired taste sensation. Poor wound healing is another feature of zinc deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: Zinc deficiency = impaired taste | Also poor wound healing | Cofactor for many enzymes"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which enzyme kinetic parameter reflects substrate affinity?","a":"Vmax","b":"Km","c":"kcat","d":"Tmax","ans":"B","e":"Km reflects substrate affinity in enzyme kinetics. A low Km means high affinity, independent of enzyme amount.<br><br>Take Notes: Km reflects affinity | Low Km, high affinity | Independent of enzyme amount"},
{"t":"Amino Acids, Proteins and Enzymes","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes hemolysis due to oxidative stress?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin D","d":"Vitamin E","ans":"D","e":"Vitamin E deficiency causes hemolysis due to oxidative stress on red cell membranes. Premature infants are particularly susceptible to this haemolysis.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin E deficiency = hemolysis | Oxidative membrane damage | Antioxidant vitamin"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which class of hormones primarily binds to intracellular receptors?","a":"Peptide hormones","b":"Glycoprotein hormones","c":"Steroid hormones","d":"Catecholamines","ans":"C","e":"Steroid hormones bind intracellular receptors because their lipid solubility lets them cross the cell membrane. Their intracellular receptors act as transcription factors, giving slow but sustained effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroids bind intracellular receptors | Cross membrane freely | Act on gene transcription"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Water-soluble hormones usually exert their effects by:","a":"Directly altering gene transcription","b":"Binding to nuclear receptors","c":"Activating cell surface receptors and second messengers","d":"Diffusing through lipid bilayer","ans":"C","e":"Water-soluble hormones act by activating cell surface receptors and generating second messengers. These second messengers give rapid responses without entering the nucleus.<br><br>Take Notes: Water-soluble hormones use surface receptors | Second messengers relay signal | Rapid effects"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone is lipid soluble?","a":"Insulin","b":"Glucagon","c":"Cortisol","d":"Growth hormone","ans":"C","e":"Cortisol is lipid soluble, allowing it to cross the membrane and bind intracellular receptors. Its intracellular receptor alters gene transcription, giving delayed but lasting effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Cortisol = lipid-soluble steroid | Intracellular receptor | Gene transcription effects"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which of the following is a water-soluble hormone?","a":"Thyroxine","b":"Aldosterone","c":"Insulin","d":"Vitamin D","ans":"C","e":"Insulin is a water-soluble peptide hormone acting on cell surface receptors. Its receptor is a tyrosine kinase, unlike the intracellular receptors of steroids.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin = water-soluble peptide | Surface receptor | Receptor tyrosine kinase"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones circulate in blood primarily:","a":"Free in plasma","b":"Bound to carrier proteins","c":"Inside erythrocytes","d":"Bound to albumin exclusively","ans":"B","e":"Lipid-soluble hormones circulate in blood bound to carrier proteins because they are poorly water soluble. Only the free, unbound fraction is biologically active, while the bound pool acts as a reserve.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipid-soluble hormones bind carrier proteins | Poor water solubility | Prolongs half-life"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which receptor type is used by peptide hormones?","a":"Nuclear receptor","b":"Cytosolic receptor","c":"G-protein-coupled receptor","d":"Intracellular transcription factor only","ans":"C","e":"Peptide hormones typically use G-protein-coupled receptors on the cell surface. They then trigger second messengers such as cAMP or IP3 for rapid signalling.<br><br>Take Notes: Peptide hormones use surface receptors | Often GPCRs | Trigger second messengers"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"The classic second messenger generated by adenylate cyclase is:","a":"cGMP","b":"IP₃","c":"DAG","d":"cAMP","ans":"D","e":"cAMP is the classic second messenger generated by adenylate cyclase. Adenylate cyclase makes it, and protein kinase A carries out most of its effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Adenylate cyclase makes cAMP | Classic second messenger | Activates protein kinase A"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Activation of adenylate cyclase is mediated by:","a":"Gi protein","b":"Gs protein","c":"Gq protein","d":"Tyrosine kinase","ans":"B","e":"Activation of adenylate cyclase is mediated by the stimulatory Gs protein. The inhibitory Gi protein has the opposite effect, lowering cAMP.<br><br>Take Notes: Gs protein activates adenylate cyclase | Raises cAMP | Gi inhibits it"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone acts via cAMP pathway?","a":"Insulin","b":"Growth hormone","c":"Glucagon","d":"Thyroxine","ans":"C","e":"Glucagon acts via the cAMP pathway to raise blood glucose. Its cAMP signal drives glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis to raise glucose.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon uses cAMP | Raises blood glucose | Stimulates glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Binding of hormone to GPCR leads to activation of:","a":"DNA polymerase","b":"Protein kinase A directly","c":"G-protein","d":"RNA polymerase","ans":"C","e":"Hormone binding to a GPCR leads to activation of a G-protein. The alpha-subunit exchanges GDP for GTP to relay the signal.<br><br>Take Notes: GPCR activation triggers G-protein | Exchanges GDP for GTP | Relays the signal"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"The PIP₂ pathway generates which two second messengers?","a":"cAMP and Ca²⁺","b":"IP₃ and DAG","c":"cGMP and NO","d":"ATP and ADP","ans":"B","e":"The PIP2 pathway generates the two second messengers IP3 and DAG. Phospholipase C cleaves PIP2 to produce these two messengers.<br><br>Take Notes: PIP2 yields IP3 and DAG | Phospholipase C cleaves PIP2 | Calcium and PKC signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"IP₃ primarily causes:","a":"Protein kinase C activation","b":"Ca²⁺ release from endoplasmic reticulum","c":"cAMP synthesis","d":"Gene transcription directly","ans":"B","e":"IP3 primarily causes calcium release from the endoplasmic reticulum. This calcium release then triggers downstream calcium-dependent responses.<br><br>Take Notes: IP3 releases calcium from ER | Raises intracellular calcium | Triggers responses"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"DAG mainly activates:","a":"Protein kinase A","b":"Protein kinase C","c":"Tyrosine kinase","d":"Guanylate cyclase","ans":"B","e":"DAG mainly activates protein kinase C. Together with IP3-driven calcium, it completes the PIP2 signalling branch.<br><br>Take Notes: DAG activates protein kinase C | Membrane-associated | Part of PIP2 pathway"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which G-protein is involved in PIP₂ pathway?","a":"Gs","b":"Gi","c":"Gq","d":"Go","ans":"C","e":"The Gq protein is involved in the PIP2 signalling pathway. It activates phospholipase C to generate IP3 and DAG.<br><br>Take Notes: Gq activates phospholipase C | Generates IP3 and DAG | Calcium signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone uses the IP₃-DAG pathway?","a":"ACTH","b":"TSH","c":"Vasopressin (V1)","d":"Glucagon","ans":"C","e":"Vasopressin acting on V1 receptors uses the IP3-DAG pathway to cause vasoconstriction. Its V1 receptors raise calcium via IP3, producing vasoconstriction.<br><br>Take Notes: Vasopressin V1 = IP3-DAG pathway | Raises calcium | Vasoconstriction"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"cGMP acts as second messenger for:","a":"Insulin","b":"Nitric oxide","c":"Glucagon","d":"Epinephrine","ans":"B","e":"cGMP acts as a second messenger for nitric oxide signalling. Nitric oxide raises cGMP to relax vascular smooth muscle.<br><br>Take Notes: Nitric oxide raises cGMP | Second messenger | Smooth muscle relaxation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Guanylate cyclase converts GTP into:","a":"ATP","b":"cAMP","c":"cGMP","d":"GMP","ans":"C","e":"Guanylate cyclase converts GTP into cGMP. Nitric oxide and ANP both activate guanylate cyclase to raise cGMP.<br><br>Take Notes: Guanylate cyclase makes cGMP | From GTP | Activated by nitric oxide and ANP"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone works through cGMP pathway?","a":"ANP","b":"Insulin","c":"Growth hormone","d":"Cortisol","ans":"A","e":"ANP works through the cGMP pathway to promote natriuresis and vasodilation. Its particulate guanylate cyclase raises cGMP to promote sodium and water loss.<br><br>Take Notes: ANP uses cGMP | Natriuresis and vasodilation | Particulate guanylate cyclase"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin receptor belongs to which receptor family?","a":"GPCR","b":"Nuclear receptor","c":"Receptor tyrosine kinase","d":"Ion channel receptor","ans":"C","e":"The insulin receptor belongs to the receptor tyrosine kinase family. It autophosphorylates on binding, then activates PI3K-Akt and MAPK pathways.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin receptor = receptor tyrosine kinase | Autophosphorylates | Activates PI3K-Akt"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin binding causes:","a":"Activation of adenylate cyclase","b":"Autophosphorylation of receptor","c":"Inhibition of PI3-kinase","d":"Increased cAMP","ans":"B","e":"Insulin binding causes autophosphorylation of the receptor, activating its kinase activity. This activated kinase recruits IRS proteins to propagate insulin signalling.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin binding = receptor autophosphorylation | Activates tyrosine kinase | Recruits IRS proteins"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which intracellular pathway is most important for insulin metabolic effects?","a":"cAMP-PKA","b":"JAK-STAT","c":"PI3K-Akt","d":"cGMP-PKG","ans":"C","e":"The PI3K-Akt pathway is most important for the metabolic effects of insulin. It drives GLUT4 translocation and glycogen synthesis, the core metabolic actions.<br><br>Take Notes: PI3K-Akt = insulin metabolic pathway | GLUT4 translocation | Glycogen and lipid synthesis"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin increases glucose uptake by translocation of:","a":"GLUT1","b":"GLUT2","c":"GLUT3","d":"GLUT4","ans":"D","e":"Insulin increases glucose uptake by translocating GLUT4 to the cell membrane. This is why insulin lowers blood glucose in muscle and adipose tissue.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin translocates GLUT4 | Muscle and adipose | Increases glucose uptake"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone antagonizes insulin action?","a":"IGF-1","b":"Glucagon","c":"Somatostatin","d":"Insulin-like growth factor","ans":"B","e":"Glucagon antagonizes insulin action, raising blood glucose. As a counter-regulatory hormone, it opposes insulin during fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon opposes insulin | Raises blood glucose | Counter-regulatory hormone"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Growth hormone receptor signals mainly via:","a":"cAMP","b":"PI3K","c":"JAK-STAT pathway","d":"cGMP","ans":"C","e":"The growth hormone receptor signals mainly via the JAK-STAT pathway. IGF-1 mediates many of its growth-promoting effects downstream.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth hormone uses JAK-STAT | Receptor-associated kinase | Gene transcription"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Growth hormone primarily increases:","a":"Glucose uptake","b":"Lipogenesis","c":"Protein synthesis","d":"Glycogen breakdown","ans":"C","e":"Growth hormone primarily increases protein synthesis, promoting growth. Much of this effect is mediated indirectly through liver-derived IGF-1.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth hormone increases protein synthesis | Anabolic | Acts partly via IGF-1"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin-like growth factors act via:","a":"GPCR","b":"Nuclear receptors","c":"Tyrosine kinase receptors","d":"Ion channels","ans":"C","e":"Insulin-like growth factors act via tyrosine kinase receptors. Like insulin, they signal through tyrosine kinase receptors to promote growth.<br><br>Take Notes: IGFs use tyrosine kinase receptors | Similar to insulin receptor | Promote growth"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone decreases blood glucose?","a":"Cortisol","b":"Epinephrine","c":"Growth hormone","d":"Insulin","ans":"D","e":"Insulin decreases blood glucose by promoting uptake and storage. It promotes uptake and storage, the opposite of glucagon's actions.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin lowers blood glucose | Promotes uptake and storage | GLUT4 translocation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormone-receptor complex directly binds to:","a":"Ribosomes","b":"Plasma membrane","c":"DNA regulatory elements","d":"Mitochondria","ans":"C","e":"A lipid-soluble hormone-receptor complex directly binds DNA regulatory elements to alter transcription. This direct DNA binding gives steroids their slow, sustained transcriptional effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid-receptor complex binds DNA | Regulatory elements | Alters gene transcription"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone has the longest half-life?","a":"Insulin","b":"Epinephrine","c":"Cortisol","d":"Glucagon","ans":"C","e":"Cortisol has the longest half-life among these hormones, being protein-bound and lipid-soluble. Its carrier binding and lipid solubility prolong its circulating half-life.<br><br>Take Notes: Cortisol = long half-life | Carrier-bound | Steroid hormone"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Water-soluble hormones usually have:","a":"Long half-life","b":"Slow onset","c":"Short half-life","d":"Nuclear receptors","ans":"C","e":"Water-soluble hormones usually have a short half-life, being rapidly cleared. Not being carrier-bound, they are cleared quickly, unlike steroids.<br><br>Take Notes: Water-soluble hormones = short half-life | Rapid clearance | Not carrier-bound"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone increases cAMP by inhibiting phosphodiesterase?","a":"Insulin","b":"Caffeine-like methylxanthines","c":"Cortisol","d":"Thyroxine","ans":"B","e":"Methylxanthines like caffeine increase cAMP by inhibiting phosphodiesterase, which normally degrades it. By blocking phosphodiesterase, they raise cAMP, explaining caffeine's effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Methylxanthines inhibit phosphodiesterase | Raise cAMP | Caffeine, theophylline"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Mechanism of action of catecholamines involves:","a":"Nuclear receptor binding","b":"GPCR and second messengers","c":"Direct DNA binding","d":"Ion channel opening only","ans":"B","e":"Catecholamines act through GPCRs and second messengers such as cAMP and calcium. Alpha and beta receptor subtypes couple to calcium and cAMP respectively.<br><br>Take Notes: Catecholamines use GPCRs | Second messengers | Alpha and beta receptor subtypes"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which pathway mediates smooth muscle relaxation by NO?","a":"cAMP-PKA","b":"IP₃-DAG","c":"cGMP-PKG","d":"PI3K-Akt","ans":"C","e":"The cGMP-PKG pathway mediates smooth muscle relaxation by nitric oxide. Nitric oxide raises cGMP, activating protein kinase G to relax muscle.<br><br>Take Notes: Nitric oxide relaxes muscle via cGMP-PKG | Lowers calcium | Vasodilation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone primarily acts via inhibition of adenylate cyclase?","a":"Glucagon","b":"Somatostatin","c":"ACTH","d":"TSH","ans":"B","e":"Somatostatin acts primarily by inhibiting adenylate cyclase through Gi. Acting through Gi, it lowers cAMP as a broad inhibitory hormone.<br><br>Take Notes: Somatostatin inhibits adenylate cyclase | Via Gi protein | Lowers cAMP"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Gi protein activation results in:","a":"Increased cAMP","b":"Decreased cAMP","c":"Increased Ca²⁺","d":"Activation of PKC","ans":"B","e":"Gi protein activation results in decreased cAMP. This is the opposite of the stimulatory Gs protein.<br><br>Take Notes: Gi lowers cAMP | Inhibits adenylate cyclase | Opposite of Gs"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone uses both metabolic and mitogenic pathways?","a":"Cortisol","b":"Insulin","c":"Aldosterone","d":"Glucagon","ans":"B","e":"Insulin uses both metabolic (PI3K-Akt) and mitogenic (MAPK) pathways. It signals through both metabolic and mitogenic pathways simultaneously.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin = metabolic plus mitogenic pathways | PI3K-Akt and MAPK | Dual signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Growth factors mainly regulate:","a":"Electrolyte balance","b":"Gene transcription via kinases","c":"Acute metabolic responses only","d":"Ion transport exclusively","ans":"B","e":"Growth factors mainly regulate gene transcription via kinase cascades. These cascades drive proliferation, as in the MAPK pathway.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth factors regulate transcription | Via kinase cascades | Drive proliferation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Epidermal growth factor receptor is:","a":"GPCR","b":"Ion channel","c":"Tyrosine kinase receptor","d":"Nuclear receptor","ans":"C","e":"The epidermal growth factor receptor is a tyrosine kinase receptor. On binding, it autophosphorylates and drives cell proliferation.<br><br>Take Notes: EGFR = tyrosine kinase receptor | Autophosphorylates | Drives proliferation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone increases intracellular Ca²⁺ via IP₃?","a":"Insulin","b":"ANP","c":"Vasopressin (V1)","d":"Glucagon","ans":"C","e":"Vasopressin V1 receptors increase intracellular calcium via IP3. Its V1 receptors couple through Gq to raise calcium.<br><br>Take Notes: Vasopressin V1 raises calcium | Via IP3 | Vasoconstriction"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"cAMP exerts most effects through activation of:","a":"Protein kinase C","b":"Protein kinase A","c":"Tyrosine kinase","d":"Phosphatase","ans":"B","e":"cAMP exerts most of its effects through activation of protein kinase A. Protein kinase A then phosphorylates target enzymes throughout the cell.<br><br>Take Notes: cAMP activates protein kinase A | Phosphorylates targets | Main cAMP effector"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormone receptors are usually located in:","a":"Cell membrane","b":"Cytosol or nucleus","c":"Lysosomes","d":"Golgi apparatus","ans":"B","e":"Lipid-soluble hormone receptors are usually located in the cytosol or nucleus. There they act as transcription factors after binding hormone.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid receptors in cytosol or nucleus | Intracellular | Act as transcription factors"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone primarily regulates growth during childhood?","a":"Cortisol","b":"Insulin","c":"Growth hormone","d":"Aldosterone","ans":"C","e":"Growth hormone primarily regulates growth during childhood. Its effects are largely mediated by liver-derived IGF-1.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth hormone drives childhood growth | Via IGF-1 | Deficiency causes dwarfism"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin decreases blood glucose by:","a":"Increasing gluconeogenesis","b":"Increasing glycogen synthesis","c":"Increasing lipolysis","d":"Increasing proteolysis","ans":"B","e":"Insulin decreases blood glucose partly by increasing glycogen synthesis. By activating glycogen synthase, insulin stores glucose as glycogen.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin increases glycogen synthesis | Stores glucose | Lowers blood glucose"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which second messenger directly activates PKC?","a":"IP₃","b":"cAMP","c":"DAG","d":"cGMP","ans":"C","e":"DAG directly activates protein kinase C. DAG from PIP2 activates protein kinase C at the membrane.<br><br>Take Notes: DAG activates protein kinase C | From PIP2 | Membrane-bound signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone works via receptor dimerization?","a":"Insulin","b":"Epinephrine","c":"Glucagon","d":"ADH (V2)","ans":"A","e":"Insulin works via receptor dimerization and autophosphorylation. This dimerisation and autophosphorylation activate downstream signalling.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin receptor dimerizes | Autophosphorylates | Activates downstream signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin deficiency results in:","a":"Hypoglycemia","b":"Decreased lipolysis","c":"Increased ketogenesis","d":"Increased protein synthesis","ans":"C","e":"Insulin deficiency results in increased ketogenesis from unrestrained fat breakdown. Unrestrained lipolysis floods the liver with fatty acids, driving ketogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin deficiency = increased ketogenesis | Unrestrained lipolysis | Diabetic ketoacidosis"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone increases sodium retention via nuclear receptor?","a":"Insulin","b":"Aldosterone","c":"ANP","d":"Glucagon","ans":"B","e":"Aldosterone increases sodium retention via a nuclear (intracellular) receptor. Its intracellular receptor drives sodium retention as a steroid hormone.<br><br>Take Notes: Aldosterone = nuclear receptor | Increases sodium retention | Steroid hormone"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Growth hormone indirectly increases growth via:","a":"Insulin","b":"IGF-1","c":"Cortisol","d":"Thyroxine","ans":"B","e":"Growth hormone indirectly increases growth via IGF-1 produced by the liver. IGF-1 is the mediator of most growth-hormone effects on tissue growth.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth hormone acts via IGF-1 | Liver-derived | Mediates growth effects"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which pathway is common to many growth factors?","a":"cAMP","b":"cGMP","c":"MAP kinase pathway","d":"IP₃ only","ans":"C","e":"The MAP kinase pathway is common to many growth factors, driving proliferation. The Ras-Raf-MEK-ERK cascade drives the resulting proliferation.<br><br>Take Notes: MAP kinase = common growth factor pathway | Drives proliferation | Ras-Raf-MEK-ERK"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin receptor activation initially phosphorylates:","a":"IRS proteins","b":"Adenylate cyclase","c":"Phosphodiesterase","d":"PKC","ans":"A","e":"Insulin receptor activation initially phosphorylates IRS proteins. IRS proteins then recruit PI3K to continue the metabolic signal.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin activates IRS proteins | Docking site | Recruits PI3K"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone increases glycogen breakdown via cAMP?","a":"Insulin","b":"Cortisol","c":"Glucagon","d":"Thyroxine","ans":"C","e":"Glucagon increases glycogen breakdown via the cAMP pathway. Its cAMP signal stimulates glycogenolysis to raise blood glucose.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon uses cAMP | Stimulates glycogenolysis | Raises blood glucose"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Water-soluble hormones generally cause:","a":"Slow genomic effects","b":"Rapid non-genomic effects","c":"Permanent cellular changes","d":"DNA mutation","ans":"B","e":"Water-soluble hormones generally cause rapid non-genomic effects through second messengers. These second-messenger effects are fast and do not require new transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: Water-soluble hormones = rapid effects | Non-genomic | Second messenger signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which second messenger is derived from membrane phospholipids?","a":"cAMP","b":"cGMP","c":"IP₃","d":"ATP","ans":"C","e":"IP3 is a second messenger derived from membrane phospholipids (PIP2). Phospholipase C generates it from PIP2 to release calcium.<br><br>Take Notes: IP3 from membrane PIP2 | Phospholipase C generated | Releases calcium"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone uses JAK-STAT signaling?","a":"Insulin","b":"Growth hormone","c":"Glucagon","d":"Cortisol","ans":"B","e":"Growth hormone uses JAK-STAT signalling. This receptor-associated kinase pathway drives gene transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth hormone = JAK-STAT | Receptor-associated kinase | Gene transcription"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"cGMP causes smooth muscle relaxation by:","a":"Increasing Ca²⁺","b":"Activating PKC","c":"Decreasing intracellular Ca²⁺","d":"Increasing ATP","ans":"C","e":"cGMP causes smooth muscle relaxation by decreasing intracellular calcium. Lower calcium via protein kinase G produces the smooth-muscle relaxation.<br><br>Take Notes: cGMP relaxes muscle by lowering calcium | Via protein kinase G | Nitric oxide effect"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone decreases cAMP levels?","a":"Glucagon","b":"Epinephrine (β)","c":"Somatostatin","d":"ACTH","ans":"C","e":"Somatostatin decreases cAMP levels by inhibiting adenylate cyclase. Acting through Gi, it is a broadly inhibitory hormone.<br><br>Take Notes: Somatostatin lowers cAMP | Via Gi | Inhibitory hormone"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin promotes which process?","a":"Lipolysis","b":"Ketogenesis","c":"Glycolysis","d":"Proteolysis","ans":"C","e":"Insulin promotes glycolysis, driving glucose utilization. By promoting glucose utilisation, insulin lowers blood glucose.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin promotes glycolysis | Glucose utilization | Anabolic effect"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones usually require:","a":"Second messengers","b":"Surface receptors","c":"Carrier proteins","d":"GPCR","ans":"C","e":"Lipid-soluble hormones usually require carrier proteins for transport in blood. Only the free fraction is active, while carrier-bound hormone forms a reserve.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipid-soluble hormones need carrier proteins | Poor water solubility | Prolongs half-life"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Growth factors mainly regulate:","a":"Acute blood glucose","b":"Cell proliferation and differentiation","c":"Electrolyte balance","d":"Acid-base balance","ans":"B","e":"Growth factors mainly regulate cell proliferation and differentiation. They act locally through tyrosine kinase receptors and the MAPK pathway.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth factors drive proliferation and differentiation | Tyrosine kinase receptors | MAPK pathway"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone increases cAMP in renal collecting ducts?","a":"Aldosterone","b":"ADH (V2)","c":"ANP","d":"Insulin","ans":"B","e":"ADH acting on V2 receptors increases cAMP in the renal collecting ducts. There it raises cAMP to insert aquaporins for water reabsorption.<br><br>Take Notes: ADH V2 raises cAMP | Renal collecting duct | Inserts aquaporins"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which second messenger activates protein kinase G?","a":"cAMP","b":"DAG","c":"IP₃","d":"cGMP","ans":"D","e":"cGMP activates protein kinase G. Protein kinase G then mediates smooth-muscle relaxation downstream.<br><br>Take Notes: cGMP activates protein kinase G | Downstream effector | Smooth muscle relaxation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin signaling is terminated by:","a":"Phosphorylation of IRS","b":"Dephosphorylation of receptor","c":"Increased PI3K","d":"Increased Akt","ans":"B","e":"Insulin signalling is terminated by dephosphorylation of the receptor by phosphatases. Phosphatases reverse the receptor's autophosphorylation to end the signal.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin signalling ends by dephosphorylation | Phosphatases remove phosphates | Reverses activation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone has both metabolic and growth-promoting effects?","a":"Cortisol","b":"Insulin","c":"Aldosterone","d":"Thyroxine","ans":"B","e":"Insulin has both metabolic and growth-promoting effects. Its metabolic action uses PI3K-Akt and its mitogenic action uses MAPK.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin = metabolic plus growth-promoting | PI3K-Akt and MAPK | Dual role"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Steroid hormone receptors act as:","a":"Enzymes","b":"Ion channels","c":"Transcription factors","d":"Second messengers","ans":"C","e":"Steroid hormone receptors act as transcription factors after binding hormone. After binding hormone, they bind DNA to regulate gene expression.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid receptors = transcription factors | Bind DNA regulatory elements | Alter gene expression"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which pathway increases intracellular Ca²⁺ most directly?","a":"cAMP","b":"cGMP","c":"IP₃","d":"MAPK","ans":"C","e":"IP3 increases intracellular calcium most directly by releasing it from the endoplasmic reticulum. This rapid calcium rise triggers calcium-dependent cellular responses.<br><br>Take Notes: IP3 raises intracellular calcium | Releases from ER | Rapid signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Growth hormone secretion is inhibited by:","a":"GHRH","b":"Somatostatin","c":"IGF-1 only","d":"Insulin","ans":"B","e":"Growth hormone secretion is inhibited by somatostatin. GHRH stimulates and somatostatin inhibits growth hormone release.<br><br>Take Notes: Somatostatin inhibits growth hormone | Hypothalamic control | Opposes GHRH"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin receptor has intrinsic:","a":"ATPase activity","b":"Tyrosine kinase activity","c":"Guanylate cyclase activity","d":"Phosphodiesterase activity","ans":"B","e":"The insulin receptor has intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity. On autophosphorylation, this kinase activates downstream signalling.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin receptor = intrinsic tyrosine kinase | Autophosphorylates | Activates signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone primarily acts through gene transcription?","a":"Epinephrine","b":"Insulin","c":"Cortisol","d":"Glucagon","ans":"C","e":"Cortisol primarily acts through gene transcription via intracellular receptors. Its intracellular receptor gives delayed but sustained gene-mediated effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Cortisol acts via gene transcription | Intracellular receptor | Delayed but sustained effects"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which messenger amplifies hormonal signal the most?","a":"Hormone itself","b":"Receptor","c":"Second messenger","d":"Transcription factor","ans":"C","e":"A second messenger amplifies the hormonal signal the most, producing many molecules per receptor event. Each receptor event generates many second-messenger molecules, amplifying the signal.<br><br>Take Notes: Second messengers amplify signals | Many molecules per receptor | Signal cascade"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin resistance is mainly due to defect at:","a":"Hormone synthesis","b":"Receptor or post-receptor signaling","c":"Nuclear receptor","d":"Carrier protein","ans":"B","e":"Insulin resistance is mainly due to a defect at the receptor or post-receptor signalling level. This impaired signalling underlies the hyperglycaemia of type 2 diabetes.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin resistance = receptor or post-receptor defect | Impaired signalling | Type 2 diabetes"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone reduces lipolysis?","a":"Cortisol","b":"Growth hormone","c":"Insulin","d":"Epinephrine","ans":"C","e":"Insulin reduces lipolysis, favouring fat storage. By inhibiting hormone-sensitive lipase, it promotes fat storage.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin reduces lipolysis | Promotes fat storage | Anti-lipolytic"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"ANP causes natriuresis via:","a":"cAMP","b":"cGMP","c":"IP₃","d":"Tyrosine kinase","ans":"B","e":"ANP causes natriuresis via cGMP signalling. Its particulate guanylate cyclase raises cGMP to drive sodium loss.<br><br>Take Notes: ANP natriuresis via cGMP | Particulate guanylate cyclase | Sodium and water loss"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone increases protein synthesis in muscle?","a":"Cortisol","b":"Insulin","c":"Glucagon","d":"Aldosterone","ans":"B","e":"Insulin increases protein synthesis in muscle. By reducing protein breakdown, insulin is net anabolic in muscle.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin increases muscle protein synthesis | Anabolic | Reduces protein breakdown"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which signaling molecule is NOT a second messenger?","a":"cAMP","b":"cGMP","c":"IP₃","d":"Insulin","ans":"D","e":"Insulin is a signalling molecule but is not itself a second messenger; it is the hormone. cAMP, IP3, DAG, and cGMP are the messengers; insulin is the first messenger.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin is a hormone, not a second messenger | cAMP, IP3, DAG, cGMP are messengers | First messenger"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Growth factors commonly activate which kinase cascade?","a":"PKA","b":"PKC","c":"MAPK","d":"PKG","ans":"C","e":"Growth factors commonly activate the MAPK kinase cascade. The Ras-Raf-MEK-ERK cascade drives the resulting proliferation.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth factors activate MAPK | Ras-Raf-MEK-ERK | Drives proliferation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones have delayed action because they:","a":"Require second messengers","b":"Act via transcription","c":"Are rapidly degraded","d":"Bind GPCR","ans":"B","e":"Lipid-soluble hormones have delayed action because they act via gene transcription. Their transcriptional mechanism explains why steroid effects are slow but lasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid hormones = delayed action | Act via transcription | Sustained effects"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone uses both cAMP and Ca²⁺ pathways?","a":"Insulin","b":"Epinephrine","c":"Cortisol","d":"Thyroxine","ans":"B","e":"Epinephrine uses both cAMP and calcium pathways through different adrenergic receptors. Beta receptors use cAMP and alpha-1 receptors use calcium.<br><br>Take Notes: Epinephrine uses cAMP and calcium | Beta via cAMP, alpha-1 via calcium | Multiple receptor subtypes"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin promotes translocation of GLUT4 by:","a":"cAMP","b":"PI3K-Akt pathway","c":"cGMP","d":"IP₃","ans":"B","e":"Insulin promotes GLUT4 translocation via the PI3K-Akt pathway. This metabolic pathway increases glucose uptake in muscle and fat.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin GLUT4 translocation via PI3K-Akt | Metabolic pathway | Increases glucose uptake"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone primarily increases basal metabolic rate?","a":"Insulin","b":"Cortisol","c":"Thyroxine","d":"Growth hormone","ans":"C","e":"Thyroxine primarily increases the basal metabolic rate. Acting through a nuclear receptor, it raises oxygen consumption.<br><br>Take Notes: Thyroxine raises basal metabolic rate | Nuclear receptor | Increases oxygen consumption"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Growth hormone has which effect on carbohydrate metabolism?","a":"Hypoglycemia","b":"No effect","c":"Hyperglycemia","d":"Increased insulin sensitivity","ans":"C","e":"Growth hormone causes hyperglycemia by antagonizing insulin (diabetogenic effect). This diabetogenic effect explains hyperglycaemia in acromegaly.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth hormone = hyperglycemia | Antagonizes insulin | Diabetogenic"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which receptor lacks intrinsic enzymatic activity?","a":"Insulin receptor","b":"EGFR","c":"GPCR","d":"IGF-1 receptor","ans":"C","e":"GPCRs lack intrinsic enzymatic activity, relying on G-proteins to relay signals. They rely on G-proteins to relay signals, unlike tyrosine kinase receptors.<br><br>Take Notes: GPCRs lack intrinsic enzyme activity | Use G-proteins | Contrast tyrosine kinase receptors"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone decreases intracellular cAMP?","a":"Glucagon","b":"Epinephrine (β)","c":"Somatostatin","d":"ACTH","ans":"C","e":"Somatostatin decreases intracellular cAMP by inhibiting adenylate cyclase. Acting through Gi, it is a broadly inhibitory hormone.<br><br>Take Notes: Somatostatin lowers cAMP | Via Gi | Inhibitory hormone"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin stimulates which enzyme?","a":"Hormone-sensitive lipase","b":"Glycogen synthase","c":"Adenylate cyclase","d":"Phosphorylase","ans":"B","e":"Insulin stimulates glycogen synthase, promoting glycogen storage. By activating glycogen synthase, it stores glucose and lowers blood glucose.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin activates glycogen synthase | Promotes glycogen synthesis | Lowers blood glucose"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which pathway is responsible for mitogenic effects of insulin?","a":"PI3K-Akt","b":"MAPK","c":"cAMP","d":"cGMP","ans":"B","e":"The MAPK pathway is responsible for the mitogenic effects of insulin. Its metabolic effects instead use the PI3K-Akt pathway.<br><br>Take Notes: MAPK = insulin mitogenic pathway | Drives proliferation | Distinct from metabolic PI3K-Akt"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone increases intracellular Ca²⁺ via Gq protein?","a":"Glucagon","b":"ACTH","c":"Vasopressin (V1)","d":"Insulin","ans":"C","e":"Vasopressin V1 receptors increase intracellular calcium via the Gq protein. Through Gq and IP3, they raise calcium to cause vasoconstriction.<br><br>Take Notes: Vasopressin V1 = Gq protein | Raises calcium via IP3 | Vasoconstriction"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones are synthesized mainly from:","a":"Amino acids","b":"Cholesterol","c":"Carbohydrates","d":"Fatty acids only","ans":"B","e":"Lipid-soluble hormones are synthesized mainly from cholesterol. Cholesterol is the common precursor of all steroid hormones.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid hormones from cholesterol | Lipid-soluble | Adrenal and gonadal synthesis"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone acts via cAMP to increase lipolysis?","a":"Insulin","b":"Epinephrine","c":"Aldosterone","d":"Thyroxine","ans":"B","e":"Epinephrine acts via cAMP to increase lipolysis in adipose tissue. Through beta receptors and cAMP, it mobilises fatty acids from fat.<br><br>Take Notes: Epinephrine raises lipolysis via cAMP | Beta receptors | Mobilizes fatty acids"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Growth factors differ from classical hormones by acting:","a":"Endocrine only","b":"Paracrine/autocrine","c":"Through GPCR exclusively","d":"Via second messengers only","ans":"B","e":"Growth factors differ from classical hormones by acting in paracrine or autocrine fashion. They act locally rather than as circulating endocrine signals.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth factors act paracrine/autocrine | Local action | Not endocrine"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone decreases protein breakdown?","a":"Cortisol","b":"Growth hormone","c":"Insulin","d":"Glucagon","ans":"C","e":"Insulin decreases protein breakdown, favouring protein storage. By reducing proteolysis, insulin favours net protein storage.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin reduces protein breakdown | Anabolic | Promotes protein synthesis"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which pathway is activated by NO?","a":"Adenylate cyclase","b":"Guanylate cyclase","c":"Tyrosine kinase","d":"MAPK","ans":"B","e":"Nitric oxide activates guanylate cyclase, raising cGMP. The resulting cGMP relaxes vascular smooth muscle.<br><br>Take Notes: Nitric oxide activates guanylate cyclase | Raises cGMP | Smooth muscle relaxation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin secretion is stimulated by:","a":"Hypoglycemia","b":"Increased plasma amino acids","c":"Sympathetic α-activity","d":"Somatostatin","ans":"B","e":"Insulin secretion is stimulated by increased plasma amino acids, along with glucose. Glucose and amino acids together drive postprandial insulin release.<br><br>Take Notes: Amino acids stimulate insulin secretion | Along with glucose | Postprandial release"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone uses V2 receptor in kidney?","a":"Aldosterone","b":"ADH","c":"ANP","d":"Cortisol","ans":"B","e":"ADH uses the V2 receptor in the kidney to promote water reabsorption. There it raises cAMP to insert aquaporins for water reabsorption.<br><br>Take Notes: ADH V2 receptor in kidney | Raises cAMP | Inserts aquaporins"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Growth hormone increases lipolysis by:","a":"Increasing insulin","b":"Antagonizing insulin","c":"Decreasing cAMP","d":"Activating PKG","ans":"B","e":"Growth hormone increases lipolysis by antagonizing insulin. This diabetogenic, lipolytic effect opposes insulin.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth hormone raises lipolysis | Antagonizes insulin | Diabetogenic and lipolytic"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which messenger is lipid-derived?","a":"cAMP","b":"cGMP","c":"DAG","d":"ATP","ans":"C","e":"DAG is a lipid-derived second messenger from membrane PIP2. From PIP2, it activates protein kinase C at the membrane.<br><br>Take Notes: DAG = lipid-derived messenger | From PIP2 | Activates protein kinase C"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin primarily lowers blood glucose by acting on:","a":"Liver only","b":"Muscle and adipose tissue","c":"Brain only","d":"Kidney only","ans":"B","e":"Insulin primarily lowers blood glucose by acting on muscle and adipose tissue. Its GLUT4 translocation increases glucose uptake in these tissues.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin acts on muscle and adipose | GLUT4 translocation | Lowers blood glucose"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone causes vasodilation via cGMP?","a":"Epinephrine","b":"Angiotensin II","c":"Nitric oxide","d":"Vasopressin","ans":"C","e":"Nitric oxide causes vasodilation via cGMP. Nitric oxide raises cGMP, relaxing vascular smooth muscle.<br><br>Take Notes: Nitric oxide vasodilation via cGMP | Relaxes smooth muscle | Endothelium-derived"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Growth hormone deficiency in children causes:","a":"Acromegaly","b":"Gigantism","c":"Dwarfism","d":"Cushing syndrome","ans":"C","e":"Growth hormone deficiency in children causes dwarfism. Recombinant growth hormone is used to treat this deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: Growth hormone deficiency = dwarfism | Reduced growth | Treated with recombinant GH"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Insulin receptor signaling begins with:","a":"Ligand-gated ion channel opening","b":"Receptor dimerization and autophosphorylation","c":"cAMP production","d":"Ca²⁺ influx","ans":"B","e":"Insulin receptor signalling begins with receptor dimerization and autophosphorylation. This activates the receptor's intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin signalling starts with dimerization | Autophosphorylation | Activates tyrosine kinase"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone increases hepatic glucose production?","a":"Insulin","b":"Glucagon","c":"IGF-1","d":"ANP","ans":"B","e":"Glucagon increases hepatic glucose production through glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. Its cAMP signal drives both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon raises hepatic glucose output | Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis | Via cAMP"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"The fastest hormonal responses are produced by:","a":"Steroid hormones","b":"Thyroid hormones","c":"Peptide hormones","d":"Vitamin D","ans":"C","e":"Peptide hormones produce the fastest hormonal responses through surface receptors and second messengers. Their surface receptors and second messengers give the fastest responses.<br><br>Take Notes: Peptide hormones = fastest responses | Surface receptors | Second messenger signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) G-proteins involved in signal transduction are best described as:","a":"Monomeric kinases","b":"Trimeric proteins with α, β, γ subunits","c":"Ion channels","d":"Nuclear transcription factors","ans":"B","e":"G-proteins are trimeric proteins with alpha, beta, and gamma subunits that transduce signals from GPCRs. The alpha-subunit binds GDP or GTP and determines which pathway is activated.<br><br>Take Notes: G-proteins = alpha, beta, gamma subunits | Trimeric | Alpha binds GTP/GDP"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Activation of a G-protein occurs when the α-subunit:","a":"Binds ATP","b":"Binds GDP","c":"Exchanges GDP for GTP","d":"Is phosphorylated","ans":"C","e":"A G-protein is activated when the alpha-subunit exchanges GDP for GTP. This exchange is triggered by the activated receptor, switching the G-protein on.<br><br>Take Notes: Activation = GDP-to-GTP exchange | On the alpha-subunit | Triggered by receptor"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"The intrinsic GTPase activity of Gα subunit results in:","a":"Signal amplification","b":"Signal termination","c":"Receptor activation","d":"Second messenger synthesis","ans":"B","e":"The intrinsic GTPase activity of the G-alpha subunit hydrolyzes GTP, causing signal termination. Toxins that block this GTPase, like cholera toxin, lock the G-protein active.<br><br>Take Notes: G-alpha GTPase terminates the signal | Hydrolyzes GTP to GDP | Self-limiting"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which G-protein stimulates adenylate cyclase?","a":"Gi","b":"Gq","c":"Gs","d":"Go","ans":"C","e":"Gs stimulates adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP. It is the target of cholera toxin, which locks it permanently active.<br><br>Take Notes: Gs stimulates adenylate cyclase | Raises cAMP | Target of cholera toxin"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which G-protein inhibits adenylate cyclase?","a":"Gs","b":"Gi","c":"Gq","d":"Gt","ans":"B","e":"Gi inhibits adenylate cyclase, lowering cAMP. It is the target of pertussis toxin, which prevents its inhibitory action.<br><br>Take Notes: Gi inhibits adenylate cyclase | Lowers cAMP | Target of pertussis toxin"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Activation of phospholipase C is mediated by:","a":"Gs","b":"Gi","c":"Gq","d":"Gt","ans":"C","e":"Gq mediates activation of phospholipase C, generating IP3 and DAG. These messengers then raise calcium and activate protein kinase C.<br><br>Take Notes: Gq activates phospholipase C | Generates IP3 and DAG | Raises calcium"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"The βγ subunits of G-proteins primarily:","a":"Bind DNA","b":"Have no function","c":"Modulate ion channels and signaling pathways","d":"Act as enzymes","ans":"C","e":"The beta-gamma subunits of G-proteins modulate ion channels and signalling pathways. They are not merely structural, but actively shape downstream signalling.<br><br>Take Notes: Beta-gamma subunits modulate ion channels | Also signalling pathways | Not just structural"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which second messenger pathway is directly activated by Gq protein?","a":"cAMP","b":"cGMP","c":"IP₃-DAG","d":"MAPK","ans":"C","e":"The Gq protein directly activates the IP3-DAG second messenger pathway. Phospholipase C cleaves PIP2 to raise calcium and activate protein kinase C.<br><br>Take Notes: Gq activates IP3-DAG pathway | Via phospholipase C | Calcium and PKC signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Cholera toxin causes persistent activation of:","a":"Gi protein","b":"Gs protein","c":"Gq protein","d":"Tyrosine kinase","ans":"B","e":"Cholera toxin causes persistent activation of the Gs protein. By locking Gs active, it raises cAMP and drives secretory diarrhoea.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholera toxin activates Gs | Locks it active | Raises cAMP persistently"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Cholera toxin exerts its effect by:","a":"Dephosphorylating Gsα","b":"ADP-ribosylating Gsα and inhibiting GTPase activity","c":"Blocking receptor binding","d":"Inhibiting adenylate cyclase","ans":"B","e":"Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates Gs-alpha and inhibits its GTPase activity, locking it active. This ADP-ribosylation blocks GTP hydrolysis, keeping cAMP persistently high.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates Gs-alpha | Blocks GTPase | Persistent activation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Persistent activation of Gs leads to:","a":"Decreased cAMP","b":"Increased cAMP","c":"Decreased IP₃","d":"Decreased Ca²⁺","ans":"B","e":"Persistent activation of Gs leads to increased cAMP. Persistent cAMP then drives chloride and water secretion into the gut.<br><br>Take Notes: Persistent Gs = increased cAMP | Adenylate cyclase stays on | Cholera toxin mechanism"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Increased cAMP in intestinal epithelial cells causes:","a":"Increased Na⁺ absorption","b":"Decreased Cl⁻ secretion","c":"Increased Cl⁻ and water secretion","d":"Reduced intestinal motility","ans":"C","e":"Increased cAMP in intestinal epithelial cells causes increased chloride and water secretion. This chloride and water efflux produces the watery diarrhoea of cholera.<br><br>Take Notes: High cAMP = chloride and water secretion | Secretory diarrhea | Cholera mechanism"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) The toxin of Vibrio cholerae causes diarrhea by acting on:","a":"Sodium-glucose transporter","b":"CFTR chloride channel","c":"Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase","d":"Aquaporins","ans":"B","e":"Vibrio cholerae toxin causes diarrhea by acting on the CFTR chloride channel. Activating CFTR drives the massive chloride and water loss of cholera.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholera toxin activates CFTR | Chloride and water efflux | Watery diarrhea"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"E. coli heat-labile toxin mechanism is similar to:","a":"Diphtheria toxin","b":"Botulinum toxin","c":"Cholera toxin","d":"Pertussis toxin","ans":"C","e":"E. coli heat-labile toxin has a mechanism similar to cholera toxin, raising cAMP. Both ADP-ribosylate Gs and raise cAMP, causing secretory diarrhoea.<br><br>Take Notes: E. coli heat-labile toxin like cholera toxin | ADP-ribosylates Gs | Raises cAMP"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) E. coli heat-stable toxin causes diarrhea by:","a":"Activating adenylate cyclase","b":"Activating guanylate cyclase","c":"Inhibiting Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase","d":"Blocking CFTR","ans":"B","e":"E. coli heat-stable toxin causes diarrhea by activating guanylate cyclase. Raising cGMP, it drives the secretory diarrhoea of enterotoxigenic E. coli.<br><br>Take Notes: Heat-stable toxin activates guanylate cyclase | Raises cGMP | Secretory diarrhea"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Increased cGMP in intestinal cells leads to:","a":"Reduced water secretion","b":"Increased water secretion","c":"Decreased chloride secretion","d":"Increased glucose absorption","ans":"B","e":"Increased cGMP in intestinal cells leads to increased water secretion. This cGMP-driven secretion parallels the cAMP mechanism of cholera toxin.<br><br>Take Notes: High cGMP = water secretion | Heat-stable toxin effect | Secretory diarrhea"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Pertussis toxin affects signal transduction by:","a":"Activating Gs","b":"Inhibiting Gi","c":"Activating Gq","d":"Inhibiting PLC","ans":"B","e":"Pertussis toxin affects signalling by inhibiting Gi. By keeping Gi inactive, it disinhibits adenylate cyclase and raises cAMP.<br><br>Take Notes: Pertussis toxin inhibits Gi | Prevents adenylate cyclase inhibition | Raises cAMP"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Pertussis toxin causes ADP-ribosylation of:","a":"Gsα preventing GTP binding","b":"Giα preventing GDP-GTP exchange","c":"βγ subunit","d":"Adenylate cyclase","ans":"B","e":"Pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylates Gi-alpha, preventing GDP-GTP exchange and locking Gi inactive. Locking Gi inactive prevents inhibition of adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP.<br><br>Take Notes: Pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylates Gi-alpha | Blocks GDP-GTP exchange | Gi cannot inhibit"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Inhibition of Gi results in:","a":"Decreased cAMP","b":"Increased cAMP","c":"Decreased Ca²⁺","d":"Increased DAG only","ans":"B","e":"Inhibition of Gi results in increased cAMP, since adenylate cyclase is no longer suppressed. With Gi disabled, adenylate cyclase runs unchecked and cAMP rises.<br><br>Take Notes: Gi inhibition = increased cAMP | Adenylate cyclase disinhibited | Pertussis mechanism"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which organism produces pertussis toxin?","a":"E. coli","b":"Vibrio cholerae","c":"Bordetella pertussis","d":"Shigella","ans":"C","e":"Bordetella pertussis produces pertussis toxin. Its toxin inhibits Gi, raising cAMP and causing whooping cough features.<br><br>Take Notes: Bordetella pertussis makes pertussis toxin | Inhibits Gi | Causes whooping cough"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Abnormal G-proteins that are constitutively active most commonly lead to:","a":"Hormone resistance","b":"Reduced signal transduction","c":"Continuous cell signaling","d":"Cell apoptosis","ans":"C","e":"Constitutively active G-proteins most commonly lead to continuous cell signalling. Hormone-independent signalling underlies both endocrine hyperfunction and some cancers.<br><br>Take Notes: Constitutive G-protein = continuous signalling | Hormone-independent | Basis of some tumors"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Oncogenic mutations in G-proteins usually involve:","a":"Loss of ligand binding","b":"Increased GTPase activity","c":"Decreased GTPase activity","d":"Loss of receptor binding","ans":"C","e":"Oncogenic mutations in G-proteins usually involve decreased GTPase activity, prolonging the active state. Staying GTP-bound, the G-protein signals continuously without a hormone.<br><br>Take Notes: Oncogenic G-protein = reduced GTPase | Stays GTP-bound | Continuous signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which oncogenic mutation locks Gα in active state?","a":"Enhanced GDP binding","b":"Impaired GTP hydrolysis","c":"Loss of βγ subunits","d":"Loss of receptor coupling","ans":"B","e":"An oncogenic mutation impairing GTP hydrolysis locks G-alpha in its active state. Unable to switch off, it drives continuous, hormone-independent signalling.<br><br>Take Notes: Impaired GTP hydrolysis locks G-alpha active | Cannot switch off | Oncogenic"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Constitutively active Gsα mutations are seen in:","a":"Retinoblastoma","b":"McCune-Albright syndrome","c":"MEN-2","d":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome","ans":"B","e":"Constitutively active Gs-alpha mutations are seen in McCune-Albright syndrome. This mosaic activating mutation causes endocrine hyperfunction and fibrous dysplasia.<br><br>Take Notes: McCune-Albright = activating Gs-alpha mutation | Mosaic | Endocrine hyperfunction"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"McCune-Albright syndrome is characterized by:","a":"Hypopigmentation and short stature","b":"Café-au-lait spots and endocrine hyperfunction","c":"Autoimmune disease","d":"Hormone resistance","ans":"B","e":"McCune-Albright syndrome features cafe-au-lait spots and endocrine hyperfunction. Fibrous dysplasia and precocious puberty complete the classic triad.<br><br>Take Notes: McCune-Albright = cafe-au-lait plus endocrine hyperfunction | Also fibrous dysplasia | Activating Gs mutation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which mutation causes hormone-independent activation of adenylate cyclase?","a":"Gi mutation","b":"Gq mutation","c":"Gs mutation","d":"PKC mutation","ans":"C","e":"A Gs mutation causes hormone-independent activation of adenylate cyclase. Constant cAMP production drives hormone-independent endocrine hyperfunction.<br><br>Take Notes: Gs mutation = hormone-independent adenylate cyclase | Constant cAMP | Endocrine hyperfunction"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Loss-of-function mutation of G-protein receptor leads to:","a":"Increased hormone action","b":"Hormone resistance","c":"Oncogenic transformation","d":"Excess second messengers","ans":"B","e":"A loss-of-function mutation of a G-protein-coupled receptor leads to hormone resistance. The signal cannot be received, so target tissues are unresponsive.<br><br>Take Notes: Receptor loss-of-function = hormone resistance | Signal cannot be received | End-organ unresponsive"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism is due to defect in:","a":"PTH synthesis","b":"PTH receptor","c":"Gs protein","d":"Adenylate cyclase","ans":"C","e":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism is due to a defect in the Gs protein, causing PTH resistance. Despite high PTH, target resistance keeps serum calcium low.<br><br>Take Notes: Pseudohypoparathyroidism = Gs defect | PTH resistance | End-organ unresponsive"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) In pseudohypoparathyroidism, levels of PTH are:","a":"Low","b":"Normal","c":"High","d":"Absent","ans":"C","e":"In pseudohypoparathyroidism, PTH levels are high because target tissues resist the hormone. This is the classic picture of end-organ hormone resistance.<br><br>Take Notes: Pseudohypoparathyroidism = high PTH | Target resistance | Low calcium despite high PTH"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones differ from water-soluble hormones by:","a":"Acting via GPCR","b":"Requiring second messengers","c":"Crossing cell membrane easily","d":"Having short half-life","ans":"C","e":"Lipid-soluble hormones differ from water-soluble ones by crossing the cell membrane easily. Their intracellular receptors then act as transcription factors.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipid-soluble hormones cross the membrane | Intracellular receptors | Gene transcription effects"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones include:","a":"Insulin and glucagon","b":"Growth hormone","c":"Steroid hormones","d":"Catecholamines","ans":"C","e":"Steroid hormones are lipid-soluble hormones. They cross the membrane freely to reach intracellular receptors.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid hormones = lipid-soluble | Cross membrane | Intracellular receptors"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Primary mechanism of action of lipid-soluble hormones is:","a":"Activation of adenylate cyclase","b":"Opening ion channels","c":"Regulation of gene transcription","d":"Phosphorylation of membrane proteins","ans":"C","e":"The primary mechanism of lipid-soluble hormones is regulation of gene transcription. Their transcriptional mechanism gives delayed but sustained effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipid-soluble hormones regulate transcription | Intracellular receptors | Delayed, sustained effects"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormone receptors are located mainly in:","a":"Cell membrane","b":"Cytosol or nucleus","c":"Lysosomes","d":"Golgi apparatus","ans":"B","e":"Lipid-soluble hormone receptors are located mainly in the cytosol or nucleus. There they act as transcription factors after binding hormone.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid receptors in cytosol or nucleus | Intracellular | Act as transcription factors"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Hormone-receptor complex of steroid hormones acts as:","a":"Second messenger","b":"Kinase","c":"Transcription factor","d":"Ion channel","ans":"C","e":"The steroid hormone-receptor complex acts as a transcription factor. It binds DNA response elements to alter gene expression.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid-receptor complex = transcription factor | Binds DNA | Alters gene expression"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone binds to intracellular receptor?","a":"Insulin","b":"ADH","c":"Aldosterone","d":"Glucagon","ans":"C","e":"Aldosterone binds to an intracellular receptor, acting as a steroid hormone. Its intracellular receptor drives sodium retention in the kidney.<br><br>Take Notes: Aldosterone = intracellular receptor | Steroid hormone | Increases sodium retention"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones usually have:","a":"Rapid onset","b":"Short duration","c":"Long half-life","d":"No carrier proteins","ans":"C","e":"Lipid-soluble hormones usually have a long half-life, being carrier-bound. Carrier binding prolongs their half-life, unlike water-soluble hormones.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipid-soluble hormones = long half-life | Carrier-bound | Slow clearance"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Steroid hormones in blood are transported mainly bound to:","a":"Hemoglobin","b":"Globulins and albumin","c":"Lipoproteins","d":"RBC membranes","ans":"B","e":"Steroid hormones in blood are transported mainly bound to globulins and albumin. Only the free fraction is active, while the bound pool is a reserve.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroids bound to globulins and albumin | Carrier proteins | Only free hormone is active"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Thyroid hormone mechanism is similar to:","a":"Peptide hormones","b":"Catecholamines","c":"Steroid hormones","d":"Growth factors","ans":"C","e":"Thyroid hormone mechanism is similar to steroid hormones, acting on intracellular receptors. Though amino-acid-derived, thyroid hormone acts like a steroid on gene transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: Thyroid hormone like steroids | Intracellular receptor | Gene transcription"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Binding of lipid-soluble hormone to receptor results in:","a":"cAMP production","b":"IP₃ generation","c":"Altered mRNA synthesis","d":"Ca²⁺ influx","ans":"C","e":"Binding of a lipid-soluble hormone to its receptor results in altered mRNA synthesis. New mRNA then directs synthesis of new proteins, giving slow effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid hormone alters mRNA synthesis | Changes gene expression | New proteins made"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"The slow onset of action of steroid hormones is due to:","a":"Low receptor affinity","b":"Requirement of protein synthesis","c":"Rapid degradation","d":"Second messenger use","ans":"B","e":"The slow onset of steroid hormones is due to the requirement for protein synthesis. Transcription and translation take time, explaining their delayed onset.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid slow onset = needs protein synthesis | Transcription and translation | Delayed effect"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which disease is due to abnormal G-protein signaling?","a":"Diabetes mellitus type 1","b":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism","c":"Phenylketonuria","d":"Sickle cell anemia","ans":"B","e":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism is a disease due to abnormal G-protein signalling. This abnormal Gs signalling produces PTH resistance with high PTH and low calcium.<br><br>Take Notes: Pseudohypoparathyroidism = abnormal Gs signalling | Hormone resistance | High PTH, low calcium"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Pertussis toxin causes lymphocytosis mainly due to:","a":"Decreased cAMP","b":"Increased cAMP","c":"Decreased IP₃","d":"Increased Ca²⁺","ans":"B","e":"Pertussis toxin causes lymphocytosis mainly due to increased cAMP. Raised cAMP impairs lymphocyte trafficking, producing the lymphocytosis of pertussis.<br><br>Take Notes: Pertussis lymphocytosis = increased cAMP | Impaired lymphocyte trafficking | Gi inhibition"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which G-protein is involved in visual signal transduction?","a":"Gs","b":"Gi","c":"Gt (transducin)","d":"Gq","ans":"C","e":"Gt (transducin) is the G-protein involved in visual signal transduction. It activates phosphodiesterase in photoreceptors to lower cGMP.<br><br>Take Notes: Transducin (Gt) = vision | Activates phosphodiesterase | Photoreceptor signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Phototransduction involves activation of:","a":"Adenylate cyclase","b":"Phosphodiesterase","c":"Guanylate cyclase","d":"Protein kinase C","ans":"B","e":"Phototransduction involves activation of phosphodiesterase, which lowers cGMP. Lower cGMP closes sodium channels, hyperpolarising the photoreceptor.<br><br>Take Notes: Phototransduction activates phosphodiesterase | Lowers cGMP | Closes sodium channels"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"In vision, light activation leads to:","a":"Increased cGMP","b":"Decreased cGMP","c":"Increased Na⁺ influx","d":"Depolarization","ans":"B","e":"In vision, light activation leads to decreased cGMP. This drop in cGMP closes sodium channels and hyperpolarises the cell.<br><br>Take Notes: Light lowers cGMP | Via transducin and phosphodiesterase | Hyperpolarizes photoreceptor"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Oncogenic mutations in GPCR pathways most commonly lead to:","a":"Apoptosis","b":"Growth arrest","c":"Malignancy","d":"Hormone deficiency","ans":"C","e":"Oncogenic mutations in GPCR pathways most commonly lead to malignancy. Loss of GTPase control gives continuous proliferative signalling.<br><br>Take Notes: GPCR pathway oncogenic mutations = malignancy | Continuous proliferation signals | Loss of GTPase control"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which toxin prevents GTP hydrolysis on G-protein?","a":"Pertussis toxin","b":"Cholera toxin","c":"Diphtheria toxin","d":"Botulinum toxin","ans":"B","e":"Cholera toxin prevents GTP hydrolysis on the G-protein, locking Gs active. This locks Gs active, raising cAMP and causing secretory diarrhoea.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholera toxin blocks GTP hydrolysis | Locks Gs active | Persistent cAMP"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"E. coli heat-stable toxin increases:","a":"cAMP","b":"cGMP","c":"IP₃","d":"DAG","ans":"B","e":"E. coli heat-stable toxin increases cGMP by activating guanylate cyclase. This cGMP rise parallels the cAMP mechanism of cholera toxin.<br><br>Take Notes: Heat-stable toxin raises cGMP | Activates guanylate cyclase | Secretory diarrhea"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormone response is terminated mainly by:","a":"Phosphodiesterase","b":"Receptor internalization","c":"Hormone metabolism and excretion","d":"GTP hydrolysis","ans":"C","e":"Lipid-soluble hormone response is terminated mainly by hormone metabolism and excretion. Being lipophilic, they are broken down slowly in the liver.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid response ends by metabolism and excretion | Hepatic breakdown | Slow termination"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which hormone does NOT use G-protein signaling?","a":"Glucagon","b":"ADH (V1)","c":"Insulin","d":"ACTH","ans":"C","e":"Insulin does not use G-protein signalling; it uses a receptor tyrosine kinase. It uses a receptor tyrosine kinase, so cholera and pertussis toxins do not affect it.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin does not use G-proteins | Receptor tyrosine kinase | Autophosphorylation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Constitutively active GPCR signaling is equivalent to:","a":"Absence of ligand","b":"Continuous ligand stimulation","c":"Competitive inhibition","d":"Receptor down-regulation","ans":"B","e":"Constitutively active GPCR signalling is equivalent to continuous ligand stimulation. Hormone-independent signalling can drive both hyperfunction and malignancy.<br><br>Take Notes: Constitutive GPCR = continuous ligand effect | Hormone-independent | Oncogenic potential"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which abnormality causes endocrine hyperfunction without hormone excess?","a":"Hormone overproduction","b":"Receptor mutation","c":"Constitutively active G-protein","d":"Hormone resistance","ans":"C","e":"A constitutively active G-protein causes endocrine hyperfunction without hormone excess. Autonomous signalling produces hyperfunction without raised hormone levels.<br><br>Take Notes: Constitutive G-protein = hyperfunction without hormone excess | McCune-Albright | Autonomous signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones act mainly by altering:","a":"Ion fluxes","b":"Enzyme phosphorylation","c":"Gene expression","d":"Second messengers","ans":"C","e":"Lipid-soluble hormones act mainly by altering gene expression. New mRNA and proteins give steroids their slow, sustained effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid hormones alter gene expression | Transcription regulation | Sustained effects"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"G-protein signaling is terminated when:","a":"GTP binds α-subunit","b":"GDP is released","c":"GTP is hydrolyzed to GDP","d":"βγ dissociates","ans":"C","e":"G-protein signalling is terminated when GTP is hydrolyzed to GDP by the alpha-subunit. The alpha-subunit then reassociates with beta-gamma to reset the system.<br><br>Take Notes: Signalling ends when GTP to GDP | Intrinsic GTPase | Alpha reassociates with beta-gamma"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which disease is associated with defective Gs signaling leading to hormone resistance?","a":"Acromegaly","b":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism","c":"Cushing syndrome","d":"Conn syndrome","ans":"B","e":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism is associated with defective Gs signalling causing hormone resistance. High PTH with low calcium is the hallmark of this resistance.<br><br>Take Notes: Pseudohypoparathyroidism = defective Gs | Hormone resistance | High PTH, low calcium"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Cholera toxin leads to watery diarrhea due to:","a":"Decreased Na⁺ absorption","b":"Increased Cl⁻ secretion","c":"Increased glucose absorption","d":"Reduced intestinal motility","ans":"B","e":"Cholera toxin leads to watery diarrhea due to increased chloride secretion. Chloride secretion via CFTR drives the watery stool of cholera.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholera diarrhea = increased chloride secretion | Water follows | Via raised cAMP and CFTR"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone's action is independent of G-proteins?","a":"Glucagon","b":"Epinephrine","c":"Insulin","d":"ACTH","ans":"C","e":"Insulin's action is independent of G-proteins, using receptor tyrosine kinase signalling. Being a receptor tyrosine kinase, it is unaffected by these G-protein toxins.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin independent of G-proteins | Receptor tyrosine kinase | PI3K-Akt pathway"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormone receptors usually form:","a":"Ion channels","b":"Dimers binding DNA","c":"G-protein complexes","d":"Enzyme complexes","ans":"B","e":"Lipid-soluble hormone receptors usually form dimers binding DNA. They act as transcription factors after binding hormone.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid receptors form dimers | Bind DNA | Act as transcription factors"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Signal amplification in G-protein pathways occurs mainly at:","a":"Hormone-receptor binding","b":"G-protein activation","c":"Second messenger generation","d":"Gene transcription","ans":"C","e":"Signal amplification in G-protein pathways occurs mainly at second messenger generation. Each receptor event yields many second-messenger molecules, amplifying the signal.<br><br>Take Notes: Amplification at second messenger step | Many molecules per receptor | Signal cascade"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which mutation would cause permanent inhibition of adenylate cyclase?","a":"Gs activation","b":"Gi activation","c":"Gq activation","d":"PLC activation","ans":"B","e":"A mutation causing Gi activation would cause permanent inhibition of adenylate cyclase. This is the opposite of Gs activation, which raises cAMP.<br><br>Take Notes: Gi activation = adenylate cyclase inhibition | Lowers cAMP | Opposite of Gs activation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Abnormal G-protein signaling is most commonly associated with:","a":"Metabolic enzyme deficiency","b":"Channelopathies","c":"Endocrine disorders and cancer","d":"Structural protein defects","ans":"C","e":"Abnormal G-protein signalling is most commonly associated with endocrine disorders and cancer. These defects produce either hormone resistance or hyperfunction.<br><br>Take Notes: Abnormal G-protein signalling = endocrine disorders and cancer | Hyperfunction or resistance | Loss of control"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which toxin ADP-ribosylates Gi protein?","a":"Cholera toxin","b":"E. coli toxin","c":"Pertussis toxin","d":"Tetanus toxin","ans":"C","e":"Pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylates the Gi protein. This ADP-ribosylation prevents GDP-GTP exchange, raising cAMP.<br><br>Take Notes: Pertussis toxin ADP-ribosylates Gi | Blocks GDP-GTP exchange | Raises cAMP"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormone effect persists after hormone removal because:","a":"Second messengers remain","b":"New proteins have been synthesized","c":"Receptor remains activated","d":"cAMP is increased","ans":"B","e":"A lipid-soluble hormone effect persists after hormone removal because new proteins have been synthesized. Because new proteins persist, the effect outlasts the hormone itself.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid effect persists after removal | New proteins already made | Genomic action"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone binds to glucocorticoid response elements?","a":"Insulin","b":"Cortisol","c":"Epinephrine","d":"Growth hormone","ans":"B","e":"Cortisol binds to glucocorticoid response elements on DNA. Binding these response elements lets cortisol regulate gene transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: Cortisol binds glucocorticoid response elements | Alters transcription | Steroid hormone"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Oncogenic G-protein mutations mainly affect:","a":"βγ subunits","b":"α-subunit GTPase activity","c":"Receptor binding","d":"Ligand synthesis","ans":"B","e":"Oncogenic G-protein mutations mainly affect the alpha-subunit GTPase activity. Impaired hydrolysis keeps the G-protein GTP-bound and continuously active.<br><br>Take Notes: Oncogenic mutations hit alpha-subunit GTPase | Impair hydrolysis | Constitutive activation"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which pathway is directly affected by cholera toxin?","a":"IP₃-DAG","b":"cAMP","c":"cGMP","d":"MAPK","ans":"B","e":"Cholera toxin directly affects the cAMP pathway. By locking Gs active, it raises cAMP and drives secretory diarrhoea.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholera toxin affects cAMP pathway | Activates Gs | Raises cAMP"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones typically do NOT require:","a":"Carrier proteins","b":"Intracellular receptors","c":"Gene transcription","d":"Second messengers","ans":"D","e":"Lipid-soluble hormones typically do not require second messengers, acting via gene transcription. Their transcriptional mechanism bypasses the need for second messengers.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid hormones need no second messengers | Direct gene transcription | Intracellular receptors"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which disease is due to activating mutation of Gs protein?","a":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism","b":"McCune-Albright syndrome","c":"Diabetes mellitus","d":"Hypothyroidism","ans":"B","e":"McCune-Albright syndrome is due to an activating mutation of the Gs protein. This activating Gs mutation causes endocrine hyperfunction and fibrous dysplasia.<br><br>Take Notes: McCune-Albright = activating Gs mutation | Endocrine hyperfunction | Cafe-au-lait and fibrous dysplasia"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Heat-labile E. coli toxin acts by:","a":"Increasing cGMP","b":"Increasing cAMP","c":"Blocking G-proteins","d":"Inhibiting PKC","ans":"B","e":"Heat-labile E. coli toxin acts by increasing cAMP, like cholera toxin. Both ADP-ribosylate Gs and raise cAMP, causing secretory diarrhoea.<br><br>Take Notes: Heat-labile toxin raises cAMP | ADP-ribosylates Gs | Secretory diarrhea"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"G-protein coupled receptors span the membrane:","a":"Once","b":"Three times","c":"Seven times","d":"Ten times","ans":"C","e":"G-protein-coupled receptors span the membrane seven times. This serpentine seven-transmembrane structure defines the GPCR family.<br><br>Take Notes: GPCRs = seven-transmembrane receptors | Serpentine structure | Couple to G-proteins"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Termination of lipid-soluble hormone action is slow because:","a":"Hormone binds irreversibly","b":"Hormone accumulates in fat","c":"Receptors lack feedback","d":"Second messengers persist","ans":"B","e":"Termination of lipid-soluble hormone action is slow because the hormone accumulates in fat. Lipophilic storage in fat prolongs the effect after the hormone is gone.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid termination slow = fat accumulation | Lipophilic storage | Prolonged effect"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone is an example of lipid-soluble hormone derived from cholesterol?","a":"Insulin","b":"Glucagon","c":"Cortisol","d":"ADH","ans":"C","e":"Cortisol is a lipid-soluble hormone derived from cholesterol. Cholesterol is the common precursor of all steroid hormones.<br><br>Take Notes: Cortisol from cholesterol | Lipid-soluble steroid | Intracellular receptor"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Constitutive activation of GPCR signaling is oncogenic because it:","a":"Reduces apoptosis","b":"Increases cell proliferation signals","c":"Decreases gene transcription","d":"Inhibits metabolism","ans":"B","e":"Constitutive activation of GPCR signalling is oncogenic because it increases cell proliferation signals. Continuous proliferative signalling underlies its oncogenic potential.<br><br>Take Notes: Constitutive GPCR = proliferation signals | Hormone-independent | Oncogenic"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which toxin causes prolonged cAMP elevation by preventing GTP hydrolysis?","a":"Pertussis toxin","b":"Cholera toxin","c":"Diphtheria toxin","d":"Shiga toxin","ans":"B","e":"Cholera toxin causes prolonged cAMP elevation by preventing GTP hydrolysis. Blocking GTP hydrolysis locks Gs active and keeps cAMP high.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholera toxin = prolonged cAMP | Prevents GTP hydrolysis | Locks Gs active"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones usually regulate genes by binding to:","a":"Promoters directly","b":"Enhancers or response elements","c":"Ribosomal RNA","d":"Introns only","ans":"B","e":"Lipid-soluble hormones usually regulate genes by binding to enhancers or response elements. These response elements let the hormone-receptor complex regulate transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid hormones bind response elements | Enhancer regions | Regulate transcription"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"G-protein signaling defects are MOST relevant to:","a":"Structural protein disorders","b":"Endocrine signaling disorders","c":"Lysosomal storage diseases","d":"Hemoglobinopathies","ans":"B","e":"G-protein signalling defects are most relevant to endocrine signalling disorders. They present as hormone resistance or excess, depending on the defect.<br><br>Take Notes: G-protein defects = endocrine disorders | Hormone resistance or excess | Signal transduction failure"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which abnormality causes resistance to multiple peptide hormones?","a":"Defective hormone synthesis","b":"Defective receptor","c":"Defective Gs protein","d":"Defective second messenger","ans":"C","e":"A defective Gs protein causes resistance to multiple peptide hormones. A defective Gs causes resistance to several peptide hormones at once.<br><br>Take Notes: Defective Gs = multi-hormone resistance | Pseudohypoparathyroidism | End-organ unresponsive"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormone receptors often act as:","a":"Repressors only","b":"DNA-binding proteins","c":"G-proteins","d":"Ion channels","ans":"B","e":"Lipid-soluble hormone receptors often act as DNA-binding proteins. After binding hormone, they bind DNA to regulate transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid receptors = DNA-binding proteins | Transcription factors | Alter gene expression"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Signal transduction by G-proteins is rapid because it involves:","a":"Gene transcription","b":"Protein synthesis","c":"Second messengers","d":"Nuclear receptors","ans":"C","e":"Signal transduction by G-proteins is rapid because it involves second messengers. Second messengers give fast, non-genomic responses.<br><br>Take Notes: G-protein signalling rapid via second messengers | Fast amplification | Non-genomic"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which hormone mechanism is NOT affected by cholera toxin?","a":"Glucagon","b":"ACTH","c":"Insulin","d":"Epinephrine","ans":"C","e":"Insulin's mechanism is not affected by cholera toxin, since it does not use Gs. It uses a receptor tyrosine kinase, so Gs-targeting toxins do not affect it.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin unaffected by cholera toxin | Not G-protein-coupled | Receptor tyrosine kinase"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Persistent activation of adenylate cyclase causes:","a":"Hyposecretion","b":"Down-regulation of receptors","c":"Increased intracellular cAMP","d":"Cell death only","ans":"C","e":"Persistent activation of adenylate cyclase causes increased intracellular cAMP. This persistent cAMP drives the secretory diarrhoea of cholera.<br><br>Take Notes: Persistent adenylate cyclase = high cAMP | Cholera toxin effect | Secretory diarrhea"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones are metabolized mainly in:","a":"Kidney","b":"Muscle","c":"Liver","d":"Brain","ans":"C","e":"Lipid-soluble hormones are metabolized mainly in the liver. Being lipophilic, they undergo slow hepatic metabolism.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid hormones metabolized in liver | Hepatic breakdown | Then excreted"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which G-protein mediates inhibition of Ca²⁺ channels in neurons?","a":"Gs","b":"Gi","c":"Gq","d":"Gt","ans":"B","e":"Gi mediates inhibition of calcium channels in neurons. This reduces neurotransmitter release as an inhibitory signal.<br><br>Take Notes: Gi inhibits neuronal calcium channels | Reduces neurotransmitter release | Inhibitory signalling"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Hormone resistance syndromes are often due to defects in:","a":"Carrier proteins","b":"G-proteins or receptors","c":"Hormone synthesis","d":"Enzyme degradation","ans":"B","e":"Hormone resistance syndromes are often due to defects in G-proteins or receptors. The signal fails to reach the target, producing end-organ unresponsiveness.<br><br>Take Notes: Hormone resistance = G-protein or receptor defect | Signal not received | End-organ unresponsive"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which toxin causes prolonged inhibition of Gi?","a":"Cholera toxin","b":"E. coli toxin","c":"Pertussis toxin","d":"Shiga toxin","ans":"C","e":"Pertussis toxin causes prolonged inhibition of Gi. This prolonged Gi inhibition raises cAMP and underlies whooping cough features.<br><br>Take Notes: Pertussis toxin = prolonged Gi inhibition | ADP-ribosylation | Raises cAMP"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormone action results in synthesis of:","a":"Second messengers","b":"Enzymes and structural proteins","c":"cAMP","d":"Ca²⁺","ans":"B","e":"Lipid-soluble hormone action results in synthesis of enzymes and structural proteins. These new proteins mediate the sustained, genomic effects of steroids.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid hormones drive protein synthesis | Enzymes and structural proteins | Genomic effect"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which pathway is NOT G-protein dependent?","a":"cAMP","b":"IP₃-DAG","c":"cGMP via NO","d":"Insulin signaling","ans":"D","e":"Insulin signalling is not G-protein dependent; it uses receptor tyrosine kinase. It uses a receptor tyrosine kinase with PI3K-Akt and MAPK pathways.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin signalling not G-protein dependent | Receptor tyrosine kinase | PI3K-Akt and MAPK"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Defective G-protein signaling may cause:","a":"Hormone excess only","b":"Hormone deficiency only","c":"Hormone resistance or excess","d":"No clinical effect","ans":"C","e":"Defective G-protein signalling may cause hormone resistance or excess. Gain- or loss-of-function determines whether hyperfunction or resistance results.<br><br>Take Notes: G-protein defects = hormone resistance or excess | Loss or gain of function | Endocrine disorders"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormones enter cells by:","a":"Carrier-mediated transport","b":"Active transport","c":"Diffusion","d":"Endocytosis","ans":"C","e":"Lipid-soluble hormones enter cells by diffusion across the membrane. Being lipophilic, they diffuse across the membrane to intracellular receptors.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid hormones enter by diffusion | Lipid-soluble | Reach intracellular receptors"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone acts via intracellular receptor but is derived from amino acid?","a":"Insulin","b":"Epinephrine","c":"Thyroxine","d":"Glucagon","ans":"C","e":"Thyroxine acts via an intracellular receptor but is derived from an amino acid (tyrosine). Though tyrosine-derived, it acts like a steroid on gene transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: Thyroxine = intracellular receptor | Derived from tyrosine | Acts like a steroid"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"G-protein signaling defects are MOST tested in past papers with:","a":"Metabolic pathways","b":"Toxin-related diarrhea","c":"Structural protein synthesis","d":"DNA replication","ans":"B","e":"G-protein signalling defects are most tested in past papers with toxin-related diarrhea. Cholera and E. coli toxins, acting via cAMP and cGMP, are the classic examples.<br><br>Take Notes: Common exam theme = toxin diarrhea | Cholera and E. coli toxins | cAMP and cGMP mechanisms"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which toxin causes cAMP-mediated secretory diarrhea?","a":"Shiga toxin","b":"Cholera toxin","c":"Tetanus toxin","d":"Botulinum toxin","ans":"B","e":"Cholera toxin causes cAMP-mediated secretory diarrhea. Activating Gs and CFTR, it drives cAMP-mediated watery diarrhoea.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholera toxin = cAMP-mediated diarrhea | Activates Gs and CFTR | Watery stool"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which hormone mechanism involves receptor dimerization and DNA binding?","a":"Insulin","b":"Steroid hormones","c":"Catecholamines","d":"Glucagon","ans":"B","e":"Steroid hormones involve receptor dimerization and DNA binding. After binding hormone, the dimerised receptor binds DNA to regulate transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid hormones = receptor dimerization and DNA binding | Transcription factors | Genomic action"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"G-protein α-subunit determines:","a":"Receptor specificity","b":"Type of second messenger generated","c":"Ligand binding","d":"Membrane anchoring","ans":"B","e":"The G-protein alpha-subunit determines the type of second messenger generated. Gs, Gi, and Gq each generate a different second-messenger signal.<br><br>Take Notes: Alpha-subunit sets second messenger | Gs, Gi, Gq differ | Determines downstream effect"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormone-receptor complex binds DNA at:","a":"Promoter region only","b":"Operator region","c":"Hormone response elements","d":"Introns","ans":"C","e":"The lipid-soluble hormone-receptor complex binds DNA at hormone response elements. These specific DNA sequences let the complex regulate transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid complex binds hormone response elements | Specific DNA sequences | Regulates transcription"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Persistent cAMP elevation due to toxin leads to:","a":"Reduced secretion","b":"Increased secretion","c":"Apoptosis","d":"Growth arrest","ans":"B","e":"Persistent cAMP elevation due to toxin leads to increased secretion. This chloride and water secretion produces secretory diarrhoea.<br><br>Take Notes: High cAMP from toxin = increased secretion | Chloride and water | Secretory diarrhea"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Which abnormality results in endocrine hyperfunction without increased hormone levels?","a":"Hormone-secreting tumor","b":"Receptor overexpression","c":"Constitutively active G-protein","d":"Reduced hormone clearance","ans":"C","e":"A constitutively active G-protein results in endocrine hyperfunction without increased hormone levels. Autonomous signalling drives hyperfunction despite normal hormone levels.<br><br>Take Notes: Constitutive G-protein = hyperfunction, normal hormone | Autonomous signalling | McCune-Albright"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which hormone action is unaffected in pertussis toxin exposure?","a":"Glucagon","b":"ACTH","c":"Insulin","d":"Epinephrine","ans":"C","e":"Insulin hormone action is unaffected in pertussis toxin exposure, as it does not use Gi. Using a receptor tyrosine kinase, it is unaffected by pertussis toxin.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin unaffected by pertussis toxin | Not G-protein-coupled | Receptor tyrosine kinase"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"Lipid-soluble hormone signaling ultimately alters:","a":"Membrane potential","b":"Enzyme kinetics directly","c":"Protein synthesis","d":"Ion gradients","ans":"C","e":"Lipid-soluble hormone signalling ultimately alters protein synthesis. New mRNA and proteins mediate these sustained genomic effects.<br><br>Take Notes: Steroid signalling alters protein synthesis | Via gene transcription | Sustained effect"},
{"t":"Hormones and Signal Transduction","q":"The MOST important distinction between G-protein signaling and lipid-soluble hormone signaling is:","a":"Speed of action","b":"Use of receptors","c":"Hormone origin","d":"Blood transport","ans":"A","e":"The most important distinction between G-protein and lipid-soluble hormone signalling is the speed of action. G-proteins act in seconds, while steroids act over hours via transcription.<br><br>Take Notes: Key distinction = speed of action | G-proteins rapid, steroids slow | Second messengers versus transcription"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Glycolysis occurs primarily in which cellular compartment?","a":"Mitochondria","b":"Cytosol","c":"Nucleus","d":"Endoplasmic reticulum","ans":"B","e":"Glycolysis occurs primarily in the cytosol, where glucose is broken down to pyruvate. No oxygen is needed for glycolysis itself, though pyruvate's fate depends on it.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycolysis = cytosol | Anaerobic and aerobic | Yields pyruvate and ATP"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"The rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis is:","a":"Hexokinase","b":"Phosphoglucose isomerase","c":"Phosphofructokinase-1","d":"Pyruvate kinase","ans":"C","e":"Phosphofructokinase-1 is the rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis. AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate activate it, while ATP and citrate inhibit it.<br><br>Take Notes: PFK-1 = rate-limiting glycolytic enzyme | Committed step | Activated by AMP, inhibited by ATP"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which glycolytic enzyme is activated by fructose-2,6-bisphosphate?","a":"Hexokinase","b":"Pyruvate kinase","c":"Phosphofructokinase-1","d":"Aldolase","ans":"C","e":"Phosphofructokinase-1 is activated by fructose-2,6-bisphosphate. This makes it the key control point coupling glycolysis to energy status.<br><br>Take Notes: Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate activates PFK-1 | Potent stimulator | Raised by insulin"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Hexokinase is inhibited by:","a":"ATP","b":"ADP","c":"Glucose-6-phosphate","d":"Fructose-6-phosphate","ans":"C","e":"Hexokinase is inhibited by its product, glucose-6-phosphate. Its product inhibition prevents glucose trapping when phosphate is limiting.<br><br>Take Notes: Hexokinase inhibited by glucose-6-phosphate | Product inhibition | Glucokinase is not"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Glucokinase differs from hexokinase by having:","a":"Low Km and low Vmax","b":"High Km and high Vmax","c":"Low Km and high Vmax","d":"High Km and low Vmax","ans":"B","e":"Glucokinase differs from hexokinase by having a high Km and high Vmax. Its high Km lets it act as a glucose sensor in liver and beta-cells.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucokinase = high Km, high Vmax | Liver and beta-cells | Glucose sensor"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Net ATP produced per glucose molecule during glycolysis is:","a":"1","b":"2","c":"3","d":"4","ans":"B","e":"The net ATP produced per glucose during glycolysis is 2. Substrate-level phosphorylation, not oxygen, generates this modest ATP yield.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycolysis net ATP = 2 | 4 made, 2 used | Plus 2 NADH"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which step of glycolysis produces NADH?","a":"Glucose → Glucose-6-phosphate","b":"Fructose-6-phosphate → Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate","c":"Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate → 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate","d":"Phosphoenolpyruvate → Pyruvate","ans":"C","e":"The glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate to 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate step produces NADH in glycolysis. This is the only step of glycolysis that generates NADH.<br><br>Take Notes: NADH made at glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate step | Via glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase | Only NADH-producing glycolytic step"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis?","a":"Phosphofructokinase-1","b":"Pyruvate kinase","c":"Hexokinase","d":"Aldolase","ans":"B","e":"Pyruvate kinase catalyzes substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis. Phosphoglycerate kinase is the other substrate-level phosphorylation step.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyruvate kinase = substrate-level phosphorylation | Makes ATP directly | Also phosphoglycerate kinase"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Anaerobic glycolysis regenerates NAD⁺ via:","a":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase","b":"Lactate dehydrogenase","c":"Malate dehydrogenase","d":"Succinate dehydrogenase","ans":"B","e":"Anaerobic glycolysis regenerates NAD+ via lactate dehydrogenase. This regenerated NAD+ lets anaerobic glycolysis continue producing ATP.<br><br>Take Notes: Lactate dehydrogenase regenerates NAD+ | Anaerobic glycolysis | Converts pyruvate to lactate"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Major tissue dependent on anaerobic glycolysis is:","a":"Liver","b":"Heart","c":"Brain","d":"RBCs","ans":"D","e":"Red blood cells are the major tissue dependent on anaerobic glycolysis, lacking mitochondria. Without mitochondria, they rely entirely on lactate production for ATP.<br><br>Take Notes: RBCs = anaerobic glycolysis | No mitochondria | Depend on lactate production"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex converts pyruvate into:","a":"Oxaloacetate","b":"Lactate","c":"Acetyl-CoA","d":"Alanine","ans":"C","e":"The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex converts pyruvate into acetyl-CoA. This irreversible step links glycolysis to the TCA cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyruvate dehydrogenase makes acetyl-CoA | Links glycolysis to TCA | Irreversible step"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase is located in:","a":"Cytosol","b":"Nucleus","c":"Mitochondrial matrix","d":"Inner mitochondrial membrane","ans":"C","e":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase is located in the mitochondrial matrix. Its location in the matrix places it at the entry to the TCA cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyruvate dehydrogenase in mitochondrial matrix | Converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA | Requires thiamine"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which vitamin is required by pyruvate dehydrogenase?","a":"Biotin","b":"Thiamine (B1)","c":"Niacin only","d":"Vitamin C","ans":"B","e":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase requires thiamine (vitamin B1) as a cofactor. Its deficiency causes lactic acidosis, especially in alcoholics.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyruvate dehydrogenase needs thiamine | As thiamine pyrophosphate | Deficiency causes lactic acidosis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase is inhibited by:","a":"ADP","b":"NAD⁺","c":"Acetyl-CoA","d":"CoA","ans":"C","e":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase is inhibited by its product acetyl-CoA. Product inhibition by acetyl-CoA, NADH, and ATP signals energy sufficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetyl-CoA inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase | Product inhibition | Also NADH and ATP inhibit"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency leads to accumulation of:","a":"Acetyl-CoA","b":"Lactate","c":"Citrate","d":"Oxaloacetate","ans":"B","e":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency leads to accumulation of lactate. Pyruvate is diverted to lactate, producing lactic acidosis.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency = lactate accumulation | Lactic acidosis | Neurological impairment"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Krebs cycle occurs in:","a":"Cytosol","b":"Inner mitochondrial membrane","c":"Mitochondrial matrix","d":"Outer mitochondrial membrane","ans":"C","e":"The Krebs cycle occurs in the mitochondrial matrix. This produces the mitochondrial matrix's central oxidative pathway.<br><br>Take Notes: Krebs cycle = mitochondrial matrix | Oxidizes acetyl-CoA | Produces NADH and FADH2"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Entry of acetyl-CoA into Krebs cycle is via condensation with:","a":"Malate","b":"Succinate","c":"Oxaloacetate","d":"Fumarate","ans":"C","e":"Acetyl-CoA enters the Krebs cycle by condensation with oxaloacetate to form citrate. Citrate synthase catalyses this first, committed step of the cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetyl-CoA condenses with oxaloacetate | Forms citrate | Citrate synthase catalyzes"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Rate-limiting enzyme of Krebs cycle is:","a":"Citrate synthase","b":"Isocitrate dehydrogenase","c":"Succinate dehydrogenase","d":"Malate dehydrogenase","ans":"B","e":"Isocitrate dehydrogenase is the rate-limiting enzyme of the Krebs cycle. ADP activates and NADH inhibits it, matching flux to energy demand.<br><br>Take Notes: Isocitrate dehydrogenase = rate-limiting TCA enzyme | Produces NADH and CO2 | Regulated by ADP and NADH"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which step of Krebs cycle produces CO₂?","a":"Citrate → Isocitrate","b":"Isocitrate → α-ketoglutarate","c":"Succinate → Fumarate","d":"Malate → Oxaloacetate","ans":"B","e":"The isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate step produces CO2 in the Krebs cycle. A second CO2 is released at the alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase step.<br><br>Take Notes: CO2 released at isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate | Via isocitrate dehydrogenase | Also at alpha-ketoglutarate step"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Which Krebs cycle enzyme is part of electron transport chain?","a":"Citrate synthase","b":"Isocitrate dehydrogenase","c":"Succinate dehydrogenase","d":"Malate dehydrogenase","ans":"C","e":"Succinate dehydrogenase is the Krebs cycle enzyme that is also part of the electron transport chain. As Complex II, it feeds FADH2 electrons directly into the chain.<br><br>Take Notes: Succinate dehydrogenase = TCA and ETC | Complex II | Produces FADH2"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Total ATP generated per acetyl-CoA via Krebs cycle is:","a":"10","b":"12","c":"15","d":"18","ans":"A","e":"The total ATP generated per acetyl-CoA via the Krebs cycle is about 10. This comes from 3 NADH, 1 FADH2, and 1 GTP per acetyl-CoA.<br><br>Take Notes: Per acetyl-CoA = about 10 ATP | 3 NADH, 1 FADH2, 1 GTP | Via oxidative phosphorylation"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) NADH enters ETC at:","a":"Complex I","b":"Complex II","c":"Complex III","d":"Complex IV","ans":"A","e":"NADH enters the electron transport chain at Complex I. Its higher-energy electrons yield about 2.5 ATP, more than FADH2.<br><br>Take Notes: NADH enters ETC at Complex I | Yields about 2.5 ATP | FADH2 enters later"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"FADH₂ enters ETC at:","a":"Complex I","b":"Complex II","c":"Complex III","d":"Complex IV","ans":"B","e":"FADH2 enters the electron transport chain at Complex II. Bypassing Complex I, it yields less ATP than NADH.<br><br>Take Notes: FADH2 enters ETC at Complex II | Yields about 1.5 ATP | Bypasses Complex I"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"The final electron acceptor in ETC is:","a":"NAD⁺","b":"FAD","c":"Oxygen","d":"Water","ans":"C","e":"Oxygen is the final electron acceptor in the electron transport chain. Accepting electrons, it forms water at Complex IV.<br><br>Take Notes: Oxygen = final electron acceptor | Forms water | At Complex IV"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) ATP synthase is located in:","a":"Cytosol","b":"Outer mitochondrial membrane","c":"Inner mitochondrial membrane","d":"Mitochondrial matrix only","ans":"C","e":"ATP synthase is located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. It harnesses the proton gradient to synthesise ATP by chemiosmosis.<br><br>Take Notes: ATP synthase in inner mitochondrial membrane | Complex V | Uses proton gradient"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Proton gradient for ATP synthesis is across:","a":"Outer mitochondrial membrane","b":"Inner mitochondrial membrane","c":"Cytosol","d":"Nuclear membrane","ans":"B","e":"The proton gradient for ATP synthesis is across the inner mitochondrial membrane. This electrochemical gradient drives ATP synthase, as Mitchell's hypothesis proposed.<br><br>Take Notes: Proton gradient across inner mitochondrial membrane | Drives ATP synthase | Chemiosmosis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Inhibitor of Complex IV of ETC is:","a":"Rotenone","b":"Antimycin A","c":"Cyanide","d":"Malonate","ans":"C","e":"Cyanide inhibits Complex IV of the electron transport chain. By blocking cytochrome oxidase, it halts oxygen use and ATP synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Cyanide inhibits Complex IV | Cytochrome oxidase | Blocks oxygen use"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Uncouplers of oxidative phosphorylation cause:","a":"Increased ATP synthesis","b":"Decreased oxygen consumption","c":"Increased heat production","d":"Inhibition of ETC","ans":"C","e":"Uncouplers of oxidative phosphorylation cause increased heat production. By dissipating the proton gradient, energy is lost as heat rather than ATP.<br><br>Take Notes: Uncouplers = increased heat | Dissipate proton gradient | ATP synthesis falls"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"2,4-Dinitrophenol acts by:","a":"Inhibiting ATP synthase","b":"Blocking electron flow","c":"Dissipating proton gradient","d":"Inhibiting NADH dehydrogenase","ans":"C","e":"2,4-Dinitrophenol acts by dissipating the proton gradient, uncoupling oxidative phosphorylation. It shuttles protons across the membrane, uncoupling respiration and producing heat.<br><br>Take Notes: 2,4-Dinitrophenol dissipates proton gradient | Uncoupler | Produces heat, not ATP"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Metabolic state immediately after meal is called:","a":"Fasting state","b":"Starvation state","c":"Absorptive state","d":"Ketotic state","ans":"C","e":"The metabolic state immediately after a meal is called the absorptive state. Insulin dominates, driving storage of glucose, fat, and protein.<br><br>Take Notes: After meal = absorptive state | Insulin dominant | Storage of fuels"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Dominant hormone in absorptive state is:","a":"Glucagon","b":"Cortisol","c":"Insulin","d":"Epinephrine","ans":"C","e":"Insulin is the dominant hormone in the absorptive state. It promotes storage of glucose, fat, and protein after eating.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin dominant after meals | Promotes storage | Absorptive state"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"In absorptive state, liver primarily performs:","a":"Glycogenolysis","b":"Ketogenesis","c":"Glycogenesis","d":"Gluconeogenesis","ans":"C","e":"In the absorptive state, the liver primarily performs glycogenesis, storing glucose. Insulin drives this glucose storage as glycogen.<br><br>Take Notes: Absorptive liver = glycogenesis | Stores glucose as glycogen | Insulin-driven"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Major fuel for brain in well-fed state is:","a":"Fatty acids","b":"Ketone bodies","c":"Glucose","d":"Amino acids","ans":"C","e":"Glucose is the major fuel for the brain in the well-fed state. Only in prolonged fasting does the brain switch to ketones.<br><br>Take Notes: Brain uses glucose when well-fed | Primary fuel | Ketones only in prolonged fasting"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) In absorptive state, adipose tissue increases:","a":"Lipolysis","b":"Ketone synthesis","c":"Lipogenesis","d":"Proteolysis","ans":"C","e":"In the absorptive state, adipose tissue increases lipogenesis, storing fat. Insulin drives this conversion of glucose to stored triglyceride.<br><br>Take Notes: Absorptive adipose = lipogenesis | Stores triglycerides | Insulin-driven"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Post-absorptive state begins approximately after:","a":"1-2 hours","b":"4-6 hours","c":"12-18 hours","d":"48 hours","ans":"B","e":"The post-absorptive state begins approximately 4 to 6 hours after a meal. Rising glucagon then begins mobilising stored fuel.<br><br>Take Notes: Post-absorptive state = 4-6 hours after eating | Glucagon rises | Glycogenolysis begins"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Dominant hormone in post-absorptive state is:","a":"Insulin","b":"Glucagon","c":"IGF-1","d":"Thyroxine","ans":"B","e":"Glucagon is the dominant hormone in the post-absorptive state. It mobilises fuel by driving glycogenolysis and lipolysis.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon dominant post-absorptive | Mobilizes fuel | Raises blood glucose"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Primary source of blood glucose in post-absorptive state is:","a":"Dietary glucose","b":"Gluconeogenesis","c":"Glycogenolysis","d":"Ketogenesis","ans":"C","e":"The primary source of blood glucose in the post-absorptive state is glycogenolysis. Once glycogen is depleted, gluconeogenesis takes over.<br><br>Take Notes: Post-absorptive glucose = glycogenolysis | Liver glycogen breakdown | Then gluconeogenesis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Major fuel used by muscle in post-absorptive state is:","a":"Glucose","b":"Ketone bodies","c":"Fatty acids","d":"Amino acids","ans":"C","e":"Fatty acids are the major fuel used by muscle in the post-absorptive state. Sparing glucose for the brain, muscle burns fatty acids.<br><br>Take Notes: Muscle uses fatty acids post-absorptive | Spares glucose | Glucagon-driven lipolysis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Liver glycogen stores are depleted after approximately:","a":"6 hours","b":"12 hours","c":"24 hours","d":"72 hours","ans":"C","e":"Liver glycogen stores are depleted after approximately 24 hours of fasting. After this, gluconeogenesis becomes the main glucose source.<br><br>Take Notes: Liver glycogen lasts about 24 hours | Then gluconeogenesis dominates | Fasting adaptation"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Prolonged fasting primarily increases:","a":"Glycogen synthesis","b":"Lipogenesis","c":"Ketogenesis","d":"Glycolysis","ans":"C","e":"Prolonged fasting primarily increases ketogenesis. This spares glucose and protein for the brain and vital tissues.<br><br>Take Notes: Prolonged fasting = increased ketogenesis | Fuels the brain | Spares glucose"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Major fuel for brain during prolonged starvation is:","a":"Glucose only","b":"Fatty acids","c":"Ketone bodies","d":"Amino acids","ans":"C","e":"Ketone bodies are the major fuel for the brain during prolonged starvation. This adaptation spares glucose and reduces muscle protein breakdown.<br><br>Take Notes: Prolonged starvation = brain uses ketones | Spares glucose | Reduces protein breakdown"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During prolonged fasting, insulin levels are:","a":"High","b":"Normal","c":"Low","d":"Absent","ans":"C","e":"During prolonged fasting, insulin levels are low. Low insulin and high glucagon drive fuel mobilisation.<br><br>Take Notes: Prolonged fasting = low insulin | High glucagon | Promotes fuel mobilization"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Gluconeogenesis during fasting mainly uses:","a":"Fatty acids","b":"Ketone bodies","c":"Lactate, glycerol, amino acids","d":"Acetyl-CoA","ans":"C","e":"Gluconeogenesis during fasting mainly uses lactate, glycerol, and amino acids. Fatty acids are not gluconeogenic, a common exam trap.<br><br>Take Notes: Gluconeogenesis substrates = lactate, glycerol, amino acids | Not fatty acids | Maintains blood glucose"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Cori cycle links:","a":"Liver and adipose tissue","b":"Liver and muscle","c":"Muscle and brain","d":"Liver and kidney","ans":"B","e":"The Cori cycle links liver and muscle, recycling lactate into glucose. It recycles lactate from anaerobic tissues back into glucose.<br><br>Take Notes: Cori cycle links liver and muscle | Lactate to glucose | Recycles anaerobic product"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Alanine cycle primarily transports:","a":"Carbon","b":"Nitrogen","c":"Fatty acids","d":"Ketone bodies","ans":"B","e":"The alanine cycle primarily transports nitrogen from muscle to liver. Alanine carries the amino group while glucose returns to muscle.<br><br>Take Notes: Alanine cycle transports nitrogen | Muscle to liver | Also delivers carbon for glucose"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) In starvation, red blood cells depend on:","a":"Fatty acids","b":"Ketone bodies","c":"Glucose","d":"Amino acids","ans":"C","e":"In starvation, red blood cells depend on glucose, as they lack mitochondria. Lacking mitochondria, they cannot use ketones or fatty acids.<br><br>Take Notes: RBCs need glucose even in starvation | No mitochondria | Cannot use ketones"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During starvation, muscle protein breakdown:","a":"Increases progressively","b":"Stops completely","c":"Decreases after adaptation","d":"Is unchanged","ans":"C","e":"During starvation, muscle protein breakdown decreases after adaptation to spare protein. Ketone use spares muscle protein as fasting continues.<br><br>Take Notes: Muscle proteolysis decreases with adaptation | Ketones spare protein | Preserves muscle"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Major site of ketone body synthesis is:","a":"Muscle","b":"Brain","c":"Liver","d":"Kidney","ans":"C","e":"The liver is the major site of ketone body synthesis. Lacking thiophorase, the liver exports ketones rather than using them.<br><br>Take Notes: Liver = site of ketogenesis | From acetyl-CoA | Exports ketones to tissues"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Ketone bodies cannot be used by:","a":"Brain","b":"Heart","c":"Liver","d":"Skeletal muscle","ans":"C","e":"Ketone bodies cannot be used by the liver, which lacks the enzyme thiophorase. So it makes ketones for other tissues but cannot consume them itself.<br><br>Take Notes: Liver cannot use ketones | Lacks thiophorase | Exports them to other tissues"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Primary purpose of ketogenesis during starvation is to:","a":"Provide glucose","b":"Reduce protein breakdown","c":"Increase fat storage","d":"Increase insulin secretion","ans":"B","e":"The primary purpose of ketogenesis during starvation is to reduce protein breakdown. By fuelling the brain with ketones, it reduces the need for gluconeogenic amino acids.<br><br>Take Notes: Ketogenesis reduces protein breakdown | Spares muscle | Fuels brain"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Rate-limiting enzyme of ketogenesis is:","a":"Thiolase","b":"HMG-CoA synthase","c":"HMG-CoA lyase","d":"Acetoacetate dehydrogenase","ans":"B","e":"HMG-CoA synthase is the rate-limiting enzyme of ketogenesis. This mitochondrial enzyme is distinct from cytosolic cholesterol synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: HMG-CoA synthase = rate-limiting for ketogenesis | Mitochondrial | Distinct from cytosolic cholesterol synthesis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During starvation, urea excretion initially:","a":"Decreases","b":"Remains constant","c":"Increases then decreases","d":"Stops","ans":"C","e":"During starvation, urea excretion initially increases then decreases as protein is spared. Early proteolysis raises urea, then protein-sparing lowers it.<br><br>Take Notes: Urea excretion rises then falls in fasting | Early proteolysis then adaptation | Ketones spare protein"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) In prolonged fasting, renal gluconeogenesis:","a":"Decreases","b":"Is absent","c":"Increases","d":"Replaces hepatic gluconeogenesis completely","ans":"C","e":"In prolonged fasting, renal gluconeogenesis increases, contributing to blood glucose. The kidney becomes a significant glucose source in prolonged fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Prolonged fasting raises renal gluconeogenesis | Kidney contributes glucose | Supplements the liver"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During prolonged fasting, major source of glycerol is:","a":"Glycogen","b":"Lipolysis","c":"Amino acid breakdown","d":"Ketone utilization","ans":"B","e":"During prolonged fasting, the major source of glycerol is lipolysis. Triglyceride breakdown supplies this gluconeogenic glycerol.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting glycerol from lipolysis | Triglyceride breakdown | Gluconeogenic substrate"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Acetyl-CoA cannot be converted to glucose because:","a":"Krebs cycle is irreversible","b":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase is irreversible","c":"Gluconeogenesis is inhibited","d":"Oxaloacetate is absent","ans":"B","e":"Acetyl-CoA cannot be converted to glucose because pyruvate dehydrogenase is irreversible. Because this step is irreversible, fat cannot be converted to glucose.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetyl-CoA cannot make glucose | Pyruvate dehydrogenase irreversible | No net glucose from fat"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) During starvation, insulin to glucagon ratio is:","a":"High","b":"Normal","c":"Low","d":"Zero","ans":"C","e":"During starvation, the insulin to glucagon ratio is low. This low ratio favours catabolism and fuel mobilisation.<br><br>Take Notes: Starvation = low insulin/glucagon ratio | Favours fuel mobilization | Catabolic state"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During fasting, hepatic acetyl-CoA is primarily used for:","a":"Krebs cycle","b":"Lipogenesis","c":"Ketogenesis","d":"Glycolysis","ans":"C","e":"During fasting, hepatic acetyl-CoA is primarily used for ketogenesis. Diverted from the TCA cycle, acetyl-CoA fuels ketogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting hepatic acetyl-CoA = ketogenesis | From fatty acid oxidation | Fuels the brain"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"The main glucose-consuming tissue in fasting state is:","a":"Muscle","b":"Brain","c":"Adipose tissue","d":"Liver","ans":"B","e":"The brain is the main glucose-consuming tissue in the fasting state. Its glucose demand drives ongoing gluconeogenesis during fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Brain = main glucose consumer in fasting | Shifts to ketones later | Drives gluconeogenesis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) During starvation, TCA cycle activity in liver is reduced because of:","a":"NADH deficiency","b":"Depletion of oxaloacetate","c":"Inhibition of citrate synthase","d":"Lack of acetyl-CoA","ans":"B","e":"During starvation, liver TCA cycle activity is reduced due to depletion of oxaloacetate. Oxaloacetate is drawn off for gluconeogenesis, slowing the cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting reduces liver TCA | Oxaloacetate used for gluconeogenesis | Acetyl-CoA diverted to ketones"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Which metabolite activates pyruvate carboxylase?","a":"ATP","b":"NADH","c":"Acetyl-CoA","d":"ADP","ans":"C","e":"Acetyl-CoA activates pyruvate carboxylase, promoting gluconeogenesis. This allosteric activation channels pyruvate toward gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetyl-CoA activates pyruvate carboxylase | Promotes gluconeogenesis | Allosteric activator"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During fasting, muscle primarily exports:","a":"Glucose","b":"Ketone bodies","c":"Lactate and alanine","d":"Fatty acids","ans":"C","e":"During fasting, muscle primarily exports lactate and alanine. These feed the Cori and alanine cycles for hepatic gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting muscle exports lactate and alanine | Cori and alanine cycles | Substrates for gluconeogenesis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Brain begins to utilize ketone bodies significantly after:","a":"6 hours","b":"12 hours","c":"2-3 days","d":"7 days","ans":"C","e":"The brain begins to utilize ketone bodies significantly after 2 to 3 days of fasting. This adaptation spares glucose and reduces protein breakdown.<br><br>Take Notes: Brain uses ketones after 2-3 days | Glucose-sparing adaptation | Reduces protein breakdown"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During starvation, insulin-sensitive tissues show:","a":"Increased glucose uptake","b":"Decreased glucose uptake","c":"Unchanged glucose uptake","d":"Increased glycolysis","ans":"B","e":"During starvation, insulin-sensitive tissues show decreased glucose uptake, sparing glucose for the brain. Low insulin spares glucose for the brain during fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Starvation = decreased peripheral glucose uptake | Spares glucose for brain | Low insulin"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Major metabolic adaptation in prolonged fasting is:","a":"Increased glycolysis","b":"Increased ketone utilization","c":"Increased lipogenesis","d":"Increased insulin secretion","ans":"B","e":"The major metabolic adaptation in prolonged fasting is increased ketone utilization. Brain ketone use spares glucose and protein in prolonged fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Prolonged fasting = increased ketone use | Spares glucose and protein | Brain adapts"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which enzyme is irreversible in glycolysis?","a":"Aldolase","b":"Enolase","c":"Phosphofructokinase-1","d":"Phosphoglycerate mutase","ans":"C","e":"Phosphofructokinase-1 is an irreversible enzyme in glycolysis. Its irreversibility means gluconeogenesis must bypass it.<br><br>Take Notes: PFK-1 = irreversible glycolytic step | Also hexokinase and pyruvate kinase | Bypassed in gluconeogenesis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"In fasting, hepatic malonyl-CoA levels are:","a":"Increased","b":"Decreased","c":"Unchanged","d":"Absent","ans":"B","e":"In fasting, hepatic malonyl-CoA levels are decreased, allowing fatty acid oxidation. Low malonyl-CoA disinhibits CPT-1, allowing fatty acid oxidation.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting = low malonyl-CoA | Disinhibits CPT-1 | Promotes fatty acid oxidation"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Decreased malonyl-CoA during fasting leads to:","a":"Inhibition of β-oxidation","b":"Activation of CPT-1","c":"Increased lipogenesis","d":"Decreased ketogenesis","ans":"B","e":"Decreased malonyl-CoA during fasting leads to activation of CPT-1, enabling fatty acid oxidation. This lets fatty acids enter mitochondria for beta-oxidation.<br><br>Take Notes: Low malonyl-CoA activates CPT-1 | Allows fatty acid entry into mitochondria | Fasting adaptation"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) During starvation, major energy source for muscle is:","a":"Glucose","b":"Ketone bodies","c":"Fatty acids","d":"Amino acids","ans":"C","e":"During starvation, the major energy source for muscle is fatty acids. Sparing glucose, muscle relies on fatty acids in starvation.<br><br>Take Notes: Starvation muscle fuel = fatty acids | Spares glucose | Then ketones"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"In prolonged fasting, hepatic gluconeogenesis relies more on:","a":"Alanine","b":"Lactate","c":"Glycerol","d":"Glucose","ans":"C","e":"In prolonged fasting, hepatic gluconeogenesis relies more on glycerol. As protein is spared, glycerol from lipolysis becomes a key substrate.<br><br>Take Notes: Prolonged fasting gluconeogenesis uses glycerol | From lipolysis | As protein is spared"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During fasting, which hormone increases lipolysis?","a":"Insulin","b":"Growth hormone","c":"Glucagon","d":"Thyroxine","ans":"C","e":"During fasting, glucagon increases lipolysis. By activating hormone-sensitive lipase, it mobilises fatty acids.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon increases lipolysis in fasting | Mobilizes fatty acids | Fuels peripheral tissues"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) ETC inhibition leads to decreased:","a":"NADH","b":"Proton gradient","c":"Oxygen consumption","d":"ATP synthesis","ans":"D","e":"Electron transport chain inhibition leads to decreased ATP synthesis. Blocked electron flow collapses the proton gradient and stops ATP synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: ETC inhibition = decreased ATP | Blocks electron flow | Halts oxidative phosphorylation"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Which enzyme produces GTP in Krebs cycle?","a":"Isocitrate dehydrogenase","b":"α-Ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","c":"Succinyl-CoA synthetase","d":"Malate dehydrogenase","ans":"C","e":"Succinyl-CoA synthetase produces GTP in the Krebs cycle by substrate-level phosphorylation. This is the only substrate-level phosphorylation step in the TCA cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Succinyl-CoA synthetase makes GTP | Substrate-level phosphorylation | Only such step in TCA"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During absorptive state, muscle primarily uses:","a":"Fatty acids","b":"Ketone bodies","c":"Glucose","d":"Amino acids","ans":"C","e":"During the absorptive state, muscle primarily uses glucose. Insulin drives this glucose uptake and glycogen storage.<br><br>Take Notes: Absorptive muscle uses glucose | Insulin-driven uptake | Stores glycogen"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) In well-fed state, excess glucose is converted to:","a":"Ketone bodies","b":"Glycogen and fat","c":"Lactate","d":"Amino acids only","ans":"B","e":"In the well-fed state, excess glucose is converted to glycogen and fat. Insulin drives storage of excess glucose in both forms.<br><br>Take Notes: Excess glucose = glycogen and fat | Storage forms | Insulin-driven"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During fasting, hepatic gluconeogenesis increases due to:","a":"Increased insulin","b":"Increased acetyl-CoA","c":"Decreased glucagon","d":"Increased malonyl-CoA","ans":"B","e":"During fasting, hepatic gluconeogenesis increases due to increased acetyl-CoA activating pyruvate carboxylase. Acetyl-CoA from fatty acid oxidation allosterically drives gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting acetyl-CoA activates pyruvate carboxylase | Drives gluconeogenesis | Maintains blood glucose"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Major metabolic role of liver during fasting is:","a":"Glucose utilization","b":"Fat storage","c":"Maintenance of blood glucose","d":"Protein synthesis","ans":"C","e":"The major metabolic role of the liver during fasting is maintenance of blood glucose. It shifts from glycogenolysis to gluconeogenesis as fasting continues.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting liver maintains blood glucose | Glycogenolysis then gluconeogenesis | Central metabolic organ"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) In prolonged starvation, urinary nitrogen excretion:","a":"Continues to increase","b":"Remains high","c":"Decreases after adaptation","d":"Stops completely","ans":"C","e":"In prolonged starvation, urinary nitrogen excretion decreases after adaptation, sparing protein. Protein-sparing adaptation reduces nitrogen loss in prolonged starvation.<br><br>Take Notes: Prolonged starvation = decreased nitrogen excretion | Protein sparing | Ketone adaptation"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"ETC complex directly pumping protons at three sites includes:","a":"Complex II only","b":"Complexes I, III, IV","c":"Complexes I and II","d":"Complexes III and IV only","ans":"B","e":"The electron transport chain complexes I, III, and IV directly pump protons. Complex II does not pump protons, a common exam distinction.<br><br>Take Notes: Complexes I, III, IV pump protons | Create the gradient | Complex II does not pump"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During starvation, ketone body utilization by brain leads to:","a":"Increased proteolysis","b":"Reduced glucose requirement","c":"Increased gluconeogenesis","d":"Increased insulin","ans":"B","e":"During starvation, ketone utilization by the brain leads to reduced glucose requirement. Brain ketone use spares glucose and preserves protein.<br><br>Take Notes: Brain ketone use = reduced glucose need | Glucose-sparing | Preserves protein"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Rate-limiting enzyme of gluconeogenesis is:","a":"Hexokinase","b":"Pyruvate carboxylase","c":"Pyruvate kinase","d":"Enolase","ans":"B","e":"Pyruvate carboxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of gluconeogenesis. It converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate, activated by acetyl-CoA.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyruvate carboxylase = rate-limiting for gluconeogenesis | Pyruvate to oxaloacetate | Activated by acetyl-CoA"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During prolonged fasting, blood pH tends to:","a":"Increase","b":"Decrease","c":"Remain constant","d":"Become alkaline","ans":"B","e":"During prolonged fasting, blood pH tends to decrease due to ketoacidosis. Acidic ketone bodies lower blood pH in prolonged fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Prolonged fasting = lower blood pH | Ketone bodies are acidic | Ketoacidosis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Ketone bodies are acidic because they are:","a":"Carboxylic acids","b":"Amino acids","c":"Alcohols","d":"Aldehydes","ans":"A","e":"Ketone bodies are acidic because they are carboxylic acids. Acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate are the acidic ketone bodies.<br><br>Take Notes: Ketone bodies = carboxylic acids | Acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate | Cause ketoacidosis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) In starvation, major glucose-sparing adaptation is:","a":"Increased glycolysis","b":"Ketone utilization by brain","c":"Increased insulin secretion","d":"Increased glycogen synthesis","ans":"B","e":"In starvation, the major glucose-sparing adaptation is ketone utilization by the brain. This ketone use is the key glucose-sparing adaptation.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucose-sparing = brain ketone use | Reduces glucose demand | Preserves protein"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During post-absorptive state, adipose tissue increases:","a":"Lipogenesis","b":"Glycolysis","c":"Lipolysis","d":"Protein synthesis","ans":"C","e":"During the post-absorptive state, adipose tissue increases lipolysis. Glucagon drives this fatty acid release from fat stores.<br><br>Take Notes: Post-absorptive adipose = lipolysis | Releases fatty acids | Glucagon-driven"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Which metabolic pathway provides NADPH during fasting?","a":"Glycolysis","b":"TCA cycle","c":"Pentose phosphate pathway","d":"β-oxidation","ans":"C","e":"The pentose phosphate pathway provides NADPH during fasting. It supplies reducing power for biosynthesis and antioxidant defence.<br><br>Take Notes: Pentose phosphate pathway makes NADPH | For biosynthesis and antioxidant defence | Cytosolic"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) During starvation, liver uses fatty acids primarily for:","a":"ATP production","b":"Glucose synthesis","c":"Ketone synthesis","d":"Protein synthesis","ans":"A","e":"During starvation, the liver uses fatty acids primarily for ATP production. Beta-oxidation also drives hepatic ketogenesis in fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting liver uses fatty acids for ATP | Beta-oxidation | Also drives ketogenesis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Which enzyme is inhibited by ATP in glycolysis?","a":"Pyruvate kinase","b":"Phosphofructokinase-1","c":"Hexokinase","d":"All of the above","ans":"D","e":"ATP inhibits hexokinase, phosphofructokinase-1, and pyruvate kinase in glycolysis. Because it inhibits all three irreversible steps, all-of-the-above is correct.<br><br>Take Notes: ATP inhibits all three irreversible glycolytic enzymes | Signals high energy | Slows glycolysis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During fasting, muscle glucose uptake decreases due to:","a":"Increased GLUT4","b":"Decreased insulin","c":"Increased glycogen","d":"Increased hexokinase","ans":"B","e":"During fasting, muscle glucose uptake decreases due to decreased insulin. Low insulin reduces GLUT4 activity, sparing glucose for the brain.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting muscle glucose uptake falls | Low insulin | Spares glucose for brain"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which tissue cannot utilize fatty acids?","a":"Liver","b":"Heart","c":"Brain","d":"Skeletal muscle","ans":"C","e":"The brain cannot utilize fatty acids, as they do not cross the blood-brain barrier well. Lacking this ability, the brain relies on glucose and later ketones.<br><br>Take Notes: Brain cannot use fatty acids | Poor BBB crossing | Uses glucose then ketones"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During starvation, primary cause of ketoacidosis is:","a":"Excess glycolysis","b":"Excess ketone production","c":"Reduced CO₂ excretion","d":"Increased insulin","ans":"B","e":"During starvation, the primary cause of ketoacidosis is excess ketone production. Acidic ketone bodies lower blood pH, causing ketoacidosis.<br><br>Take Notes: Starvation ketoacidosis = excess ketone production | From fatty acid oxidation | Lowers blood pH"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"ETC inhibitors decrease ATP synthesis by:","a":"Increasing NADH","b":"Decreasing proton gradient","c":"Increasing FADH₂","d":"Increasing substrate-level phosphorylation","ans":"B","e":"Electron transport chain inhibitors decrease ATP synthesis by decreasing the proton gradient. Blocked electron flow collapses the gradient that drives ATP synthase.<br><br>Take Notes: ETC inhibitors reduce proton gradient | Less ATP synthesis | Block electron flow"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During well-fed state, liver exports:","a":"Ketone bodies","b":"Glucose and lipoproteins","c":"Amino acids","d":"Lactate only","ans":"B","e":"During the well-fed state, the liver exports glucose and lipoproteins. It packages endogenous triglycerides into VLDL for export.<br><br>Take Notes: Well-fed liver exports glucose and lipoproteins | VLDL synthesis | Distributes fuel"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) In post-absorptive state, kidney contributes to glucose by:","a":"Glycogenolysis","b":"Gluconeogenesis","c":"Glycolysis","d":"Ketogenesis","ans":"B","e":"In the post-absorptive state, the kidney contributes to glucose by gluconeogenesis. The kidney supplements hepatic glucose output in prolonged fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Kidney does gluconeogenesis post-absorptive | Supplements the liver | Rises in prolonged fasting"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During fasting, insulin inhibits which enzyme least?","a":"Hormone-sensitive lipase","b":"Glycogen synthase","c":"Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","d":"Pyruvate kinase","ans":"A","e":"During fasting, insulin least inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase, which becomes active. Low insulin disinhibits it, driving lipolysis during fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting activates hormone-sensitive lipase | Low insulin disinhibits it | Drives lipolysis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Which metabolic pathway is exclusively mitochondrial?","a":"Glycolysis","b":"Pentose phosphate pathway","c":"Krebs cycle","d":"Cori cycle","ans":"C","e":"The Krebs cycle is the metabolic pathway that is exclusively mitochondrial. Glycolysis is cytosolic, a common contrast on exams.<br><br>Take Notes: Krebs cycle = exclusively mitochondrial | Glycolysis is cytosolic | Some pathways span both"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) During starvation, major adaptation to spare glucose is:","a":"Increased gluconeogenesis","b":"Increased ketone body synthesis","c":"Increased insulin sensitivity","d":"Increased glycogenolysis","ans":"B","e":"During starvation, the major adaptation to spare glucose is increased ketone body synthesis. Ketones fuel the brain and spare muscle protein.<br><br>Take Notes: Starvation glucose-sparing = increased ketogenesis | Brain shifts to ketones | Preserves protein"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During fasting, acetyl-CoA accumulation inhibits:","a":"Pyruvate kinase","b":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase","c":"Citrate synthase","d":"Malate dehydrogenase","ans":"B","e":"During fasting, acetyl-CoA accumulation inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase. This conserves pyruvate for gluconeogenesis during fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting acetyl-CoA inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase | Conserves pyruvate for gluconeogenesis | Spares glucose"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"During prolonged fasting, blood glucose is maintained primarily by:","a":"Glycogenolysis","b":"Dietary intake","c":"Gluconeogenesis","d":"Ketogenesis","ans":"C","e":"During prolonged fasting, blood glucose is maintained primarily by gluconeogenesis. After glycogen is depleted, gluconeogenesis maintains blood glucose.<br><br>Take Notes: Prolonged fasting glucose = gluconeogenesis | After glycogen depletion | From lactate, glycerol, amino acids"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) ETC requires which molecule for continued function?","a":"NAD⁺","b":"FAD","c":"Oxygen","d":"ATP","ans":"C","e":"The electron transport chain requires oxygen for continued function. Without oxygen as the final acceptor, electron flow and ATP synthesis stop.<br><br>Take Notes: ETC needs oxygen | Final electron acceptor | Without it, electron flow stops"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"The MOST critical metabolic goal during prolonged starvation is:","a":"Maintenance of muscle mass","b":"Maintenance of blood glucose for brain","c":"Storage of fat","d":"Increased ATP synthesis","ans":"B","e":"The most critical metabolic goal during prolonged starvation is maintenance of blood glucose for the brain. Gluconeogenesis and ketones together sustain this survival priority.<br><br>Take Notes: Starvation goal = blood glucose for the brain | Gluconeogenesis and ketones | Survival priority"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Primary site of lipid digestion is:","a":"Mouth","b":"Stomach","c":"Small intestine","d":"Colon","ans":"C","e":"The primary site of lipid digestion is the small intestine, aided by bile and pancreatic lipase. Bile emulsifies fat and pancreatic lipase then hydrolyses it to absorbable products.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipid digestion = small intestine | Bile emulsifies | Pancreatic lipase acts"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Lingual and gastric lipases mainly act on:","a":"Cholesterol esters","b":"Phospholipids","c":"Triglycerides","d":"Bile salts","ans":"C","e":"Lingual and gastric lipases mainly act on triglycerides, beginning fat digestion. Their contribution is minor, with the bulk of digestion occurring in the small intestine.<br><br>Take Notes: Lingual and gastric lipases act on triglycerides | Minor early digestion | Main digestion is intestinal"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Major enzyme responsible for intestinal triglyceride digestion is:","a":"Pancreatic amylase","b":"Pancreatic lipase","c":"Phospholipase A2","d":"Cholesterol esterase","ans":"B","e":"Pancreatic lipase is the major enzyme responsible for intestinal triglyceride digestion. It needs colipase to anchor it against bile salts on the fat droplet.<br><br>Take Notes: Pancreatic lipase = main triglyceride digestion | Needs colipase | Small intestine"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Bile salts primarily function to:","a":"Hydrolyze triglycerides","b":"Activate lipase","c":"Emulsify dietary fats","d":"Absorb cholesterol","ans":"C","e":"Bile salts primarily function to emulsify dietary fats, increasing surface area for digestion. This greatly increases the surface area available to pancreatic lipase.<br><br>Take Notes: Bile salts emulsify fats | Increase surface area | Aid lipase action"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Absorption of fatty acids and monoglycerides occurs via:","a":"Active transport","b":"Simple diffusion from micelles","c":"Pinocytosis","d":"Facilitated diffusion only","ans":"B","e":"Fatty acids and monoglycerides are absorbed by simple diffusion from micelles. Micelles deliver them to the enterocyte, where they are re-esterified.<br><br>Take Notes: Fatty acid absorption = simple diffusion from micelles | Micelles deliver to enterocyte | Then re-esterified"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Re-esterification of fatty acids to triglycerides occurs in:","a":"Liver","b":"Enterocyte smooth ER","c":"Golgi apparatus","d":"Plasma","ans":"B","e":"Re-esterification of fatty acids to triglycerides occurs in the enterocyte smooth endoplasmic reticulum. The reformed triglycerides are then packaged into chylomicrons.<br><br>Take Notes: Re-esterification in enterocyte smooth ER | Reforms triglycerides | Packaged into chylomicrons"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Chylomicrons are synthesized in:","a":"Liver","b":"Adipose tissue","c":"Intestinal mucosa","d":"Muscle","ans":"C","e":"Chylomicrons are synthesized in the intestinal mucosa. They carry dietary lipid and enter the circulation through the lymphatics.<br><br>Take Notes: Chylomicrons made in intestinal mucosa | Carry dietary lipids | Enter lymphatics"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Major apolipoprotein of chylomicrons is:","a":"ApoA-I","b":"ApoB-48","c":"ApoB-100","d":"ApoE","ans":"B","e":"ApoB-48 is the major apolipoprotein of chylomicrons. It is the intestinal structural apolipoprotein, distinct from hepatic ApoB-100.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoB-48 = chylomicron apolipoprotein | Intestinal origin | Structural for chylomicrons"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Chylomicrons transport primarily:","a":"Endogenous triglycerides","b":"Cholesterol from liver","c":"Dietary triglycerides","d":"Phospholipids only","ans":"C","e":"Chylomicrons transport primarily dietary triglycerides. They have the highest triglyceride content of all lipoproteins.<br><br>Take Notes: Chylomicrons carry dietary triglycerides | From intestine | Highest triglyceride content"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Lipoprotein lipase is activated by:","a":"ApoB-48","b":"ApoC-II","c":"ApoE","d":"ApoA-I","ans":"B","e":"Lipoprotein lipase is activated by ApoC-II. ApoC-II deficiency mimics lipoprotein lipase deficiency, causing chylomicronaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoC-II activates lipoprotein lipase | Hydrolyzes triglycerides | On capillary endothelium"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Lipoprotein lipase is located on:","a":"Hepatocyte surface","b":"Adipocyte cytosol","c":"Capillary endothelium","d":"Intestinal lumen","ans":"C","e":"Lipoprotein lipase is located on the capillary endothelium. There it hydrolyses triglycerides in chylomicrons and VLDL.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipoprotein lipase on capillary endothelium | Hydrolyzes lipoprotein triglycerides | Activated by ApoC-II"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Deficiency of lipoprotein lipase causes:","a":"Type II hyperlipidemia","b":"Type I hyperlipidemia","c":"Type III hyperlipidemia","d":"Type IV hyperlipidemia","ans":"B","e":"Deficiency of lipoprotein lipase causes type I hyperlipidemia with elevated chylomicrons. This is type I hyperlipidaemia, which carries a risk of pancreatitis.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipoprotein lipase deficiency = type I hyperlipidemia | High chylomicrons | Risk of pancreatitis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Chylomicron remnants are taken up by liver via:","a":"LDL receptor","b":"Scavenger receptor","c":"ApoE-mediated receptor","d":"HDL receptor","ans":"C","e":"Chylomicron remnants are taken up by the liver via an ApoE-mediated receptor. Defective ApoE impairs this uptake, causing type III hyperlipidaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: Chylomicron remnants cleared via ApoE receptor | Hepatic uptake | ApoE-dependent"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Major apolipoprotein required for hepatic uptake of remnants is:","a":"ApoA-I","b":"ApoB-100","c":"ApoC-II","d":"ApoE","ans":"D","e":"ApoE is the major apolipoprotein required for hepatic uptake of remnants. Defective ApoE causes type III hyperlipidaemia from impaired remnant clearance.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoE = hepatic remnant uptake | Chylomicron and VLDL remnants | Defective in type III"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"VLDL is synthesized primarily in:","a":"Intestine","b":"Adipose tissue","c":"Liver","d":"Muscle","ans":"C","e":"VLDL is synthesized primarily in the liver. It carries endogenous triglycerides and matures into IDL then LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: VLDL made in the liver | Carries endogenous triglycerides | ApoB-100"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"VLDL transports:","a":"Dietary triglycerides","b":"Endogenous triglycerides","c":"Cholesterol to liver","d":"Phospholipids only","ans":"B","e":"VLDL transports endogenous triglycerides made by the liver. Progressive triglyceride removal converts it to IDL and then LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: VLDL carries endogenous triglycerides | Liver origin | Becomes IDL then LDL"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Major apolipoprotein of VLDL is:","a":"ApoB-48","b":"ApoA-I","c":"ApoB-100","d":"ApoE only","ans":"C","e":"ApoB-100 is the major apolipoprotein of VLDL. ApoB-100 is also recognised by the LDL receptor for uptake.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoB-100 = VLDL apolipoprotein | Also LDL | Recognized by LDL receptor"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Conversion of VLDL to IDL requires:","a":"Hepatic lipase","b":"Lipoprotein lipase","c":"LCAT","d":"ACAT","ans":"B","e":"Conversion of VLDL to IDL requires lipoprotein lipase to remove triglycerides. ApoC-II activates this enzyme to hydrolyse VLDL triglycerides.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipoprotein lipase converts VLDL to IDL | Removes triglycerides | ApoC-II activated"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"IDL is also known as:","a":"Chylomicron remnant","b":"VLDL remnant","c":"LDL remnant","d":"HDL precursor","ans":"B","e":"IDL is also known as VLDL remnant. IDL is then either cleared or converted to cholesterol-rich LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: IDL = VLDL remnant | Intermediate density | Becomes LDL or is cleared"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) LDL is derived from:","a":"Chylomicrons","b":"HDL","c":"VLDL via IDL","d":"Dietary cholesterol directly","ans":"C","e":"LDL is derived from VLDL via IDL. This progressive triglyceride loss yields cholesterol-rich LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: LDL from VLDL via IDL | Progressive triglyceride loss | Cholesterol-rich end product"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Major function of LDL is to:","a":"Remove cholesterol from tissues","b":"Deliver cholesterol to peripheral tissues","c":"Transport triglycerides","d":"Activate lipoprotein lipase","ans":"B","e":"The major function of LDL is to deliver cholesterol to peripheral tissues. High LDL is the main atherogenic lipoprotein delivering cholesterol to vessels.<br><br>Take Notes: LDL delivers cholesterol to tissues | Via LDL receptor | Atherogenic when high"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"LDL receptor recognizes which apolipoprotein?","a":"ApoA-I","b":"ApoC-II","c":"ApoE only","d":"ApoB-100","ans":"D","e":"The LDL receptor recognizes ApoB-100. Defects in this receptor cause familial hypercholesterolaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: LDL receptor recognizes ApoB-100 | Mediates endocytosis | Defective in familial hypercholesterolemia"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Increased LDL is most strongly associated with:","a":"Pancreatitis","b":"Atherosclerosis","c":"Steatorrhea","d":"Ketosis","ans":"B","e":"Increased LDL is most strongly associated with atherosclerosis. Oxidised LDL is taken up by macrophages to form foam cells.<br><br>Take Notes: High LDL = atherosclerosis | Deposits cholesterol in vessels | Bad cholesterol"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"HDL is synthesized in:","a":"Liver only","b":"Intestine only","c":"Liver and intestine","d":"Adipose tissue","ans":"C","e":"HDL is synthesized in the liver and intestine. It matures by taking up cholesterol for reverse transport.<br><br>Take Notes: HDL made in liver and intestine | Reverse cholesterol transport | Cardioprotective"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Primary role of HDL is:","a":"Triglyceride transport","b":"Reverse cholesterol transport","c":"Fatty acid synthesis","d":"Ketone transport","ans":"B","e":"The primary role of HDL is reverse cholesterol transport, moving cholesterol to the liver. It moves excess cholesterol from tissues to the liver for excretion.<br><br>Take Notes: HDL = reverse cholesterol transport | Tissues to liver | Cardioprotective"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Major apolipoprotein of HDL is:","a":"ApoB-48","b":"ApoB-100","c":"ApoA-I","d":"ApoE","ans":"C","e":"ApoA-I is the major apolipoprotein of HDL. ApoA-I also activates LCAT to esterify cholesterol on HDL.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoA-I = HDL apolipoprotein | Activates LCAT | Reverse cholesterol transport"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"LCAT is activated by:","a":"ApoB-100","b":"ApoC-II","c":"ApoA-I","d":"ApoE","ans":"C","e":"LCAT is activated by ApoA-I. It esterifies free cholesterol on HDL for reverse transport.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoA-I activates LCAT | Esterifies cholesterol | On HDL surface"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"LCAT catalyzes:","a":"Esterification of intracellular cholesterol","b":"Hydrolysis of triglycerides","c":"Esterification of free cholesterol in plasma","d":"Lipoprotein uptake","ans":"C","e":"LCAT catalyzes esterification of free cholesterol in plasma. This esterification matures HDL and traps cholesterol in its core.<br><br>Take Notes: LCAT esterifies plasma cholesterol | Forms cholesterol esters | Matures HDL"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Tangier disease is characterized by:","a":"Increased HDL","b":"Decreased LDL","c":"Absent HDL","d":"Increased VLDL","ans":"C","e":"Tangier disease is characterized by absent HDL due to defective cholesterol efflux. The defect is in ABCA1, blocking cholesterol efflux onto HDL.<br><br>Take Notes: Tangier disease = absent HDL | ABCA1 defect | Cholesterol accumulates in tissues"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Fatty acid synthesis occurs primarily in:","a":"Mitochondria","b":"Cytosol","c":"Nucleus","d":"Peroxisomes","ans":"B","e":"Fatty acid synthesis occurs primarily in the cytosol. It uses acetyl-CoA and NADPH, separate from mitochondrial oxidation.<br><br>Take Notes: Fatty acid synthesis = cytosol | Uses acetyl-CoA and NADPH | Distinct from mitochondrial oxidation"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Rate-limiting enzyme of fatty acid synthesis is:","a":"Fatty acid synthase","b":"Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","c":"Citrate synthase","d":"Malic enzyme","ans":"B","e":"Acetyl-CoA carboxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of fatty acid synthesis. It makes malonyl-CoA and is activated by citrate and insulin.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetyl-CoA carboxylase = rate-limiting | Makes malonyl-CoA | Activated by citrate and insulin"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Acetyl-CoA carboxylase requires which cofactor?","a":"Thiamine","b":"Biotin","c":"Niacin","d":"Riboflavin","ans":"B","e":"Acetyl-CoA carboxylase requires biotin as a cofactor. Biotin lets it carboxylate acetyl-CoA to malonyl-CoA.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetyl-CoA carboxylase needs biotin | Carboxylation reaction | Forms malonyl-CoA"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Malonyl-CoA inhibits:","a":"Fatty acid synthase","b":"Hormone-sensitive lipase","c":"Carnitine palmitoyltransferase-1","d":"Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","ans":"C","e":"Malonyl-CoA inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase-1, preventing fatty acid oxidation. This prevents simultaneous synthesis and oxidation of fatty acids.<br><br>Take Notes: Malonyl-CoA inhibits CPT-1 | Blocks fatty acid entry into mitochondria | Prevents simultaneous synthesis and oxidation"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Primary source of NADPH for fatty acid synthesis is:","a":"Glycolysis","b":"TCA cycle","c":"Pentose phosphate pathway","d":"β-oxidation","ans":"C","e":"The pentose phosphate pathway is the primary source of NADPH for fatty acid synthesis. The pentose phosphate pathway and malic enzyme supply this NADPH.<br><br>Take Notes: Pentose phosphate pathway supplies NADPH | For fatty acid synthesis | Also malic enzyme"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Fatty acid synthase complex synthesizes:","a":"Oleic acid","b":"Stearic acid","c":"Palmitic acid","d":"Linoleic acid","ans":"C","e":"The fatty acid synthase complex synthesizes palmitic acid. This 16-carbon fatty acid is the primary product of the synthase.<br><br>Take Notes: Fatty acid synthase makes palmitic acid | 16-carbon fatty acid | Uses acetyl-CoA and malonyl-CoA"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Insulin stimulates fatty acid synthesis by:","a":"Inhibiting ACC","b":"Activating ACC via dephosphorylation","c":"Increasing CPT-1","d":"Decreasing malonyl-CoA","ans":"B","e":"Insulin stimulates fatty acid synthesis by activating acetyl-CoA carboxylase via dephosphorylation. Dephosphorylation activates the enzyme, promoting fatty acid synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin activates acetyl-CoA carboxylase | By dephosphorylation | Promotes fatty acid synthesis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Major site of triglyceride storage is:","a":"Liver","b":"Muscle","c":"Adipose tissue","d":"Brain","ans":"C","e":"Adipose tissue is the major site of triglyceride storage. It releases fatty acids by lipolysis when hormone-sensitive lipase is active.<br><br>Take Notes: Adipose tissue = triglyceride storage | Main fat depot | Released by lipolysis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Hormone-sensitive lipase is activated by:","a":"Insulin","b":"Glucagon","c":"Thyroxine","d":"IGF-1","ans":"B","e":"Hormone-sensitive lipase is activated by glucagon. Glucagon and adrenaline drive this fasting lipolysis.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon activates hormone-sensitive lipase | Drives lipolysis | Fasting state"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Hormone-sensitive lipase is inhibited by:","a":"Glucagon","b":"Cortisol","c":"Insulin","d":"Epinephrine","ans":"C","e":"Hormone-sensitive lipase is inhibited by insulin. Insulin blocks lipolysis, promoting fat storage in the fed state.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase | Prevents lipolysis | Promotes fat storage"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) During fasting, adipose tissue releases:","a":"Triglycerides","b":"Fatty acids and glycerol","c":"Ketone bodies","d":"Cholesterol","ans":"B","e":"During fasting, adipose tissue releases fatty acids and glycerol via lipolysis. These fuel peripheral tissues and supply glycerol for gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting adipose releases fatty acids and glycerol | Lipolysis | Fuels other tissues"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Type I hyperlipoproteinemia shows elevated:","a":"LDL","b":"VLDL","c":"Chylomicrons","d":"HDL","ans":"C","e":"Type I hyperlipoproteinemia shows elevated chylomicrons. Lipoprotein lipase or ApoC-II deficiency underlies it, with pancreatitis risk.<br><br>Take Notes: Type I = elevated chylomicrons | Lipoprotein lipase or ApoC-II deficiency | Pancreatitis risk"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Type IIa hyperlipoproteinemia is characterized by increased:","a":"Chylomicrons","b":"VLDL","c":"LDL","d":"HDL","ans":"C","e":"Type IIa hyperlipoproteinemia is characterized by increased LDL. It reflects LDL receptor defects in familial hypercholesterolaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: Type IIa = increased LDL | Familial hypercholesterolemia | Atherosclerosis risk"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Familial hypercholesterolemia is due to defect in:","a":"ApoC-II","b":"LCAT","c":"LDL receptor","d":"Lipoprotein lipase","ans":"C","e":"Familial hypercholesterolemia is due to a defect in the LDL receptor. This causes high LDL and premature atherosclerosis.<br><br>Take Notes: Familial hypercholesterolemia = LDL receptor defect | High LDL | Early atherosclerosis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Type III hyperlipoproteinemia is associated with defective:","a":"ApoA-I","b":"ApoB-100","c":"ApoE","d":"ApoC-II","ans":"C","e":"Type III hyperlipoproteinemia is associated with defective ApoE. Impaired remnant clearance causes palmar and tuberous xanthomas.<br><br>Take Notes: Type III = defective ApoE | Impaired remnant clearance | Palmar xanthomas"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Type IV hyperlipoproteinemia shows increased:","a":"Chylomicrons","b":"LDL","c":"VLDL","d":"HDL","ans":"C","e":"Type IV hyperlipoproteinemia shows increased VLDL. It is linked to diabetes, obesity, and the metabolic syndrome.<br><br>Take Notes: Type IV = increased VLDL | Elevated triglycerides | Associated with diabetes and obesity"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Hypertriglyceridemia increases risk of:","a":"Stroke","b":"Pancreatitis","c":"Gallstones","d":"Anemia","ans":"B","e":"Hypertriglyceridemia increases the risk of pancreatitis. Severe elevation from chylomicronaemia is the usual trigger.<br><br>Take Notes: Hypertriglyceridemia = pancreatitis risk | Severe elevation | Chylomicrons and VLDL"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"LDL is cleared from plasma mainly by:","a":"Kidney","b":"Muscle","c":"Liver","d":"Spleen","ans":"C","e":"LDL is cleared from plasma mainly by the liver via LDL receptors. It recognises ApoB-100 to internalise LDL by endocytosis.<br><br>Take Notes: LDL cleared by the liver | Via LDL receptors | Recognizes ApoB-100"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) HDL is cardioprotective because it:","a":"Increases triglycerides","b":"Delivers cholesterol to tissues","c":"Removes cholesterol from tissues","d":"Inhibits lipoprotein lipase","ans":"C","e":"HDL is cardioprotective because it removes cholesterol from tissues. This reverse cholesterol transport makes HDL cardioprotective.<br><br>Take Notes: HDL removes tissue cholesterol | Reverse cholesterol transport | Cardioprotective"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"CETP mediates transfer of:","a":"Cholesterol esters from HDL to VLDL","b":"Triglycerides from LDL to HDL","c":"Fatty acids to albumin","d":"Cholesterol from LDL to HDL","ans":"A","e":"CETP mediates transfer of cholesterol esters from HDL to VLDL. It exchanges these for triglycerides, redistributing cholesterol among lipoproteins.<br><br>Take Notes: CETP transfers cholesterol esters | HDL to VLDL | Exchanges for triglycerides"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Absence of bile salts leads to deficiency of:","a":"Water-soluble vitamins","b":"Proteins","c":"Fat-soluble vitamins","d":"Carbohydrates","ans":"C","e":"Absence of bile salts leads to deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins. Fat malabsorption impairs uptake of vitamins A, D, E, and K.<br><br>Take Notes: No bile salts = fat-soluble vitamin deficiency | A, D, E, K | Impaired fat absorption"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Lipid digestion is impaired in:","a":"Pancreatitis","b":"Achlorhydria","c":"Renal failure","d":"Diabetes insipidus","ans":"A","e":"Lipid digestion is impaired in pancreatitis due to reduced pancreatic lipase. Reduced pancreatic lipase causes steatorrhoea in pancreatitis.<br><br>Take Notes: Pancreatitis impairs lipid digestion | Reduced pancreatic lipase | Steatorrhea"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Chylomicrons enter circulation via:","a":"Portal vein","b":"Hepatic vein","c":"Lymphatics","d":"Inferior vena cava","ans":"C","e":"Chylomicrons enter circulation via the lymphatics. They bypass the portal blood, reaching the circulation via the thoracic duct.<br><br>Take Notes: Chylomicrons enter via lymphatics | Not portal blood | Reach circulation through thoracic duct"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Primary function of bile salts is:","a":"Absorption of glucose","b":"Digestion of proteins","c":"Emulsification of fats","d":"Activation of lipase","ans":"C","e":"The primary function of bile salts is emulsification of fats. This increases the surface area for pancreatic lipase to act.<br><br>Take Notes: Bile salts emulsify fats | Increase surface area | Aid lipase digestion"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) ApoC-II deficiency leads to:","a":"Increased LDL","b":"Decreased HDL","c":"Hyperchylomicronemia","d":"Hypocholesterolemia","ans":"C","e":"ApoC-II deficiency leads to hyperchylomicronemia, since lipoprotein lipase cannot be activated. It mimics lipoprotein lipase deficiency, causing type I-like hyperlipidaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoC-II deficiency = hyperchylomicronemia | Lipoprotein lipase not activated | Like type I hyperlipidemia"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"LDL oxidation contributes to:","a":"Ketogenesis","b":"Fatty acid synthesis","c":"Atherosclerosis","d":"Lipolysis","ans":"C","e":"LDL oxidation contributes to atherosclerosis by promoting foam cell formation. Foam cell formation initiates the atherosclerotic plaque.<br><br>Take Notes: Oxidized LDL drives atherosclerosis | Taken up by macrophages | Forms foam cells"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Statins primarily lower cholesterol by inhibiting:","a":"ACAT","b":"LCAT","c":"HMG-CoA reductase","d":"Lipoprotein lipase","ans":"C","e":"Statins primarily lower cholesterol by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase. Blocking this raises hepatic LDL receptors and lowers LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase | Rate-limiting cholesterol synthesis | Upregulate LDL receptors"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) HMG-CoA reductase is inhibited by:","a":"Insulin","b":"Glucagon","c":"Cholesterol","d":"Bile salts","ans":"C","e":"HMG-CoA reductase is inhibited by cholesterol through feedback regulation. This feedback keeps cholesterol synthesis matched to cellular need.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholesterol inhibits HMG-CoA reductase | Feedback control | Rate-limiting synthesis enzyme"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"VLDL remnants are also called:","a":"LDL","b":"IDL","c":"HDL","d":"Chylomicrons","ans":"B","e":"VLDL remnants are also called IDL. IDL is then cleared or converted to cholesterol-rich LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: VLDL remnants = IDL | Intermediate density | Become LDL or are cleared"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Primary lipid synthesized in liver during fed state is:","a":"Ketone bodies","b":"Fatty acids","c":"Cholesterol only","d":"Phospholipids only","ans":"B","e":"The primary lipid synthesized in the liver during the fed state is fatty acids. These are packaged into VLDL for export as triglycerides.<br><br>Take Notes: Fed-state liver synthesizes fatty acids | For VLDL export | Insulin-driven lipogenesis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) ApoB-48 is synthesized in:","a":"Liver","b":"Intestine","c":"Adipose tissue","d":"Muscle","ans":"B","e":"ApoB-48 is synthesized in the intestine. ApoB-100 is instead the hepatic form on VLDL and LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoB-48 = intestinal | Chylomicron structural protein | ApoB-100 is hepatic"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"ApoB-100 is required for:","a":"LCAT activation","b":"HDL formation","c":"LDL receptor binding","d":"Lipoprotein lipase activation","ans":"C","e":"ApoB-100 is required for LDL receptor binding. This binding lets the LDL receptor internalise LDL and VLDL.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoB-100 for LDL receptor binding | On VLDL and LDL | Mediates uptake"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Lipid storage form in adipose tissue is:","a":"Free fatty acids","b":"Triglycerides","c":"Phospholipids","d":"Cholesterol esters","ans":"B","e":"The lipid storage form in adipose tissue is triglycerides. They are mobilised by lipolysis during fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Adipose stores triglycerides | Energy reserve | Mobilized by lipolysis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Excess carbohydrate intake promotes:","a":"Ketogenesis","b":"Lipogenesis","c":"Proteolysis","d":"Gluconeogenesis","ans":"B","e":"Excess carbohydrate intake promotes lipogenesis. Insulin drives this conversion of excess carbohydrate to fat.<br><br>Take Notes: Excess carbohydrate = lipogenesis | Converted to fat | Insulin-driven"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Fatty acid synthesis requires transport of acetyl-CoA as:","a":"Acetate","b":"Citrate","c":"Pyruvate","d":"Lactate","ans":"B","e":"Fatty acid synthesis requires transport of acetyl-CoA out of mitochondria as citrate. The citrate shuttle exports acetyl-CoA for cytosolic fatty acid synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetyl-CoA exported as citrate | For cytosolic fatty acid synthesis | Citrate shuttle"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Malic enzyme produces:","a":"NADH","b":"NADPH","c":"FADH₂","d":"ATP","ans":"B","e":"Malic enzyme produces NADPH for fatty acid synthesis. It supplements the pentose phosphate pathway as an NADPH source.<br><br>Take Notes: Malic enzyme makes NADPH | Supplements pentose phosphate pathway | For fatty acid synthesis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Primary lipid abnormality in type IIa hyperlipidemia is:","a":"Hypertriglyceridemia","b":"Hypercholesterolemia","c":"Low HDL","d":"Increased chylomicrons","ans":"B","e":"The primary lipid abnormality in type IIa hyperlipidemia is hypercholesterolemia. Elevated LDL drives the premature atherosclerosis of this disorder.<br><br>Take Notes: Type IIa = hypercholesterolemia | Elevated LDL | Familial hypercholesterolemia"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"HDL acquires cholesterol from tissues via:","a":"LDL receptor","b":"Scavenger receptor","c":"ABC transporters","d":"Lipoprotein lipase","ans":"C","e":"HDL acquires cholesterol from tissues via ABC transporters. ABCA1 effluxes cholesterol onto HDL for reverse transport.<br><br>Take Notes: HDL takes up cholesterol via ABC transporters | ABCA1 effluxes cholesterol | Reverse transport"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Reverse cholesterol transport terminates at:","a":"Adipose tissue","b":"Muscle","c":"Liver","d":"Kidney","ans":"C","e":"Reverse cholesterol transport terminates at the liver. Cholesterol is then excreted in bile after HDL delivers it.<br><br>Take Notes: Reverse cholesterol transport ends at the liver | Cholesterol excreted in bile | HDL delivers it"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Which lipoprotein has highest triglyceride content?","a":"LDL","b":"HDL","c":"VLDL","d":"Chylomicrons","ans":"D","e":"Chylomicrons have the highest triglyceride content of all lipoproteins. They are the least dense, carrying dietary fat.<br><br>Take Notes: Chylomicrons = highest triglyceride content | Least dense | Carry dietary fat"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Which lipoprotein has highest cholesterol content?","a":"VLDL","b":"HDL","c":"LDL","d":"Chylomicrons","ans":"C","e":"LDL has the highest cholesterol content of the lipoproteins. This makes LDL the main cholesterol-delivering, atherogenic particle.<br><br>Take Notes: LDL = highest cholesterol content | Delivers cholesterol to tissues | Atherogenic"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Lipoprotein with smallest size is:","a":"Chylomicron","b":"VLDL","c":"LDL","d":"HDL","ans":"D","e":"HDL is the lipoprotein with the smallest size. Being protein-rich, it is the densest and smallest lipoprotein.<br><br>Take Notes: HDL = smallest lipoprotein | Highest density | Most protein-rich"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Lipoprotein with greatest density is:","a":"Chylomicron","b":"VLDL","c":"LDL","d":"HDL","ans":"D","e":"HDL is the lipoprotein with the greatest density. Its high protein content makes it the densest lipoprotein.<br><br>Take Notes: HDL = greatest density | Highest protein content | Reverse cholesterol transport"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"ApoE polymorphism is most associated with:","a":"Type I hyperlipidemia","b":"Type II hyperlipidemia","c":"Type III hyperlipidemia","d":"Type IV hyperlipidemia","ans":"C","e":"ApoE polymorphism is most associated with type III hyperlipidemia. Impaired remnant clearance produces broad-beta disease.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoE polymorphism = type III hyperlipidemia | Impaired remnant clearance | Broad beta disease"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Major cause of fatty liver in insulin resistance is:","a":"Decreased lipogenesis","b":"Increased VLDL secretion","c":"Increased free fatty acid influx to liver","d":"Decreased triglyceride synthesis","ans":"C","e":"The major cause of fatty liver in insulin resistance is increased free fatty acid influx to the liver. This triglyceride accumulation produces non-alcoholic fatty liver.<br><br>Take Notes: Fatty liver = increased free fatty acid influx | Insulin resistance | Triglyceride accumulation"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Primary function of VLDL is to transport:","a":"Dietary fat","b":"Cholesterol to liver","c":"Endogenous triglycerides","d":"Phospholipids","ans":"C","e":"The primary function of VLDL is to transport endogenous triglycerides. ApoB-100 lets it mature into IDL and then LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: VLDL transports endogenous triglycerides | Liver origin | ApoB-100"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Cholesterol esterification inside cells is catalyzed by:","a":"LCAT","b":"ACAT","c":"CETP","d":"Lipase","ans":"B","e":"Cholesterol esterification inside cells is catalyzed by ACAT. Intracellular ACAT stores cholesterol, while LCAT acts in plasma.<br><br>Take Notes: ACAT esterifies intracellular cholesterol | For storage | LCAT acts in plasma"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"HDL donates ApoC-II and ApoE to:","a":"LDL","b":"VLDL and chylomicrons","c":"IDL only","d":"Albumin","ans":"B","e":"HDL donates ApoC-II and ApoE to VLDL and chylomicrons. This transfer lets them be metabolised by lipoprotein lipase and cleared.<br><br>Take Notes: HDL donates ApoC-II and ApoE | To VLDL and chylomicrons | Enables their metabolism"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Increased risk of atherosclerosis correlates MOST with:","a":"High HDL","b":"High LDL","c":"High chylomicrons","d":"High bile salts","ans":"B","e":"Increased risk of atherosclerosis correlates most with high LDL. Oxidised LDL forms foam cells, driving plaque formation.<br><br>Take Notes: High LDL = highest atherosclerosis risk | Delivers cholesterol to vessels | Bad cholesterol"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Lipoprotein metabolism disorder with eruptive xanthomas is:","a":"Type I","b":"Type IIa","c":"Type III","d":"Type IV","ans":"A","e":"Type I lipoprotein metabolism disorder presents with eruptive xanthomas. Elevated chylomicrons underlie the eruptive xanthomas of type I.<br><br>Take Notes: Type I = eruptive xanthomas | Elevated chylomicrons | Lipoprotein lipase deficiency"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Xanthelasma is commonly seen in:","a":"Type I","b":"Type II hyperlipidemia","c":"Type IV","d":"Type V","ans":"B","e":"Xanthelasma is commonly seen in type II hyperlipidemia. High LDL cholesterol deposits as these eyelid lesions.<br><br>Take Notes: Xanthelasma = type II hyperlipidemia | High LDL cholesterol | Eyelid deposits"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Fatty acid synthesis is inhibited during fasting due to:","a":"Increased insulin","b":"Increased malonyl-CoA","c":"Decreased acetyl-CoA","d":"Phosphorylation of ACC","ans":"D","e":"Fatty acid synthesis is inhibited during fasting due to phosphorylation of acetyl-CoA carboxylase. Glucagon-driven phosphorylation inactivates the enzyme during fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting phosphorylates acetyl-CoA carboxylase | Inhibits fatty acid synthesis | Glucagon-driven"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Primary carrier of free fatty acids in plasma is:","a":"LDL","b":"HDL","c":"Albumin","d":"VLDL","ans":"C","e":"The primary carrier of free fatty acids in plasma is albumin. Only the free, unesterified fatty acids travel bound to albumin.<br><br>Take Notes: Albumin carries free fatty acids | Non-esterified | Delivered to tissues"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Lipolysis in adipose tissue releases:","a":"Triglycerides","b":"Free fatty acids and glycerol","c":"Ketone bodies","d":"Cholesterol","ans":"B","e":"Lipolysis in adipose tissue releases free fatty acids and glycerol. These fuel peripheral tissues and supply gluconeogenic glycerol.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipolysis releases fatty acids and glycerol | From triglycerides | Fasting fuel"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Primary cause of type I hyperlipoproteinemia is deficiency of:","a":"LDL receptor","b":"ApoE","c":"ApoC-II or LPL","d":"LCAT","ans":"C","e":"The primary cause of type I hyperlipoproteinemia is deficiency of ApoC-II or lipoprotein lipase. Either deficiency prevents triglyceride clearance, causing chylomicronaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: Type I = ApoC-II or lipoprotein lipase deficiency | High chylomicrons | Pancreatitis risk"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"HDL-mediated cholesterol efflux from macrophages prevents:","a":"Ketosis","b":"Fatty liver","c":"Foam cell formation","d":"Lipolysis","ans":"C","e":"HDL-mediated cholesterol efflux from macrophages prevents foam cell formation. This efflux prevents plaque progression, making HDL anti-atherogenic.<br><br>Take Notes: HDL efflux prevents foam cells | Removes macrophage cholesterol | Anti-atherogenic"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"CETP deficiency leads to:","a":"Low HDL","b":"High HDL","c":"High LDL","d":"High VLDL","ans":"B","e":"CETP deficiency leads to high HDL, as cholesterol esters are not transferred out. Less cholesterol transfer to VLDL keeps HDL high and is cardioprotective.<br><br>Take Notes: CETP deficiency = high HDL | Less cholesterol transfer to VLDL | Cardioprotective"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Lipid digestion is MOST impaired in absence of:","a":"Pancreatic amylase","b":"Bile salts","c":"Trypsin","d":"Pepsin","ans":"B","e":"Lipid digestion is most impaired in the absence of bile salts. Without emulsification, fat digestion and absorption fail.<br><br>Take Notes: No bile salts = impaired lipid digestion | No emulsification | Fat malabsorption"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Primary fate of glycerol during fasting is:","a":"Ketogenesis","b":"Gluconeogenesis","c":"Lipogenesis","d":"Oxidation only","ans":"B","e":"The primary fate of glycerol during fasting is gluconeogenesis. Glycerol from lipolysis is a key gluconeogenic substrate in fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Fasting glycerol = gluconeogenesis | From lipolysis | Maintains blood glucose"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Lipoprotein cleared fastest from plasma is:","a":"LDL","b":"HDL","c":"VLDL","d":"Chylomicron","ans":"D","e":"Chylomicrons are the lipoprotein cleared fastest from plasma. Lipoprotein lipase rapidly hydrolyses their triglycerides.<br><br>Take Notes: Chylomicrons cleared fastest | Short half-life | Rapidly hydrolyzed by lipoprotein lipase"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Most atherogenic lipoprotein is:","a":"HDL","b":"VLDL","c":"LDL","d":"Chylomicrons","ans":"C","e":"LDL is the most atherogenic lipoprotein. Oxidised LDL forms foam cells, initiating atherosclerotic plaques.<br><br>Take Notes: LDL = most atherogenic | Deposits cholesterol in vessels | Oxidized LDL forms foam cells"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"ApoA-I deficiency leads to:","a":"Increased LDL","b":"Decreased HDL","c":"Increased VLDL","d":"Increased chylomicrons","ans":"B","e":"ApoA-I deficiency leads to decreased HDL. Impaired reverse transport raises cardiovascular risk.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoA-I deficiency = decreased HDL | Impaired reverse transport | Increased cardiovascular risk"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Fatty acid synthesis consumes:","a":"NADH","b":"FADH₂","c":"NADPH","d":"ATP only","ans":"C","e":"Fatty acid synthesis consumes NADPH. NADPH from the pentose phosphate pathway supplies the reducing power.<br><br>Take Notes: Fatty acid synthesis uses NADPH | From pentose phosphate pathway | Reducing power for synthesis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Key regulatory hormone promoting lipid storage is:","a":"Glucagon","b":"Cortisol","c":"Insulin","d":"Epinephrine","ans":"C","e":"Insulin is the key regulatory hormone promoting lipid storage. It activates lipogenesis and inhibits lipolysis in the fed state.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin promotes lipid storage | Activates lipogenesis | Inhibits lipolysis"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Lipid absorption defect most likely causes deficiency of:","a":"Vitamin C","b":"Vitamin B12","c":"Vitamin A","d":"Iron","ans":"C","e":"A lipid absorption defect most likely causes deficiency of vitamin A (a fat-soluble vitamin). Fat malabsorption impairs the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipid malabsorption = fat-soluble vitamin deficiency | Vitamin A example | Also D, E, K"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"LDL uptake into cells occurs via:","a":"Pinocytosis","b":"Phagocytosis","c":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis","d":"Diffusion","ans":"C","e":"LDL uptake into cells occurs via receptor-mediated endocytosis. The LDL receptor binds ApoB-100 in clathrin-coated pits.<br><br>Take Notes: LDL uptake = receptor-mediated endocytosis | LDL receptor binds ApoB-100 | Clathrin-coated pits"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) ApoE is required for clearance of:","a":"LDL","b":"HDL","c":"Chylomicron remnants","d":"Albumin-bound fatty acids","ans":"C","e":"ApoE is required for clearance of chylomicron remnants. Defective ApoE impairs this clearance, causing type III hyperlipidaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoE clears chylomicron remnants | Hepatic uptake | Defective in type III hyperlipidemia"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Primary defect in type III hyperlipoproteinemia is impaired clearance of:","a":"LDL","b":"HDL","c":"Chylomicrons only","d":"Remnant lipoproteins","ans":"D","e":"The primary defect in type III hyperlipoproteinemia is impaired clearance of remnant lipoproteins. Defective ApoE causes accumulation of IDL and chylomicron remnants.<br><br>Take Notes: Type III = impaired remnant clearance | Defective ApoE | Accumulated IDL and chylomicron remnants"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"Lipid synthesis is increased when:","a":"Glucagon predominates","b":"Insulin predominates","c":"Fasting state","d":"Starvation","ans":"B","e":"Lipid synthesis is increased when insulin predominates. The fed state promotes storage of glucose, fat, and protein.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin predominance = increased lipid synthesis | Fed state | Promotes storage"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"(JCAT/FCPS Part 1) Most important risk factor for atherosclerosis is:","a":"Low triglycerides","b":"Low LDL","c":"High LDL","d":"High HDL","ans":"C","e":"The most important risk factor for atherosclerosis is high LDL. High LDL is modifiable, and statins lower it to reduce risk.<br><br>Take Notes: High LDL = key atherosclerosis risk | Deposits cholesterol | Modifiable with statins"},
{"t":"Overview of Energy Metabolism","q":"The MOST important clinical consequence of severe hypertriglyceridemia is:","a":"Stroke","b":"Atherosclerosis","c":"Pancreatitis","d":"Fatty liver","ans":"C","e":"The most important clinical consequence of severe hypertriglyceridemia is pancreatitis. Severe chylomicronaemia is the usual trigger for this emergency.<br><br>Take Notes: Severe hypertriglyceridemia = pancreatitis | From chylomicronemia | Medical emergency"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which nitrogenous base is found in RNA but not in DNA?","a":"Thymine","b":"Uracil","c":"Adenine","d":"Cytosine","ans":"B","e":"Uracil is found in RNA but not DNA, replacing thymine. Thymine is essentially methylated uracil, which is why DNA uses it for stability.<br><br>Take Notes: Uracil in RNA, not DNA | Replaces thymine | Thymine is methylated uracil"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The enzyme that prevents supercoiling ahead of the replication fork is:","a":"DNA ligase","b":"Topoisomerase (gyrase)","c":"DNA polymerase I","d":"Helicase","ans":"B","e":"Topoisomerase (gyrase) prevents supercoiling ahead of the replication fork. Fluoroquinolones target bacterial gyrase, causing lethal DNA breaks.<br><br>Take Notes: Topoisomerase (gyrase) relieves supercoiling | Ahead of the fork | Target of quinolones"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which enzyme synthesizes RNA primers during DNA replication?","a":"DNA primase","b":"DNA polymerase III","c":"RNase H","d":"DNA ligase","ans":"A","e":"DNA primase synthesizes RNA primers during DNA replication. Because DNA polymerase cannot start de novo, primase is essential to begin synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Primase makes RNA primers | Provides 3-OH for DNA pol | Essential for initiation"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"In prokaryotes, transcription termination that requires rho factor is called:","a":"Rho-independent termination","b":"Rho-dependent termination","c":"Attenuation","d":"Splicing","ans":"B","e":"Transcription termination requiring rho factor is called rho-dependent termination. Rho-independent termination instead uses a GC-rich hairpin followed by uracils.<br><br>Take Notes: Rho-dependent termination needs rho factor | Prokaryotic | Rho-independent uses hairpins"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The wobble hypothesis explains degeneracy at which codon position?","a":"First position","b":"Second position","c":"Third position","d":"Stop codon only","ans":"C","e":"The wobble hypothesis explains degeneracy at the third codon position. This flexibility lets one tRNA read several synonymous codons.<br><br>Take Notes: Wobble = third codon position | Allows flexible base pairing | Reduces tRNA needed"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Reverse transcription uses which enzyme?","a":"DNA polymerase I","b":"RNA polymerase II","c":"Reverse transcriptase","d":"RNase P","ans":"C","e":"Reverse transcription uses reverse transcriptase to make DNA from RNA. Retroviruses and RT-PCR both depend on this RNA-to-DNA step.<br><br>Take Notes: Reverse transcriptase makes DNA from RNA | Retroviruses | Used in RT-PCR"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"A thymine dimer produced by UV light is repaired by which mechanism in humans?","a":"Base excision repair","b":"Nucleotide excision repair","c":"Mismatch repair","d":"Non-homologous end joining","ans":"B","e":"A UV-induced thymine dimer is repaired by nucleotide excision repair in humans. Its failure causes xeroderma pigmentosum with extreme photosensitivity.<br><br>Take Notes: Thymine dimer = nucleotide excision repair | Removes bulky lesion | Defective in xeroderma pigmentosum"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which process fixes double-strand breaks using a homologous chromosome as template?","a":"Non-homologous end joining","b":"Homologous recombination","c":"Base excision repair","d":"Mismatch repair","ans":"B","e":"Homologous recombination fixes double-strand breaks using a homologous chromosome as template. Being template-based, it is error-free and confined to S and G2 phases.<br><br>Take Notes: Homologous recombination repairs double-strand breaks | Uses homologous template | Error-free"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes the conversion of ribonucleotides to deoxyribonucleotides?","a":"Ribonuclease H","b":"Ribonucleotide reductase","c":"DNA ligase","d":"DNA polymerase α","ans":"B","e":"Ribonucleotide reductase catalyzes conversion of ribonucleotides to deoxyribonucleotides. It is inhibited by hydroxyurea, exploited in sickle cell and myeloproliferative disease.<br><br>Take Notes: Ribonucleotide reductase makes deoxyribonucleotides | Rate-limiting for DNA synthesis | Target of hydroxyurea"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which mutation changes an amino acid to a stop codon?","a":"Missense","b":"Nonsense","c":"Silent","d":"Frameshift","ans":"B","e":"A nonsense mutation changes an amino acid codon into a stop codon. The premature stop yields a truncated, usually nonfunctional protein.<br><br>Take Notes: Nonsense mutation = premature stop codon | Truncated protein | Often nonfunctional"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The major DNA repair pathway correcting replication mismatches is:","a":"Nucleotide excision repair","b":"Base excision repair","c":"Mismatch repair","d":"Homologous recombination","ans":"C","e":"Mismatch repair is the major pathway correcting replication mismatches. Its failure causes Lynch syndrome with microsatellite instability.<br><br>Take Notes: Mismatch repair corrects replication mismatches | Post-replication | Defective in Lynch syndrome"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Microsatellite instability is a hallmark of which hereditary cancer syndrome?","a":"BRCA1-related breast cancer","b":"Lynch syndrome (HNPCC)","c":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome","d":"Familial adenomatous polyposis","ans":"B","e":"Microsatellite instability is a hallmark of Lynch syndrome (HNPCC). Screening tumours for it identifies patients who may have Lynch syndrome.<br><br>Take Notes: Microsatellite instability = Lynch syndrome | Mismatch repair defect | Early colorectal cancer"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Cytosine deamination converts cytosine into which base?","a":"Thymine","b":"Uracil","c":"Adenine","d":"Guanine","ans":"B","e":"Cytosine deamination converts cytosine into uracil. If unrepaired, this uracil pairs with adenine and fixes a C-to-T mutation.<br><br>Take Notes: Cytosine deaminates to uracil | Forms U:G mismatch | Removed by uracil-DNA glycosylase"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The enzyme that removes uracil from DNA during base excision repair is:","a":"DNA glycosylase","b":"Endonuclease V","c":"DNA ligase","d":"DNA polymerase β","ans":"A","e":"DNA glycosylase removes uracil from DNA during base excision repair. This leaves an abasic site that AP endonuclease then processes.<br><br>Take Notes: DNA glycosylase removes uracil | Base excision repair | Creates an AP site"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Xeroderma pigmentosum patients have defects in which repair pathway?","a":"Base excision repair","b":"Nucleotide excision repair","c":"Mismatch repair","d":"Homologous recombination","ans":"B","e":"Xeroderma pigmentosum patients have defects in nucleotide excision repair. These patients develop skin cancers in childhood, so sun avoidance is vital.<br><br>Take Notes: Xeroderma pigmentosum = NER defect | UV lesions unrepaired | Skin cancer risk"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which base modification causes GC → AT transition when unrepaired?","a":"8-oxoguanine","b":"Deaminated cytosine","c":"Thymine dimer","d":"Alkylated adenine","ans":"B","e":"Deaminated cytosine (uracil) causes a GC to AT transition when unrepaired. This is one of the most common spontaneous point mutations in the genome.<br><br>Take Notes: Deaminated cytosine = GC to AT transition | Uracil pairs with adenine | Common mutation"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which enzyme adds telomeric repeats to chromosome ends?","a":"Telomerase","b":"Telomere ligase","c":"DNA polymerase δ","d":"Topoisomerase","ans":"A","e":"Telomerase adds telomeric repeats to chromosome ends. Its reactivation in cancer cells confers replicative immortality.<br><br>Take Notes: Telomerase extends telomeres | Prevents end shortening | Active in stem and cancer cells"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"In somatic cells, telomerase activity is typically:","a":"High","b":"Low or absent","c":"Variable with cell cycle only","d":"Restricted to S phase only","ans":"B","e":"In somatic cells, telomerase activity is typically low or absent. Progressive telomere shortening limits how many times a cell can divide.<br><br>Take Notes: Somatic cells = low telomerase | Telomeres shorten with division | Limits replicative lifespan"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which cellular process primarily occurs in the nucleolus?","a":"DNA replication","b":"rRNA transcription and ribosome assembly","c":"mRNA splicing","d":"Protein glycosylation","ans":"B","e":"The nucleolus is primarily the site of rRNA transcription and ribosome assembly. Its prominence reflects the high demand for ribosomes in growing cells.<br><br>Take Notes: Nucleolus = rRNA transcription and ribosome assembly | Within the nucleus | Ribosome subunit production"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The Shine-Dalgarno sequence helps initiate translation in:","a":"Eukaryotes","b":"Prokaryotes","c":"Mitochondria only","d":"Archaea only","ans":"B","e":"The Shine-Dalgarno sequence helps initiate translation in prokaryotes. Eukaryotes instead rely on the 5-prime cap for ribosome positioning.<br><br>Take Notes: Shine-Dalgarno = prokaryotic translation initiation | Aligns ribosome | Eukaryotes use the 5-prime cap"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which antibiotic inhibits peptidyl transferase on the bacterial ribosome?","a":"Tetracycline","b":"Chloramphenicol","c":"Streptomycin","d":"Erythromycin","ans":"B","e":"Chloramphenicol inhibits peptidyl transferase on the bacterial ribosome. Binding the 50S subunit, it is a bacteriostatic broad-spectrum antibiotic.<br><br>Take Notes: Chloramphenicol inhibits peptidyl transferase | 50S subunit | Bacteriostatic"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases ensure fidelity by:","a":"Proofreading charged tRNA","b":"Editing wrong amino acid prior to attachment","c":"Both A and B","d":"None of the above","ans":"C","e":"Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases ensure fidelity by both accurate charging and proofreading. This two-step fidelity ensures the correct amino acid is loaded onto each tRNA.<br><br>Take Notes: Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases charge and proofread | Ensure correct amino acid | Two-step fidelity"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which cofactor is essential for transamination reactions?","a":"Thiamine pyrophosphate","b":"Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP)","c":"Biotin","d":"NAD⁺","ans":"B","e":"Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP) is essential for transamination reactions. Isoniazid depletes it, causing neuropathy unless pyridoxine is co-administered.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyridoxal phosphate = transamination cofactor | Vitamin B6 derivative | Transfers amino groups"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency leads to accumulation of:","a":"Urea","b":"Ammonia","c":"Citrulline only","d":"Tyrosine","ans":"B","e":"Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency leads to accumulation of ammonia. This is the most common urea cycle disorder and is X-linked.<br><br>Take Notes: Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency = hyperammonemia | Urea cycle defect | X-linked"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The primary amino group donor for most transaminations is:","a":"Alanine","b":"Aspartate","c":"Glutamate","d":"Glutamine","ans":"C","e":"Glutamate is the primary amino group donor for most transaminations. It links amino acid metabolism to the TCA cycle via alpha-ketoglutarate.<br><br>Take Notes: Glutamate = main amino donor | Central to nitrogen metabolism | Links to alpha-ketoglutarate"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which pathway provides NADPH for biosynthetic reactions?","a":"Glycolysis","b":"TCA cycle","c":"Pentose phosphate pathway","d":"β-oxidation","ans":"C","e":"The pentose phosphate pathway provides NADPH for biosynthetic reactions. It supplies reducing power for biosynthesis and antioxidant defence.<br><br>Take Notes: Pentose phosphate pathway makes NADPH | For biosynthesis and antioxidant defence | Cytosolic"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The oxidative branch of the pentose phosphate pathway generates:","a":"ATP and NADH","b":"NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate","c":"FADH₂ only","d":"Acetyl-CoA","ans":"B","e":"The oxidative branch of the pentose phosphate pathway generates NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate. NADPH fuels biosynthesis while ribose-5-phosphate feeds nucleotide synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Oxidative PPP branch = NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate | For biosynthesis | Irreversible"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency predisposes to hemolysis due to:","a":"Lack of ATP","b":"Reduced NADPH and impaired glutathione regeneration","c":"Increased 2,3-BPG","d":"Excess H₂O₂ production only","ans":"B","e":"G6PD deficiency predisposes to hemolysis due to reduced NADPH and impaired glutathione regeneration. Antioxidant drugs and fava beans can trigger haemolysis in these patients.<br><br>Take Notes: G6PD deficiency = low NADPH | Impaired glutathione regeneration | Oxidative hemolysis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The primary storage form of glucose in animals is:","a":"Cellulose","b":"Glycogen","c":"Starch","d":"Fructan","ans":"B","e":"The primary storage form of glucose in animals is glycogen. Its highly branched structure allows rapid mobilisation of glucose.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycogen = glucose storage in animals | Liver and muscle | Branched polymer"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Glycogen branching enzyme deficiency (Andersen disease) leads to:","a":"Accumulation of normal glycogen","b":"Limit dextrinosis with abnormal glycogen structure","c":"Increased glycogen storage in liver only","d":"Hypoglycemia only","ans":"B","e":"Glycogen branching enzyme deficiency (Andersen disease) leads to limit dextrinosis with abnormal glycogen structure. The abnormal glycogen triggers cirrhosis and early liver failure.<br><br>Take Notes: Andersen disease = branching enzyme deficiency | Abnormal glycogen | Cirrhosis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes the rate-limiting step of glycolysis?","a":"Hexokinase","b":"Phosphofructokinase-1","c":"Pyruvate kinase","d":"Aldolase","ans":"B","e":"Phosphofructokinase-1 catalyzes the rate-limiting step of glycolysis. AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate activate it, while ATP inhibits it.<br><br>Take Notes: PFK-1 = rate-limiting glycolysis | Committed step | Activated by AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Glucokinase differs from hexokinase by having:","a":"Lower Km for glucose","b":"Higher Km and liver-specific regulation","c":"Inhibition by fructose-6-phosphate","d":"Localization to mitochondria","ans":"B","e":"Glucokinase differs from hexokinase by having a higher Km and liver-specific regulation. Its high Km lets it act as a glucose sensor in liver and beta-cells.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucokinase = high Km, liver-specific | Glucose sensor | Not product-inhibited"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Net ATP yield per glucose from glycolysis alone (cytosolic) is:","a":"1 ATP","b":"2 ATP","c":"4 ATP","d":"6 ATP","ans":"B","e":"The net ATP yield per glucose from glycolysis alone is 2 ATP. Substrate-level phosphorylation, not oxygen, produces this modest yield.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycolysis net = 2 ATP | Plus 2 NADH | Substrate-level phosphorylation"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Lactate dehydrogenase regenerates NAD⁺ by converting pyruvate into:","a":"Acetyl-CoA","b":"Oxaloacetate","c":"Lactate","d":"Alanine","ans":"C","e":"Lactate dehydrogenase regenerates NAD+ by converting pyruvate into lactate. This regenerated NAD+ lets anaerobic glycolysis continue.<br><br>Take Notes: Lactate dehydrogenase makes lactate | Regenerates NAD+ | Anaerobic glycolysis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex requires all EXCEPT:","a":"Thiamine pyrophosphate (B1)","b":"Lipoic acid","c":"FAD","d":"Vitamin C","ans":"D","e":"The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex requires thiamine, lipoate, FAD, NAD, and CoA, but not vitamin C. Vitamin C is the distractor, since it is not a pyruvate dehydrogenase cofactor.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyruvate dehydrogenase needs 5 cofactors | Thiamine, lipoate, FAD, NAD, CoA | Not vitamin C"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase is inhibited by high levels of:","a":"AMP","b":"NAD⁺","c":"Acetyl-CoA","d":"Pyruvate","ans":"C","e":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase is inhibited by high levels of acetyl-CoA. NADH and ATP also inhibit it, signalling energy sufficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetyl-CoA inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase | Product inhibition | Also NADH and ATP"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency typically produces:","a":"Hypolactatemia","b":"Lactic acidosis and neurological dysfunction","c":"Hyperketonemia only","d":"Hypoglycemia exclusively","ans":"B","e":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency typically produces lactic acidosis and neurological dysfunction. Pyruvate is diverted to lactate, producing lactic acidosis.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency = lactic acidosis | Neurological impairment | Pyruvate diverted to lactate"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The TCA cycle generates which reduced cofactors per acetyl-CoA?","a":"1 NADH","b":"2 NADH and 1 FADH₂","c":"3 NADH and 1 FADH₂","d":"4 NADH and 2 FADH₂","ans":"C","e":"The TCA cycle generates 3 NADH and 1 FADH2 per acetyl-CoA. With three proton-pumping complexes, this drives most ATP synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: TCA per acetyl-CoA = 3 NADH, 1 FADH2 | Plus 1 GTP | Feeds the ETC"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Succinyl-CoA synthetase catalyzes formation of which high-energy molecule?","a":"ATP or GTP","b":"NADH","c":"FADH₂","d":"Acetyl-CoA","ans":"A","e":"Succinyl-CoA synthetase catalyzes formation of ATP or GTP by substrate-level phosphorylation. This is the only substrate-level phosphorylation step in the TCA cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Succinyl-CoA synthetase makes GTP or ATP | Substrate-level phosphorylation | Only such TCA step"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Complex I of ETC transfers electrons from NADH to:","a":"Ubiquinone (coenzyme Q)","b":"Cytochrome c","c":"Oxygen","d":"Complex III directly","ans":"A","e":"Complex I of the ETC transfers electrons from NADH to ubiquinone (coenzyme Q). Its higher-energy electrons yield about 2.5 ATP, more than FADH2.<br><br>Take Notes: Complex I: NADH to ubiquinone | Pumps protons | Entry point for NADH"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"FADH₂ contributes electrons to the ETC at which complex?","a":"Complex I","b":"Complex II","c":"Complex III","d":"Complex IV","ans":"B","e":"FADH2 contributes electrons to the ETC at Complex II. Bypassing Complex I, it yields less ATP than NADH.<br><br>Take Notes: FADH2 enters at Complex II | Bypasses Complex I | Yields less ATP"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The final electron acceptor in the electron transport chain is:","a":"NAD⁺","b":"FAD","c":"Oxygen","d":"ATP","ans":"C","e":"The final electron acceptor in the electron transport chain is oxygen. Accepting electrons, it forms water at Complex IV.<br><br>Take Notes: Oxygen = final electron acceptor | Forms water | At Complex IV"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Uncouplers of oxidative phosphorylation increase:","a":"ATP synthesis","b":"Proton gradient","c":"Heat production","d":"Electron transport inhibition only","ans":"C","e":"Uncouplers of oxidative phosphorylation increase heat production. Brown fat uses this to generate heat instead of ATP.<br><br>Take Notes: Uncouplers increase heat | Dissipate proton gradient | Reduce ATP synthesis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Brown fat thermogenesis uses which protein to dissipate proton gradient?","a":"ATP synthase","b":"UCP1 (thermogenin)","c":"Complex IV","d":"Cytochrome c","ans":"B","e":"Brown fat thermogenesis uses UCP1 (thermogenin) to dissipate the proton gradient. By dissipating the proton gradient, it produces heat instead of ATP.<br><br>Take Notes: Brown fat uses UCP1 (thermogenin) | Dissipates proton gradient | Generates heat"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"During absorptive (fed) state, insulin stimulates:","a":"Glycogenolysis","b":"Lipolysis","c":"Glycogenesis and lipogenesis","d":"Gluconeogenesis","ans":"C","e":"During the fed state, insulin stimulates glycogenesis and lipogenesis. Insulin drives storage of glucose as glycogen and fat.<br><br>Take Notes: Fed state insulin = glycogenesis and lipogenesis | Fuel storage | Anabolic"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Post-absorptive state is characterized by increased:","a":"Insulin","b":"Glycogenolysis and lipolysis","c":"Protein synthesis","d":"Fatty acid synthesis","ans":"B","e":"The post-absorptive state is characterized by increased glycogenolysis and lipolysis. Rising glucagon mobilises stored fuel between meals.<br><br>Take Notes: Post-absorptive = glycogenolysis and lipolysis | Fuel mobilization | Glucagon-driven"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"During prolonged fasting, main source of blood glucose is:","a":"Dietary glucose","b":"Hepatic gluconeogenesis","c":"Muscle glycogen","d":"Intestinal absorption","ans":"B","e":"During prolonged fasting, the main source of blood glucose is hepatic gluconeogenesis. Once glycogen is depleted, gluconeogenesis becomes the main glucose source.<br><br>Take Notes: Prolonged fasting glucose = hepatic gluconeogenesis | After glycogen depletion | From lactate, glycerol, amino acids"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Ketone bodies are synthesized from acetyl-CoA in the:","a":"Muscle","b":"Liver mitochondria","c":"Cytosol","d":"Adipose tissue","ans":"B","e":"Ketone bodies are synthesized from acetyl-CoA in the liver mitochondria. Lacking thiophorase, the liver exports rather than uses ketones.<br><br>Take Notes: Ketogenesis in liver mitochondria | From acetyl-CoA | Fuels peripheral tissues"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The major ketone body used by peripheral tissues is:","a":"Acetoacetate","b":"β-Hydroxybutyrate","c":"Acetone","d":"Citrate","ans":"B","e":"The major ketone body used by peripheral tissues is beta-hydroxybutyrate. It interconverts with acetoacetate to fuel peripheral tissues.<br><br>Take Notes: Beta-hydroxybutyrate = major ketone | Peripheral fuel | Interconverts with acetoacetate"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Gluconeogenesis requires which irreversible glycolytic enzyme replacement?","a":"Hexokinase replaced by glucose-6-phosphatase","b":"Phosphofructokinase replaced by FBPase-1","c":"Pyruvate kinase replaced by pyruvate carboxylase and PEP carboxykinase","d":"All of the above","ans":"D","e":"Gluconeogenesis requires replacement of all irreversible glycolytic enzymes by bypass enzymes. These bypass enzymes include pyruvate carboxylase and PEPCK.<br><br>Take Notes: Gluconeogenesis bypasses 3 irreversible glycolytic steps | Pyruvate carboxylase, PEPCK, and others | Reverses glycolysis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which molecule is a gluconeogenic substrate?","a":"Acetyl-CoA only","b":"Fatty acids only","c":"Lactate, glycerol, and glucogenic amino acids","d":"Ketone bodies","ans":"C","e":"Gluconeogenic substrates include lactate, glycerol, and glucogenic amino acids. Fatty acids are notably not gluconeogenic, a common exam trap.<br><br>Take Notes: Gluconeogenic substrates = lactate, glycerol, glucogenic amino acids | Not fatty acids | Maintain blood glucose"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The Randle cycle (glucose-fatty acid cycle) describes:","a":"Mutual inhibition of glucose and fatty acid oxidation","b":"Activation of glycogen synthase by fatty acids","c":"Ketone inhibition of glycolysis","d":"Gluconeogenesis activation by insulin","ans":"A","e":"The Randle cycle describes mutual inhibition of glucose and fatty acid oxidation. This fuel competition determines whether glucose or fat is oxidised.<br><br>Take Notes: Randle cycle = glucose-fatty acid competition | Mutual inhibition | Fuel selection"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes the committed step of fatty acid synthesis?","a":"Fatty acid synthase","b":"Acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC)","c":"Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I","d":"Hormone-sensitive lipase","ans":"B","e":"Acetyl-CoA carboxylase catalyzes the committed step of fatty acid synthesis. It makes malonyl-CoA and is activated by citrate and insulin.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetyl-CoA carboxylase = committed fatty acid synthesis step | Makes malonyl-CoA | Rate-limiting"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Malonyl-CoA inhibits which enzyme to prevent simultaneous fatty acid oxidation?","a":"Hormone-sensitive lipase","b":"Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I (CPT-1)","c":"Fatty acid synthase","d":"Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","ans":"B","e":"Malonyl-CoA inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase I to prevent simultaneous fatty acid oxidation. This reciprocal control prevents futile synthesis and oxidation together.<br><br>Take Notes: Malonyl-CoA inhibits CPT-1 | Blocks fatty acid oxidation during synthesis | Reciprocal regulation"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Fatty acid β-oxidation occurs in the:","a":"Cytosol","b":"Mitochondrial matrix","c":"Endoplasmic reticulum","d":"Nucleus","ans":"B","e":"Fatty acid beta-oxidation occurs in the mitochondrial matrix. Fatty acids enter via the carnitine shuttle to be oxidised.<br><br>Take Notes: Beta-oxidation = mitochondrial matrix | Fatty acids enter via carnitine shuttle | Produces acetyl-CoA"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which cofactor is required for carboxylation reactions (e.g., ACC)?","a":"Biotin","b":"Thiamine","c":"Niacin","d":"Pyridoxal phosphate","ans":"A","e":"Biotin is required for carboxylation reactions such as acetyl-CoA carboxylase. It also serves pyruvate carboxylase in gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Biotin = carboxylation cofactor | Acetyl-CoA carboxylase, pyruvate carboxylase | Carrier of CO2"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The primary lipoprotein that transports dietary triglycerides is:","a":"VLDL","b":"LDL","c":"Chylomicron","d":"HDL","ans":"C","e":"The chylomicron is the primary lipoprotein that transports dietary triglycerides. They have the highest triglyceride content of all lipoproteins.<br><br>Take Notes: Chylomicron carries dietary triglycerides | Intestinal origin | Highest triglyceride content"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which apolipoprotein activates lipoprotein lipase?","a":"ApoA-I","b":"ApoB-100","c":"ApoC-II","d":"ApoE","ans":"C","e":"ApoC-II activates lipoprotein lipase. Its deficiency mimics lipoprotein lipase deficiency, causing chylomicronaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoC-II activates lipoprotein lipase | Enables triglyceride hydrolysis | On chylomicrons and VLDL"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"LDL particles are enriched in which lipid component?","a":"Triglycerides","b":"Unesterified cholesterol","c":"Cholesterol esters","d":"Phospholipids only","ans":"C","e":"LDL particles are enriched in cholesterol esters. This makes LDL the main cholesterol-delivering, atherogenic particle.<br><br>Take Notes: LDL enriched in cholesterol esters | Delivers cholesterol to tissues | Atherogenic"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Reverse cholesterol transport primarily involves which lipoprotein?","a":"LDL","b":"VLDL","c":"HDL","d":"Chylomicrons","ans":"C","e":"Reverse cholesterol transport primarily involves HDL. HDL moves cholesterol from tissues back to the liver.<br><br>Take Notes: HDL = reverse cholesterol transport | Tissues to liver | Cardioprotective"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Familial hypercholesterolemia commonly results from mutation in:","a":"ApoC-II","b":"LDL receptor","c":"LCAT","d":"CETP","ans":"B","e":"Familial hypercholesterolemia commonly results from mutation in the LDL receptor. Defects in this receptor cause familial hypercholesterolaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: Familial hypercholesterolemia = LDL receptor mutation | High LDL | Early atherosclerosis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Tangier disease is characterized by very low HDL due to defect in:","a":"ABCA1 cholesterol transporter","b":"LDL receptor","c":"ApoB-100","d":"LPL","ans":"A","e":"Tangier disease is characterized by very low HDL due to a defect in the ABCA1 cholesterol transporter. Cholesterol accumulates in tissues because efflux onto HDL fails.<br><br>Take Notes: Tangier disease = ABCA1 defect | Very low HDL | Cholesterol accumulates in tissues"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Type I hyperlipoproteinemia involves accumulation of:","a":"LDL","b":"VLDL","c":"Chylomicrons","d":"HDL","ans":"C","e":"Type I hyperlipoproteinemia involves accumulation of chylomicrons. Lipoprotein lipase or ApoC-II deficiency underlies it, with pancreatitis risk.<br><br>Take Notes: Type I = chylomicron accumulation | Lipoprotein lipase or ApoC-II deficiency | Pancreatitis risk"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Elevated triglycerides are most strongly associated with increased risk of:","a":"Coronary artery disease only","b":"Pancreatitis","c":"Liver failure","d":"Stroke exclusively","ans":"B","e":"Elevated triglycerides are most strongly associated with increased risk of pancreatitis. Severe chylomicronaemia is the usual trigger for this.<br><br>Take Notes: High triglycerides = pancreatitis risk | Severe elevation | Chylomicronemia"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting enzyme of:","a":"Fatty acid oxidation","b":"Cholesterol synthesis","c":"Ketone body formation","d":"Bile acid conjugation","ans":"B","e":"HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis. It is the target of statins, which raise LDL receptors and lower LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: HMG-CoA reductase = rate-limiting cholesterol synthesis | Target of statins | Feedback-inhibited by cholesterol"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Statins lower LDL cholesterol primarily by inhibiting:","a":"ACAT","b":"HMG-CoA reductase","c":"LCAT","d":"Lipoprotein lipase","ans":"B","e":"Statins lower LDL cholesterol primarily by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase. This upregulates LDL receptors, further lowering plasma LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase | Upregulate LDL receptors | Lower LDL"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Cholesterol is a precursor for all EXCEPT:","a":"Bile acids","b":"Steroid hormones","c":"Vitamin C","d":"Vitamin D (via skin conversion)","ans":"C","e":"Cholesterol is a precursor for steroid hormones, bile acids, and vitamin D, but not vitamin C. Vitamin C is dietary and not made from cholesterol, the distractor here.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholesterol makes steroids, bile acids, vitamin D | Not vitamin C | Vitamin C is dietary"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which enzyme esterifies cholesterol in plasma on HDL?","a":"ACAT","b":"LCAT","c":"CETP","d":"HMG-CoA reductase","ans":"B","e":"LCAT esterifies cholesterol in plasma on HDL. ApoA-I activates it, and it matures HDL by trapping cholesterol.<br><br>Take Notes: LCAT esterifies plasma cholesterol | On HDL | Activated by ApoA-I"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Small, dense LDL particles are more atherogenic because they:","a":"Are larger and easily cleared","b":"Penetrate endothelium and are oxidized more readily","c":"Are inert","d":"Increase HDL levels","ans":"B","e":"Small, dense LDL particles are more atherogenic because they penetrate endothelium and oxidize more readily. They penetrate the endothelium and oxidise readily, driving plaque.<br><br>Take Notes: Small dense LDL = more atherogenic | Penetrate endothelium | Oxidize easily"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Pregnant women have increased levels of which lipoprotein due to estrogen?","a":"HDL","b":"LDL","c":"VLDL","d":"Chylomicrons","ans":"C","e":"Pregnant women have increased VLDL due to estrogen. This oestrogen-driven rise is a normal feature of pregnancy.<br><br>Take Notes: Pregnancy raises VLDL | Estrogen-driven | Physiological hyperlipidemia"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Lipoprotein lipase deficiency leads to accumulation of:","a":"LDL","b":"Chylomicrons and triglycerides","c":"HDL","d":"VLDL remnants","ans":"B","e":"Lipoprotein lipase deficiency leads to accumulation of chylomicrons and triglycerides. This is type I hyperlipidaemia, which carries pancreatitis risk.<br><br>Take Notes: Lipoprotein lipase deficiency = chylomicron and triglyceride accumulation | Type I hyperlipidemia | Pancreatitis risk"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which enzyme catalyzes esterification of intracellular cholesterol?","a":"LCAT","b":"ACAT","c":"CETP","d":"HMG-CoA reductase","ans":"B","e":"ACAT catalyzes esterification of intracellular cholesterol for storage. In plasma, LCAT esterifies cholesterol on HDL instead.<br><br>Take Notes: ACAT esterifies intracellular cholesterol | For storage | LCAT acts in plasma"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"PCSK9 inhibitors lower LDL by:","a":"Increasing HMG-CoA reductase activity","b":"Enhancing LDL receptor recycling","c":"Inhibiting LCAT","d":"Blocking CETP","ans":"B","e":"PCSK9 inhibitors lower LDL by enhancing LDL receptor recycling. PCSK9 normally degrades LDL receptors, so blocking it lowers LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: PCSK9 inhibitors enhance LDL receptor recycling | More receptors, lower LDL | PCSK9 normally degrades receptors"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which lipoprotein is richest in protein content relative to lipid?","a":"Chylomicrons","b":"VLDL","c":"LDL","d":"HDL","ans":"D","e":"HDL is the lipoprotein richest in protein content relative to lipid. Its high protein content also makes it the densest lipoprotein.<br><br>Take Notes: HDL = highest protein content | Highest density | Reverse cholesterol transport"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Apolipoprotein B-48 is essential for:","a":"VLDL assembly in liver","b":"Chylomicron formation in intestine","c":"HDL maturation","d":"LCAT activation","ans":"B","e":"Apolipoprotein B-48 is essential for chylomicron formation in the intestine. ApoB-100 is instead the hepatic form on VLDL and LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoB-48 = chylomicron formation | Intestinal | Structural apolipoprotein"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"APOE isoforms influence risk of which lipid disorder?","a":"Type I hyperlipoproteinemia only","b":"Type III hyperlipoproteinemia (remnant accumulation)","c":"Tangier disease","d":"LCAT deficiency","ans":"B","e":"APOE isoforms influence risk of type III hyperlipoproteinemia (remnant accumulation). Defective ApoE impairs remnant clearance in this disorder.<br><br>Take Notes: APOE isoforms affect type III hyperlipidemia | Remnant accumulation | Impaired clearance"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The primary pathway for hepatic uptake of chylomicron remnants is mediated by:","a":"LDL receptor recognizing ApoB-100","b":"SR-B1 recognizing HDL","c":"ApoE-mediated receptors","d":"LPL receptor only","ans":"C","e":"The primary pathway for hepatic uptake of chylomicron remnants is mediated by ApoE-mediated receptors. Defective ApoE causes accumulation of IDL and chylomicron remnants.<br><br>Take Notes: Chylomicron remnants cleared via ApoE receptors | Hepatic uptake | ApoE-dependent"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Increased hepatic VLDL secretion occurs in insulin resistance due to:","a":"Decreased FFA flux to liver","b":"Increased free fatty acid influx to liver","c":"Decreased de novo lipogenesis","d":"Increased LPL activity","ans":"B","e":"Increased hepatic VLDL secretion in insulin resistance is due to increased free fatty acid influx to the liver. Excess hepatic triglyceride is packaged and exported as VLDL.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin resistance raises VLDL | Increased free fatty acid influx | Hepatic triglyceride overproduction"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which test best estimates atherogenic LDL particle number clinically?","a":"Total cholesterol","b":"HDL cholesterol","c":"ApoB concentration","d":"Triglycerides only","ans":"C","e":"ApoB concentration best estimates atherogenic LDL particle number clinically. One ApoB sits on each atherogenic particle, so it counts particle number.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoB = atherogenic particle number | One ApoB per LDL particle | Better than LDL cholesterol alone"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Bile acid sequestrants lower LDL by:","a":"Increasing intestinal cholesterol absorption","b":"Decreasing bile acid excretion","c":"Interrupting enterohepatic circulation and increasing hepatic LDL uptake","d":"Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase","ans":"C","e":"Bile acid sequestrants lower LDL by interrupting enterohepatic circulation and increasing hepatic LDL uptake. The liver then upregulates LDL receptors, lowering plasma LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: Bile acid sequestrants interrupt enterohepatic circulation | Liver upregulates LDL receptors | Lowers LDL"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"A mutation causing defective ApoC-II would lead to:","a":"Hypercholesterolemia","b":"Hypertriglyceridemia and defective chylomicron/VLDL clearance","c":"Low triglycerides","d":"High HDL","ans":"B","e":"A mutation causing defective ApoC-II would lead to hypertriglyceridemia and defective chylomicron and VLDL clearance. Both prevent lipoprotein lipase activation, causing chylomicronaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: Defective ApoC-II = hypertriglyceridemia | Lipoprotein lipase not activated | Impaired clearance"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which enzyme converts acetyl-CoA to HMG-CoA in cholesterol synthesis?","a":"HMG-CoA reductase","b":"HMG-CoA synthase","c":"Squalene synthase","d":"Farnesyl transferase","ans":"B","e":"HMG-CoA synthase converts acetyl-CoA to HMG-CoA in cholesterol synthesis. It acts before HMG-CoA reductase in the cholesterol pathway.<br><br>Take Notes: HMG-CoA synthase makes HMG-CoA | Before HMG-CoA reductase | Cholesterol and ketone pathways"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Desaturase enzymes introduce double bonds into fatty acids and require which cofactor?","a":"NAD⁺ only","b":"NADPH and O₂","c":"FADH₂ only","d":"ATP exclusively","ans":"B","e":"Desaturase enzymes introduce double bonds into fatty acids and require NADPH and oxygen. Humans cannot desaturate beyond carbon 9, hence essential fatty acids.<br><br>Take Notes: Desaturases need NADPH and oxygen | Introduce double bonds | Cannot go beyond carbon 9 in humans"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Essential fatty acids include:","a":"Palmitic and stearic acid","b":"Linoleic (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic (omega-3) acids","c":"Oleic acid only","d":"Arachidonic acid only","ans":"B","e":"Essential fatty acids include linoleic (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic (omega-3) acids. Humans cannot make these, so they must come from the diet.<br><br>Take Notes: Essential fatty acids = linoleic and alpha-linolenic | Cannot be synthesized | Dietary requirement"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Synthesis of triacylglycerol in adipose tissue requires:","a":"Glycerol kinase activity","b":"Glycerol-3-phosphate from glycolysis or glycerol","c":"Direct uptake of triglycerides only","d":"Peroxisomal enzymes only","ans":"B","e":"Synthesis of triacylglycerol in adipose tissue requires glycerol-3-phosphate from glycolysis. Adipocytes lack glycerol kinase, so they rely on glycolysis for it.<br><br>Take Notes: Adipose triacylglycerol needs glycerol-3-phosphate | From glycolysis | Adipocytes lack glycerol kinase"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which lipoprotein remnant is most atherogenic after prolonged circulation and modification?","a":"HDL remnant","b":"Chylomicron remnant","c":"VLDL remnant/IDL","d":"Albumin-bound FFA","ans":"C","e":"The VLDL remnant (IDL) is most atherogenic after prolonged circulation and modification. Its cholesterol enrichment makes the remnant atherogenic.<br><br>Take Notes: IDL (VLDL remnant) = atherogenic | Prolonged circulation | Cholesterol-enriched"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"ApoA-I is the main apolipoprotein of:","a":"VLDL","b":"LDL","c":"HDL","d":"Chylomicrons","ans":"C","e":"ApoA-I is the main apolipoprotein of HDL. ApoA-I also activates LCAT for reverse cholesterol transport.<br><br>Take Notes: ApoA-I = main HDL apolipoprotein | Activates LCAT | Reverse cholesterol transport"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Cholesteryl ester transfer protein (CETP) transfers cholesteryl esters from HDL to:","a":"LDL and VLDL","b":"Albumin","c":"Chylomicrons only","d":"Adipose tissue directly","ans":"A","e":"CETP transfers cholesteryl esters from HDL to LDL and VLDL. It exchanges these for triglycerides, redistributing cholesterol.<br><br>Take Notes: CETP transfers cholesteryl esters | HDL to LDL and VLDL | Exchanges for triglycerides"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which dietary component most increases LDL cholesterol levels?","a":"Unsaturated fats","b":"Soluble fiber","c":"Saturated and trans fats","d":"Polyunsaturated omega-3 fats","ans":"C","e":"Saturated and trans fats most increase LDL cholesterol levels. These raise cardiovascular risk and are the main dietary target.<br><br>Take Notes: Saturated and trans fats raise LDL | Dietary influence | Increase cardiovascular risk"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Lp(a) is an LDL-like particle with an added apolipoprotein that increases risk of:","a":"Hemorrhage","b":"Thrombosis and atherosclerosis","c":"Pancreatitis","d":"Hypocholesterolemia","ans":"B","e":"Lp(a) is an LDL-like particle with an added apolipoprotein that increases risk of thrombosis and atherosclerosis. Its level is genetically determined and independent of diet.<br><br>Take Notes: Lp(a) = LDL-like with apo(a) | Increases thrombosis and atherosclerosis | Genetically determined"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which enzyme deficiency causes familial chylomicronemia with eruptive xanthomas?","a":"Hepatic lipase","b":"Lipoprotein lipase or ApoC-II deficiency","c":"LCAT deficiency","d":"CETP deficiency","ans":"B","e":"Lipoprotein lipase or ApoC-II deficiency causes familial chylomicronemia with eruptive xanthomas. Eruptive xanthomas and pancreatitis risk accompany the chylomicronaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: Familial chylomicronemia = lipoprotein lipase or ApoC-II deficiency | Eruptive xanthomas | Pancreatitis risk"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"HDL mediates reverse cholesterol transport to liver via interaction with which receptor?","a":"LDL receptor","b":"SR-BI (scavenger receptor class B type I)","c":"LPL","d":"HMG-CoA reductase","ans":"B","e":"HDL mediates reverse cholesterol transport to the liver via the SR-BI receptor. It delivers cholesterol to the liver for excretion in bile.<br><br>Take Notes: HDL delivers cholesterol via SR-BI | Hepatic receptor | Reverse cholesterol transport"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Statin therapy primarily reduces cardiovascular risk by lowering:","a":"Triglycerides only","b":"LDL cholesterol (apoB-containing particles)","c":"HDL cholesterol only","d":"Bile acid synthesis exclusively","ans":"B","e":"Statin therapy primarily reduces cardiovascular risk by lowering LDL cholesterol (ApoB-containing particles). Lowering these ApoB-containing particles reduces cardiovascular events.<br><br>Take Notes: Statins lower LDL cholesterol | ApoB-containing particles | Reduce cardiovascular events"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Fatty acid elongation and desaturation in humans cannot introduce double bonds beyond carbon:","a":"9","b":"12","c":"15","d":"18","ans":"B","e":"Fatty acid elongation and desaturation in humans cannot introduce double bonds beyond carbon 9. Beyond carbon 9 humans cannot desaturate, hence essential fatty acids.<br><br>Take Notes: Human desaturases limited to carbon 9 | Cannot make omega-3 or omega-6 | Hence essential fatty acids"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The enzyme that transfers fatty acyl groups to carnitine for mitochondrial import is:","a":"Acyl-CoA synthetase","b":"CPT-1 (carnitine palmitoyltransferase I)","c":"Hormone-sensitive lipase","d":"Lipoprotein lipase","ans":"B","e":"CPT-1 (carnitine palmitoyltransferase I) transfers fatty acyl groups to carnitine for mitochondrial import. Malonyl-CoA inhibits it, coupling fatty acid synthesis to reduced oxidation.<br><br>Take Notes: CPT-1 transfers acyl to carnitine | Mitochondrial import | Inhibited by malonyl-CoA"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Impaired bile acid synthesis or bile salt deficiency leads to malabsorption of:","a":"Water-soluble vitamins only","b":"Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K)","c":"Minerals only","d":"Proteins only","ans":"B","e":"Impaired bile acid synthesis or bile salt deficiency leads to malabsorption of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K). This impairs uptake of vitamins A, D, E, and K.<br><br>Take Notes: Bile salt deficiency = fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption | A, D, E, K | Impaired fat absorption"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Familial combined hyperlipidemia typically features elevations in:","a":"LDL only","b":"Triglycerides and LDL (variable phenotypes)","c":"HDL only","d":"Chylomicrons only","ans":"B","e":"Familial combined hyperlipidemia typically features elevations in triglycerides and LDL with variable phenotypes. It is a common inherited dyslipidaemia with variable lipid patterns.<br><br>Take Notes: Familial combined hyperlipidemia = high triglycerides and LDL | Variable phenotype | Common inherited dyslipidemia"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which enzyme is upregulated by insulin to promote lipogenesis in liver?","a":"Hormone-sensitive lipase","b":"Acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC) via dephosphorylation","c":"CPT-1","d":"Glucagon receptor","ans":"B","e":"Insulin upregulates acetyl-CoA carboxylase via dephosphorylation to promote hepatic lipogenesis. This dephosphorylation promotes hepatic fatty acid synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin activates acetyl-CoA carboxylase | By dephosphorylation | Promotes lipogenesis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Measurement of non-HDL cholesterol equals total cholesterol minus:","a":"LDL","b":"HDL","c":"Triglycerides","d":"HDL and VLDL","ans":"B","e":"Non-HDL cholesterol equals total cholesterol minus HDL. It captures all atherogenic particles, making it a useful risk marker.<br><br>Take Notes: Non-HDL cholesterol = total minus HDL | Captures all atherogenic particles | Useful risk marker"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The causal role of LDL cholesterol in atherosclerosis is supported by epidemiology and intervention trials showing that lowering LDL reduces ASCVD events.","a":"True","b":"False","c":"Only observational studies support it","d":"LDL has no role","ans":"A","e":"The causal role of LDL cholesterol in atherosclerosis is supported by epidemiology and intervention trials showing lowering LDL reduces events. Trials lowering LDL reduce events, confirming causation rather than mere correlation.<br><br>Take Notes: LDL is causal in atherosclerosis | Supported by trials | Lowering LDL reduces cardiovascular events"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which amino acid is both glucogenic and ketogenic?","a":"Leucine","b":"Lysine","c":"Isoleucine","d":"Alanine","ans":"C","e":"Isoleucine is both glucogenic and ketogenic, yielding precursors for both glucose and ketones. Being partly ketogenic, it cannot be fully converted to glucose.<br><br>Take Notes: Isoleucine = glucogenic and ketogenic | Dual fate | Branched-chain amino acid"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The rate-limiting enzyme of the urea cycle is:","a":"Arginase","b":"Ornithine transcarbamylase","c":"Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I","d":"Argininosuccinate lyase","ans":"C","e":"Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I is the rate-limiting enzyme of the urea cycle. It is activated by N-acetylglutamate, whose loss also causes hyperammonaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I = rate-limiting urea cycle | Mitochondrial | Activated by N-acetylglutamate"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Hyperammonemia primarily affects the brain because ammonia:","a":"Inhibits glycolysis","b":"Depletes α-ketoglutarate","c":"Inhibits pentose phosphate pathway","d":"Increases ATP synthesis","ans":"B","e":"Hyperammonemia affects the brain because ammonia depletes alpha-ketoglutarate, impairing the TCA cycle. This is why hyperammonaemia causes the encephalopathy of liver failure.<br><br>Take Notes: Ammonia depletes alpha-ketoglutarate | Impairs brain TCA cycle | Neurotoxic"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The cofactor required for carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I activation is:","a":"ATP","b":"N-acetylglutamate","c":"Biotin","d":"NAD⁺","ans":"B","e":"N-acetylglutamate is the cofactor required for carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I activation. Without it, carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I cannot initiate the urea cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: N-acetylglutamate activates carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I | Essential activator | Urea cycle initiation"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Maple syrup urine disease results from deficiency of:","a":"Branched-chain aminotransferase","b":"Branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase","c":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase","d":"Isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase","ans":"B","e":"Maple syrup urine disease results from deficiency of branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase. The branched-chain amino acids accumulate, giving urine a sweet, maple smell.<br><br>Take Notes: Maple syrup urine disease = branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase deficiency | Accumulates branched-chain amino acids | Sweet-smelling urine"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which amino acid is purely ketogenic?","a":"Valine","b":"Isoleucine","c":"Leucine","d":"Threonine","ans":"C","e":"Leucine is a purely ketogenic amino acid. Along with lysine, it cannot contribute to gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Leucine = purely ketogenic | Also lysine | Yields only ketone precursors"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Phenylketonuria is caused by deficiency of:","a":"Tyrosine hydroxylase","b":"Phenylalanine hydroxylase","c":"DOPA decarboxylase","d":"Homogentisate oxidase","ans":"B","e":"Phenylketonuria is caused by deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase. Dietary phenylalanine restriction prevents the intellectual disability.<br><br>Take Notes: PKU = phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency | Phenylalanine accumulates | Intellectual disability if untreated"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"In untreated PKU, accumulation of phenylalanine causes:","a":"Increased melanin synthesis","b":"Severe intellectual disability","c":"Hypoglycemia","d":"Hyperammonemia","ans":"B","e":"In untreated PKU, accumulation of phenylalanine causes severe intellectual disability. Restricting dietary phenylalanine prevents its neurotoxic accumulation.<br><br>Take Notes: Untreated PKU = intellectual disability | From phenylalanine accumulation | Prevented by dietary restriction"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Tyrosine becomes an essential amino acid in deficiency of:","a":"Tyrosinase","b":"Phenylalanine hydroxylase","c":"DOPA decarboxylase","d":"Monoamine oxidase","ans":"B","e":"Tyrosine becomes an essential amino acid in phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency (PKU). Blocked conversion means tyrosine must be supplied in the diet.<br><br>Take Notes: Tyrosine essential in PKU | Phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency | Cannot make tyrosine"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Alkaptonuria results from deficiency of:","a":"Phenylalanine hydroxylase","b":"Homogentisate oxidase","c":"Tyrosinase","d":"Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase","ans":"B","e":"Alkaptonuria results from deficiency of homogentisate oxidase. Homogentisic acid accumulates and darkens cartilage, causing ochronosis.<br><br>Take Notes: Alkaptonuria = homogentisate oxidase deficiency | Homogentisic acid accumulates | Dark urine"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Darkening of urine on standing is characteristic of:","a":"PKU","b":"Alkaptonuria","c":"MSUD","d":"Tyrosinemia type I","ans":"B","e":"Darkening of urine on standing is characteristic of alkaptonuria. Homogentisic acid oxidises on standing, turning the urine black.<br><br>Take Notes: Urine darkens on standing = alkaptonuria | Homogentisic acid oxidizes | Also ochronosis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The primary source of nitrogen for urea synthesis is:","a":"Aspartate and ammonia","b":"Glutamine only","c":"Alanine only","d":"Arginine only","ans":"A","e":"The primary source of nitrogen for urea synthesis is aspartate and ammonia. Aspartate and free ammonia supply the two nitrogen atoms of urea.<br><br>Take Notes: Urea nitrogen from aspartate and ammonia | Two nitrogen sources | Enter the urea cycle"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Citrulline is synthesized in the:","a":"Cytosol","b":"Mitochondria","c":"Nucleus","d":"Lysosome","ans":"B","e":"Citrulline is synthesized in the mitochondria during the urea cycle. It is then exported to the cytosol to continue the urea cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Citrulline made in mitochondria | Then exported to cytosol | Urea cycle intermediate"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Arginine is cleaved by arginase to form:","a":"Ornithine and urea","b":"Citrulline and ammonia","c":"Aspartate and fumarate","d":"Glutamate and urea","ans":"A","e":"Arginine is cleaved by arginase to form ornithine and urea. This is the final step, regenerating ornithine to restart the cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Arginase cleaves arginine | Yields ornithine and urea | Final urea cycle step"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Deficiency of ornithine transcarbamylase causes elevation of:","a":"Citrulline","b":"Orotic acid","c":"Arginine","d":"Urea","ans":"B","e":"Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency causes elevation of orotic acid. Diverted carbamoyl phosphate raises orotic acid, distinguishing it from other defects.<br><br>Take Notes: OTC deficiency = elevated orotic acid | Carbamoyl phosphate diverted | Also hyperammonemia"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The most common urea cycle disorder is:","a":"CPS-I deficiency","b":"Arginase deficiency","c":"OTC deficiency","d":"ASS deficiency","ans":"C","e":"OTC deficiency is the most common urea cycle disorder. Being X-linked, it presents with hyperammonaemia and raised orotic acid.<br><br>Take Notes: OTC deficiency = most common urea cycle disorder | X-linked | Elevated orotic acid"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which vitamin deficiency causes pellagra?","a":"Riboflavin","b":"Niacin","c":"Thiamine","d":"Pyridoxine","ans":"B","e":"Niacin deficiency causes pellagra. It can also arise from tryptophan deficiency, since niacin is made from tryptophan.<br><br>Take Notes: Niacin deficiency = pellagra | Dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia | Also from tryptophan deficiency"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Pellagra is classically associated with:","a":"Anemia, neuropathy, glossitis","b":"Dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia","c":"Bleeding gums and bruising","d":"Night blindness","ans":"B","e":"Pellagra is classically associated with dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia. The three Ds, dermatitis, diarrhoea, and dementia, are the classic mnemonic.<br><br>Take Notes: Pellagra = three Ds | Dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia | Niacin deficiency"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Niacin can be synthesized endogenously from:","a":"Tyrosine","b":"Tryptophan","c":"Phenylalanine","d":"Histidine","ans":"B","e":"Niacin can be synthesized endogenously from tryptophan. Impaired tryptophan absorption in Hartnup disease can therefore cause pellagra.<br><br>Take Notes: Niacin made from tryptophan | Endogenous synthesis | Deficiency in both causes pellagra"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Hartnup disease primarily affects absorption of:","a":"Basic amino acids","b":"Acidic amino acids","c":"Neutral amino acids","d":"Sulfur-containing amino acids","ans":"C","e":"Hartnup disease primarily affects absorption of neutral amino acids. Impaired tryptophan uptake explains its pellagra-like features.<br><br>Take Notes: Hartnup disease = impaired neutral amino acid absorption | Includes tryptophan | Pellagra-like symptoms"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The primary coenzyme form of vitamin B6 is:","a":"Pyridoxine","b":"Pyridoxal phosphate","c":"Pyridoxamine","d":"Pyridine nucleotide","ans":"B","e":"The primary coenzyme form of vitamin B6 is pyridoxal phosphate. As the active form, it serves transamination and decarboxylation reactions.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin B6 = pyridoxal phosphate | Transamination and decarboxylation | Amino acid metabolism"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Vitamin B6 deficiency impairs synthesis of:","a":"Heme","b":"Collagen","c":"Steroid hormones","d":"Fatty acids","ans":"A","e":"Vitamin B6 deficiency impairs synthesis of heme. This impairs ALA synthase, causing sideroblastic anaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: B6 deficiency impairs heme synthesis | ALA synthase needs pyridoxal phosphate | Sideroblastic anemia"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which reaction requires tetrahydrofolate?","a":"Transamination","b":"One-carbon transfer","c":"Decarboxylation","d":"Deamination","ans":"B","e":"Tetrahydrofolate is required for one-carbon transfer reactions. It carries one-carbon units for nucleotide and amino acid synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Tetrahydrofolate = one-carbon transfer | DNA and amino acid synthesis | Folate derivative"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Methionine synthase requires which vitamin?","a":"Vitamin B6","b":"Vitamin B12","c":"Vitamin B1","d":"Vitamin B2","ans":"B","e":"Methionine synthase requires vitamin B12. It also serves methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, linking B12 to odd-chain fatty acids.<br><br>Take Notes: Methionine synthase needs vitamin B12 | Also folate | Links B12 and folate metabolism"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Deficiency of vitamin B12 leads to:","a":"Microcytic anemia","b":"Megaloblastic anemia with neuropathy","c":"Sideroblastic anemia","d":"Hemolytic anemia","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin B12 deficiency leads to megaloblastic anemia with neuropathy. Neurological signs distinguish it from folate deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: B12 deficiency = megaloblastic anemia plus neuropathy | Neurological involvement | Unlike folate deficiency"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Elevated methylmalonic acid is seen in deficiency of:","a":"Folate","b":"Vitamin B12","c":"Vitamin B6","d":"Niacin","ans":"B","e":"Elevated methylmalonic acid is seen in vitamin B12 deficiency. This distinguishes it from folate deficiency, where it is normal.<br><br>Take Notes: Elevated methylmalonic acid = B12 deficiency | Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase impaired | Distinguishes from folate deficiency"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Folate deficiency differs from B12 deficiency by absence of:","a":"Anemia","b":"Neurological symptoms","c":"Elevated homocysteine","d":"Macrocytosis","ans":"B","e":"Folate deficiency differs from B12 deficiency by absence of neurological symptoms. Its large hepatic stores mean deficiency takes years to appear.<br><br>Take Notes: Folate deficiency = no neurological signs | B12 deficiency has neuropathy | Both cause megaloblastic anemia"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The major storage form of vitamin B12 is in the:","a":"Kidney","b":"Liver","c":"Muscle","d":"Brain","ans":"B","e":"The major storage form of vitamin B12 is in the liver. Its lack, as after gastrectomy or in pernicious anaemia, causes deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin B12 stored in the liver | Large reserves | Deficiency takes years to appear"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Absorption of vitamin B12 requires:","a":"Transferrin","b":"Intrinsic factor","c":"Albumin","d":"Bile salts","ans":"B","e":"Absorption of vitamin B12 requires intrinsic factor. Toxic galactose-1-phosphate causes cataracts, liver damage, and intellectual disability.<br><br>Take Notes: B12 absorption needs intrinsic factor | Parietal cell product | Absorbed in terminal ileum"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which enzyme is defective in classic galactosemia?","a":"Galactokinase","b":"Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase","c":"UDP-galactose epimerase","d":"Lactase","ans":"B","e":"Classic galactosemia is caused by a defect in galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase. Galactitol also accumulates, causing the osmotic cataracts.<br><br>Take Notes: Classic galactosemia = galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase defect | Galactose-1-phosphate accumulates | Cataracts and liver damage"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate causes:","a":"Cataracts and liver damage","b":"Ketoacidosis","c":"Hypoglycemia only","d":"Neuropathy","ans":"A","e":"Accumulation of galactose-1-phosphate causes cataracts and liver damage. Aldose reductase converts galactose to this osmotically active galactitol.<br><br>Take Notes: Galactose-1-phosphate accumulation = cataracts and liver damage | Classic galactosemia | Toxic metabolite"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Cataracts in galactosemia are due to accumulation of:","a":"Sorbitol","b":"Galactitol","c":"Fructose","d":"Lactate","ans":"B","e":"Cataracts in galactosemia are due to accumulation of galactitol. Fructose-1-phosphate traps phosphate, inhibiting glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Galactosemia cataracts from galactitol | Osmotic lens damage | Aldose reductase product"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Hereditary fructose intolerance results from deficiency of:","a":"Fructokinase","b":"Aldolase B","c":"Aldolase A","d":"Hexokinase","ans":"B","e":"Hereditary fructose intolerance results from deficiency of aldolase B. Trapped phosphate blocks glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, causing hypoglycaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: Hereditary fructose intolerance = aldolase B deficiency | Fructose-1-phosphate accumulates | Hypoglycemia after fructose"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"In hereditary fructose intolerance, fructose ingestion causes:","a":"Hyperglycemia","b":"Hypoglycemia","c":"Ketoacidosis","d":"Hyperammonemia","ans":"B","e":"In hereditary fructose intolerance, fructose ingestion causes hypoglycemia. Trapped fructose-1-phosphate depletes phosphate, blocking glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Fructose intolerance = hypoglycemia | Fructose-1-phosphate traps phosphate | Inhibits gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Essential fatty acid deficiency presents with:","a":"Night blindness","b":"Dermatitis and alopecia","c":"Osteomalacia","d":"Anemia","ans":"B","e":"Essential fatty acid deficiency presents with dermatitis and alopecia. Linoleic acid is the omega-6 counterpart, both being essential.<br><br>Take Notes: Essential fatty acid deficiency = dermatitis and alopecia | Impaired skin barrier | Poor wound healing"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which fatty acid is an omega-3 fatty acid?","a":"Linoleic acid","b":"Arachidonic acid","c":"α-Linolenic acid","d":"Oleic acid","ans":"C","e":"Alpha-linolenic acid is an omega-3 fatty acid. Prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes all derive from it.<br><br>Take Notes: Alpha-linolenic acid = omega-3 | Essential fatty acid | Linoleic acid is omega-6"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Eicosanoids are derived primarily from:","a":"Linoleic acid","b":"Arachidonic acid","c":"Oleic acid","d":"Stearic acid","ans":"B","e":"Eicosanoids are derived primarily from arachidonic acid. This is the basis of the anti-inflammatory action of NSAIDs.<br><br>Take Notes: Eicosanoids from arachidonic acid | Prostaglandins, thromboxanes, leukotrienes | 20-carbon fatty acid"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Cyclooxygenase inhibition blocks synthesis of:","a":"Leukotrienes","b":"Prostaglandins","c":"Thromboxane only","d":"Lipoxins","ans":"B","e":"Cyclooxygenase inhibition blocks synthesis of prostaglandins. This irreversible acetylation is why aspirin's platelet effect lasts days.<br><br>Take Notes: COX inhibition blocks prostaglandins | Also thromboxanes | NSAID mechanism"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Aspirin irreversibly inhibits cyclooxygenase by:","a":"Competitive inhibition","b":"Acetylation","c":"Phosphorylation","d":"Deamidation","ans":"B","e":"Aspirin irreversibly inhibits cyclooxygenase by acetylation. A carboxylase then converts it to succinyl-CoA for the TCA cycle.<br><br>Take Notes: Aspirin acetylates cyclooxygenase | Irreversible inhibition | Blocks prostaglandin and thromboxane synthesis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"β-oxidation of odd-chain fatty acids yields:","a":"Only acetyl-CoA","b":"Propionyl-CoA","c":"Succinyl-CoA directly","d":"Malonyl-CoA","ans":"B","e":"Beta-oxidation of odd-chain fatty acids yields propionyl-CoA. Deficiency of either cofactor causes methylmalonic acidaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: Odd-chain fatty acids yield propionyl-CoA | Then succinyl-CoA | Enters the TCA cycle"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Propionyl-CoA is converted to succinyl-CoA using:","a":"Biotin and vitamin B12","b":"Thiamine and niacin","c":"FAD and NAD⁺","d":"Lipoic acid","ans":"A","e":"Propionyl-CoA is converted to succinyl-CoA using biotin and vitamin B12. It is the target of statins, which raise LDL receptors and lower LDL.<br><br>Take Notes: Propionyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA needs biotin and B12 | Carboxylase then mutase | Enters TCA cycle"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis is located in the:","a":"Cytosol","b":"Mitochondria","c":"Endoplasmic reticulum","d":"Nucleus","ans":"C","e":"The rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis (HMG-CoA reductase) is located in the endoplasmic reticulum. It activates HMG-CoA reductase, matching synthesis to the fed state.<br><br>Take Notes: HMG-CoA reductase in endoplasmic reticulum | Rate-limiting cholesterol synthesis | Target of statins"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Cholesterol synthesis is increased by:","a":"Glucagon","b":"Insulin","c":"Cortisol","d":"Fasting","ans":"B","e":"Cholesterol synthesis is increased by insulin. 7-alpha-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of this pathway.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin increases cholesterol synthesis | Activates HMG-CoA reductase | Fed-state anabolism"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Bile acids are synthesized from cholesterol in the:","a":"Kidney","b":"Intestine","c":"Liver","d":"Gallbladder","ans":"C","e":"Bile acids are synthesized from cholesterol in the liver. Glycine conjugation improves its solubility for fat emulsification.<br><br>Take Notes: Bile acids made from cholesterol in the liver | 7-alpha-hydroxylase rate-limiting | Aid fat digestion"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The major conjugated bile acid is:","a":"Cholic acid","b":"Chenodeoxycholic acid","c":"Glycocholate","d":"Deoxycholic acid","ans":"C","e":"The major conjugated bile acid is glycocholate. Chenodeoxycholic acid is the other primary bile acid.<br><br>Take Notes: Glycocholate = major conjugated bile acid | Glycine-conjugated | Improves solubility"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Primary bile acids include:","a":"Deoxycholic acid","b":"Lithocholic acid","c":"Cholic acid","d":"Ursodeoxycholic acid","ans":"C","e":"Primary bile acids include cholic acid. Bacterial dehydroxylation forms deoxycholic and lithocholic acids.<br><br>Take Notes: Cholic acid = primary bile acid | Also chenodeoxycholic acid | Made in the liver"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Secondary bile acids are formed by:","a":"Liver enzymes","b":"Pancreatic enzymes","c":"Intestinal bacteria","d":"Kidney enzymes","ans":"C","e":"Secondary bile acids are formed by intestinal bacteria. This impairs uptake of vitamins A, D, E, and K.<br><br>Take Notes: Secondary bile acids from intestinal bacteria | Deoxycholic and lithocholic acid | Bacterial dehydroxylation"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Deficiency of bile salts primarily impairs absorption of:","a":"Carbohydrates","b":"Proteins","c":"Fat-soluble vitamins","d":"Water-soluble vitamins","ans":"C","e":"Deficiency of bile salts primarily impairs absorption of fat-soluble vitamins. It is especially protective of red cell membranes.<br><br>Take Notes: Bile salt deficiency = fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption | A, D, E, K | Impaired fat absorption"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Lipid peroxidation is prevented by which vitamin?","a":"Vitamin A","b":"Vitamin C","c":"Vitamin E","d":"Vitamin K","ans":"C","e":"Lipid peroxidation is prevented by vitamin E. Premature infants are particularly vulnerable to this haemolysis.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin E prevents lipid peroxidation | Membrane antioxidant | Protects red cells"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Vitamin E deficiency primarily causes:","a":"Night blindness","b":"Hemolytic anemia","c":"Rickets","d":"Bleeding tendency","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin E deficiency primarily causes hemolytic anemia. This calcium-binding modification is blocked by warfarin.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin E deficiency = hemolytic anemia | Oxidative membrane damage | Especially in premature infants"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Vitamin K is required for γ-carboxylation of:","a":"Collagen","b":"Glutamate residues in clotting factors","c":"Proline residues","d":"Lysine residues","ans":"B","e":"Vitamin K is required for gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues in clotting factors. This blocks vitamin K recycling, impairing clotting factor synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin K carboxylates glutamate in clotting factors | Factors II, VII, IX, X | Enables calcium binding"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Warfarin acts by inhibiting:","a":"Vitamin K synthesis","b":"Vitamin K epoxide reductase","c":"Thrombin","d":"Platelet aggregation","ans":"B","e":"Warfarin acts by inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase. Avidin in raw egg white binds biotin and blocks its absorption.<br><br>Take Notes: Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase | Blocks vitamin K recycling | Impairs clotting factor synthesis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Biotin deficiency may be caused by:","a":"Raw egg white consumption","b":"High glucose intake","c":"Excess niacin","d":"Iron deficiency","ans":"A","e":"Biotin deficiency may be caused by raw egg white consumption, which contains avidin. This is why raw egg white ingestion causes biotin deficiency.<br><br>Take Notes: Raw egg white causes biotin deficiency | Avidin binds biotin | Blocks absorption"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Avidin binds strongly to:","a":"Thiamine","b":"Biotin","c":"Riboflavin","d":"Niacin","ans":"B","e":"Avidin binds strongly to biotin. Their deficiency causes glossitis, cheilosis, and corneal vascularisation.<br><br>Take Notes: Avidin binds biotin | In raw egg white | Causes biotin deficiency"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Riboflavin deficiency affects which coenzymes?","a":"NAD⁺/NADP⁺","b":"FAD/FMN","c":"TPP","d":"PLP","ans":"B","e":"Riboflavin deficiency affects the FAD and FMN coenzymes. It is the target of no drug but essential for electron flow.<br><br>Take Notes: Riboflavin deficiency affects FAD and FMN | Redox coenzymes | Glossitis and cheilosis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The electron carrier that shuttles electrons from Complex I to III is:","a":"Cytochrome c","b":"Coenzyme Q","c":"Iron-sulfur protein","d":"ATP synthase","ans":"B","e":"Coenzyme Q shuttles electrons from Complex I to Complex III. This blocks oxygen use, causing rapid cellular hypoxia.<br><br>Take Notes: Coenzyme Q shuttles electrons | Complex I to III | Mobile electron carrier"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Cyanide poisoning inhibits which complex of ETC?","a":"Complex I","b":"Complex II","c":"Complex III","d":"Complex IV","ans":"D","e":"Cyanide poisoning inhibits Complex IV of the electron transport chain. Ferric iron cannot bind oxygen, so methylene blue is used to reduce it.<br><br>Take Notes: Cyanide inhibits Complex IV | Cytochrome oxidase | Blocks oxygen use"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Methemoglobinemia involves oxidation of iron to:","a":"Fe²⁺","b":"Fe³⁺","c":"Fe⁴⁺","d":"Fe¹⁺","ans":"B","e":"Methemoglobinemia involves oxidation of iron to the ferric (Fe3+) state. This is why G6PD deficiency causes oxidative haemolysis.<br><br>Take Notes: Methemoglobinemia = iron oxidized to Fe3+ | Cannot bind oxygen | Reduced by methylene blue"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"NADPH is essential for:","a":"Glycolysis","b":"Glutathione reduction","c":"TCA cycle","d":"β-oxidation","ans":"B","e":"NADPH is essential for glutathione reduction, maintaining antioxidant defence. This defence fails in G6PD deficiency, causing oxidative haemolysis.<br><br>Take Notes: NADPH reduces glutathione | Antioxidant defence | From pentose phosphate pathway"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The major antioxidant system in RBCs depends on:","a":"NADH","b":"NADPH","c":"FADH₂","d":"ATP","ans":"B","e":"The major antioxidant system in red blood cells depends on NADPH. It mobilises fatty acids during fasting when insulin is low.<br><br>Take Notes: RBC antioxidant defence needs NADPH | Regenerates glutathione | From pentose phosphate pathway"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which hormone activates hormone-sensitive lipase?","a":"Insulin","b":"Glucagon","c":"Thyroxine","d":"IGF-1","ans":"B","e":"Glucagon activates hormone-sensitive lipase. It recycles lactate from anaerobic tissues back into glucose.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon activates hormone-sensitive lipase | Drives lipolysis | Fasting state"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The Cori cycle links:","a":"Liver and adipose tissue","b":"Liver and muscle","c":"Muscle and kidney","d":"Brain and liver","ans":"B","e":"The Cori cycle links liver and muscle, recycling lactate into glucose. Alanine carries the amino group while glucose returns to muscle.<br><br>Take Notes: Cori cycle links liver and muscle | Lactate to glucose | Recycles anaerobic product"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Alanine cycle primarily transports:","a":"Carbon","b":"Nitrogen","c":"Fatty acids","d":"Ketone bodies","ans":"B","e":"The alanine cycle primarily transports nitrogen from muscle to liver. Ketone use spares muscle protein as fasting continues.<br><br>Take Notes: Alanine cycle transports nitrogen | Muscle to liver | Also delivers carbon for glucose"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"During starvation, muscle protein breakdown eventually:","a":"Increases continuously","b":"Stops abruptly","c":"Decreases after adaptation","d":"Remains constant","ans":"C","e":"During starvation, muscle protein breakdown eventually decreases after adaptation. It also uses ketones and lactate as alternative fuels.<br><br>Take Notes: Muscle proteolysis decreases with adaptation | Ketones spare protein | Preserves muscle"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The primary fuel for heart muscle is:","a":"Glucose","b":"Ketone bodies","c":"Fatty acids","d":"Amino acids","ans":"C","e":"The primary fuel for heart muscle is fatty acids. This ketone use spares glucose and reduces protein breakdown.<br><br>Take Notes: Heart uses fatty acids | Primary fuel | Also ketones and lactate"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Brain begins significant ketone utilization after:","a":"6 hours","b":"12 hours","c":"2-3 days","d":"1 week","ans":"C","e":"The brain begins significant ketone utilization after 2 to 3 days of fasting. Lacking thiophorase, the liver exports rather than uses ketones.<br><br>Take Notes: Brain uses ketones after 2-3 days | Glucose-sparing | Reduces protein breakdown"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Ketone bodies cannot be utilized by:","a":"Brain","b":"Heart","c":"Liver","d":"Skeletal muscle","ans":"C","e":"Ketone bodies cannot be utilized by the liver, which lacks thiophorase. This mitochondrial enzyme is distinct from cytosolic cholesterol synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Liver cannot use ketones | Lacks thiophorase | Exports them to tissues"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Rate-limiting enzyme of ketogenesis is:","a":"Thiolase","b":"HMG-CoA synthase","c":"HMG-CoA lyase","d":"Acetoacetate dehydrogenase","ans":"B","e":"HMG-CoA synthase is the rate-limiting enzyme of ketogenesis. Unrestrained lipolysis floods the liver with fatty acids for ketogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: HMG-CoA synthase = rate-limiting ketogenesis | Mitochondrial | Distinct from cholesterol synthesis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Diabetic ketoacidosis is characterized by:","a":"High insulin and low ketones","b":"Low insulin and high ketones","c":"High insulin and high ketones","d":"Normal insulin and ketones","ans":"B","e":"Diabetic ketoacidosis is characterized by low insulin and high ketones. These acidic ketone bodies produce the anion-gap acidosis of DKA.<br><br>Take Notes: DKA = low insulin, high ketones | Unrestrained lipolysis | Metabolic acidosis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Major cause of acidosis in DKA is:","a":"Lactate","b":"Acetoacetate and β-hydroxybutyrate","c":"Pyruvate","d":"Citrate","ans":"B","e":"The major cause of acidosis in DKA is acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate. Ketone use reduces glucose demand and spares protein.<br><br>Take Notes: DKA acidosis from acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate | Ketone bodies are acidic | Anion gap acidosis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The most important glucose-sparing mechanism in starvation is:","a":"Increased glycolysis","b":"Ketone utilization by brain","c":"Increased insulin secretion","d":"Increased glycogen synthesis","ans":"B","e":"The most important glucose-sparing mechanism in starvation is ketone utilization by the brain. It is the first, committed step, activated by acetyl-CoA.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucose-sparing = brain ketone use | Reduces glucose demand | Preserves protein"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The enzyme that converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate is:","a":"Pyruvate kinase","b":"Pyruvate carboxylase","c":"PEP carboxykinase","d":"Malate dehydrogenase","ans":"B","e":"Pyruvate carboxylase converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate. Its biotin cofactor is shared with other carboxylases.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyruvate carboxylase = pyruvate to oxaloacetate | First gluconeogenesis step | Activated by acetyl-CoA"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Pyruvate carboxylase requires:","a":"Thiamine","b":"Biotin","c":"PLP","d":"FAD","ans":"B","e":"Pyruvate carboxylase requires biotin. This allosteric activation channels pyruvate toward glucose synthesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Pyruvate carboxylase needs biotin | Carboxylation reaction | Gluconeogenesis enzyme"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Acetyl-CoA activates which gluconeogenic enzyme?","a":"PEP carboxykinase","b":"Pyruvate carboxylase","c":"Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase","d":"Glucose-6-phosphatase","ans":"B","e":"Acetyl-CoA activates pyruvate carboxylase, the gluconeogenic enzyme. AMP signals low energy, favouring glycolysis over gluconeogenesis.<br><br>Take Notes: Acetyl-CoA activates pyruvate carboxylase | Promotes gluconeogenesis | Allosteric activator"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase is inhibited by:","a":"Citrate","b":"ATP","c":"AMP","d":"Acetyl-CoA","ans":"C","e":"Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase is inhibited by AMP. Insulin and glucagon control its level to switch between the pathways.<br><br>Take Notes: AMP inhibits fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase | Signals low energy | Slows gluconeogenesis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The key regulatory metabolite linking glycolysis and gluconeogenesis is:","a":"Glucose-6-phosphate","b":"Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate","c":"Pyruvate","d":"Lactate","ans":"B","e":"The key regulatory metabolite linking glycolysis and gluconeogenesis is fructose-2,6-bisphosphate. It activates glycolysis while inhibiting fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase.<br><br>Take Notes: Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate links glycolysis and gluconeogenesis | Activates PFK-1, inhibits FBPase-1 | Controlled by insulin and glucagon"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate activates:","a":"Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase","b":"Pyruvate kinase","c":"Phosphofructokinase-1","d":"Glucose-6-phosphatase","ans":"C","e":"Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate activates phosphofructokinase-1. This raises the metabolite, promoting glycolysis in the fed state.<br><br>Take Notes: Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate activates PFK-1 | Promotes glycolysis | Inhibits fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Insulin increases fructose-2,6-bisphosphate by:","a":"Activating PFK-2","b":"Inhibiting PFK-2","c":"Activating FBPase-2","d":"Inhibiting glucokinase","ans":"A","e":"Insulin increases fructose-2,6-bisphosphate by activating PFK-2. This lowers the metabolite, promoting gluconeogenesis in fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin activates PFK-2 | Raises fructose-2,6-bisphosphate | Promotes glycolysis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Glucagon decreases fructose-2,6-bisphosphate by:","a":"Activating PFK-2","b":"Inhibiting FBPase-2","c":"Activating FBPase-2","d":"Inhibiting PFK-1","ans":"C","e":"Glucagon decreases fructose-2,6-bisphosphate by activating FBPase-2. The kidney contributes more to this in prolonged fasting.<br><br>Take Notes: Glucagon activates FBPase-2 | Lowers fructose-2,6-bisphosphate | Promotes gluconeogenesis"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The primary site of gluconeogenesis during prolonged fasting is:","a":"Muscle","b":"Brain","c":"Liver and kidney","d":"Adipose tissue","ans":"C","e":"The primary site of gluconeogenesis during prolonged fasting is the liver and kidney. It translocates to the membrane in response to insulin.<br><br>Take Notes: Gluconeogenesis in liver and kidney | Kidney contributes more in prolonged fasting | Maintains blood glucose"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The glucose transporter responsible for insulin-dependent uptake is:","a":"GLUT-1","b":"GLUT-2","c":"GLUT-3","d":"GLUT-4","ans":"D","e":"GLUT-4 is the glucose transporter responsible for insulin-dependent uptake. Its high Km lets it act as a glucose sensor in these tissues.<br><br>Take Notes: GLUT-4 = insulin-dependent | Muscle and adipose | Translocates with insulin"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"GLUT-2 is characteristic of:","a":"Brain","b":"Muscle","c":"Liver and pancreatic β-cells","d":"Adipose tissue","ans":"C","e":"GLUT-2 is characteristic of the liver and pancreatic beta-cells. Uptake rises with blood glucose, suiting its sensor role.<br><br>Take Notes: GLUT-2 in liver and beta-cells | High Km glucose sensor | Insulin-independent"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The Km of GLUT-2 is:","a":"Very low","b":"Moderate","c":"High","d":"Zero","ans":"C","e":"The Km of GLUT-2 is high, making it a glucose sensor. Rising ATP closes these channels, depolarising the cell to release insulin.<br><br>Take Notes: GLUT-2 = high Km | Glucose sensor | Uptake proportional to blood glucose"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Insulin secretion from β-cells is triggered by:","a":"Decreased ATP","b":"Opening of K⁺ channels","c":"Closure of K⁺ channels","d":"Decreased Ca²⁺ influx","ans":"C","e":"Insulin secretion from beta-cells is triggered by closure of potassium channels. Closing these channels triggers insulin release, but risks hypoglycaemia.<br><br>Take Notes: Insulin secretion = potassium channel closure | Depolarizes beta-cell | Calcium influx triggers release"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Sulfonylureas act by:","a":"Activating insulin receptors","b":"Opening K⁺ channels","c":"Closing K⁺ channels in β-cells","d":"Inhibiting glucagon","ans":"C","e":"Sulfonylureas act by closing potassium channels in beta-cells, stimulating insulin release. Impaired insulin action, not deficiency, is the primary problem.<br><br>Take Notes: Sulfonylureas close beta-cell potassium channels | Stimulate insulin release | Risk of hypoglycemia"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The primary defect in type 2 diabetes mellitus is:","a":"Absolute insulin deficiency","b":"Insulin resistance","c":"Autoimmune β-cell destruction","d":"Absence of GLUT-4","ans":"B","e":"The primary defect in type 2 diabetes mellitus is insulin resistance. These glycation products drive chronic diabetic tissue damage.<br><br>Take Notes: Type 2 diabetes = insulin resistance | Impaired insulin action | Relative insulin deficiency"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Advanced glycation end products are formed by:","a":"Enzymatic reactions","b":"Non-enzymatic glycation","c":"Oxidative phosphorylation","d":"β-oxidation","ans":"B","e":"Advanced glycation end products are formed by non-enzymatic glycation. It reflects glucose control over the red cell lifespan.<br><br>Take Notes: Advanced glycation end products = non-enzymatic glycation | From chronic hyperglycemia | Tissue damage in diabetes"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"HbA1c reflects average blood glucose over:","a":"1 week","b":"2 weeks","c":"2-3 months","d":"6 months","ans":"C","e":"HbA1c reflects average blood glucose over 2 to 3 months. Osmotic damage from sorbitol underlies diabetic cataracts and neuropathy.<br><br>Take Notes: HbA1c = average glucose over 2-3 months | Red cell lifespan | Diabetes monitoring"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Polyol pathway flux is increased in diabetes leading to accumulation of:","a":"Lactate","b":"Sorbitol","c":"Galactitol","d":"Fructose-1-phosphate","ans":"B","e":"Polyol pathway flux is increased in diabetes, leading to accumulation of sorbitol. Sorbitol dehydrogenase then converts it to fructose.<br><br>Take Notes: Diabetes increases polyol pathway | Sorbitol accumulates | Osmotic tissue damage"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Sorbitol accumulation contributes to:","a":"Cataracts and neuropathy","b":"Ketoacidosis","c":"Hypoglycemia","d":"Hyperuricemia","ans":"A","e":"Sorbitol accumulation contributes to cataracts and neuropathy. Aldose reductase is the first, rate-limiting polyol enzyme.<br><br>Take Notes: Sorbitol accumulation = cataracts and neuropathy | Osmotic damage | Diabetic complications"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Aldose reductase converts glucose to:","a":"Fructose","b":"Sorbitol","c":"Galactose","d":"Lactate","ans":"B","e":"Aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol. Slow sorbitol clearance drives osmotic tissue damage in diabetes.<br><br>Take Notes: Aldose reductase makes sorbitol | Polyol pathway | Increased in diabetes"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The enzyme converting sorbitol to fructose is:","a":"Aldolase","b":"Sorbitol dehydrogenase","c":"Lactate dehydrogenase","d":"Alcohol dehydrogenase","ans":"B","e":"Sorbitol dehydrogenase converts sorbitol to fructose. Reduced glutathione fails, causing oxidative haemolysis.<br><br>Take Notes: Sorbitol dehydrogenase makes fructose | From sorbitol | Second polyol pathway step"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The main source of NADPH in most cells is:","a":"Glycolysis","b":"TCA cycle","c":"Pentose phosphate pathway","d":"β-oxidation","ans":"C","e":"The main source of NADPH in most cells is the pentose phosphate pathway. Denatured haemoglobin forms Heinz bodies, seen in this disorder.<br><br>Take Notes: Pentose phosphate pathway = main NADPH source | For biosynthesis and antioxidant defence | Cytosolic"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Which disorder is associated with deficiency of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase?","a":"Sickle cell disease","b":"Hemolytic anemia","c":"Megaloblastic anemia","d":"Iron deficiency anemia","ans":"B","e":"G6PD deficiency is associated with hemolytic anemia. NADPH regenerates it via glutathione reductase.<br><br>Take Notes: G6PD deficiency = hemolytic anemia | Low NADPH | Oxidative red cell damage"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Heinz bodies are composed of:","a":"Lipids","b":"Denatured hemoglobin","c":"RNA","d":"DNA","ans":"B","e":"Heinz bodies are composed of denatured hemoglobin. Without it, oxidative haemolysis and Heinz bodies occur.<br><br>Take Notes: Heinz bodies = denatured hemoglobin | Seen in G6PD deficiency | Oxidative damage"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Oxidative stress in RBCs is normally prevented by:","a":"Catalase only","b":"Superoxide dismutase only","c":"Reduced glutathione","d":"ATP","ans":"C","e":"Oxidative stress in red blood cells is normally prevented by reduced glutathione. Without it, defective collagen causes bleeding gums and poor healing.<br><br>Take Notes: Reduced glutathione prevents oxidative stress | Regenerated by NADPH | Protects red cells"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Reduced glutathione is regenerated by:","a":"NADH","b":"NADPH","c":"FADH₂","d":"ATP","ans":"B","e":"Reduced glutathione is regenerated by NADPH via glutathione reductase. Without this hydroxylation, collagen is unstable, producing scurvy.<br><br>Take Notes: NADPH regenerates reduced glutathione | Via glutathione reductase | Antioxidant defence"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The primary biochemical defect in scurvy is impaired synthesis of:","a":"Keratin","b":"Elastin","c":"Collagen","d":"Myelin","ans":"C","e":"The primary biochemical defect in scurvy is impaired synthesis of collagen. Its antioxidant action also protects against oxidative damage.<br><br>Take Notes: Scurvy = impaired collagen synthesis | Vitamin C deficiency | Bleeding gums and poor healing"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"Vitamin C is required for hydroxylation of:","a":"Lysine and proline","b":"Glycine and alanine","c":"Valine and leucine","d":"Methionine and cysteine","ans":"A","e":"Vitamin C is required for hydroxylation of lysine and proline in collagen. Without this hydroxylation, collagen is unstable, producing scurvy.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin C for lysine and proline hydroxylation | Collagen synthesis | Deficiency causes scurvy"},
{"t":"MIXED","q":"The MOST critical biochemical role of vitamin C is acting as:","a":"A hormone","b":"A transcription factor","c":"A reducing agent","d":"An enzyme","ans":"C","e":"The most critical biochemical role of vitamin C is acting as a reducing agent. This reducing action also regenerates other antioxidants and aids iron absorption.<br><br>Take Notes: Vitamin C = reducing agent | Hydroxylation reactions | Antioxidant"}
];